const r=String.raw,y={id:"2.1.1",module:5,title:"Cause & Effect / Fishbone Diagrams",subtitle:"Brainstorm every possible X — organised by the 6Ms",minutes:13,objectives:["Explain the purpose of a cause-and-effect (fishbone / Ishikawa) diagram and where it sits in the Measure phase.","Write a precise, measurable problem statement for the head of the fish and organise causes under the 6Ms or a service variant such as the 4Ps.","Facilitate a fishbone session and drill each branch with the 5 Whys until you reach an actionable potential cause.","Classify every cause as Controllable (C), Noise (N) or Procedural/SOP and explain why the tag matters.","Route fishbone output correctly: controllable X’s to the X-Y matrix, procedural and noise X’s to the FMEA.","Treat every fishbone cause as a hypothesis that must be verified with data in Analyze."],overview:r`A [[fishbone|cause-and-effect diagram]] — also called a fishbone or Ishikawa diagram — is a structured brainstorming picture. The **effect** (the problem, your Y) sits at the head of the fish; the **potential causes** (candidate X’s) hang off the bones, grouped into categories so that nothing obvious is forgotten. The training book’s glossary describes it as a pictorial diagram showing all possible variables that could affect a given process output measure.

In DMAIC the fishbone belongs to **Measure — Process Discovery**. Its job is breadth, not proof: the Measure-phase goal is to *define, explore and classify* X variables using detailed process mapping, fishbone diagrams, X-Y matrices and FMEA. A good fishbone is wide (every plausible cause is on it), deep (each branch is drilled with “why?”) and tagged (each cause is marked controllable, noise or procedural). It is then handed to the [[x-y-matrix|X-Y matrix]] and the [[fmea|FMEA]] for prioritisation — and every cause on it is still only a **hypothesis** until Analyze proves or disproves it with data. Our running example throughout: *P2 incident MTTR is above the 4-hour SLA*.`,sections:[{id:"purpose",heading:"Purpose: one effect, every plausible cause",body:r`Remember [[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]]. Define gave you a well-scoped Y; Measure must now find **all** the X’s that might drive it, because you cannot improve a Y while an important X is invisible. The book names three primary sources of X’s: **process mapping**, **fishbone analysis** and **basic data analysis** (graphical and statistical). The fishbone is the one that taps the team’s experience most directly.

What a fishbone does well:

- **Widens thinking.** Categories (the bones) prompt the team to look beyond the cause they already “know” — usually People.
- **Organises a messy brainstorm.** Forty sticky notes become six readable branches.
- **Builds shared understanding.** Engineers, dispatchers and vendor staff see the whole problem on one wall.
- **Feeds the next tools.** The list of potential causes becomes the rows of the X-Y matrix and the “potential causes of failure” column of the FMEA.

What it does **not** do: it does not rank causes, it does not measure anything, and it does not prove causation. The book’s quiz states it plainly — the fishbone is a tool to **generate ideas about possible causes** for defects. Its warning about the X-Y matrix applies equally here: this is *organised brainstorming*, not real data, and the team may later learn that a cause it felt sure about is insignificant.

> **Where it sits.** In the book’s Measure roadmap the sequence is: detailed problem statement → detailed process mapping → *identify all process X’s causing problems (fishbone, process map)* → *select the vital few X’s (X-Y matrix, FMEA)* → measurement system → stability → capability.`},{id:"anatomy",heading:"Anatomy: the head, the spine and the 6Ms",body:r`**The head = the problem statement.** Write the effect as a specific, measurable symptom of the Y — never a cause and never a solution. Weak head: “Slow incident handling.” Strong head: “**P2 incident MTTR averaged 5.15 h against a 4 h SLA in Q3; 38% of P2 incidents breached.**” A vague head produces vague causes; a head that contains a cause (“MTTR high because of the vendor”) closes the brainstorm before it begins. Reuse the [[problem-statement|problem statement]] from the charter, sharpened with baseline data.

**The spine** is the horizontal arrow into the head. **Major bones** are the categories; **minor bones** are causes; smaller bones off those are sub-causes found by asking “why?”.

**The 6Ms** are the classic manufacturing categories — memorise them:

| Category | Ask… | MTTR example |
|---|---|---|
| **Man / People** | Skills, staffing, handovers, workload? | L2 engineers not yet skilled on the new core platform |
| **Machine** | Tools, systems, equipment, software? | Alarm flood: monitoring has no event correlation |
| **Method** | Procedures, sequence, rules, work instructions? | Field team dispatched before remote diagnosis |
| **Material** | Inputs consumed: parts, data, information? | Spare line cards out of stock at the regional depot |
| **Measurement** | How the Y and X’s are measured and recorded? | Restore time stamped at closure, not at restoration |
| **Mother Nature / Environment** | Conditions outside the process? | Summer heat causing site power and cooling faults |

**Service variants.** Transactional teams often find the **4Ps** more natural: **Policies** (rules and decisions from above), **Procedures** (how work is done), **People**, and **Plant** (buildings, equipment, systems — sometimes called Place). Another common service set is the 4Ss: Surroundings, Suppliers, Systems, Skills. Categories are a prompt, not a law: you may also use the **major steps of your process map** as bones (a *process fishbone*), which works well once the Level 1 map exists. Whatever set you choose, the exam expects you to recognise the 6Ms and the 4Ps by name.`},{id:"session",heading:"Running a fishbone session",body:r`The book’s exercise starts in exactly the right place: *retrieve the high-level process map for your project and use it to complete a fishbone, ideally with your project team.* A practical sequence:

1. **Prepare the head.** Agree the problem statement wording with the process owner before the session. Draw the spine and the category bones on a wall or whiteboard.
2. **Invite the right people.** Cross-functional: people who do the work (NOC engineers, field technicians, dispatchers), people who receive it (service desk), data owners and — where a vendor runs the process — vendor team leads. Five to eight participants is a workable size.
3. **Brainstorm silently first.** Each person writes one cause per sticky note for 8–10 minutes. Silent writing stops the loudest voice or the most senior person from steering the list.
4. **Place and cluster.** Participants read their notes aloud and stick them under a category. Merge duplicates; do not argue about which bone a note belongs on — placement is a convenience, not a classification test.
5. **Drill each branch.** Ask “why does that happen?” repeatedly (the 5 Whys, below) and add sub-bones.
6. **Check balance.** An empty bone is a prompt (“nothing at all under Measurement?”). A crowded People bone is a warning sign that the team is blaming individuals instead of the system.
7. **Tag each cause C, N or SOP** (next section).
8. **Close with next steps.** The output is a list of candidate X’s for the X-Y matrix and FMEA — not a list of solutions to implement on Monday.

**Facilitation rules:** no criticism during generation; causes, not solutions (“no spares at depot”, not “buy more spares”); no names (“handover gap on night shift”, not “Ahmed forgets”); keep the head visible at all times.`},{id:"five-whys",heading:"Drilling with the 5 Whys",body:r`A cause written on the first pass is usually a **symptom one level down**. The 5 Whys pushes each branch towards a cause the team could actually act on. Take the Material bone:

1. *Why is MTTR above SLA?* — Many P2 faults wait for a replacement line card.
2. *Why do they wait?* — The regional depot is frequently out of stock.
3. *Why is it out of stock?* — Reorder points were set from failure rates measured three years ago.
4. *Why were they not updated?* — There is no periodic review of the spares model.
5. *Why is there no review?* — No role owns spares planning since the managed-service transition.

“Five” is a guideline, not a rule: stop when the answer is something the process could control or change, and stop before you drift into philosophy (“because budgets are finite”). Three cautions:

- **Branch, don’t chain blindly.** Most “whys” have more than one answer; each answer becomes its own sub-bone.
- **Verify each link.** Ask “how do we know?” — every link is a hypothesis, too.
- **Avoid the blame stop.** “Because the engineer made a mistake” is rarely a root cause; ask why the system allowed the mistake.

The result is a deeper bone: *no owner for spares planning → stale reorder points → depot stock-outs → waiting for parts → MTTR above SLA*.`},{id:"cn-sop",heading:"Tagging causes: Controllable, Noise, Procedural (C/N/SOP)",body:r`The book classifies every process input into three types, and the classification travels with the cause from the fishbone into the X-Y matrix and the FMEA “Class” column:

- **Controllable (C)** — [[controllable-input|inputs you can adjust or control while the process is running]]: in manufacturing speed, feed rate, temperature or pressure; in operations, spare stock levels, alarm correlation rules, dispatch rules, staffing per shift.
- **Procedural (P, often written SOP)** — inputs governed by a standardised set of activities that brings a step to completion: work instructions, checklists, fixed data-entry forms, escalation matrices, handover procedures.
- **Noise (N)** — [[noise-input|things you think you cannot control]], are unaware of, or that are too expensive or difficult to control: ambient temperature, weather, road distance to remote sites, the individual caller, supplier RMA lead time.

Why tag? Because each type is handled differently:

| Tag | What you do next | MTTR examples |
|---|---|---|
| **C** | Carry into the **X-Y matrix** to find the vital few; breakthrough comes mainly from controllable X’s. | Spare stock level; alarm correlation; dispatch rule |
| **SOP** | First check **compliance**: is the procedure actually followed? The book insists all procedures must be in total compliance — fixing non-compliance can remove defects and let you focus on the controllable X’s. | Escalation matrix; shift handover; clock-stop recording |
| **N** | Carry into the **FMEA**: noise raises risk (and the RPN) under current methods and points to where investment or robust design is justified. | Heatwaves; remote-site access; vendor RMA time |

Two subtleties examiners like. First, even a controllable input is never *perfectly* controlled — it still varies naturally within its limits. Second, a tag can change: “site access time” is noise today, but becomes controllable if you pre-position spares at a hub. Ask of every cause: *is it, or should it be, controlled?* If you decide it should be controlled, you have implied a requirement and a measurement for it.`},{id:"link-xy-fmea",heading:"From fishbone to X-Y matrix and FMEA",body:r`The fishbone is the first link in the Measure chain. The book’s next exercise is literally “using the fishbone diagram created earlier, create an X-Y matrix”, and its FMEA pages say the *potential causes of failure* — and their C/P/N class — should be obtained from the fishbone.

**Into the X-Y matrix (2.1.3, preview).** List the controllable X’s as rows; list the Y’s as columns with importance weights (1–10); score each X’s effect on each Y as 0, 1, 3 or 9; multiply and add. With weights MTTR = 10 and first-time-fix = 6:

| Potential X | MTTR (10) | FTF (6) | Score |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Spare stock at depot | 9 | 3 | 10×9 + 6×3 = **108** |
| Alarm correlation | 3 | 9 | 10×3 + 6×9 = **84** |
| Ticket colour scheme | 1 | 1 | 10×1 + 6×1 = **16** |

**Into the FMEA (2.1.4, preview).** Procedural and noise causes become failure causes with Severity × Occurrence × Detection ratings; e.g. “heatwave cooling failure” rated S = 8, O = 5, D = 6 gives RPN = **240**.

> **Exam-tools tip:** X-Y and RPN arithmetic is simple multiplication — use the exam’s virtual calculator or the notepad, write each product separately, then add. The fishbone itself has no formula in the Reference Document; questions on it test purpose, structure, categories and sequence.`},{id:"hypotheses",heading:"Causes are hypotheses — verified in Analyze",advanced:!0,body:r`A fishbone records **opinion** — informed, valuable opinion, but opinion. The DMAIC roadmap is explicit: Measure *identifies and prioritises* all X’s; Analyze *proves or disproves the impact X’s have on the problem*. Writing “root cause” on a fishbone note is premature; the correct term at this stage is **potential** cause.

How the hypotheses get tested later:

- **Stratify the Y by the X.** Compare MTTR for incidents that waited for spares versus those that did not (graphs in 2.2.4, hypothesis tests in Analyze).
- **Multi-vari studies** (Analyze 3.1) separate positional, cyclical and temporal variation — e.g. does MTTR vary more by region, by shift or by month?
- **Formal tests** — two-sample t, ANOVA, chi-square — return a p-value; if p < α you reject H₀ and have evidence that the X matters; otherwise you *fail to reject* and the cause stays unproven.

A quick data sanity check is still worth doing in Measure. A sample of ten P2 restoration times — 3.2, 4.8, 6.1, 2.9, 7.4, 5.5, 4.1, 8.0, 3.6 and 5.9 h — has a mean of **5.15 h** (median also 5.15 h) and 7 of 10 above the 4 h SLA, so the head of the fish is supported by data before the team spends two hours brainstorming.

**Common fishbone failures:** a head that is a solution (“need more engineers”); stopping at symptoms; a People bone full of names; treating the most-voted cause as proven; and never updating the diagram when Analyze disproves a branch. The diagram is a living document — strike through disproved causes and add new ones the data reveals.`}],whyItMatters:r`Most failed improvement projects did not fail at statistics — they failed because the team fixed the cause it *assumed* rather than the cause that *mattered*. The fishbone protects against that by forcing breadth before focus, and its C/N/SOP tags decide which tool handles each cause next. On the exam, expect questions on what goes in the head, the names of the 6Ms and 4Ps, which classification a cause belongs to, where fishbone output goes next, and — very often — whether fishbone causes are proven (they are not).

For a service-operations engineer, it is also a fair, non-confrontational way to examine vendor performance: the diagram describes the **system**, not individuals, so internal and vendor teams can work on the same wall.`,businessExample:{title:"P2 MTTR above SLA in a managed-services operation",body:r`A DSO unit monitors a vendor-run incident process. Over Q3, **156 of 410** P2 incidents (about **38%**) breached the 4-hour restoration SLA. Rather than issue a finding, the Green Belt runs a fishbone workshop with NOC engineers, field dispatch and the vendor’s shift leads.

The head reads: *“P2 MTTR averaged 5.15 h vs 4 h SLA in Q3 (sample); 38% breached.”* Thirty-one notes land on six bones. Drilling the Material bone exposes the spares-planning gap; the Measurement bone raises a question about whether “restored” timestamps are recorded at restoration or at ticket closure — an SOP cause that could shift the baseline itself.

The vendor-facing summary is written as requests: *the workshop identified potential causes that require clarification; the team is asked to confirm how restoration timestamps are recorded and to provide spares-availability data for the affected incidents*. Controllable X’s go to the X-Y matrix; SOP and noise causes go to the FMEA; nothing is called a root cause until Analyze tests it.`},visual:"fishboneBuild",visualCaption:"A fishbone for “P2 MTTR above the 4 h SLA” builds bone by bone through the 6Ms. One branch is drilled with the 5 Whys, and every cause is tagged C (controllable), N (noise) or SOP (procedural). Controllable causes go to the X-Y matrix, while SOP and noise causes go to the FMEA. All of them stay hypotheses until Analyze.",definitions:[{term:"Cause-and-effect (fishbone / Ishikawa) diagram",definition:"A diagram shaped like a fish skeleton that shows all possible variables (causes) that could affect a given process output (the effect at the head)."},{term:"Effect (head of the fish)",definition:"The specific, measurable problem or Y being investigated — written as a symptom, never as a cause or a solution."},{term:"Major bone (category)",definition:"A heading such as Machine or Method that groups related potential causes and prompts the team to look in that area."},{term:"6Ms",definition:"Man/People, Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, Mother Nature/Environment — the classic fishbone categories."},{term:"4Ps",definition:"Policies, Procedures, People, Plant (or Place) — a common category set for service and transactional processes."},{term:"Potential cause",definition:"A suspected X written on the fishbone; a hypothesis until data verify it in Analyze."},{term:"5 Whys",definition:"Asking “why?” repeatedly (about five times) to drill from a symptom to an actionable underlying cause."},{term:"Controllable input (C)",definition:"An input that can be adjusted or controlled while the process is running."},{term:"Procedural input (SOP / P)",definition:"An input governed by a standardised set of activities (procedure, checklist, fixed form) that brings a step to completion."},{term:"Noise input (N)",definition:"An input you believe you cannot control, are unaware of, or that is too expensive or difficult to control."},{term:"Process fishbone",definition:"A variant whose major bones are the main steps of the process map rather than the 6Ms."}],comparisons:[{title:"Controllable vs Procedural vs Noise inputs",headers:["","Controllable (C)","Procedural (SOP)","Noise (N)"],rows:[["Meaning","Adjustable while the process runs","Set by a standardised procedure","Not controlled — can’t, won’t or unaware"],["Telecom example","Spare stock level; alarm correlation rules","Escalation matrix; handover checklist","Heatwave; remote-site distance; caller behaviour"],["Next tool","X-Y matrix (find vital few)","Compliance check, then FMEA","FMEA (raises risk / RPN)"],["Typical action","Optimise and control the setting","Enforce, mistake-proof, standardise","Design robustness; justify investment"]]},{title:"6Ms vs 4Ps category sets",headers:["","6Ms","4Ps"],rows:[["Categories","Man/People, Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, Mother Nature","Policies, Procedures, People, Plant/Place"],["Best fit","Manufacturing, network operations, field work","Service, transactional, administrative processes"],["Strength","Explicit Measurement and Environment bones","Separates management policy from working procedure"],["Watch out","Feels “factory” to office teams","No explicit Measurement bone — add one if data quality is suspect"]]},{title:"Fishbone vs X-Y matrix vs FMEA",headers:["","Fishbone (2.1.1)","X-Y matrix (2.1.3)","FMEA (2.1.4)"],rows:[["Question","What could cause the effect?","Which X’s matter most to the Y’s?","How could each step fail, and how risky is it?"],["Output","Categorised list of potential causes","Ranked X’s (weight × score)","RPN-ranked failure modes and actions"],["Quantified?","No","Team scores (opinion)","Team ratings (opinion), updated with data"],["Proves causation?","No","No","No — Analyze does"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"The fishbone identifies the root cause.",reality:"It lists potential causes. Root causes are verified with data in Analyze."},{myth:"You must use exactly the 6Ms.",reality:"Categories are prompts. 4Ps, 4Ss or the process steps are equally valid if they fit the process."},{myth:"The head should name the main suspected cause.",reality:"The head is the effect — a measurable problem statement. Putting a cause there narrows the brainstorm."},{myth:"The 5 Whys always needs exactly five questions.",reality:"Five is a guideline; stop when you reach a cause the process can act on, and branch where there are several answers."},{myth:"Noise inputs can be ignored because nothing can be done about them.",reality:"Noise goes to the FMEA, where it raises risk and can justify robust design or investment; some noise can become controllable."},{myth:"A crowded People bone means the people are the problem.",reality:"It usually means the team is blaming individuals. Ask why the system allows the error."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“What is placed at the head of a fishbone diagram?” with a tempting option “the root cause”.",explanation:"The head is the effect / problem statement (the Y). Root causes are not known yet."},{trap:"“Which of the following is NOT one of the 6Ms?” — options include Management or Money.",explanation:"The 6Ms are Man/People, Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, Mother Nature. Management and Money are not among them.",tag:"not"},{trap:"“The fishbone has identified the root cause, so the team should…”",explanation:"A fishbone cannot verify a root cause. The best next step is to prioritise (X-Y/FMEA) and then test the causes with data in Analyze.",tag:"verified-root-cause"},{trap:"“Ambient temperature in an outdoor cabinet is best classified as…”",explanation:"Noise (N): outside the team’s practical control under the current design."},{trap:"“Which fishbone outputs should go FIRST into the X-Y matrix?”",explanation:"The controllable X’s. Procedural and noise X’s are carried to the FMEA.",tag:"first"},{trap:"“The PRIMARY purpose of a cause-and-effect diagram is to…” with options such as “rank causes” or “quantify impact”.",explanation:"Its primary purpose is to identify and organise potential causes. Ranking is the X-Y matrix’s job; quantifying is Analyze’s.",tag:"primary"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Writing the head of the fish",problem:r`A team proposes the head “Engineers are too slow fixing faults because of the vendor.” Rewrite it so that it supports a good brainstorm.`,steps:["Remove the embedded cause (“because of the vendor”) and the blame (“engineers too slow”).","Name the measurable Y and its scope: P2 incident MTTR, Q3, all regions.","Add the gap against the requirement with baseline data: mean 5.15 h vs 4 h SLA; 156 of 410 (≈38%) breached.","Final head: **“P2 MTTR averaged 5.15 h vs 4 h SLA in Q3; 38% of P2 incidents breached.”**"],answer:"A measurable effect statement with scope and gap, free of causes and solutions.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Head = “Vendor engineers lack skills.”",whyWrong:"That is a single suspected cause. Placed at the head, it stops the team from considering Machine, Material, Measurement and Environment causes."}},{title:"Sorting causes onto bones and tagging C / N / SOP",problem:r`Classify these MTTR causes into a 6M category and a C/N/SOP tag: (a) spare line cards out of stock at depot; (b) escalation matrix not followed at night; (c) summer heat causing cooling faults; (d) restore time stamped at ticket closure; (e) alarm flood with no correlation.`,steps:["(a) **Material**, **C** — stock levels are adjustable while the process runs.","(b) **Method**, **SOP** — a standard procedure exists; the first check is compliance.","(c) **Mother Nature / Environment**, **N** — not practically controllable today.","(d) **Measurement**, **SOP** — a recording procedure; it may also distort the baseline Y.","(e) **Machine**, **C** — correlation rules in the monitoring tool can be configured."],answer:"a Material/C · b Method/SOP · c Environment/N · d Measurement/SOP · e Machine/C",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Tag (b) as People/N because “people don’t follow it”.",whyWrong:"A defined procedure that is not followed is a procedural (SOP) issue. Labelling it noise discards it from improvement and blames individuals rather than the system."}},{title:"5 Whys on the Material bone",problem:r`Starting from “P2 faults wait for replacement line cards”, drill to an actionable cause.`,steps:["Why wait? → Regional depot frequently out of stock.","Why out of stock? → Reorder points use failure rates from three years ago.","Why not updated? → No periodic review of the spares model.","Why no review? → No role owns spares planning since the managed-service transition.","Stop: this is something the organisation can act on. Record it as a **potential** cause to verify."],answer:"Potential cause: no owner for spares planning → stale reorder points → stock-outs → waiting → MTTR breach.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Stop at “depot out of stock” and buy more spares.",whyWrong:"That stops at a symptom and jumps to a solution. Without fixing the planning process, stock-outs will recur, and the cause has not even been verified with data yet."}},{title:"Routing fishbone output to the next tools",problem:r`From the tagged list, which causes go to the X-Y matrix and which go to the FMEA? Using Y weights MTTR = 10 and first-time-fix = 6, score “spare stock at depot” (9, 3) and “alarm correlation” (3, 9).`,steps:["Controllable X’s (spares, alarm correlation) → X-Y matrix.","Procedural (escalation, timestamps) → compliance check, then FMEA. Noise (heat) → FMEA.","Spare stock: 10 × 9 + 6 × 3 = 90 + 18 = **108**.","Alarm correlation: 10 × 3 + 6 × 9 = 30 + 54 = **84**.","Spare stock ranks higher — still a team opinion until Analyze verifies it."],answer:"C → X-Y (108 vs 84); SOP and N → FMEA.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Score = 9 + 3 = 12 for spares and 3 + 9 = 12 for correlation — a tie.",whyWrong:"Adding raw scores ignores how important each Y is. Each score must be multiplied by its Y weight before summing."}}],lab:"fishbone",check:[{id:"chk-2.1.1-1",topic:"2.1.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"What should be written at the **head** of a fishbone diagram?",options:["The verified root cause of the problem","The effect — a specific, measurable problem statement","The proposed solution the team will implement","The list of 6M categories"],answer:1,explanation:"The head holds the effect (the Y) as a measurable problem statement. Causes hang off the bones.",whyWrong:["Root causes are not known yet; the diagram exists to find potential causes.","","Solutions do not belong on a fishbone; they come in Improve.","The categories are the major bones, not the head."]},{id:"chk-2.1.1-2",topic:"2.1.1",category:"incorrect-statement",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Which of the following is **NOT** one of the classic 6M fishbone categories?",options:["Measurement","Mother Nature","Management","Material"],answer:2,explanation:"The 6Ms are Man/People, Machine, Method, Material, Measurement and Mother Nature/Environment. Management is not one of them.",whyWrong:["Measurement is one of the 6Ms.","Mother Nature (environment) is one of the 6Ms.","","Material is one of the 6Ms."],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-2.1.1-3",topic:"2.1.1",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"On an MTTR fishbone, “road distance to remote cell sites” is tagged. Under current operations, which classification fits **best**?",options:["Controllable (C)","Procedural (SOP)","Noise (N)","Critical X (proven)"],answer:2,explanation:"Distance to remote sites cannot practically be adjusted while the process runs, so it is noise. It goes to the FMEA, where it raises risk and may justify spares hubs.",whyWrong:["Controllable inputs can be adjusted while the process runs; site distance cannot.","It is not governed by a procedure.","","Nothing on a fishbone is proven; “critical X” status needs Analyze data."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-2.1.1-4",topic:"2.1.1",category:"dmaic-boundary",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A team finishes its fishbone and votes “no spares at depot” as the top cause. What is the **best** next step?",options:["Implement a spares purchase immediately","Close Measure and declare the root cause found","Prioritise the causes with the X-Y matrix and FMEA, then verify them with data in Analyze","Remove all noise causes from the diagram"],answer:2,explanation:"Fishbone causes are hypotheses. They are prioritised in Measure (X-Y, FMEA) and proven or disproven with data in Analyze before solutions are chosen.",whyWrong:["Jumping to a solution skips verification.","A vote is opinion, not evidence; no root cause is verified yet.","","Noise causes are kept and carried to the FMEA."],traps:["verified-root-cause","best"]},{id:"chk-2.1.1-5",topic:"2.1.1",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which fishbone causes should be carried **first** into the X-Y matrix to find the vital few?",options:["Noise causes","Controllable causes","Procedural (SOP) causes","Only causes on the People bone"],answer:1,explanation:"Breakthrough mainly comes from controllable X’s, which feed the X-Y matrix. Procedural causes need a compliance check and, with noise causes, go to the FMEA.",whyWrong:["Noise causes go to the FMEA, where they raise risk.","","SOP causes are checked for compliance first and then handled in the FMEA.","Category does not decide routing; the C/N/SOP tag does."],traps:["first"]},{id:"chk-2.1.1-6",topic:"2.1.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"When should a team stop asking “why?” in a 5 Whys drill?",options:["After exactly five questions","When it reaches a cause the process or organisation can act on","When it reaches a person to hold accountable","When the team runs out of time"],answer:1,explanation:"“Five” is a guideline. Stop at an actionable cause, then record it as a potential cause to verify.",whyWrong:["The count is a rule of thumb, not a requirement.","","Stopping at a person is the blame trap; ask why the system allowed the error.","Time pressure is not a criterion; an unfinished branch stays open."]}],takeaways:["A fishbone organises potential causes (X’s) of one effect (Y); it widens thinking but proves nothing.","The head is a measurable problem statement — not a cause, not a solution.","6Ms: Man/People, Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, Mother Nature. Service variant 4Ps: Policies, Procedures, People, Plant.","Run it with a cross-functional team, silent brainstorming first, and the process map in view.","Drill branches with the 5 Whys until the cause is actionable; avoid stopping at blame.","Tag each cause C, N or SOP: C → X-Y matrix; SOP → compliance check, then FMEA; N → FMEA.","Every fishbone cause is a hypothesis to be verified with data in Analyze."],flashcards:[{front:"Other names for the fishbone",back:"Cause-and-effect diagram; Ishikawa diagram."},{front:"What goes at the head?",back:"The effect: a specific, measurable problem statement (the Y)."},{front:"The 6Ms",back:"Man/People, Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, Mother Nature/Environment."},{front:"The 4Ps (service)",back:"Policies, Procedures, People, Plant/Place."},{front:"Controllable input (C)",back:"Can be adjusted or controlled while the process runs."},{front:"Procedural input (SOP)",back:"Governed by a standardised procedure; check compliance first."},{front:"Noise input (N)",back:"Not controlled — can’t, too costly, or unaware. Goes to FMEA."},{front:"Where do controllable fishbone X’s go next?",back:"The X-Y matrix, to find the vital few."},{front:"Where do SOP and noise X’s go?",back:"The FMEA (after a compliance check for SOPs)."},{front:"When to stop the 5 Whys",back:"At an actionable cause, not at exactly five or at a person."},{front:"Are fishbone causes proven?",back:"No. They are hypotheses, verified in Analyze."},{front:"Fishbone’s phase in the syllabus",back:"Measure 2.1.1 — Process Definition / Process Discovery."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The supplied book pages start at printed p. 98, so the book’s main fishbone teaching pages (before p. 98) were not available. Book support used here: the fishbone exercise (p. 98), the glossary definition (p. 584), the X sources and C/N/SOP routing (pp. 118–119), the X-Y exercise from the fishbone (p. 123), the FMEA class and causes column (p. 131), the C/P/N definitions (pp. 113–114) and the Measure roadmap (pp. 228, 230).","The 6M list, the 4Ps and 4Ss service variants, the 5 Whys technique and the facilitation steps are standard Lean Six Sigma practice written independently; they are not quoted from the supplied pages.","The X-Y scores and RPN shown are previews of lessons 2.1.3 and 2.1.4.","All MTTR, incident and spares figures are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Measure topic 2.1.1 “Cause & Effect / Fishbone Diagrams” (2.1 Process Definition)."},{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 9",pdfPage:9,note:"Exam weighting: Measure 32%."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 98",pdfPage:1,note:"Cause & effect diagram exercise: use the high-level process map to build a fishbone with the team."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 113–114",pdfPage:16,note:"Controllable, procedural and noise inputs, with definitions and examples."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 118",pdfPage:21,note:"Three sources of X’s (process mapping, fishbone, data analysis); warning that team tools are organised brainstorming, not data."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 119",pdfPage:22,note:"Controllable X’s from the fishbone go to the X-Y matrix; procedural and noise X’s go to the FMEA; procedures must be in compliance."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 123",pdfPage:26,note:"Exercise: build the X-Y matrix from the fishbone."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 131",pdfPage:34,note:"FMEA class column (C/P/N) and potential causes come from the fishbone."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 228",pdfPage:31,note:"Measure goal: define, explore and classify X variables (process mapping, fishbone, X-Y, FMEA)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 230",pdfPage:33,note:"Roadmap: Measure identifies X’s (fishbone, process map); Analyze proves or disproves their impact."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 234",pdfPage:37,note:"Quiz item: the fishbone generates ideas about possible causes for defects."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 584",pdfPage:87,note:"Glossary: cause and effect (fishbone) diagram definition."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 586",pdfPage:89,note:"Glossary: fishbone and Ishikawa diagram cross-references."}]},D=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:y},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),d=String.raw,v={id:"2.1.2",module:5,title:"Process Mapping, SIPOC, Value Stream Map",subtitle:"See the process as it really runs, then find the waste and the X’s",minutes:18,objectives:["Explain why process mapping is the first activity of Measure, and contrast the as-is, should-be and could-be views.","Read and draw standard flowchart symbols, and choose the right map level (L1 macro, L2 worker, L3 micro).","Use swimlane maps to expose handoffs, queues and ownership gaps.","Build a SIPOC in the recommended order: Process first (5–7 steps), then Outputs, Customers, Inputs and Suppliers.","Read a value stream map: process and data boxes, inventory triangles, push and pull arrows, and the timeline ladder.","Calculate queue time, lead time and process cycle efficiency (VA time ÷ lead time), and link map findings to X’s and waste."],overview:d`You cannot improve a process you cannot see. The training book calls [[process-mapping|process mapping]] the most important and powerful tool for improving a process, and makes it **the first activity of the Measure phase**: describe the process under investigation with a generic name, a map of the workflow and an operational description of its purpose.

This lesson covers the family of maps the syllabus groups under **Measure 2.1.2**. **Flowcharts** at three levels of detail show *how* work flows. **Swimlane maps** add *who* does each step. The **[[sipoc|SIPOC]]** shows the process boundary — suppliers, inputs, process, outputs, customers. The **[[value-stream-map|value stream map]]** adds *time and inventory*, so you can see how long work waits compared with how long it is worked on. Many teams draft a SIPOC in Define for scoping, but the syllabus classifies SIPOC and VSM here, in Measure. Our running example is a fibre-to-the-home (FTTH) installation, from customer order to an active service.`,sections:[{id:"why-views",heading:"Why map: the views of a process",body:d`The book lists what a good map gives you: the process inputs (X’s) and outputs (Y’s), supplier requirements, real customer needs, value-added and non-value-added steps, data-collection points (cycle times, defects, inventory levels, COPQ), decision points, quick fixes and control needs. One tool sets up the next — very little work is wasted.

It also warns that there are several **views** of every process:

1. **What you think it is.** Almost always understated: smaller, simpler and cheaper than reality.
2. **What it actually is — the as-is.** Found only by walking the process with the people who do it. This is where the **[[hidden-factory|hidden factory]]** appears: rework loops, re-approvals, side spreadsheets.
3. **What it should be — the should-be.** The improved, realistic future state that your project delivers.

Many practitioners add a fourth view, the **could-be**: the ideal process with constraints removed (no legacy systems, no approval layers). It is useful for stretching thinking, but the project commits to the should-be.

> **Rule:** always map the as-is *first*, and verify it by observation — not from the procedure manual. A map of the documented process hides exactly the deviations you are looking for.`},{id:"symbols-levels",heading:"Symbols and the three levels of detail",body:d`**Standard symbols** (the book’s set; available in Visio, Office and similar tools):

| Symbol | Meaning | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| Ellipse (oval) | Start / end | Exactly one start trigger; clear end point |
| Rectangle | Activity / step | Starts with a verb: “Check line feasibility” |
| Diamond | Decision | Only two exits: Yes and No |
| Parallelogram | Data / input–output | e.g. “Survey report” |
| Arrow | Direction of flow | No arrow, no sequence |
| Circle with letter/number | Connector to another page | Matches its partner |

As maps mature you may add a document, a database, a delay (D) or an inventory triangle.

**Levels** work like road maps — world, country, city:

- **Level 1 — macro (management view).** Major steps only, typically 4–8; shows where the process fits in the bigger picture and is ideal for reporting scope to management. When drawing it, include at least one step *before* and one *after* the area you believe holds the problem.
- **Level 2 — process map (worker view).** The steps, decisions and loops one role actually performs. Scope it by selecting the problem area from the L1 map and mapping it at natural start and stop points.
- **Level 3 — micro map (improvement view).** Same flow as L2 but **characterised with data**: specifications, SOPs, inputs and outputs tagged controllable or noise, DPU, yield, [[rty|RTY]], value-added or non-value-added status, and — where speed matters — cycle times and queue sizes. You usually build L3 only for the steps linked to your problem statement.

**Team mapping workshop** (the book’s recommended approach): agree the L1 map; tape one sheet per macro step on the wall; each person writes one task per sticky note, with **actual task time (excluding wait time)**; place the notes in flow order; consolidate into an L2 map straight away; then **walk the process** to verify it.`},{id:"swimlanes",heading:"Linear maps and swimlane maps",body:d`The book describes four map types: **linear flow**, **[[swimlane|swimlane]] (deployment)**, **SIPOC** and **value stream map**. Each type can be drawn at any of the three levels.

A **linear flow map** lays steps out left to right. It is the usual starting point. A **swimlane map** gives each role or department its own horizontal lane — for FTTH: Customer · Contact centre · Network planning · Field operations · NOC. Two benefits:

- **Ownership becomes visible.** Each box sits in exactly one lane, so a step that “belongs to nobody” stands out.
- **Handoffs become visible.** Every time work crosses a lane boundary there is a supplier–customer interaction, and that is usually **where bottlenecks and queues form**. Count them: an order that crosses lanes eight times has eight places to wait and eight chances to lose information.

The book’s capital-equipment swimlane adds a time line under the lanes, showing each group’s elapsed days. That is a short step from a value stream map, and it often shows that most of the elapsed time is spent between lanes, not inside them.

**Telecom reading tip:** on a provisioning swimlane, look for (1) loops back to a previous lane (rework), (2) decisions with no owner, (3) data re-keyed in each lane (opportunities for defects), and (4) the lane where orders wait longest.`},{id:"sipoc",heading:"SIPOC: boundaries, inputs and customers on one page",body:d`A **SIPOC** lists **S**uppliers → **I**nputs → **P**rocess → **O**utputs → **C**ustomers, often with requirements columns at both ends. It answers: who supplies what, what specifications are placed on the inputs, what the process produces, who the true customers are and what they require.

**Build order.** Begin with the **Process** — a high-level map of **5–7 steps** (the book says 4–8, and an L2 map is even better) placed under the P. Then work outward:

1. **P** — name the process, set the start and end triggers, and list 5–7 macro steps.
2. **O** — the outputs of the overall process.
3. **C** — who receives each output (internal and external), plus their preliminary requirements.
4. **I** — the inputs the process needs.
5. **S** — who provides each input, plus preliminary input requirements.

Starting from the customer end keeps the team anchored on what the process exists to deliver. Starting with suppliers tends to produce long lists of inputs that nobody can connect to an output.

**FTTH example**

| S | I | P | O | C |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Customer; sales channel | Signed order, address, plan | 1 Capture order | Active FTTH service | Residential customer |
| GIS / network inventory | Coverage data, port availability | 2 Check feasibility | Installation record | Billing |
| Workforce scheduler | Appointment slot | 3 Install drop and ONT | Updated inventory | Network inventory team |
| Logistics | ONT, drop cable | 4 Activate and test | Completion notification | Contact centre |
| NOC; OSS | Service profile, test tools | 5 Close and confirm | KPI data (install time) | DSO / management |

Customer requirements must pass the **RUMBA** test (reasonable, understandable, measurable, believable, achievable) — e.g. “installed within 5 working days, with right-first-time activation”. The SIPOC then becomes the basis for the input and output analysis sheets and roughly 70–80% of the detail needed for the L3 map.`},{id:"vsm",heading:"Value stream map: time, inventory and flow",body:d`A **value stream map** is a specialised map built around the **speed** of the process: it shows how fast transactions move, how much work is queued and how much of the elapsed time adds value. It works in both manufacturing and non-manufacturing processes. Its parts are:

- **Process boxes** — one per major step, with the number of people or resources.
- **Data boxes** under each process — **C/T** (cycle time per unit), **C/O** (changeover time), **uptime**, available time, batch size, FTY or defect rate.
- **Inventory triangles (I)** between boxes — units waiting (orders in a queue, tickets in a backlog).
- **Push arrows** (striped) — work is pushed to the next step whether or not it is ready. **Pull arrows / supermarkets / kanban** — the next step signals when it needs work ([[pull|pull]]).
- **Information flow** — schedules, orders and system messages, drawn along the top.
- **Timeline ladder** along the bottom — the **upper rungs show waiting time** (inventory converted into time) and the **lower rungs show value-added time**. Summing them gives the [[lead-time|lead time]] and the total VA time.

**Converting inventory to time.** Use queue ÷ demand rate (Little’s law): 200 orders waiting for installation with demand of 40 orders/day means ≈ **5 days** of waiting.

**Process cycle efficiency.** [[process-cycle-efficiency|PCE]] relates value-added time to lead time:

$$\text{PCE}=\frac{\text{value-added time}}{\text{total lead time}}$$

**FTTH value stream** (demand 40 orders/day; 8-hour working days):

| Step | VA C/T | Queue before | Wait |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Capture order | 15 min | 20 | 0.5 d |
| Check feasibility | 30 min | 40 | 1 d |
| Install drop & ONT | 120 min | 200 | 5 d |
| Activate & test | 20 min | 60 | 1.5 d |
| Close & confirm | 5 min | 20 | 0.5 d |
| **Total** | **190 min** | | **8.5 d** |

Lead time = 8.5 × 480 + 190 = **4,270 working minutes** (about 71 working hours). PCE = 190 ÷ 4,270 = **4.4%**. Measured in calendar time (24-hour days), PCE drops to about 1.5%, so always state your time basis. Pulling installation work from a 40-order buffer instead of pushing 200 orders cuts lead time to 2,350 minutes and roughly doubles PCE to **8.1%**, with no change to the 190 VA minutes.

**The book’s example** (cheque processing, p. 105) works the same way. Available time per day = (8 h − 0.5 h breaks) × uptime: 7.5 × 0.95 = 7.125 h = 25,650 s. Days of work in queue = inventory × C/T ÷ available seconds: for Disposition, 1,700 × 255 ÷ 25,650 ≈ **16.9 days**. Across the five steps the queues hold ≈ **49.2 days** of work, against only **460 seconds** of processing per item. The same map carries yields: RTY = .92 × .94 × .59 × .96 × .96 ≈ **.47**.

> **Exam-tools tip:** PCE, queue days and RTY are not always printed in the Reference Document, so learn the ratios. On the virtual calculator, (1) convert every time to one unit (minutes or seconds), (2) total the waits and the VA times separately on the notepad, (3) divide VA by **(waits + VA)**. A PCE above 100% means you inverted the ratio.`},{id:"xs-waste",heading:"From maps to X’s and waste",advanced:!0,body:d`Maps are not wall art; they are **data sources** for the rest of DMAIC.

**Finding X’s.** On the L3 map each step gets a mini-SIPOC. Its outputs (Y’s) have customers, metrics and specifications. Its inputs (X’s) are tagged **controllable (C)** or **noise (N)**, with SOPs noted. This input/output sheet feeds the [[fishbone|fishbone]], the [[x-y-matrix|X-Y matrix]] and the [[fmea|FMEA]] directly. If you focus on one Y at a time, as the book recommends, only the steps linked to that Y need full characterisation.

**Value-added test.** For each step ask two questions. Does it change the *form, fit or function* of the item? Is the customer willing to pay for it? Two “yes” answers make it [[value-added|value-added]]. Otherwise it is [[non-value-added|non-value-added]], and you mark it with a red X or the label NVA. Some NVA work is still required, such as regulatory data capture; that is [[business-value-added|business-value-added]]. The book has a handy hint: verbs starting with **re-** (rework, re-enter, review, re-test) usually signal waste.

**Seeing the seven wastes on a map**

| Map clue | Waste |
|---|---|
| Big inventory triangle; long top rung of the ladder | Waiting, Inventory |
| Loop back to an earlier box | Correction (rework) |
| Many lane crossings; files moved between systems | Conveyance |
| Engineer walks or drives between sites or tools | Motion |
| Extra approvals or duplicate checks | Overprocessing |
| Work pushed before the next step is ready | Overproduction |

**Yields on the map.** If the five FTTH steps have first-time yields of .96, .90, .82, .95 and .98, then RTY = 0.96 × 0.90 × 0.82 × 0.95 × 0.98 ≈ **0.66**. The average of 0.922 would hide this. The install step (0.82) is both the biggest queue and the lowest yield, so the X’s that drive its first-time failures are the prime candidates for the fishbone and the X-Y matrix.`}],whyItMatters:d`Every later Measure and Analyze tool depends on the map. Data-collection points come from the map. So do the candidate X’s on the fishbone, the steps in the FMEA and the strata in a multi-vari study. A wrong or idealised map produces wrong X’s.

For service operations, the VSM view changes the conversation. Most of a customer’s wait is queue time between teams, not the work itself: 190 working minutes inside 8.5 days. That shifts improvement effort from “work faster” to “wait less”. On the exam, expect questions on symbols, map levels, the SIPOC sequence, swimlane handoffs, VA/NVA tests and PCE or queue-time arithmetic.`,businessExample:{title:"FTTH installation lead time in a telecom operator",body:d`Customers complain that fibre installation “takes forever”. The vendor’s report shows technicians averaging 120 minutes per installation, which is well within standard. The Green Belt maps the as-is process with a swimlane and then a value stream map.

The ladder shows **190 VA minutes inside 8.5 days of queues**, a PCE of about **4.4%**. The largest triangle, 200 orders, sits before installation. The swimlane shows six handoffs on every order, and eight when the feasibility rework loop between planning and field operations fires.

The vendor-facing note is phrased as a request: *based on the value stream analysis, approximately 5 of the 8.5 days are spent waiting for an installation slot; the team is asked to clarify how appointments are scheduled and to share slot-utilisation data*. The install queue becomes the primary X to investigate. A pull-based slot plan is modelled to cut lead time to about 2,350 working minutes (PCE ≈ 8.1%), to be verified with data before any change.`},visual:"sipocValueStream",visualCaption:"From SIPOC to value stream for an FTTH installation. First the SIPOC frames the process boundary, and its P column expands into a swimlane map that exposes handoffs. The process then becomes a value stream with data boxes, inventory triangles, push arrows and a timeline ladder. The ladder shows 190 VA minutes inside 8.5 days of waiting, a PCE of 190 ÷ 4,270 ≈ 4.4%.",definitions:[{term:"Process map (flowchart)",definition:"A picture of the tasks, decisions and flow needed to produce a product or service, drawn with standard symbols."},{term:"As-is / should-be / could-be",definition:"The current actual process; the realistic improved target; and the ideal process with constraints removed."},{term:"Level 1 (macro) map",definition:"Management view with 4–8 major steps, showing where the process fits in the bigger picture."},{term:"Level 2 map",definition:"Worker view of the steps, decisions and loops within the problem area."},{term:"Level 3 (micro) map",definition:"Improvement view: the L2 flow characterised with data such as specs, C/N inputs, SOPs, DPU, yield, RTY, VA/NVA, cycle times and queues."},{term:"Swimlane (deployment) map",definition:"A map with one lane per role or department, showing who does each step and where handoffs occur."},{term:"SIPOC",definition:"Suppliers, Inputs, Process, Outputs, Customers: a one-page view of the process boundary and its relationships."},{term:"Value stream map (VSM)",definition:"A map of the flow of material and information with time and inventory data, used to see speed, queues and value-added ratios."},{term:"Inventory triangle",definition:"VSM symbol for units waiting between steps; converted to waiting time as queue ÷ demand rate."},{term:"Timeline ladder",definition:"VSM time line with waiting time on the upper rungs and value-added time on the lower rungs; the totals give lead time and VA time."},{term:"Process cycle efficiency (PCE)",definition:"Value-added time ÷ total lead time, in the same time unit."},{term:"Push vs pull",definition:"Push sends work forward regardless of downstream readiness; pull releases work only when the next step signals a need."}],comparisons:[{title:"Map types: what each one shows best",headers:["","Linear flow","Swimlane","SIPOC","Value stream map"],rows:[["Main question","What is the sequence?","Who does each step?","What are the boundaries and relationships?","Where does the time go?"],["Key content","Steps, decisions, loops","Lanes, handoffs","S, I, P (5–7 steps), O, C + requirements","C/T, uptime, inventory, push/pull, ladder"],["Reveals","Rework loops, decisions","Queues at handoffs, ownership gaps","Missing customers or inputs, scope","Waiting, low PCE, bottlenecks"],["FTTH use","Install flow","Five lanes, 6 handoffs (8 with rework)","Scope from order to active service","190 min VA in 8.5 d → PCE 4.4%"]]},{title:"Level 1 vs Level 2 vs Level 3 maps",headers:["","Level 1 (macro)","Level 2","Level 3 (micro)"],rows:[["Viewpoint","Management","Worker","Improvement"],["Detail","4–8 major steps","All steps, decisions and loops in scope","L2 plus data for each step"],["Data shown","Minimal","Little","Specs, C/N, SOPs, DPU, yield, RTY, VA/NVA, C/T, queues"],["Use","Scope; report to management","Understand how work gets done","Find and characterise the X’s"]]},{title:"Cycle time vs lead time vs PCE",headers:["","Cycle time (C/T)","Lead time","PCE"],rows:[["Measures","Time to complete one unit at a step","Total elapsed time through the stream, including waits","Share of lead time that adds value"],["On the VSM","Data box; lower rung of the ladder (if VA)","Sum of all rungs","Lower total ÷ (upper + lower)"],["FTTH","Install 120 min","4,270 working min","4.4%"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Map the process as the procedure manual describes it.",reality:"Map the as-is process by walking it with the people who do it. The deviations from the manual are what you are looking for."},{myth:"A SIPOC starts with Suppliers because that is the first letter.",reality:"Draft the Process (5–7 steps) first, then Outputs and Customers, then Inputs and Suppliers."},{myth:"More detail is always better.",reality:"Match the level to the purpose. An L3 map of the whole process wastes time; characterise only the steps linked to the problem."},{myth:"If every step is fast, the process is fast.",reality:"Lead time is mostly waiting. Steps can total 190 minutes while the customer waits 8.5 days."},{myth:"A low PCE means people are not working hard.",reality:"Low PCE comes from queues, batching and handoffs, which are system design issues, not effort."},{myth:"SIPOC and VSM are Define tools in the syllabus.",reality:"Teams often draft a SIPOC in Define, but the syllabus lists SIPOC and VSM under Measure 2.1.2."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“Which element of a SIPOC should the team identify FIRST?”",explanation:"Start with the Process (5–7 high-level steps), then Outputs, Customers, Inputs, Suppliers. The book’s workshop order after the map is outputs, customers, requirements, inputs, suppliers.",tag:"first"},{trap:"PCE options include the inverted ratio (lead time ÷ VA).",explanation:"PCE = VA ÷ lead time and can never exceed 100%. An answer like “32” is the inverted ratio."},{trap:"Lead time is given in days and VA time in minutes.",explanation:"Convert to one unit and state the basis (working vs calendar days) before dividing. Mixing units gives answers out by a factor of 480 or 1,440."},{trap:"“The map that BEST shows which department is responsible for each step and where handoffs occur.”",explanation:"A swimlane (deployment) map, not a linear flow map or SIPOC.",tag:"best"},{trap:"A diamond with three exits on a flowchart.",explanation:"A decision diamond has only two exits: Yes and No."},{trap:"“Level 3 maps are used mainly to report scope to management.”",explanation:"That is Level 1. Level 3 is the data-rich improvement view."},{trap:"Queue days computed with 8 hours per day when the stem gives breaks and uptime.",explanation:"Available time = (hours − breaks) × uptime. 1,700 × 255 ÷ 28,800 ≈ 15.1 days is wrong; ÷ 25,650 gives 16.9 days."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Building an FTTH SIPOC in the right order",problem:d`Build a SIPOC for fibre installation, from “customer signs order” to “service active and confirmed”.`,steps:["**P** — name it “FTTH installation”; start = signed order, end = service confirmed; 5 steps: capture order → check feasibility → install drop and ONT → activate and test → close and confirm.","**O** — active service, installation record, updated inventory, completion notice, KPI data.","**C** — residential customer (external); billing, inventory team, contact centre, DSO (internal). Requirement: installed ≤ 5 working days, right first time.","**I** — signed order, address, coverage data, appointment slot, ONT and drop cable, service profile.","**S** — customer and sales channel, GIS, workforce scheduler, logistics, NOC/OSS."],answer:"A one-page boundary built P → O → C → I → S, with 5 macro steps and requirements at the customer end.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Start by listing 25 suppliers and every input anyone can think of.",whyWrong:"Starting at S produces inputs that are not linked to any output or customer requirement. Anchor on the process and its outputs first."}},{title:"FTTH value stream: lead time and PCE",problem:d`Demand is 40 orders/day (8-hour working days). Queues before the five steps are 20, 40, 200, 60 and 20 orders. VA cycle times are 15, 30, 120, 20 and 5 minutes. Find the waits, lead time and PCE.`,steps:["Waits = queue ÷ demand: 20/40 = 0.5, 40/40 = 1, 200/40 = 5, 60/40 = 1.5, 20/40 = 0.5 → **8.5 days**.","VA total = 15 + 30 + 120 + 20 + 5 = **190 min**.","Lead time = 8.5 × 480 + 190 = 4,080 + 190 = **4,270 min**.","PCE = 190 ÷ 4,270 = **0.0445 ≈ 4.4%**.","Calendar basis (1,440 min/day): 190 ÷ 12,430 ≈ 1.5%. State which basis you used."],answer:"Lead time ≈ 4,270 working minutes; PCE ≈ 4.4%",wrongApproach:{calculation:"PCE = 4,270 ÷ 190 = 22.5 (2,247%)",whyWrong:"This inverts the ratio. Value-added time goes on top, so PCE is always between 0 and 100%."}},{title:"Book VSM: converting inventory to days of work",problem:d`At the Disposition step: 1,700 items queued, C/T = 255 s, 8-hour shift with 0.5 h breaks, uptime 0.95. How many days of work are in the queue?`,steps:["Available time = (8 − 0.5) × 0.95 = 7.125 h = 7.125 × 3,600 = **25,650 s/day**.","Work in queue = 1,700 × 255 = 433,500 s.","Days = 433,500 ÷ 25,650 ≈ **16.9 days**.","Across all five steps: 2.65 + 20.47 + 16.90 + 1.60 + 7.57 ≈ **49.2 days**, against 460 s of total processing."],answer:"≈ 16.9 days at Disposition; ≈ 49.2 days in the whole stream",wrongApproach:{calculation:"1,700 × 255 ÷ (8 × 3,600) = 15.1 days",whyWrong:"This ignores the breaks and the uptime. Available time must reflect the time the step can actually run."}},{title:"RTY along the value stream",problem:d`The five FTTH steps have first-time yields of 96%, 90%, 82%, 95% and 98%. Find RTY and name the step to investigate first.`,steps:["Running product: 0.96 → 0.864 → 0.70848 → 0.673056 → **0.6596**.","RTY ≈ **66%**: about two in three orders pass every step first time.","Install (82%) has both the lowest yield and the largest queue, so start the fishbone and X-Y work there."],answer:"RTY ≈ 66%; investigate the install step first",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Average = (96 + 90 + 82 + 95 + 98) ÷ 5 = 92.2%",whyWrong:"An order must pass every step, so the yields multiply. The average overstates end-to-end first-pass performance by about 26 points."}}],lab:"processMap",check:[{id:"chk-2.1.2-1",topic:"2.1.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A team has drafted the **Process** column of its SIPOC as six high-level steps. According to the recommended sequence, what should it identify **next**?",options:["Suppliers","Inputs","Outputs","Customer requirements for suppliers"],answer:2,explanation:"After the process steps, identify the Outputs, then the Customers and their requirements, then the Inputs, and finally the Suppliers.",whyWrong:["Suppliers come last; starting there disconnects inputs from outputs.","Inputs come after outputs and customers.","","Input (supplier) requirements are the final step, not the next one."],traps:["first","after"]},{id:"chk-2.1.2-2",topic:"2.1.2",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"On a standard process map, which symbol represents a **decision**, and how many paths leave it?",options:["Rectangle; one path","Diamond; two paths (Yes / No)","Ellipse; two paths","Parallelogram; three or more paths"],answer:1,explanation:"A diamond marks a decision point with exactly two exits, Yes and No.",whyWrong:["A rectangle is an activity.","","An ellipse marks the start or end.","A parallelogram shows data."]},{id:"chk-2.1.2-3",topic:"2.1.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!0,stem:"A broadband fault repair contains **30 minutes** of value-added work. Its lead time is **2 working days** (8-hour days). What is the process cycle efficiency?",options:["≈ 3.1%","≈ 1.0%","32","≈ 6.3%"],answer:0,explanation:"Lead time = 2 × 8 × 60 = 960 min. PCE = 30 ÷ 960 = 0.03125 ≈ 3.1%.",whyWrong:["","This uses 24-hour calendar days (30 ÷ 2,880). The stem specifies 8-hour working days.","This inverts the ratio (960 ÷ 30). PCE cannot exceed 100%.","This uses only one day (30 ÷ 480)."]},{id:"chk-2.1.2-4",topic:"2.1.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!0,stem:"A VSM step has **1,700** items queued and a C/T of **255 s**. The shift is 8 h with 0.5 h of breaks, and uptime is 0.95 (available time = 25,650 s/day). How many days of work are queued?",options:["≈ 15.1 days","≈ 16.9 days","≈ 6.7 days","≈ 0.06 days"],answer:1,explanation:"1,700 × 255 = 433,500 s; 433,500 ÷ 25,650 ≈ 16.9 days.",whyWrong:["This divides by 28,800 s (8 full hours), ignoring the breaks and uptime.","","This is 1,700 ÷ 255, which has no meaning here.","This inverts the ratio (25,650 ÷ 433,500)."]},{id:"chk-2.1.2-5",topic:"2.1.2",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A provisioning order passes between five departments, and the team suspects queues form at the handoffs. Which map **best** exposes this?",options:["Level 1 linear macro map","Swimlane (deployment) map","SIPOC","Fishbone diagram"],answer:1,explanation:"Swimlanes show who performs each step. Every lane crossing is a supplier–customer handoff, where bottlenecks and queues usually form.",whyWrong:["A macro map shows major steps but not ownership or handoffs.","","A SIPOC shows boundaries and relationships, not the handoffs inside the flow.","A fishbone lists potential causes; it is not a flow map."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-2.1.2-6",topic:"2.1.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement about a **Level 3** process map is correct?",options:["It shows only 4–8 major steps for management reporting","It characterises steps with data such as C/N inputs, SOPs, DPU, yield, RTY and VA/NVA","It should always cover the whole business before any analysis begins","It replaces the need for a SIPOC"],answer:1,explanation:"Level 3 is the improvement view: the L2 flow plus specifications, input classifications, yields, VA/NVA status and, where relevant, cycle times and queues.",whyWrong:["That describes a Level 1 macro map.","","Characterise the steps linked to the problem first; mapping everything at L3 wastes effort.","The SIPOC supplies much of the L3 detail; the two complement each other."]}],takeaways:["Process mapping is the first activity of Measure: name the process, map the workflow and write an operational description.","Map the as-is by walking the process; the should-be is the target, and the could-be is the unconstrained ideal.","Symbols: ellipse = start/end, rectangle = activity (verb), diamond = decision (Yes/No), parallelogram = data, arrow = flow.","L1 macro (4–8 steps, management), L2 (worker), L3 micro (data-rich improvement view).","Swimlanes show ownership; every lane crossing is a handoff where queues form.","SIPOC: draft the P (5–7 steps) first, then O → C → I → S, adding requirements (RUMBA).","VSM: data boxes (C/T, C/O, uptime), inventory triangles, push vs pull, and a ladder that gives lead time and VA time.","PCE = VA time ÷ lead time, in one time unit. FTTH example: 190 ÷ 4,270 ≈ 4.4%. Most lead time is waiting."],flashcards:[{front:"First activity of the Measure phase",back:"Describe the process: generic name, process map and operational description."},{front:"Three views of a process (book)",back:"What you think it is, what it actually is, what it should be."},{front:"Could-be map",back:"The ideal process with constraints removed; beyond the realistic should-be."},{front:"Diamond symbol",back:"Decision. Only two exits: Yes and No."},{front:"Level 1 vs Level 3 map",back:"L1: 4–8 macro steps for management. L3: micro, data-rich improvement view."},{front:"Why swimlanes?",back:"They show who does each step; handoffs across lanes are where queues form."},{front:"SIPOC build order",back:"P (5–7 steps) → O → C (+ requirements) → I → S (+ requirements)."},{front:"Inventory triangle → time",back:"Queue ÷ demand rate (e.g. 200 ÷ 40/day = 5 days)."},{front:"Timeline ladder",back:"Upper rungs = waiting; lower rungs = value-added; the sums give lead time and VA time."},{front:"PCE formula",back:"Value-added time ÷ total lead time (same units); never above 100%."},{front:"Push vs pull",back:"Push sends work regardless of readiness; pull releases work on a downstream signal."},{front:"Where are SIPOC and VSM in the syllabus?",back:"Measure 2.1.2, although SIPOC is often drafted in Define for scoping."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The “could-be” view is standard practice wording. The book uses three views: think, actually is, should be.","Process cycle efficiency (VA ÷ lead time) and converting a queue to time with Little’s law (queue ÷ demand) are general Lean practice. The supplied book pages show the VSM ladder data and days of work in queue (p. 105) but do not use the term PCE.","VSM symbol details beyond the book’s example (changeover C/O, push and pull arrows, supermarkets, information flow) are standard VSM conventions written independently.","The SIPOC build order (P first, then O, C, I, S) follows standard practice and agrees with the book: start from a 4–8 step map, then outputs, customers, requirements, inputs, suppliers (p. 111).","All FTTH figures are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data. PCE uses 8-hour working days unless stated."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Measure topic 2.1.2 “Process Mapping, SIPOC, Value Stream Map” (2.1 Process Definition)."},{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 9",pdfPage:9,note:"Exam weighting: Measure 32%."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 99",pdfPage:2,note:"Overview of process mapping; first activity of the Measure phase; reasons to map."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 100",pdfPage:3,note:"Information from mapping (X’s, Y’s, VA/NVA, data points); the three views of a process."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 101",pdfPage:4,note:"Standard process mapping symbols."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 102–103",pdfPage:5,note:"Levels 1–3; the four map types; the swimlane and its handoffs."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 104",pdfPage:7,note:"Examples: linear map, swimlane with timeline, SIPOC for a customer-order process."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 105",pdfPage:8,note:"Value stream map: C/T, uptime, available seconds, days of work in queue, IPY and RTY .47."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 106–108",pdfPage:9,note:"Individual vs team mapping approach; mapping workshop; Level 1 steps (one step before and after the problem area)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 110",pdfPage:13,note:"Scoping the Level 2 map; SIPOC gives 70–80% of the Level 3 detail."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 111",pdfPage:14,note:"Building a SIPOC: questions it answers; begin from a 4–8 step map; preferred order."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 112",pdfPage:15,note:"Customer requirements and the RUMBA test."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 115–116",pdfPage:18,note:"Level 3 map content: SOPs, C/N inputs, DPU, yield, RTY, VA/NVA, value stream parameters."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 117",pdfPage:20,note:"VA/NVA tests (form, fit, function; customer willing to pay); the “re-” hint."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 587",pdfPage:90,note:"Glossary: non-value added; process; process flow diagram."}]},q=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:v},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),l=String.raw,w={id:"2.1.3",module:5,title:"X-Y Diagram",subtitle:"Rank the inputs that matter most to the outputs customers care about",minutes:12,objectives:["Explain the purpose of the X-Y diagram (X-Y matrix, cause & effect matrix) as a team-based prioritisation tool for potential X’s.","Build the matrix: list output Y’s with customer-importance weights (1–10) and input X’s from the fishbone and process map.","Rate each X against each Y on the 0 / 1 / 3 / 9 relationship scale and justify why a spread-out scale is preferred.","Calculate each X’s weighted score as Σ(weight × rating), its percentage of the total, and its cumulative percentage.","Rank the X’s with a Pareto view and choose a defensible cut-off for the vital few.","Describe how the ranked X’s feed the FMEA, the data-collection plan and later Analyze-phase tests — and the limits of an opinion-based tool."],overview:l`By the time a Green Belt reaches this tool, the team has usually produced *too many* candidate causes. The [[fishbone|fishbone]] for slow incident restoration may hold forty ideas; the detailed [[process-map|process map]] adds another twenty inputs. Nobody can collect data on sixty variables. The **[[x-y-matrix|X-Y diagram]]** — also called the X-Y matrix or cause & effect (C&E) matrix — is the step that turns that long list into a short, ranked list worth investigating.

The idea is simple and very much in the spirit of [[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]]. Put the outputs the customer cares about (the Y’s) across the top and weight them by importance. Put the candidate inputs (the X’s) down the side. Rate how strongly each X is believed to influence each Y, multiply each rating by the weight of its Y, and add across the row. The X’s with the biggest totals are the team’s best bet for the [[vital-few|vital few]]. The training book describes the matrix as a tool to collate potential X’s and assess their relative impact on **multiple** Y’s, created for every project and updated as knowledge improves — with one loud warning: **it is organised brainstorming, not real data.**`,sections:[{id:"purpose",heading:"What the X-Y diagram is for — and what it is not",body:l`The X-Y diagram answers one question: **“Of all the inputs we have listed, which should we study first?”** It is a *prioritisation* tool for the **independent variables** (the X’s). It does not prove that any X causes anything, and it does not choose the Y’s — those come from the [[voc|voice of the customer]], the [[ctq|CTQs]] and the project charter’s [[primary-metric|primary]] and [[secondary-metric|secondary]] metrics.

The book lists the three main sources of candidate X’s:

1. **Process mapping** — every step’s inputs, especially those marked on the level-3 map.
2. **Fishbone analysis** — the brainstormed causes, organised by the [[six-ms|6Ms]].
3. **Basic data analysis** — graphs and simple statistics already available.

It also recommends feeding the matrix mainly with **[[controllable-input|controllable]]** X’s, because breakthrough comes from inputs you can actually adjust. **Procedural** inputs (which should simply be complied with) and **[[noise-input|noise]]** inputs (which you cannot or choose not to control) are carried into the **[[fmea|FMEA]]**, where noise typically raises risk.

What the matrix is *not*:

- **Not evidence.** Ratings reflect the team’s collective (“tribal”) knowledge. Analyze-phase data may show that an X the team rated 9 has no measurable effect. That is normal, not a failure.
- **Not a one-off.** The book stresses that the matrix is “never completed” — it is updated whenever new data or a parameter change appears.
- **Not a ranking of outputs.** The weights rank the Y’s; the matrix exists to rank the X’s. Exam options that say the X-Y diagram “prioritises the dependent variables” are wrong.`},{id:"build",heading:"Building the matrix step by step",body:l`The book’s template follows a fixed sequence. Here it is with a telecom running example: a **Network Operations Centre (NOC) incident-restoration process** whose primary metric is restoration within the SLA target.

**Step 1 — List the Y’s across the top.** Include the primary metric and the secondary metrics; add any other customer-focused output the team agrees matters. Ours:

| Y | Output | Weight |
|---|---|---:|
| Y₁ | Service restored within SLA | 10 |
| Y₂ | No repeat incident / reopen within 7 days | 8 |
| Y₃ | Customer status updates on time | 6 |
| Y₄ | Ticket data accurate for SLA reporting | 4 |

**Step 2 — Weight each Y on a 1–10 scale** (10 = most important to the customer and business). Equal weights are allowed. If a new metric surfaces while weighting — very common — you may need to add a secondary metric to the charter or sharpen the primary metric.

**Step 3 — List the X’s down the side**, taken from the fishbone and the process map. Use judgement: not every fishbone bone deserves a row.

**Step 4 — Rate every X against every Y.** The book recommends **0 / 1 / 3 / 9**: 9 = strong relationship, 3 = moderate, 1 = weak, 0 = none. Rate one Y column at a time across all X’s; it keeps the team’s judgement consistent.

**Step 5 — Calculate each X’s weighted score:**

$$\text{Score}(X_j)=\sum_{i}\big(w_i\times r_{ij}\big)$$

where $w_i$ is the weight of output $Y_i$ and $r_{ij}$ is the rating of $X_j$ against $Y_i$.

**Step 6 — Sort, convert to percentages and draw a Pareto chart.** Percent of total = score ÷ sum of all scores; the cumulative percentage shows how much of the total “believed influence” the top X’s carry.`},{id:"scale",heading:"Why 0 / 1 / 3 / 9 and not 1 to 10?",body:l`The book is explicit: you may use any scale, but a 1–10 relationship scale invites endless debate — *“is it a 6 or a 7?”* — and adds no real information because the ratings are opinions anyway. A coarse, **non-linear** scale forces the team to decide whether a relationship is strong, moderate, weak or absent.

The gaps matter. Because 9 is **three times** 3 and **nine times** 1, a single strong relationship to a heavily weighted Y dominates several weak ones. This *spreads* the scores so that the vital few stand out instead of every X landing in the middle.

| Rating | Meaning | Ask yourself |
|:---:|---|---|
| 9 | Strong | “If this X goes wrong, this Y almost certainly suffers.” |
| 3 | Moderate | “It contributes, but other things matter more.” |
| 1 | Weak | “There is a plausible but small link.” |
| 0 | None | “No credible path from this X to this Y.” |

Two practical rules: keep the **weights** (1–10, about the Y’s) and the **ratings** (0/1/3/9, about the X–Y relationship) mentally separate, and agree an operational meaning for each rating *before* scoring so that one enthusiastic expert does not inflate a whole row.`},{id:"worked-noc",heading:"The full NOC example: scores, ranking and Pareto",body:l`The team rated eight X’s. Each cell shows the rating; the score multiplies by the weights 10, 8, 6, 4.

| X (input) | Y₁ (10) | Y₂ (8) | Y₃ (6) | Y₄ (4) | Score | % | Cum % |
|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|--:|--:|--:|
| Priority classification at logging | 9 | 1 | 3 | 9 | **152** | 17.7 | 17.7 |
| Alarm correlation / triage accuracy | 9 | 3 | 3 | 3 | **144** | 16.8 | 34.5 |
| Spare-part availability | 9 | 3 | 1 | 0 | **120** | 14.0 | 48.5 |
| Shift-handover quality | 3 | 3 | 9 | 1 | **112** | 13.1 | 61.5 |
| Field dispatch delay | 9 | 0 | 3 | 0 | **108** | 12.6 | 74.1 |
| Runbook / knowledge-base quality | 3 | 9 | 0 | 0 | **102** | 11.9 | 86.0 |
| Root-cause closure discipline | 1 | 9 | 0 | 1 | **86** | 10.0 | 96.0 |
| Monitoring polling interval | 3 | 0 | 0 | 1 | **34** | 4.0 | 100.0 |
| **Total** | | | | | **858** | 100 | |

Top row in full: $9\times10+1\times8+3\times6+9\times4=90+8+18+36=152$; and $152\div858=17.7\%$.

**Reading the result.** Priority classification and triage accuracy lead. Notice that the Pareto here is fairly *flat* — the top four X’s hold about 62% and the top five about 74%. X-Y Paretos often look like this because the ratings are coarse opinions; there is rarely a clean 80/20 break. The team therefore uses judgement and a natural gap: here the obvious low-impact X is the polling interval (34, a clear drop), and the team decides to carry the **top five** (≥ 108) into the FMEA and the data-collection plan.

**Why weighting matters.** Add the raw ratings without weights and shift handover (3+3+9+1 = 16) beats spare-part availability (9+3+1+0 = 13). Weighted, spare parts (120) beat handover (112), because spare parts drive the most important Y. Unweighted totals ignore what the customer values most.

**Sensitivity check.** If the business raises the customer-update weight from 6 to 10, handover rises to 148 and overtakes spare parts (124). A quick “what if the weights were different?” test shows which rankings are robust and which hinge on one debatable weight — useful to report to the sponsor.`},{id:"book-example",heading:"Reading a completed summary: the book’s laminating example",body:l`The training book shows the summary sheet of a completed X-Y diagram for a **laminating** process. Five Y’s are weighted — broken (10), unbonded area (9), smears (8), thickness (7), foreign material (6) — and nine X’s are ranked by their weighted totals, from temperature (162) and human handling (159) down to clean-room cleanliness (78). The template divides each ranking by the grand total, **1,087**, to give the rank %: $162\div1{,}087=14.9\%$, $159\div1{,}087=14.6\%$, $78\div1{,}087=7.2\%$. The top three X’s together carry only about **41.5%**, another reminder that X-Y Paretos are usually gentler than defect Paretos.

Two lessons from the book’s example for the exam: the **ranking is a weighted sum**, and the **rank %** is that sum’s share of the total of all rankings — not a probability and not a percentage of defects.`},{id:"feeds",heading:"Where the ranked X’s go next",body:l`The X-Y diagram sits in the middle of the Measure phase’s **narrowing funnel**: fishbone and process map (all possible X’s) → **X-Y matrix** (prioritised X’s) → **FMEA** (risk of each X’s failure modes) → data collection and [[msa|MSA]] → Analyze-phase proof.

- **FMEA.** The book’s FMEA exercise is built “using the information generated from the Process Map, Fishbone Diagram and X-Y Matrix”. High-scoring X’s become the potential causes analysed for [[severity|severity]], [[occurrence|occurrence]] and [[detection|detection]].
- **Data collection plan.** Each top X needs an [[operational-definition|operational definition]], a measurement method and a sampling plan, so that Analyze can test it.
- **Analyze phase.** The book notes that the X’s for a [[multi-vari|multi-vari]] study can be pulled straight from the X-Y matrix, and [[hypothesis-test|hypothesis tests]] then confirm or reject each one.
- **Improve and Control.** The book’s *improvement selection matrix* reuses the X-Y matrix’s outputs and their importance ratings to rank proposed solutions — so keep the matrix up to date.`},{id:"pitfalls",heading:"Pitfalls, variants and good facilitation",advanced:!0,body:l`*Advanced practice notes.*

- **Rating by the loudest voice.** Score silently first, then discuss the cells where people disagree by more than one step (a 1 versus a 9 is a conversation worth having).
- **Too many Y’s.** Five or six weighted outputs is plenty. Twenty Y’s dilute the weights so that every X looks similar.
- **Mixed-level X’s.** “Process” is not an X; “priority classification at logging” is. Keep X’s at a level where data could actually be collected.
- **Confusing correlation with causation.** A 9 means *the team believes* there is a strong link. Only data — later — can show it, and even a measured correlation is not proof of cause.
- **Scale variants.** Some organisations use 0/1/3/5 or 1–5 ratings; the arithmetic is identical. The exam expects you to know the weight × rating logic and the book’s recommended 0/1/3/9 scale. *(The QFD “house of quality” uses the same 1/3/9 idea — supplementary context.)*
- **Freezing the matrix.** The book’s phrase “never completed” is deliberate: update it after MSA, after Analyze and whenever a parameter changes.`}],whyItMatters:l`Measure is the heaviest exam phase (32%), and the X-Y diagram is the hinge between brainstorming and data. Without it, teams either chase the cause the most senior person favours or try to measure everything and finish nothing. With it, the team has a transparent, weighted, documented rationale for *why* these five X’s were measured first — exactly what a sponsor or tollgate reviewer wants to see.

For a service-operations engineer the habit transfers directly to vendor governance: when an SLA trend slips, a quick weighted matrix of candidate drivers (triage, dispatch, spares, handover) against the outputs the business values keeps the discussion focused on evidence-gathering rather than blame.`,businessExample:{title:"Prioritising drivers of NOC restoration time with a managed-services partner",body:l`A DSO team and its managed-services vendor see restoration-within-SLA slip for three months. A joint workshop produces 38 fishbone causes. Using the X-Y diagram above, the team weights four outputs (restore within SLA 10, no repeat 8, customer updates 6, ticket accuracy 4) and rates eight controllable X’s.

Priority classification (152) and triage accuracy (144) lead the ranking; the top five carry about 74% of the weighted total. Polling interval (34) is parked. The procedural input “follow the major-incident runbook” and the noise input “severe weather” go straight to the FMEA rather than the matrix.

In the joint report the result is framed as a basis for evidence-gathering, not a verdict: *based on the team’s X-Y prioritisation, priority classification at logging is the highest-ranked candidate driver; the vendor team is asked to provide classification data for the last quarter so the relationship can be tested.* The matrix is attached and dated, and both teams agree to re-score it once MSA on the priority field is complete.`},visual:"xyMatrixRanking",visualCaption:"A NOC incident-restoration X-Y matrix fills in: four outputs weighted 10, 8, 6 and 4; eight inputs rated 0/1/3/9; each weighted score Σ(weight × rating) appears; then the bars re-sort into a Pareto with cumulative percentage. The top five X’s (≥ 108, ~74% cumulative) go forward to the FMEA and data collection.",definitions:[{term:"X-Y diagram (X-Y matrix)",definition:"A team-based matrix that rates candidate inputs (X’s) against weighted outputs (Y’s) to prioritise which X’s to study first. Also called a cause & effect (C&E) matrix."},{term:"Y (output)",definition:"A dependent variable the customer or business cares about — the primary metric, secondary metrics or other CTQ outputs."},{term:"X (input)",definition:"An independent variable — a candidate cause from the fishbone, process map or basic data analysis."},{term:"Output weight",definition:"A 1–10 importance score given to each Y (10 = most important); equal weights are allowed."},{term:"Relationship rating",definition:"The team’s judgement of how strongly an X influences a Y, usually on the 0 / 1 / 3 / 9 scale (none / weak / moderate / strong)."},{term:"Weighted score (ranking)",definition:"Σ(output weight × relationship rating) across all Y’s for one X."},{term:"Rank %",definition:"An X’s weighted score ÷ the sum of all X scores; cumulated in a Pareto view."},{term:"Vital few",definition:"The small set of X’s believed to drive most of the variation in the Y’s; the X-Y diagram nominates candidates, data confirms them."},{term:"Controllable input (C)",definition:"An input that can be adjusted while the process runs; the main content of the X-Y matrix."},{term:"Procedural input (P)",definition:"An input governed by a standard set of activities (an SOP or form); compliance issues go to the FMEA."},{term:"Noise input (N)",definition:"An input you cannot, or choose not to, control (weather, ambient conditions, individual differences); handled in the FMEA as a risk raiser."},{term:"Tribal knowledge",definition:"The experience-based collective opinion of the team — the basis of X-Y ratings before hard data exists."}],comparisons:[{title:"Output weights vs relationship ratings",headers:["","Output weight (w)","Relationship rating (r)"],rows:[["Describes","How important a Y is","How strongly an X affects a Y"],["Scale (book)","1–10; 10 highest; ties allowed","0 / 1 / 3 / 9 recommended"],["Set by","VOC, CTQs, charter metrics, sponsor input","Team knowledge of the process"],["How many","One per Y (column)","One per X–Y cell"],["NOC example","Restore within SLA = 10","Priority classification → restore = 9"]]},{title:"Fishbone vs X-Y diagram vs FMEA",headers:["","Fishbone (2.1.1)","X-Y diagram (2.1.3)","FMEA (2.1.4)"],rows:[["Main question","What could cause the problem?","Which X’s matter most to our Y’s?","How can each step/X fail, and how risky is it?"],["Output","Long list of causes by category","Ranked list of X’s (weighted scores)","Ranked failure modes (RPN) with actions"],["Quantified?","No","Yes — opinion-based scores","Yes — S, O, D ratings and RPN"],["Handles many Y’s?","One effect (head of the fish)","Yes — several weighted Y’s","Effects on the Y’s per failure mode"],["Typical inputs","Brainstorming, 6Ms","Fishbone, process map, basic data","Process map, fishbone, X-Y matrix"]]},{title:"Unweighted vs weighted ranking (NOC example)",headers:["X","Unweighted sum of ratings","Weighted score"],rows:[["Shift-handover quality","16 (ranks above spare parts)","112"],["Spare-part availability","13","120 (ranks above handover)"],["Why they flip","Treats every Y as equally important","Spare parts drive the weight-10 Y strongly"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"The X-Y diagram proves which X’s cause the problem.",reality:"It ranks the team’s opinions. The book warns it is organised brainstorming, not real data; Analyze-phase data may overturn it."},{myth:"The X-Y diagram prioritises the Y’s.",reality:"Weights rank the Y’s as an input; the purpose of the tool is to prioritise the X’s (the independent variables)."},{myth:"A finer 1–10 rating scale gives a more accurate matrix.",reality:"Ratings are judgements; 1–10 invites “6 or 7?” debates. The book recommends 0/1/3/9, which also spreads scores so the vital few stand out."},{myth:"You can simply add each X’s ratings across the row.",reality:"Each rating must be multiplied by its Y’s weight first; unweighted sums can reverse the ranking (handover 16 vs spare parts 13, but 112 vs 120 weighted)."},{myth:"An X-Y Pareto always shows a clear 80/20 split.",reality:"Coarse opinion scores usually give a flat Pareto (NOC top five ≈ 74%; book’s top three ≈ 41.5%). The cut-off is a reasoned team decision."},{myth:"Once scored, the matrix is finished.",reality:"The book calls it “never completed” — update it whenever data or a process parameter changes."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“The X-Y diagram is used primarily to prioritise the dependent variables.”",explanation:"False. It prioritises the independent variables (X’s). Y’s are weighted as inputs to the calculation.",tag:"primary"},{trap:"An option computes an X’s score by adding its ratings, ignoring weights.",explanation:"Score = Σ(weight × rating). With weights 10, 7, 4 and ratings 9, 3, 1, the score is 115, not 13."},{trap:"Options mixing the scales: “weight the X’s 0/1/3/9 and rate the Y’s 1–10”.",explanation:"The book weights the Y’s 1–10 and rates each X–Y relationship 0/1/3/9. Swapping them is a distractor."},{trap:"“Which tool should be used FIRST to narrow 40 fishbone causes before collecting data?”",explanation:"The X-Y diagram (then FMEA). A hypothesis test or regression needs data the team has not yet collected.",tag:"first"},{trap:"“The X-Y diagram is based on statistical data and therefore objective.”",explanation:"It is based on the team’s collective knowledge. Treat statements calling it objective proof as incorrect.",tag:"not"},{trap:"Rank % misread as “percentage of defects caused by this X”.",explanation:"Rank % is the X’s share of the total weighted score (e.g. 162 ÷ 1,087 = 14.9%) — a share of believed influence, not of defects."},{trap:"“Noise and procedural inputs should be placed in the X-Y matrix.”",explanation:"The book uses mainly controllable X’s in the matrix; procedural and noise inputs are carried into the FMEA.",tag:"best"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Full NOC matrix: 4 Y’s × 8 X’s",problem:l`Weights: restore within SLA 10, no repeat 8, customer updates 6, ticket accuracy 4. Ratings (Y₁, Y₂, Y₃, Y₄) — priority classification 9,1,3,9; triage 9,3,3,3; spare parts 9,3,1,0; handover 3,3,9,1; dispatch delay 9,0,3,0; runbook 3,9,0,0; root-cause closure 1,9,0,1; polling interval 3,0,0,1. Score and rank the X’s, and give the top-five cumulative %.`,steps:["Priority classification: 9×10 + 1×8 + 3×6 + 9×4 = 90 + 8 + 18 + 36 = **152**.","Triage: 90 + 24 + 18 + 12 = **144**. Spare parts: 90 + 24 + 6 + 0 = **120**. Handover: 30 + 24 + 54 + 4 = **112**.","Dispatch: 90 + 0 + 18 + 0 = **108**. Runbook: 30 + 72 = **102**. Root-cause closure: 10 + 72 + 0 + 4 = **86**. Polling: 30 + 4 = **34**.","Total = 152 + 144 + 120 + 112 + 108 + 102 + 86 + 34 = **858**.","Rank % of the leader = 152 ÷ 858 = **17.7%**; cumulative top five = (152 + 144 + 120 + 112 + 108) ÷ 858 = 636 ÷ 858 = **74.1%**.","Decision: carry the top five (≥ 108) to the FMEA and the data-collection plan; park the polling interval."],answer:"Ranking 152, 144, 120, 112, 108, 102, 86, 34 (total 858); top five ≈ 74.1% of the weighted total.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Rank by unweighted sums: priority 22, triage 18, handover 16, spare parts 13 …",whyWrong:"Ignoring weights treats “ticket accuracy” (weight 4) as equal to “restore within SLA” (weight 10). It wrongly puts handover above spare parts."}},{title:"One row, by hand, with the exam calculator",problem:l`Three Y’s are weighted **10, 7 and 4**. An input is rated **9, 3 and 1** against them. What is its weighted score?`,steps:["Multiply each rating by its own column weight: 9×10 = 90; 3×7 = 21; 1×4 = 4.","Add: 90 + 21 + 4 = **115**.","**Exam-tools tip:** on the virtual calculator, enter each product and use the memory-add key (M+) or write each product on the notepad, then sum — do not chain “9 × 10 + 3 × 7 …” on a basic calculator that evaluates left to right."],answer:"115",wrongApproach:{calculation:"(9 + 3 + 1) × (10 + 7 + 4) ÷ 3 = 13 × 21 ÷ 3 = 91",whyWrong:"Weights must pair with the rating in the same column; averaging or cross-multiplying totals mixes columns and has no meaning."}},{title:"Book summary sheet: rank % and cumulative %",problem:l`The training book’s laminating example ranks nine X’s with weighted scores 162, 159, 130, 126, 120, 120, 102, 90 and 78. Find the rank % of temperature (162) and the cumulative % of the top three.`,steps:["Total = 162 + 159 + 130 + 126 + 120 + 120 + 102 + 90 + 78 = **1,087**.","Temperature: 162 ÷ 1,087 = **14.9%** (the book’s template shows 14.90%).","Top three: (162 + 159 + 130) ÷ 1,087 = 451 ÷ 1,087 = **41.5%**.","**Exam-tools tip:** compute the total once, store it in calculator memory, then divide each score by MR."],answer:"Temperature 14.9%; top three ≈ 41.5% cumulative.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"162 ÷ 9 X’s = 18%",whyWrong:"Rank % is a share of the total of all scores, not a score divided by the number of X’s."}},{title:"Sensitivity: does the ranking survive a different weight?",problem:l`In the NOC matrix the customer-updates weight is challenged: the commercial team argues it should be **10**, not 6. Recalculate handover (3,3,9,1) and spare parts (9,3,1,0).`,steps:["New weights: 10, 8, 10, 4.","Handover = 3×10 + 3×8 + 9×10 + 1×4 = 30 + 24 + 90 + 4 = **148**.","Spare parts = 9×10 + 3×8 + 1×10 + 0×4 = 90 + 24 + 10 + 0 = **124**.","Handover now overtakes spare parts. Both remain in the top five, so the data-collection plan is unchanged — the ranking among them is weight-sensitive and worth noting to the sponsor."],answer:"Handover 148, spare parts 124 — order flips; the top-five shortlist is stable.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Only the Y₃ cell changes, so handover = 112 + 9 = 121.",whyWrong:"The weight changes by 4, so the Y₃ contribution changes by 4 × rating = 4 × 9 = 36: 112 + 36 = 148."}}],lab:"xyMatrix",check:[{id:"chk-2.1.3-1",topic:"2.1.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!0,stem:"An X-Y matrix has two outputs weighted **9** and **5**. An input is rated **3** against the first and **9** against the second. What is its weighted score?",options:["12","72","96","14"],answer:1,explanation:"Score = Σ(weight × rating) = 9×3 + 5×9 = 27 + 45 = 72.",whyWrong:["12 adds the ratings only (3 + 9), ignoring the weights.","","96 pairs each rating with the wrong weight (9×9 + 5×3) — always multiply within the same column.","14 adds the weights only (9 + 5)."]},{id:"chk-2.1.3-2",topic:"2.1.3",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement BEST describes the main purpose of the X-Y diagram?",options:["To prioritise the independent variables (X’s) by their believed impact on weighted outputs","To prioritise the dependent variables (Y’s)","To prove statistically which X causes the defect","To set the project scope and boundaries"],answer:0,explanation:"The X-Y diagram is a team-based prioritisation tool for potential X’s, rating each against several weighted Y’s.",whyWrong:["","The Y’s are weighted as inputs; the tool ranks the X’s.","It is based on team opinion, not statistical proof — Analyze tests do that.","Scope belongs to the charter / SIPOC work, not the X-Y diagram."],traps:["best","primary"]},{id:"chk-2.1.3-3",topic:"2.1.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Outputs are weighted **10, 6 and 3**. Input A is rated **9, 1, 3**; input B is rated **3, 9, 9**. Which input ranks higher, and with what score?",options:["A, with 105","A, with 13","B, with 21","B, with 111"],answer:3,explanation:"A = 10×9 + 6×1 + 3×3 = 90 + 6 + 9 = 105. B = 10×3 + 6×9 + 3×9 = 30 + 54 + 27 = 111. B ranks higher.",whyWrong:["105 is A’s correct score, but B’s 111 is higher.","13 is A’s unweighted sum; and A does not rank higher.","21 is B’s unweighted sum (3 + 9 + 9) — weights ignored.",""]},{id:"chk-2.1.3-4",topic:"2.1.3",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Why does the training book recommend a 0 / 1 / 3 / 9 rating scale rather than 1 to 10?",options:["Because Minitab cannot calculate with 1–10 scales","Because ratings of 9 represent proven statistical correlations","It avoids unproductive “is it a 6 or a 7?” debates and spreads the scores so the vital few stand out","Because weights and ratings must use the same scale"],answer:2,explanation:"The coarse, non-linear scale forces clear strong/moderate/weak/none decisions and separates high-impact X’s.",whyWrong:["There is no software limitation; the book says any scale may be used.","Ratings are opinions, not proven correlations.","","Weights use 1–10 while ratings use 0/1/3/9 — they differ by design."]},{id:"chk-2.1.3-5",topic:"2.1.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!0,stem:"A completed X-Y summary shows nine X scores totalling **1,087**. The top X scores **162**. What rank % does the template report for it?",options:["14.9%","16.2%","18.0%","6.7%"],answer:0,explanation:"Rank % = 162 ÷ 1,087 = 0.149 = 14.9%.",whyWrong:["","16.2% treats the score as a percentage of 1,000.","18% divides the score by the number of X’s (162 ÷ 9).","6.7% inverts the ratio (1,087 ÷ 162)."]},{id:"chk-2.1.3-6",topic:"2.1.3",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"A team has 40 fishbone causes for long fibre-repair times and no data yet. Which step should come NEXT to decide which causes to investigate first?",options:["Run a two-sample t-test on each cause","Implement solutions for all 40 causes","Build an X-Y diagram and carry the top-ranked X’s into the FMEA and data-collection plan","Fit a multiple regression with all 40 X’s"],answer:2,explanation:"With no data, the X-Y diagram is the Measure-phase tool to prioritise X’s; the shortlisted X’s then go to the FMEA and data collection.",whyWrong:["Hypothesis tests need data and belong to Analyze.","Implementing solutions skips Measure and Analyze.","","Regression needs data on every X and belongs to Improve."],traps:["first"]}],takeaways:["The X-Y diagram (X-Y / C&E matrix) prioritises potential X’s against several weighted Y’s.","Y’s come from the charter metrics and CTQs, weighted 1–10; X’s come from the process map, fishbone and basic data.","Rate relationships 0 / 1 / 3 / 9 (none / weak / moderate / strong) to avoid false precision and spread the scores.","Score = Σ(weight × rating); rank % = score ÷ total of all scores; view it as a Pareto.","Unweighted sums can reverse the ranking — always multiply by the weights.","X-Y Paretos are usually flat; choose the cut-off with judgement and a natural gap.","It is organised brainstorming, not data: top X’s go to the FMEA, data collection and Analyze-phase tests.","The matrix is a living document — update it when data or parameters change."],flashcards:[{front:"Other names for the X-Y diagram",back:"X-Y matrix; cause & effect (C&E) matrix."},{front:"What does the X-Y diagram prioritise?",back:"The X’s (independent variables), by their believed impact on several weighted Y’s."},{front:"Y weighting scale (book)",back:"1–10; 10 = most important; equal weights allowed."},{front:"Relationship rating scale (book)",back:"0 / 1 / 3 / 9 — none, weak, moderate, strong."},{front:"Weighted score formula",back:"Score(X) = Σ (weight of Y × rating of X against Y)."},{front:"Weights 10, 7, 4; ratings 9, 3, 1",back:"90 + 21 + 4 = 115."},{front:"Rank %",back:"An X’s score ÷ the total of all X scores (e.g. 162 ÷ 1,087 = 14.9%)."},{front:"Three sources of candidate X’s",back:"Process mapping, fishbone analysis, basic data analysis (graphical & statistical)."},{front:"Where do procedural and noise inputs go?",back:"Mainly into the FMEA; the X-Y matrix focuses on controllable X’s."},{front:"The book’s warning about the X-Y diagram",back:"It is organised brainstorming, not real data — results may be overturned by analysis."},{front:"Why avoid a 1–10 rating scale?",back:"False precision and “6 or 7?” debates; 0/1/3/9 forces clear choices and spreads scores."},{front:"What comes after the X-Y diagram?",back:"FMEA on the top X’s, then data collection / MSA, then Analyze-phase tests."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The NOC incident-restoration matrix, its weights and ratings are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data.","The book’s main text recommends rating on 1, 3 or 9 (with 1 labelled “weak (none)”), while its side note lists 0, 1, 3 or 9; this lesson uses 0 for “no relationship” and 1 for “weak”, which is the common practice.","Facilitation advice (silent scoring, limiting the number of Y’s, sensitivity checks) and the QFD house-of-quality link are general Lean Six Sigma practice, not taken from the supplied book pages.","Exam-tools tips refer to the PeopleCert exam environment (virtual calculator, notepad, formula Reference Document)."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Measure topic 2.1.3 “X-Y Diagram”."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 118",pdfPage:21,note:"Definition: tool to identify/collate potential X’s and assess their relative impact on multiple Y’s; team-based prioritisation; “not real data”; three sources of X’s."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 119",pdfPage:22,note:"Use controllable X’s from the fishbone; procedural and noise X’s go to the FMEA."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 120",pdfPage:23,note:"Steps: X’s in rows, Y’s (primary and secondary metrics) in columns, weight Y’s 1–10."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 121",pdfPage:24,note:"Rate each X on 0/1/3/9 (9 strong, 3 moderate, 1 weak); 1–10 scales cause uncertainty; ranking = Σ(rating × weight)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 122",pdfPage:25,note:"Completed laminating X-Y summary with weights, rankings, rank % and Pareto (total 1,087)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 113–114",pdfPage:16,note:"Controllable, procedural and noise inputs."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 136",pdfPage:39,note:"FMEA exercise built from the process map, fishbone and X-Y matrix."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 230",pdfPage:33,note:"Measure roadmap: select the vital few X’s causing problems (X-Y matrix, FMEA)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 235",pdfPage:38,note:"Measure quiz: the X-Y diagram helps prioritise the independent variables."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 245",pdfPage:48,note:"Multi-vari X’s can be pulled from the X-Y matrix."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"pp. 559–560",pdfPage:62,note:"Improvement selection matrix reuses the X-Y matrix outputs and importance ratings."}]},W=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:w},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),c=String.raw,k={id:"2.1.4",module:5,title:"Failure Modes & Effects Analysis (FMEA)",subtitle:"Severity × Occurrence × Detection — prioritise risk before it bites",minutes:18,objectives:["Explain the purpose of an FMEA and distinguish system, design (DFMEA), process (PFMEA) and equipment FMEAs.","Name and complete every column of a process FMEA, from process step to re-scored RPN.","Apply the 1–10 severity, occurrence and detection scales correctly — including that a detection score of 10 means the failure is hardest to detect.","Calculate RPN = S × O × D, rank failure modes, and explain why high-severity modes are addressed first even with a lower RPN.","Choose actions that reduce occurrence or improve detection, recognise that severity changes only through a design change, and re-score after actions.","Describe the limitations of RPN and how the FMEA links to the X-Y matrix, control plan and response plan."],overview:c`An [[x-y-matrix|X-Y diagram]] tells the team *which* inputs probably matter. A **[[fmea|Failure Modes & Effects Analysis]]** asks the next, sharper question: *for each process step and input, how could it fail, how bad would that be, how often would it happen, and would we catch it before the customer does?* Each answer becomes a 1–10 score, and the three scores multiply into a **[[rpn|Risk Priority Number]]** that ranks where to act first.

The training book defines the FMEA as a structured approach to predict failures and prevent their occurrence, identify the ways a process can fail to meet critical customer requirements (the Y’s), estimate the **severity, occurrence and detection (SOD)** of defects, evaluate the current controls, and prioritise actions with the RPN. In Measure it is built from team knowledge — process map, fishbone and X-Y matrix — and later updated with real data. The book is clear that the FMEA is not a tool for *eliminating* X’s but for **controlling** them and prioritising which to evaluate. It is a **living document** that follows the project into Control.`,sections:[{id:"types",heading:"Types of FMEA, history and when to use one",body:c`FMEA began in aerospace in the 1960s (the Apollo programme), was formalised by the US military in the 1970s, and spread into the automotive industry, where the Automotive Industry Action Group (AIAG) maintains the design and process FMEA standards. The basic premise is the same for every type:

- **System FMEA** — early concept level, where modules tie together into a system (for a broadband service: access network, core, OSS/BSS and home gateway).
- **Design FMEA (DFMEA)** — during product or service design, before release, to find how the design could fail. The **severity** of a design failure mode must be carried into the process FMEA.
- **Process FMEA (PFMEA)** — on manufacturing **and transactional** processes, to find failure modes that could escape to the customer. This is the type a Green Belt uses most.
- **Equipment FMEA** — on the equipment used to make or test the product (for example, the test set that checks fibre loss).

**Who and when.** It is a team tool: anyone who defines, runs or changes the process — operators, technical experts, supervisors, managers. Process FMEAs are started at conceptual design and updated whenever the process changes, when an existing process is reused in a new environment, and when a problem-solving project is completed and needs documenting.`},{id:"columns",heading:"The FMEA worksheet, column by column",body:c`The book’s template reads left to right like a story: *step → what goes wrong → so what → why → how often → what stops it → would we see it → risk → what we will do → new risk*.

| Column | What goes in it | Typical source |
|---|---|---|
| **#, Process step** | Steps in the same order as the process map | Process map |
| **Potential [[failure-mode|failure mode]]** | How the step could fail — essentially the defect | Historical defect data |
| **Potential failure effect** | Impact on the step output, the Y’s and the customer | Process map, VOC |
| **Severity (S)** | 1–10 seriousness of the effect | Agreed severity scale |
| **Class** | Controllable (C), procedural (P) or noise (N) input | Fishbone, process map |
| **Potential cause (X)** | How the failure could occur | Fishbone, X-Y matrix |
| **Occurrence (O)** | 1–10 likelihood the cause/failure happens | Capability studies, defect history |
| **Current process controls** | What prevents or detects it today | SOPs, SPC, poka-yoke, inspection |
| **Detection (D)** | 1–10 chance the control will *not* catch it | MSA studies, process map |
| **RPN** | S × O × D | Calculated |
| **Recommended actions** | Activity to prevent the cause or improve detection | Team |
| **Responsible person & target date** | Named owner and date | Team / sponsor |
| **Actions taken** | What was done and its effective date | Owner |
| **Re-scored S, O, D and RPN** | New ratings after the action | Team, preferably with data |

One failure mode can have **several causes** — each cause gets its own row with its own occurrence and detection, while the effect and severity repeat. The book’s quick reminder: “a failure mode is a fancy name for a defect.”

The book lists three kinds of **current process control**: [[spc|statistical process control]], **[[mistake-proofing|poka-yoke]]** (mistake proofing) and **detection after failure** (inspection). Prevention-type controls lower occurrence; detection-type controls lower the detection score.`},{id:"scales",heading:"The three 1–10 scales — and which way detection runs",body:c`Every scale runs from 1 (good, low risk) to 10 (bad, high risk). Students rarely trip on severity or occurrence; they trip on **detection**.

**[[severity|Severity]]** rates the seriousness of the *effect*. On the AIAG scale, 10 and 9 are hazardous (safety or regulatory non-compliance, 10 without warning, 9 with warning), 8 is loss of primary function with very dissatisfied customers, 5 is reduced performance with some dissatisfaction, and 1 is no effect. The book stresses that the wording matters less than **consistency**, and it offers a transactional scale for service businesses: 10 endangers the company’s ability to do business, 9 endangers the customer relationship, 7 is a major disruption with near-100% rework, 5 moderate, 3 low and 1 none. It also describes severity as a measure of the business impact of a failure in the output.

**[[occurrence|Occurrence]]** rates how often the cause and failure are expected. The AIAG table links scores to failure rates and capability: 10 ≈ 1 in 2 or worse (Cpk < 0.33), 5 ≈ 1 in 400 (Cpk ≥ 1.00), 1 ≤ 1 in 1,500,000 (Cpk ≥ 1.67). Use defect history or capability data where you can.

**[[detection|Detection]]** rates the probability that the current control will **fail to catch** the failure before it moves on. **10 = almost impossible to detect; 1 = almost certain to detect.** On the AIAG wording, a score of 1 corresponds to controls that catch about 99.5% of failures, 3 about 95%, 5 about 90%, and 10 to controls that catch fewer than 80% — or no control at all. The book says detection ratings should draw on [[msa|measurement system analysis]], because a control is only as good as its measurement.

| Score | Severity (effect) | Occurrence (likelihood) | Detection (current control) |
|:--:|---|---|---|
| 10 | Hazardous / regulatory, no warning | Almost inevitable (≥ 1 in 2) | Almost impossible to detect |
| 5 | Moderate disruption, some dissatisfaction | Occasional (≈ 1 in 400) | Moderate (≈ 90% caught) |
| 1 | No effect | Remote (≤ 1 in 1.5 million) | Almost certain to detect |

> **Memory hook:** in an FMEA, a **high number is always bad**. A high detection number means a **poor** ability to detect.`},{id:"rpn",heading:"RPN = S × O × D and how to prioritise",body:c`$$\text{RPN}=S\times O\times D$$

Because each factor runs 1–10, the RPN ranges from **1 to 1,000**. The team sorts failure modes by RPN — a Pareto ranking — and works down the list.

**Running example — fibre-to-the-home (FTTH) provisioning.** Five failure modes, scored by a cross-functional team:

| # | Step | Failure mode | S | O | D | RPN |
|:-:|---|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|--:|
| 1 | Feasibility check | Address wrongly marked serviceable | 7 | 6 | 5 | **210** |
| 2 | Appointment | Technician arrives, no access to premises | 5 | 7 | 8 | **280** |
| 3 | Splicing / drop install | High-loss splice from dirty connector | 8 | 5 | 6 | **240** |
| 4 | Indoor install | Drill strikes concealed power cable | 10 | 2 | 7 | **140** |
| 5 | ONT activation | Wrong service profile / VLAN applied | 8 | 4 | 3 | **96** |

Ranked by RPN alone: 280, 240, 210, 140, 96. But failure mode 4 has **severity 10** — a safety hazard to the technician and the customer. Common FMEA practice, and plain good sense, says **high-severity failure modes (typically 9–10) get action regardless of RPN**. A severity-10 mode at RPN 140 is reviewed *before* a severity-5 mode at RPN 280.

**Why RPN alone can mislead.** Three very different risks share RPN = 100: S10 × O2 × D5 (rare but catastrophic), S5 × O4 × D5 (middling) and S2 × O5 × D10 (trivial and invisible). The number hides which factor drives it. Always look at the individual S, O and D scores, not just the product.`},{id:"actions",heading:"Actions, ownership and re-scoring",body:c`Recommended actions attack the **cause** (to lower occurrence) or the **control** (to lower the detection score). Severity is a property of the *effect*; it **changes only if the design changes** so that the effect itself becomes less serious — for example, a network redesign with automatic failover so a link failure no longer causes an outage. Better inspection never reduces severity.

| Failure mode | Action (owner assigned, date set) | Factor attacked | New S × O × D | New RPN |
|---|---|---|---|--:|
| 1 Feasibility | Automated GIS feasibility check against live plant records | O and D | 7 × 3 × 3 | **63** |
| 2 Appointment | 24-hour SMS confirmation with reschedule link | O and D | 5 × 3 × 4 | **60** |
| 3 Splice loss | Cleaning kit + mandatory power-meter test before job closure | O and D | 8 × 3 × 2 | **48** |
| 4 Drill hazard | Mandatory cable-detector scan; route through existing ducts | O and D | 10 × 1 × 2 | **20** |
| 5 Wrong profile | Template-driven provisioning (poka-yoke) | O and D | 8 × 2 × 2 | **32** |

Total RPN falls from **966 to 223**, a **76.9%** reduction; every severity is unchanged. The new ranking (63, 60, 48, 32, 20) guides the next round — the FMEA is never “finished”.

**Re-score with evidence.** The book places re-scoring after the action has actually been *taken*, not merely planned, and the Control-phase checklist asks to “show improvement in RPN through action items” with revised occurrence and controls. A prevention action earns a lower occurrence only when data show fewer failures; a new check earns a lower detection score only when it is proven to catch the failure.

**Detection versus prevention.** Improving only detection on failure mode 2 (D 8 → 3) gives 5 × 7 × 3 = 105 — a 62.5% drop — but technicians still make wasted trips; you have simply found the problem earlier. Lean thinking prefers preventing the cause (lower O) or mistake-proofing it away.`},{id:"links",heading:"How the FMEA connects to the rest of DMAIC",body:c`- **Inputs.** Process steps come from the [[process-map|process map]]; causes from the [[fishbone|fishbone]] and the X-Y matrix; the **Class** column reuses the controllable / procedural / [[noise-input|noise]] labels. The book notes that noise inputs increase risk and therefore raise the RPN.
- **Measure → Analyze.** High-RPN causes become hypotheses to test; capability and MSA results refine occurrence and detection.
- **Improve.** The recommended-actions column is effectively an action plan; mistake-proofing and SPC are typical actions.
- **Control.** The book recommends the FMEA as a source for the monitoring and **[[response-plan|response plan]]**: the highest-risk X’s are the ones to monitor, and each failure mode needs a trigger, a response and an owner. The updated FMEA is part of the **[[control-plan|control plan]]** documentation handed to the process owner.`},{id:"limitations",heading:"Limitations of RPN and modern variants",advanced:!0,body:c`*Advanced / partly supplementary.*

- **Ordinal scales.** S, O and D are rankings, not measurements. An RPN of 200 is not “twice as risky” as 100, and multiplying ordinal numbers is mathematically shaky.
- **Gaps and duplicates.** Only 120 distinct values exist between 1 and 1,000, and many combinations collide (the three RPN-100 cases above).
- **Severity can be swamped.** A low occurrence and good detection can bury a catastrophic effect — hence the severity-first rule.
- **Thresholds are local.** Rules such as “act on anything above 100” are organisational conventions, not part of any standard; the exam expects ranking and judgement, not a magic number.
- **Gaming.** Teams under pressure can nudge O and D down; insist on data and the agreed scales.
- **Supplementary:** some organisations also look at **criticality** (S × O), and the 2019 AIAG-VDA FMEA handbook replaced RPN ranking with an **Action Priority** (high / medium / low) table that weights severity first. The Green Belt syllabus and the training book use RPN, so learn S × O × D for the exam.`}],whyItMatters:c`The FMEA is the Measure phase’s risk register. It turns a list of plausible causes into a ranked, owned action list, documents *why* the team acted where it did, and hands the process owner a ready-made basis for the control and response plans. Exam questions test the column order, the scale directions (especially detection), the RPN arithmetic, and the judgement calls: severity first, actions lower O or D, severity changes only by design.

In service operations the same discipline protects SLAs before they breach. An FMEA of a provisioning or incident process shows where a single unchecked manual step can cascade into missed installation dates, repeat visits or safety incidents — and gives operator and vendor a shared, numbered list of controls to agree and evidence.`,businessExample:{title:"An FTTH provisioning FMEA agreed between operator and field-services vendor",body:c`Installation lead time and repeat visits are rising. The DSO team runs a two-hour FMEA workshop with the field-services vendor, the GIS team and the activation desk, using the five failure modes above.

Before actions the total RPN is 966; the highest RPN is the no-access appointment (280), but the team escalates the **severity-10 drilling hazard** first and agrees a mandatory cable-detector scan within a week. Owners and target dates are recorded for all five actions. Three months later, re-scored with installation data and audit results, total RPN is 223 (−76.9%) with no change to any severity.

In the governance pack the vendor-facing wording stays evidence-based: *based on the joint FMEA, the no-access appointment rate is the largest contributor to process risk; the team is asked to share visit-outcome data for the period so the occurrence score can be validated.* The FMEA is then attached to the control plan, and its top failure modes become the triggers in the response plan.`},visual:"fmeaRpnBuild",visualCaption:"One FMEA row is built cell by cell for a high-loss splice in fibre provisioning: effect → severity 8, cause → occurrence 5, current control → detection 6 (10 = hardest to detect), then 8 × 5 × 6 = RPN 240. Five failure modes are ranked by RPN with the severity-10 drilling hazard flagged first; after actions the bars shrink as O and D fall (S unchanged) and total RPN drops from 966 to 223.",definitions:[{term:"FMEA",definition:"Failure Modes & Effects Analysis: a structured, team-based method to identify how a process or design can fail, rate the risk of each failure and prioritise actions."},{term:"Failure mode",definition:"The way a process step or design could fail to meet its requirement — essentially the defect."},{term:"Failure effect",definition:"The consequence of the failure mode on the step output, the Y’s and the customer."},{term:"Severity (S)",definition:"1–10 rating of how serious the effect is; 10 = hazardous / regulatory without warning, 1 = no effect."},{term:"Potential cause",definition:"How the failure mode could occur — an X from the fishbone or X-Y matrix; each cause gets its own row."},{term:"Occurrence (O)",definition:"1–10 rating of how likely the cause and failure are; 10 = almost inevitable, 1 = remote."},{term:"Current process controls",definition:"Existing means of preventing or detecting the failure: SPC, poka-yoke (mistake proofing) or inspection after failure."},{term:"Detection (D)",definition:"1–10 rating of the likelihood that current controls will NOT catch the failure before it moves on; 10 = almost impossible to detect, 1 = almost certain."},{term:"RPN (Risk Priority Number)",definition:"S × O × D, from 1 to 1,000; used to rank failure modes for action."},{term:"DFMEA / PFMEA",definition:"Design FMEA analyses product or service design failures before release; Process FMEA analyses manufacturing or transactional process failures. DFMEA severity carries into the PFMEA."},{term:"Re-scored RPN",definition:"The new S × O × D after actions have been taken; shows the risk reduction achieved."},{term:"Living document",definition:"A document updated throughout the project and after it, as data and process changes appear — as the FMEA should be."}],comparisons:[{title:"Severity vs occurrence vs detection",headers:["","Severity (S)","Occurrence (O)","Detection (D)"],rows:[["Rates","Seriousness of the effect","Likelihood of the cause/failure","Chance the current control misses it"],["10 means","Hazardous / regulatory, no warning","Almost inevitable (≥ 1 in 2)","Almost impossible to detect"],["1 means","No effect","Remote (≤ 1 in 1.5 million)","Almost certain to detect"],["Best data source","Customer / business impact scale","Defect history, capability (Cpk)","MSA studies, control effectiveness"],["Lowered by","Design change only","Removing or preventing the cause","Better, proven controls (poka-yoke, SPC, testing)"]]},{title:"Design FMEA vs process FMEA",headers:["","Design FMEA (DFMEA)","Process FMEA (PFMEA)"],rows:[["Focus","How the product or service design could fail","How the process that delivers it could fail"],["When","Early design, before release","Quality planning; when processes change; in DMAIC projects"],["Typical causes","Material, specification, design choices","People, methods, machines, measurement, environment"],["Telecom example","Home gateway without battery backup","Wrong VLAN profile applied at activation"],["Link","Severity carried forward into the PFMEA","Uses the DFMEA severity for shared effects"]]},{title:"Before vs after actions (FTTH example)",headers:["Failure mode","S","Before O × D → RPN","After O × D → RPN"],rows:[["Feasibility wrongly serviceable","7","6 × 5 → 210","3 × 3 → 63"],["Appointment, no access","5","7 × 8 → 280","3 × 4 → 60"],["High-loss splice","8","5 × 6 → 240","3 × 2 → 48"],["Drill hits power cable","10","2 × 7 → 140","1 × 2 → 20"],["Wrong service profile","8","4 × 3 → 96","2 × 2 → 32"],["Total","—","966","223 (−76.9%)"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"A detection score of 10 means excellent detection.",reality:"It means the failure is almost impossible to detect with current controls. In an FMEA a high number is always bad."},{myth:"Always fix the highest RPN first.",reality:"High-severity failure modes (9–10, safety or regulatory) are addressed first even when their RPN is lower; RPN ranks the rest."},{myth:"Adding inspection lowers severity.",reality:"Severity rates the effect; it changes only when the design changes the effect. Inspection improves detection."},{myth:"Two failure modes with the same RPN carry the same risk.",reality:"S10×O2×D5, S5×O4×D5 and S2×O5×D10 all give 100 but describe very different risks; look at S, O and D individually."},{myth:"The FMEA is completed once in Measure and filed.",reality:"It is a living document: re-scored after actions, updated with data, and used to build the control and response plans."},{myth:"The FMEA eliminates X’s from the project.",reality:"The book says it does not eliminate X’s but helps control them and prioritise which to evaluate."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“Which detection rating indicates the failure is MOST likely to reach the customer?”",explanation:"10 — the hardest to detect. Students who think “10 = best” pick 1.",tag:"most"},{trap:"Options list an action that “reduces severity by adding a final inspection”.",explanation:"Inspection improves detection only. Severity changes only through a design change to the effect.",tag:"not"},{trap:"“Which failure mode should be addressed FIRST?” with a severity-10 mode at RPN 140 and a severity-5 mode at RPN 280.",explanation:"The severity-10 (safety) mode — high severity is acted on regardless of RPN.",tag:"first"},{trap:"RPN calculated as S + O + D or as an average.",explanation:"RPN = S × O × D. For 8, 5, 6 the RPN is 240, not 19 or 6.33."},{trap:"“The FMEA abbreviation stands for Failure Measures Effect Analysis.”",explanation:"It is Failure Modes and Effects Analysis (the book’s own quiz uses this trap)."},{trap:"Re-scoring the RPN BEFORE the action is implemented.",explanation:"Re-score after the action is taken and verified; planned actions earn no reduction.",tag:"after"},{trap:"“Which column is obtained from the fishbone diagram?”",explanation:"Potential causes (and the C/P/N class). Effects come mainly from the process map; failure modes from defect history."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Full FTTH provisioning FMEA, before and after",problem:c`Five failure modes with (S, O, D): feasibility 7,6,5; appointment 5,7,8; splice loss 8,5,6; drill hazard 10,2,7; wrong profile 8,4,3. After actions (S unchanged) the (O, D) become 3,3; 3,4; 3,2; 1,2; 2,2. Compute every RPN, both totals and the percentage reduction. Which mode is addressed first?`,steps:["Before: 7×6×5 = **210**; 5×7×8 = **280**; 8×5×6 = **240**; 10×2×7 = **140**; 8×4×3 = **96**. Total = **966**.","RPN ranking: appointment 280 > splice 240 > feasibility 210 > drill 140 > profile 96.","Severity check: the drill hazard has S = 10 (safety) — it is escalated first despite ranking fourth on RPN.","After: 7×3×3 = **63**; 5×3×4 = **60**; 8×3×2 = **48**; 10×1×2 = **20**; 8×2×2 = **32**. Total = **223**.","Reduction = (966 − 223) ÷ 966 = 743 ÷ 966 = **76.9%**.","**Exam-tools tip:** multiply the two smaller numbers first (e.g. 6 × 5 = 30, then × 7 = 210) and write each RPN on the notepad before ranking."],answer:"RPNs 210, 280, 240, 140, 96 (total 966) → 63, 60, 48, 20, 32 (total 223), a 76.9% reduction; the severity-10 drill hazard is acted on first.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Rank purely by RPN and schedule the drill hazard fourth.",whyWrong:"RPN can bury a catastrophic effect behind a low occurrence. Severity 9–10 failure modes are addressed regardless of RPN."}},{title:"Same RPN, different risk",problem:c`Three failure modes all have RPN 100: A (S10, O2, D5), B (S5, O4, D5), C (S2, O5, D10). Are they equally urgent?`,steps:["Check: 10×2×5 = 100; 5×4×5 = 100; 2×5×10 = 100.","A has a catastrophic effect that is rare and moderately detectable → highest priority because of severity.","B is middling on every dimension.","C is trivial in effect though invisible → lowest real risk; a cheap detection improvement may be enough."],answer:"No — despite identical RPNs, A (severity 10) comes first and C last.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“All three RPNs are 100, so the order does not matter.”",whyWrong:"RPN multiplies ordinal scores and hides which factor drives the risk. Always read S, O and D individually."}},{title:"Detection-only improvement vs prevention",problem:c`For the no-access appointment (S5, O7, D8, RPN 280) the team compares two options: (a) a pre-visit phone call that detects most no-access cases (D → 3); (b) SMS confirmation that prevents most of them and flags the rest (O → 3, D → 4). Compare the RPNs.`,steps:["Option (a): 5 × 7 × 3 = **105** — a 62.5% reduction ((280 − 105) ÷ 280).","Option (b): 5 × 3 × 4 = **60** — a 78.6% reduction.","Option (a) only finds the problem earlier; the failed appointments still happen and must be rebooked.","Option (b) attacks the cause, so it is preferred — Lean favours prevention over detection."],answer:"(a) RPN 105; (b) RPN 60 — prevention gives the bigger and more valuable reduction.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Option (a) lowers severity to 2, so RPN = 2 × 7 × 3 = 42.",whyWrong:"A detection control does not change how serious the effect is; severity stays 5."}},{title:"Reading the detection scale",problem:c`The current control for a wrong VLAN profile is an automated activation test that, according to the MSA study, catches about **95%** of failures. Using the AIAG-style detection criteria in the book, what detection score fits, and what is the RPN if S = 8 and O = 4?`,steps:["AIAG wording: ≈ 99.5% caught → 1; ≈ 97.5% → 2; ≈ 95% → 3; ≈ 90% → 5; < 80% → 10.","95% detection → **D = 3** (“high” detection ability, low score).","RPN = 8 × 4 × 3 = **96**."],answer:"D = 3; RPN = 96.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"95% is very good detection, so D = 9 or 10 → RPN = 8 × 4 × 10 = 320.",whyWrong:"The detection scale runs the other way: good detection means a LOW score. 10 is reserved for almost impossible detection."}}],lab:"fmea",check:[{id:"chk-2.1.4-1",topic:"2.1.4",category:"calculation",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!0,stem:"A failure mode has severity **6**, occurrence **4** and detection **7**. What is its RPN?",options:["17","5.67","168","42"],answer:2,explanation:"RPN = S × O × D = 6 × 4 × 7 = 168.",whyWrong:["17 adds the three scores instead of multiplying.","5.67 is the average of the scores.","","42 multiplies only S and D (6 × 7), omitting occurrence."]},{id:"chk-2.1.4-2",topic:"2.1.4",category:"concept",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"In a process FMEA, what does a **detection** score of 10 indicate?",options:["The current controls are almost certain to detect the failure","The failure is almost impossible to detect with current controls","The failure occurs in almost every unit","The failure has the most severe effect"],answer:1,explanation:"Detection rates the chance the control will NOT catch the failure; 10 = almost impossible to detect.",whyWrong:["That describes a detection score of 1.","","That is a high occurrence score.","That is a high severity score."],traps:["most"]},{id:"chk-2.1.4-3",topic:"2.1.4",category:"scenario",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!0,stem:"Failure mode A: S = 9, O = 2, D = 3. Failure mode B: S = 4, O = 6, D = 8. Which statement reflects good FMEA practice?",options:["Address B first because its RPN (192) is higher than A’s (54) and ignore A","Neither needs action because both RPNs are below 200","A and B are equal risks because both total 14 when added","Address A with priority because of its high severity, even though its RPN (54) is lower than B’s (192)"],answer:3,explanation:"A: 9 × 2 × 3 = 54; B: 4 × 6 × 8 = 192. High-severity modes (9–10) are acted on regardless of RPN; B is then ranked by RPN.",whyWrong:["Ignoring a severity-9 failure mode because of its RPN is the trap the severity-first rule prevents.","There is no universal RPN threshold; 200 is an arbitrary number.","RPN multiplies; adding scores is meaningless.",""],traps:["first"]},{id:"chk-2.1.4-4",topic:"2.1.4",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A team adds a 100% final inspection of activated fibre lines. Which FMEA rating should this action change?",options:["Severity","Occurrence","Detection","Severity and occurrence"],answer:2,explanation:"Inspection catches failures after they occur, so it lowers the detection score. It does not change how often the cause occurs or how serious the effect is.",whyWrong:["Severity changes only through a design change to the effect.","Inspection does not prevent the cause, so occurrence is unchanged.","","Neither severity nor occurrence is affected by inspection."],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-2.1.4-5",topic:"2.1.4",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A failure mode is rated S = 7, O = 3, D = 5. After a poka-yoke check is installed and proven, detection is re-scored to **2**. By what percentage does the RPN fall?",options:["60%","40%","3%","63%"],answer:0,explanation:"Before: 7 × 3 × 5 = 105. After: 7 × 3 × 2 = 42. Reduction = (105 − 42) ÷ 105 = 60%.",whyWrong:["","40% is the after/before ratio (42 ÷ 105), not the reduction.","3% treats the change in D (5 − 2 = 3) as a percentage.","63% is the RPN drop in points (105 − 42 = 63), not a percentage."],traps:["after"]},{id:"chk-2.1.4-6",topic:"2.1.4",category:"incorrect-statement",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement about the FMEA is NOT correct?",options:["It is a living document updated as the process and data change","Potential causes are typically drawn from the fishbone and X-Y matrix","Once RPNs are calculated in Measure, the FMEA is complete and need not be revisited","It can feed the response plan in the Control phase"],answer:2,explanation:"The FMEA is re-scored after actions and updated throughout the project and into Control; it is never “complete”.",whyWrong:["Correct — the book calls it a living document.","Correct — causes come from the fishbone, with priorities from the X-Y matrix.","","Correct — the book recommends the FMEA for the monitoring and response plan."],traps:["not"]}],takeaways:["FMEA identifies how each step or input can fail, rates the risk and prioritises action; PFMEA is the Green Belt’s main type.","Columns: step → failure mode → effect → S → class → cause → O → current controls → D → RPN → actions → owner/date → actions taken → re-scored S, O, D, RPN.","All three scales run 1–10 with 10 = worst; detection 10 = almost impossible to detect.","RPN = S × O × D (1–1,000); rank failure modes by RPN, but act on severity 9–10 regardless of RPN.","Actions lower occurrence (prevent the cause) or detection (better, proven controls); severity changes only by design.","Re-score after actions are implemented and verified, preferably with data.","RPN is ordinal and can hide very different risks (S10×O2×D5 = S2×O5×D10 = 100).","Inputs: process map, fishbone, X-Y matrix; outputs feed Analyze, Improve, the control plan and the response plan."],flashcards:[{front:"FMEA stands for…",back:"Failure Modes and Effects Analysis."},{front:"RPN formula and range",back:"Severity × Occurrence × Detection; 1 to 1,000."},{front:"Detection score 10 means…",back:"Almost impossible to detect with current controls (worst)."},{front:"Detection score 1 means…",back:"Almost certain to detect (best)."},{front:"What lowers severity?",back:"Only a design change that makes the effect less serious."},{front:"Prevention action lowers…",back:"Occurrence (and often detection)."},{front:"Inspection / testing action lowers…",back:"Detection score only."},{front:"Severity-first rule",back:"Failure modes with severity 9–10 get action regardless of their RPN."},{front:"Three types of current control (book)",back:"SPC, poka-yoke (mistake proofing), detection after failure (inspection)."},{front:"DFMEA vs PFMEA",back:"Design failures before release vs process failures that could escape to the customer; DFMEA severity carries into the PFMEA."},{front:"S 8, O 5, D 6",back:"RPN = 240."},{front:"Main limitation of RPN",back:"Ordinal scores multiplied; identical RPNs can hide very different risks and severity can be swamped."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The FTTH provisioning failure modes, scores and actions are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data.","The “severity 9–10 first regardless of RPN” rule is widely used FMEA practice (and reflected in the AIAG-VDA Action Priority approach); the supplied book pages rank by RPN with a Pareto and do not state the rule explicitly.","The AIAG-VDA (2019) Action Priority method, criticality (S × O) and the count of distinct RPN values are supplementary context, not part of the Green Belt syllabus text.","Scale anchor wording is paraphrased from the AIAG tables reproduced in the book; organisations are expected to define and standardise their own scales."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Measure topic 2.1.4 “Failure Modes & Effects Analysis (FMEA)”."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 123",pdfPage:26,note:"Definition and purposes of FMEA (predict failures, SOD, evaluate controls, prioritise with RPN); controls X’s rather than eliminating them."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 124",pdfPage:27,note:"History (Apollo, MIL-STD-1629, AIAG) and types: system, design, process, equipment; DFMEA severity carried into PFMEA."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 125",pdfPage:28,note:"Purpose of FMEA; who creates it and when it is started and updated."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 126–128",pdfPage:29,note:"FMEA template columns; process step, failure modes (from historical defect data), effects (from the process map)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 129–130",pdfPage:32,note:"AIAG severity scale; transactional severity example; consistency of definitions."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 131",pdfPage:34,note:"Class column (C/P/N) and potential causes from the fishbone."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 132",pdfPage:35,note:"Occurrence scale with failure rates and Cpk."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 133",pdfPage:36,note:"Current process controls (SPC, poka-yoke, inspection); detection draws on MSA."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 134",pdfPage:37,note:"Detection ranking (10 almost impossible … 1 almost certain); RPN = SEV × OCC × DET."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 135",pdfPage:38,note:"Recommended actions, responsible person & date, actions taken; living document; adjusted RPN."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 119",pdfPage:22,note:"Procedural and noise X’s go to the FMEA; noise raises RPN."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 235",pdfPage:38,note:"Measure quiz items on the FMEA abbreviation and purpose."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"pp. 570–571",pdfPage:73,note:"FMEA as a tool for the monitoring and response plan; monitor the highest-risk X’s."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 579",pdfPage:82,note:"Complete RPN assessment with revised frequency and controls; show improvement in RPN through action items."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 588",pdfPage:91,note:"Glossary: Risk Priority Number."}]},_=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:k},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),i=String.raw,P={id:"2.2.1",module:6,title:"Basic Statistics",subtitle:"Data types, populations, samples and the language of variation",minutes:16,objectives:["Explain why statistics is the working language of the Measure phase and what “basic statistics” is for.","Classify any metric as continuous, discrete (count) or attribute data, and place it on the nominal, ordinal, interval or ratio scale.","Distinguish a population from a sampling frame and a sample, and a parameter (μ, σ, N) from a statistic (x̄, s, n).","Read the standard notation table and choose the sample (n − 1) or population (N) formula correctly.","Describe how the precision of an estimate grows with sample size through the standard error s ÷ √n.","Separate descriptive from inferential statistics, outline a basic data collection plan, and convert attribute data into continuous data or rates where possible."],overview:i`Every Six Sigma project eventually reaches a moment where opinions stop counting and data take over. The Measure phase is that moment. Before you can calculate capability, run a hypothesis test or build a control chart, you need a shared vocabulary for **what kind of data you hold**, **where it came from** and **what a number computed from it can and cannot tell you**. That vocabulary is basic statistics.

The training book frames the purpose neatly: statistics gives a numerical summary of the data, a basis for inferences about the future, a foundation for assessing process capability and a common language across the organisation. Six Sigma does not turn people into statisticians; it turns them into problem solvers who use the right statistical tool at the right time. In this lesson you will learn to classify data before analysing it, to tell a population parameter from a sample statistic, to read the notation used in every later module, and to understand why a bigger, better sample gives a sharper answer. Lesson 2.2.2 then puts the numbers to work.`,sections:[{id:"why-statistics",heading:"Why statistics sits at the heart of Measure",body:i`A process “speaks” through its data. Resolution times, dropped-call counts, billing errors and latency readings are the process telling you how it behaves. Statistics is how you listen without being misled by noise, anecdotes or the loudest voice in the room.

Three ideas make statistics indispensable in Measure:

1. **Variation is the enemy, and you can only reduce what you can describe.** “Tickets take too long” is a complaint; “mean resolution time is 37 minutes with a standard deviation of 6 minutes” is a baseline you can improve against. Statistics converts experience into a [[baseline|baseline]].
2. **Decisions are made on samples.** You will rarely examine every ticket, call or invoice. Statistics tells you how far a sample can be trusted and how big it must be.
3. **Every later tool assumes you have classified your data.** Capability indices, hypothesis tests and control charts all come in versions for continuous data and versions for discrete data. Choose the wrong family and the answer is wrong before the first calculation.

Statistics also provides the **common language** the book stresses: when a vendor, a process owner and a Green Belt all mean the same thing by “average”, “spread” and “sample”, arguments about whose spreadsheet is right become far less frequent.`},{id:"data-types",heading:"Types of data: continuous, discrete and attribute",body:i`The first question to ask of any dataset is *what kind of data is this?* The answer decides which graphs, summaries and tests are legitimate.

**[[continuous-data|Continuous data]]** (also called variable or quantitative data) are measured on a scale that can be subdivided meaningfully: time, money, length, temperature, latency. 42.5 minutes is a real value; with a better measuring device you could record 42.53. Continuous data carry the most information per observation, so you need fewer of them to detect a change.

**[[discrete-data|Discrete (count) data]]** are whole-number counts of events: defects on an invoice, dropped calls per day, customer returns. There is no such thing as half a dropped call. Counts can be large and are often analysed as rates.

**[[attribute-data|Attribute data]]** describe a quality or category rather than an amount: pass/fail, SLA met / not met, incident category, priority, a satisfaction rating. The training book defines attribute data at its most basic as **binary** (yes/no, go/no-go) and notes that attribute results must be *counted* — turned into discrete data — before they can be analysed.

> **Terminology warning.** Different sources draw the boundary slightly differently. The supplied book separates binary *attribute* data from *discrete count* data; many other texts and Minitab menus use “attribute” for both (attribute control charts, for example, cover defect counts and proportions). On the exam, read the stem: what matters is whether the value is **measured on a continuous scale** or **counted / categorised**.

A quick sorting rule works for almost every metric you will meet:

1. *Can it be meaningfully subdivided into decimals?* → continuous.
2. *Is it a count of whole events?* → discrete.
3. *Is it a label, a category or a judgement?* → attribute.

The animation above applies exactly these three questions to nine operational metrics.`},{id:"scales",heading:"Measurement scales: nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio",body:i`A second, finer classification describes *what arithmetic is allowed*. Each scale permits everything the one before it does, plus one more operation.

| Scale | What you can do | Telecom example | Typical summary |
|---|---|---|---|
| **[[nominal|Nominal]]** | Name and count categories (=, ≠) | Incident category; SLA met yes/no; region | Counts, proportions, mode |
| **[[ordinal|Ordinal]]** | Also put in order (<, >) — gaps undefined | Priority P1–P4; CSAT 1–5 | Median, percentiles |
| **Interval** | Also subtract — equal gaps, but no true zero | Server-room temperature in °C; calendar dates | Mean, standard deviation, differences |
| **Ratio** | Also multiply and divide — true zero exists | Resolution time; latency; cost; counts | Everything, including ratios and CV |

Two consequences matter in practice:

- **Numbers are not always quantities.** Priority “1” to “4” and CSAT “1” to “5” are *codes*. The difference between P1 and P2 is not the same as between P3 and P4, so averaging priority codes is meaningless. Use the median or the proportion in each category.
- **Ratios need a true zero.** A ticket resolved in 60 minutes took twice as long as one resolved in 30 — ratio scale. But 30 °C is not “twice as hot” as 15 °C, because 0 °C is not an absence of heat — interval scale. The book’s IQ example makes the same point: a 20-point gap is meaningful, but a score of 120 is not “1.2 times” a score of 100.`},{id:"population-sample",heading:"Population, sampling frame and sample — parameters versus statistics",body:i`The **[[population|population]]** is every item that has the property you care about, carefully defined: *all incident tickets closed by the managed-services vendor in September*. The **sampling frame** is the identifiable list from which you can actually draw — say, the ITSM export for September. Ideally it matches the population exactly, with each element listed once; in practice it may miss tickets logged by e-mail or include test records, and any gap becomes a bias in every result. The **[[sample|sample]]** is the smaller subset you actually examine.

A number that describes a whole population is a **[[parameter|parameter]]**; the matching number computed from a sample is a **[[statistic|statistic]]**. By convention parameters use Greek letters and statistics use Latin letters:

| Quantity | Population parameter | Sample statistic |
|---|---|---|
| Size | $N$ | $n$ |
| Mean | $\mu$ (mu) | $\bar{x}$ (x-bar) |
| Standard deviation | $\sigma$ (sigma) | $s$ |
| Variance | $\sigma^2$ | $s^2$ |
| Proportion | $P$ (or $\pi$) | $p$ |

Why sample at all? The book’s answer is economic: a well-drawn sample gives a sufficiently accurate inference for far less time, money and effort, and it is the foundation for formal statistical inference. Some processes can *only* be sampled — you cannot audit every call recording, and destructive tests consume the unit.

**Which formula?** The formulas for a mean are identical; only the symbol changes. For the standard deviation they differ: the population formula divides by $N$, the sample formula by $n-1$. Because Six Sigma work is almost always done on samples, Minitab’s “StDev” and Excel’s STDEV.S use $n-1$. Lesson 2.2.2 explains why.`},{id:"notation",heading:"The notation you will meet in every later module",body:i`The book offers a “cheat sheet” of symbols and advises not to memorise it all — but you must recognise these on sight, because the exam Reference Document uses them without explanation.

| Symbol | Read as | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| $x_i$ | x-sub-i | One individual observation; $x_1$ is the first |
| $\sum$ | sigma (capital) | Add up everything that follows |
| $n$, $N$ | n, N | Sample size, population size |
| $\bar{x}$, $\mu$ | x-bar, mu | Sample mean, population mean |
| $\bar{\bar{x}}$ | x-double-bar | Grand mean (mean of subgroup means) |
| $s$, $\sigma$ | s, sigma | Sample and population standard deviation |
| $s^2$, $\sigma^2$ | s-squared, sigma-squared | Sample and population variance |
| $R$, $\bar{R}$ | R, R-bar | Range; average range |
| $p$, $P$ | p, P | Sample and population proportion |
| $\lvert x \rvert$ | absolute value | Size ignoring sign |

**Location, spread and shape.** Descriptive measures fall into three families, each answering a different question:

- **Location (central tendency)** — *where is the centre?* [[mean|Mean]], [[median|median]], [[mode|mode]].
- **Spread (dispersion)** — *how much do values vary?* [[range|Range]], [[iqr|interquartile range]], [[variance|variance]], [[standard-deviation|standard deviation]].
- **Shape** — *how are values distributed around the centre?* Symmetry ([[skewness|skewness]]), tail weight ([[kurtosis|kurtosis]]), number of peaks and granularity.

A Six Sigma problem is nearly always a problem of **centring**, **spread** or **both** — the book’s Measure checklist asks exactly that question. Never report location without spread: two vendors with the same 37-minute mean resolution time can deliver completely different customer experiences.`},{id:"precision-n",heading:"Accuracy of estimates grows with sample size",advanced:!0,body:i`A sample mean is an estimate, and different samples give slightly different means. How much do they wander? The answer is the **[[standard-error|standard error]] of the mean**:

$$SE_{\bar{x}}=\frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}$$

Two things follow, and both are exam favourites:

1. **Precision improves with the square root of $n$, not with $n$.** With $s = 12$ ms, a sample of 9 latency readings gives $SE = 12 \div 3 = 4$ ms; 36 readings give $12 \div 6 = 2$ ms; 144 readings give 1 ms. To **halve** the standard error you must **quadruple** the sample.
2. **Diminishing returns.** The step from 4 to 16 observations buys far more precision than the step from 64 to 76. The book notes that the rate of change of the standard error flattens out at around 30 observations, which is one reason “about 30” appears so often as a practical minimum.

Minitab prints this quantity as “SE Mean”. In the book’s descriptive output for 200 measurements with a standard deviation of 0.01007, SE Mean is $0.01007 \div \sqrt{200} \approx 0.000712$ — the mean of 200 parts is known far more precisely than any single part.

Standard error measures *precision*, not *accuracy*. A huge sample drawn from a biased frame — only the tickets the vendor chose to export — gives a very precise estimate of the wrong number. Sampling methods, the [[central-limit-theorem|Central Limit Theorem]] and [[confidence-interval|confidence intervals]] are developed fully in Analyze (3.2).`},{id:"descriptive-inferential",heading:"Descriptive versus inferential statistics",body:i`**Descriptive statistics** summarise the data you actually have: “the 240 sampled tickets had a mean resolution time of 3.4 hours.” Nothing is claimed beyond those 240 tickets. Measure relies heavily on descriptive statistics and graphs to establish the baseline.

**Inferential statistics** use a sample to draw conclusions about a population or a future process, with a stated level of risk: “we are 95% confident the mean resolution time of *all* September tickets lies between 3.1 and 3.7 hours,” or “the new routing rule reduced mean resolution time; p = 0.01.” [[inference|Inference]], [[hypothesis-test|hypothesis tests]] and regression live mainly in Analyze and Improve.

| | Descriptive | Inferential |
|---|---|---|
| Question | What do these data look like? | What can we conclude beyond these data? |
| Output | Mean, median, s, IQR, graphs | Confidence intervals, p-values, predictions |
| Risk statement | None needed | Always (α, confidence level) |
| DMAIC home | Measure (baseline) | Analyze, Improve, Control |

The two are partners: a careful description (shape, outliers, stability) tells you which inferential tool is valid.`},{id:"collection-plan",heading:"Data collection plan basics — and converting attribute to continuous",body:i`Good statistics start before the first number is recorded. A simple **data collection plan** answers:

1. **What** will be measured, with an [[operational-definition|operational definition]]? (“Resolution time = timestamp *Resolved* minus timestamp *Logged*, in minutes, excluding *Pending customer*.”)
2. **Which data type** is it, and can a more informative type be recorded instead?
3. **Where and how** — the data collection points, the source system and the measurement method (whose reliability is tested in [[msa|measurement system analysis]], 2.3).
4. **How many and how chosen** — sample size and sampling method ([[random-sampling|random]], [[stratified-sampling|stratified]] …).
5. **Who and when** — responsibility, frequency and time period.
6. **Stratification factors** — the X’s recorded alongside Y so that you can slice the data later: shift, day of week, region, resolver group, vendor, technician experience. The book lists time, location, operator and procedure as typical sources.

Recording stratification factors costs almost nothing at collection time and is impossible to recover afterwards.

**Convert attribute to continuous wherever you can.** Continuous data reveal *how far* from the target each unit fell, so they need far smaller samples and support more powerful tools. The book’s examples: “15 scratches” is less useful than “total scratch length 9.25”; “200 defects” is less useful than “25 defects per hour”.

In a service operation:

- Instead of “SLA met: yes/no”, record the **actual resolution time**. 18 breaches in 240 tickets (7.5%) tells you how often you failed; the resolution times tell you whether the other 222 tickets finished with minutes or hours to spare.
- Instead of “54 dropped calls”, report **30 dropped calls per 1,000 calls** — a rate that can be compared across days with different traffic.
- Instead of “customer satisfied: yes/no”, keep the full 1–5 rating (ordinal), and where possible a measured driver such as wait time in seconds.`}],whyItMatters:i`The Measure phase carries the largest share of the exam (32%), and nearly every question in it assumes you can classify data and read statistical notation instantly. Misclassify a CSAT score as continuous, or report a vendor’s sample mean as if it were the population mean, and every later conclusion inherits the error.

For an operational-excellence engineer this is also governance. SLA reports often summarise attribute data (“met / breached”) drawn from a sampling frame the vendor controls. Knowing to ask which population the figures represent, how the sample was drawn and whether the underlying continuous data are available is the difference between accepting a headline and understanding a process.`,businessExample:{title:"Re-baselining incident resolution in a Digital Service Operations unit",body:i`A DSO team wants a baseline for incident resolution before a routing improvement. The monthly vendor report states “92.5% of P2 incidents resolved within SLA”.

- **Classify.** “Within SLA” is binary attribute data; the underlying resolution time is continuous ratio data; priority is ordinal.
- **Define the population and frame.** Population: all P2 incidents closed in the quarter. Frame: the ITSM export. A check finds some P2 incidents raised by phone were logged late, so the frame is reconciled first.
- **Sample and describe.** A random sample of 240 tickets is drawn with resolution time, resolver group, shift and region recorded. In the sample, 18 tickets (7.5%) breached — consistent with the report — but the continuous data show a long right tail concentrated in one resolver group.
- **Frame the finding for the vendor.** Written as a request: *based on the sample analysis, approximately 7.5% of P2 incidents exceeded the target, with longer resolution times concentrated in one resolver group; the team is asked to clarify the queue-assignment rules applied and to provide supporting data for the affected tickets.*

The attribute KPI described *how often* the target was missed; the continuous data showed *where* and *by how much* — the starting point for Analyze.`},visual:"dataTypeSorter",visualCaption:"Two animations. Data-type sorter: nine telecom metrics answer three questions (subdivisible? a count? a category?) and drop into continuous, discrete and attribute bins, then receive their nominal / ordinal / interval / ratio scale. Population → sample: a population of 2,400 tickets (parameters μ, σ), a sampling frame, a random sample giving statistics x̄ = 37.0 and s = 6.0 minutes, and the standard error s ÷ √n shrinking from 3 to 1.5 to 0.75 as n grows from 4 to 16 to 64.",definitions:[{term:"Statistics",definition:"The science of collecting, summarising and interpreting data so that decisions can be made under variation and uncertainty."},{term:"Continuous data",definition:"Measurements on a scale that can be meaningfully subdivided (time, cost, latency); also called variable or quantitative data."},{term:"Discrete (count) data",definition:"Whole-number counts of events or items (defects, dropped calls); values cannot be meaningfully subdivided."},{term:"Attribute data",definition:"Categorical or pass/fail information describing a quality rather than an amount; must be counted to be analysed."},{term:"Nominal scale",definition:"Categories with names only and no order (incident category, region)."},{term:"Ordinal scale",definition:"Ordered categories whose gaps are not equal or not defined (priority P1–P4, CSAT 1–5)."},{term:"Interval scale",definition:"Ordered values with equal, meaningful differences but no true zero (temperature in °C)."},{term:"Ratio scale",definition:"Interval data with a true zero, so ratios are meaningful (time, cost, counts)."},{term:"Population",definition:"Every item that has the property of interest, defined precisely in scope and time."},{term:"Sampling frame",definition:"The identifiable list from which a sample is actually drawn; ideally identical to the population."},{term:"Sample",definition:"A subset of the population examined in order to draw conclusions about the whole."},{term:"Parameter",definition:"A numerical value that describes a population (μ, σ, σ², P, N); usually unknown."},{term:"Statistic",definition:"A numerical value computed from a sample (x̄, s, s², p, n) used to estimate a parameter."},{term:"Standard error of the mean",definition:"s ÷ √n — the typical amount a sample mean varies from sample to sample; shrinks as n grows."}],comparisons:[{title:"Continuous vs discrete vs attribute data",headers:["","Continuous","Discrete (count)","Attribute"],rows:[["Nature","Measured on a scale","Counted in whole numbers","Category or pass/fail"],["Subdivisible?","Yes — decimals meaningful","No — no half defect","No — labels only"],["Telecom example","Resolution time, latency","Dropped calls per day","SLA met?, incident category"],["Information per point","Highest","Medium","Lowest"],["Sample size needed","Smallest","Larger","Largest"],["Typical summary","Mean, s, median, IQR","Count, rate, DPU","Proportion, mode"]]},{title:"Population parameter vs sample statistic",headers:["","Population (parameter)","Sample (statistic)"],rows:[["Scope","Every item of interest","A subset actually examined"],["Symbols","N, μ, σ, σ², P","n, x̄, s, s², p"],["Known?","Usually not","Yes — calculated"],["SD divisor","N","n − 1"],["Example","All 2,400 tickets in September","240 randomly sampled tickets"]]},{title:"Descriptive vs inferential statistics",headers:["","Descriptive","Inferential"],rows:[["Purpose","Summarise the data in hand","Draw conclusions about a population or future"],["Tools","Mean, median, s, IQR, histograms, box plots","Confidence intervals, hypothesis tests, regression"],["Risk statement","None","α, β, confidence level"],["Main DMAIC phase","Measure","Analyze / Improve / Control"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"If it is a number, it is continuous data.",reality:"Codes such as priority 1–4 or CSAT 1–5 are ordinal; counts of dropped calls are discrete. Ask whether the value is measured on a subdivisible scale."},{myth:"A sample statistic is the true value for the process.",reality:"x̄ and s are estimates of μ and σ. A different sample would give slightly different values; the standard error says by how much."},{myth:"Doubling the sample size halves the uncertainty.",reality:"The standard error falls with √n. Doubling n reduces it by about 29%; you must quadruple n to halve it."},{myth:"A big sample fixes everything.",reality:"Size improves precision, not accuracy. A large sample from a biased frame is precisely wrong."},{myth:"Attribute data are as good as continuous data if you collect enough.",reality:"Pass/fail data discard how far each unit was from the target, so they need far larger samples and support weaker tools. Record the continuous measure when you can."},{myth:"Six Sigma requires you to become a statistician.",reality:"The book is explicit: the aim is to make people excellent problem solvers who apply appropriate statistical tools, usually with software such as Minitab."}],examTraps:[{trap:"An option labels “the average handle time of 50 sampled calls” as μ.",explanation:"A value computed from a sample is a statistic (x̄). μ is the population mean, usually unknown.",tag:"sample-vs-population"},{trap:"“How must the sample size change to HALVE the standard error?” — options include “double it”.",explanation:"SE = s ÷ √n, so n must be multiplied by 4 (9 → 36 halves 4 ms to 2 ms).",tag:"standard-error"},{trap:"Customer-satisfaction scores 1–5 are listed among the continuous variables.",explanation:"They are ordered categories (ordinal). Equal gaps between ratings cannot be assumed, so averaging them is questionable."},{trap:"“Which of the following is NOT continuous data?” with the count of defects among measured times and costs.",explanation:"A count of defects is discrete; time, cost and latency are continuous.",tag:"not"},{trap:"Temperature in °C described as ratio data because “0 °C exists”.",explanation:"0 °C is not an absence of heat, so ratios are meaningless: it is interval data. Time and cost have a true zero: ratio."},{trap:"A question asks which data type is MOST desirable for analysis.",explanation:"Continuous data — they carry the most information and need the smallest samples. Convert attribute data to continuous or to rates where possible.",tag:"most"},{trap:"Population and sample standard deviation formulas presented as interchangeable.",explanation:"The population formula divides by N; the sample formula by n − 1. Minitab’s StDev and Excel’s STDEV.S use n − 1.",tag:"sample-vs-population"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Classify nine DSO metrics",problem:i`Classify each metric as continuous, discrete or attribute, and give its scale: (a) ticket resolution time in minutes, (b) network latency in ms, (c) number of dropped calls per day, (d) SLA met yes/no, (e) incident category, (f) priority P1–P4, (g) CSAT 1–5, (h) server-room temperature in °C, (i) defects on an invoice.`,steps:["Ask “can it be meaningfully subdivided?” → (a) resolution time, (b) latency and (h) temperature are **continuous**.","Ask “is it a count of whole events?” → (c) dropped calls and (i) defects per invoice are **discrete counts**.","Ask “is it a label or judgement?” → (d) SLA met, (e) category, (f) priority and (g) CSAT are **attribute** data.","Scales: (d) and (e) are **nominal**; (f) and (g) are **ordinal**; (h) is **interval** (no true zero); (a), (b), (c) and (i) are **ratio** (true zero, ratios meaningful)."],answer:"Continuous: a, b, h · Discrete: c, i · Attribute: d, e, f, g. Nominal d, e · Ordinal f, g · Interval h · Ratio a, b, c, i.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“Priority and CSAT are numbers, so they are continuous; mean priority = 2.4.”",whyWrong:"They are codes for ordered categories. The gap between P1 and P2 is not equal to the gap between P3 and P4, so the mean of the codes has no operational meaning; use the median or proportions."}},{title:"Statistic or parameter? Average handle time",problem:i`A team leader samples **5** calls from one agent’s month of calls and records handle times of **5, 7, 4, 6 and 8** minutes. Calculate the sample mean and sample standard deviation. Then suppose instead these five values were the *entire* population of interest (for example, the five agents’ monthly averages in a tiny team): what is σ?`,steps:["Mean: Σx = 5 + 7 + 4 + 6 + 8 = 30; x̄ = 30 ÷ 5 = **6 min** (a statistic, because it comes from a sample).","Deviations from 6: −1, 1, −2, 0, 2. Squares: 1, 1, 4, 0, 4. Sum of squares = **10**.","Sample variance s² = 10 ÷ (n − 1) = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5; s = √2.5 = **1.58 min**.","Population version: σ² = 10 ÷ N = 10 ÷ 5 = 2; σ = √2 = **1.41 min**.","Interpretation: the sample s is larger because dividing by n − 1 corrects for measuring deviations from x̄ rather than the unknown μ.","**Exam-tools tip:** on the virtual calculator, type each deviation, square it (x² or × itself) and press **M+**; after the fifth, **MR** shows 10. Then ÷ 4 = 2.5, then √ = 1.5811. Write x̄ and Σ(x − x̄)² on the notepad before dividing so you can switch between n − 1 and N without re-keying."],answer:"x̄ = 6 min, s = 1.58 min (sample); σ = 1.41 min if the five values were the whole population.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"s = √(10 ÷ 5) = 1.41 min for the five sampled calls",whyWrong:"That is the population formula. The five calls are a sample from the agent’s month, so the divisor must be n − 1 = 4, giving 1.58 min."}},{title:"How big a sample for a sharper estimate?",problem:i`Latency readings on a core link have a standard deviation of about **12 ms**. Find the standard error of the mean for samples of **9**, **36** and **144** readings, and state how n must change to halve the standard error.`,steps:["SE = s ÷ √n.","n = 9: √9 = 3 → SE = 12 ÷ 3 = **4 ms**.","n = 36: √36 = 6 → SE = 12 ÷ 6 = **2 ms**.","n = 144: √144 = 12 → SE = 12 ÷ 12 = **1 ms**.","Each halving of SE required **four times** the sample (9 → 36 → 144).","**Exam-tools tip:** compute √n first with the √ key and store it (**M+**), then key 12 ÷ **MR** =. Checking with a perfect square (9, 36, 144) is a quick sanity test of the method on the notepad."],answer:"SE = 4, 2 and 1 ms; quadruple n to halve the standard error.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"SE = 12 ÷ 36 = 0.33 ms for n = 36",whyWrong:"The divisor is √n, not n. Dividing by n understates the uncertainty six-fold and would make a small sample look far more precise than it is."}},{title:"Turning attribute results into more useful data",problem:i`A month’s report states: **18 of 240** sampled P2 tickets breached the 4-hour SLA, and the network logged **54 dropped calls** across **1,800** calls on one cell. A factory example in the book records **200 defects** in an **8-hour** shift. Express each as a rate and say what extra data would be better still.`,steps:["SLA breach proportion = 18 ÷ 240 = 0.075 = **7.5%** (attribute → proportion).","Dropped-call rate = 54 ÷ 1,800 × 1,000 = **30 per 1,000 calls** — comparable across days with different traffic.","Book example: 200 ÷ 8 = **25 defects per hour**.","Better still: record the **resolution time in minutes** for all 240 tickets (continuous, ratio). It shows how close the 222 compliant tickets came to the limit and supports means, standard deviations and capability analysis.","**Exam-tools tip:** for “per 1,000” rates key 54 ÷ 1800 × 1000 = in one chain; for percentages × 100. Write the unit (“per 1,000 calls”, “%”) on the notepad next to each answer — exam distractors often differ only by the scale factor."],answer:"7.5% breached; 30 dropped calls per 1,000 calls; 25 defects per hour. Recording the continuous resolution time is the most informative option.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“54 dropped calls is worse than yesterday’s 40.”",whyWrong:"Raw counts ignore the size of the opportunity. If yesterday carried 1,000 calls, 40 is 40 per 1,000 — a higher rate than today’s 30 per 1,000."}},{title:"Population, frame or sample? A vendor SLA report",problem:i`A vendor states: “Mean resolution time for September was 3.4 h (n = 240).” The DSO team knows 2,400 tickets were closed in September and that the ITSM export omits tickets logged by e-mail. Identify the population, the frame, the sample and whether 3.4 h is a parameter or a statistic.`,steps:["Population: all **2,400** tickets closed in September (N = 2,400).","Sampling frame: the **ITSM export** — it excludes e-mail-logged tickets, so it is smaller than the population.","Sample: the **240** tickets used in the calculation (n = 240).","3.4 h is a **statistic** (x̄), an estimate of μ — and only for the tickets the frame could reach.","Appropriate response: ask how the 240 tickets were selected and whether e-mail-logged tickets behave differently, phrased as a request for clarification and supporting data."],answer:"Population = 2,400 September tickets; frame = ITSM export (incomplete); sample = 240 tickets; 3.4 h is a statistic.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“n = 240 is large, so 3.4 h is the true September mean μ.”",whyWrong:"Sample size improves precision but cannot remove frame bias; and any value computed from a sample remains a statistic."}}],lab:"statsExplorer",check:[{id:"chk-2.2.1-1",topic:"2.2.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which of the following is **continuous** data?",options:["Number of tickets reopened this week","Incident priority (P1–P4)","Time to restore service, in minutes","Whether the change was approved (Yes/No)"],answer:2,explanation:"Time can be measured on a scale and meaningfully subdivided (42.5 min), so it is continuous (ratio) data.",whyWrong:["A count of whole tickets is discrete data.","Priority is an ordered category — ordinal attribute data.","","Yes/No is binary attribute (nominal) data."]},{id:"chk-2.2.1-2",topic:"2.2.1",category:"definition",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Customer-satisfaction scores are collected on a 1–5 scale (1 = very dissatisfied, 5 = very satisfied). What is the measurement scale?",options:["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],answer:1,explanation:"The categories have a natural order, but the gaps between them are not guaranteed to be equal — ordinal data.",whyWrong:["Nominal categories have no order; satisfaction ratings clearly do.","","Interval requires equal, meaningful gaps between values, which a rating scale does not guarantee.","Ratio requires a true zero and meaningful ratios; a “4” is not twice a “2”."]},{id:"chk-2.2.1-3",topic:"2.2.1",category:"wording",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"A Green Belt randomly samples 50 calls and finds an average handle time of 6.2 minutes. Which statement is correct?",options:["6.2 minutes is the population parameter μ.","6.2 minutes is a statistic, x̄, that estimates μ.","6.2 minutes is the population standard deviation σ.","6.2 minutes is a parameter because the sample is larger than 30."],answer:1,explanation:"Any value computed from a sample is a statistic. The sample mean x̄ is used to estimate the unknown population mean μ.",whyWrong:["μ describes the whole population of calls, which was not measured.","","It is a mean, not a standard deviation, and it comes from a sample.","Sample size does not turn a statistic into a parameter."],traps:["sample-vs-population"]},{id:"chk-2.2.1-4",topic:"2.2.1",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Latency readings have a standard deviation of **12 ms**. What is the standard error of the mean for a sample of **36** readings?",options:["0.33 ms","2 ms","4 ms","12 ms"],answer:1,explanation:"SE = s ÷ √n = 12 ÷ √36 = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 ms.",whyWrong:["12 ÷ 36 divides by n instead of √n.","","4 ms is the standard error for n = 9, not 36.","12 ms is the standard deviation of individual readings, not of the mean."],traps:["standard-error"]},{id:"chk-2.2.1-5",topic:"2.2.1",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Five sampled handle times are **5, 7, 4, 6 and 8** minutes. What is the **sample** standard deviation?",options:["1.41 min","1.58 min","2.50 min","0.71 min"],answer:1,explanation:"x̄ = 6; squared deviations 1, 1, 4, 0, 4 sum to 10; s² = 10 ÷ (5 − 1) = 2.5; s = √2.5 ≈ 1.58 min.",whyWrong:["1.41 = √(10 ÷ 5) uses the population divisor N instead of n − 1.","","2.50 is the variance s², not the standard deviation.","0.71 is the standard error of the mean, s ÷ √n = 1.58 ÷ √5 — not the spread of individual calls."],traps:["sample-vs-population"]},{id:"chk-2.2.1-6",topic:"2.2.1",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A project currently records only “SLA met: Yes/No” for each incident. Which change would **most** improve the data for Measure-phase analysis?",options:["Record the actual resolution time of each incident in minutes","Increase the sample of Yes/No results from 100 to 120 incidents","Report the monthly percentage of “Yes” to two decimal places","Replace Yes/No with a colour code (green/amber/red)"],answer:0,explanation:"Converting attribute data to continuous data captures how far each incident was from the target, needs smaller samples and supports more powerful tools.",whyWrong:["","A slightly bigger attribute sample still discards the distance from the target.","Extra decimal places add apparent precision but no new information.","A three-level colour code is still attribute (ordinal) data."],traps:["most"]}],takeaways:["Statistics is the common language of Six Sigma: it summarises data, supports inference, underpins capability and lets everyone describe a process the same way.","Classify before you analyse: continuous (measured, subdivisible), discrete (whole counts) or attribute (categories, pass/fail).","Scales add operations step by step: nominal (=), ordinal (<), interval (−), ratio (÷, true zero).","Population → sampling frame → sample. A poor frame biases every result, whatever the sample size.","Parameters describe populations (μ, σ, N); statistics describe samples (x̄, s, n) and estimate parameters.","Precision grows with √n: SE = s ÷ √n, so quadruple n to halve the standard error.","Descriptive statistics summarise the data in hand; inferential statistics draw conclusions beyond it with a stated risk.","Plan collection (what, how, where, how many, who, when, stratification factors) and record continuous data instead of pass/fail wherever possible."],flashcards:[{front:"Continuous data",back:"Measured on a subdivisible scale — time, cost, latency. Most information per point."},{front:"Discrete data",back:"Whole-number counts — defects, dropped calls. No “half” values."},{front:"Attribute data",back:"Categories or pass/fail judgements; must be counted to be analysed."},{front:"Nominal vs ordinal",back:"Nominal = labels with no order (category); ordinal = ordered labels with unequal gaps (priority, CSAT)."},{front:"Interval vs ratio",back:"Both have equal gaps; ratio also has a true zero, so ratios are meaningful (time yes, °C no)."},{front:"Parameter vs statistic",back:"Parameter describes the population (μ, σ, N); statistic describes a sample (x̄, s, n)."},{front:"Sampling frame",back:"The actual list a sample is drawn from; gaps in it bias the results."},{front:"Symbol x̄",back:"Sample mean (x-bar). Population mean is μ."},{front:"Sample SD divisor",back:"n − 1 (population SD divides by N)."},{front:"Standard error of the mean",back:"SE = s ÷ √n; quadruple n to halve it."},{front:"Descriptive vs inferential",back:"Descriptive summarises the data you have; inferential concludes about the population with a stated risk."},{front:"Why convert attribute to continuous?",back:"More information, smaller samples, stronger tools — e.g. resolution minutes instead of “SLA met”."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["Data collection plan elements (what/how/where/how many/who/when) are written from standard Lean Six Sigma practice; the supplied book supports the stratification sources (p. 159) and the Measure checklist questions on data collection points and data sources (p. 231), but has no dedicated data-collection-plan page in this chapter.","Descriptive vs inferential statistics is explained from general statistical knowledge; inference is taught in the book’s Analyze chapter (pp. 267–280).","The note that many texts and Minitab use “attribute” for both pass/fail and count data reflects common usage; the book itself separates binary attribute data from discrete count data (p. 142).","All telecom, ticket and latency figures are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Measure topic 2.2.1 “Basic Statistics” within 2.2 Six Sigma Statistics."},{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 9",pdfPage:9,note:"Exam weighting: Measure 32% of questions."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 139–140",pdfPage:42,note:"Purpose of basic statistics; statistics as the language of Six Sigma; statistical notation cheat sheet."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 141",pdfPage:44,note:"Population, frame and sample; parameters (μ, σ, P, σ², N) vs statistics (x̄, s, p, s², n); purpose of sampling."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 142–143",pdfPage:45,note:"Types of data: attribute (binary), discrete (count) and continuous; examples of discrete and continuous variables."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 144–146",pdfPage:47,note:"Nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio scales with examples (IQ interval example, ratio with absolute zero)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 146",pdfPage:49,note:"Converting attribute data to continuous data or rates (scratch length, defects per hour)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 147",pdfPage:50,note:"Descriptive statistics: measures of location and of variation."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 148",pdfPage:51,note:"Minitab descriptive output: N = 200, StDev 0.0101, SE Mean 0.000712."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 159",pdfPage:62,note:"Data sources and stratification factors: time, location, operator, procedure."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 231",pdfPage:34,note:"Measure checklist: where are the data collection points, where did the data come from, centring vs spread."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 278–279",pdfPage:81,note:"Standard error of the mean and its behaviour as sample size grows (flattening near n ≈ 30) — Analyze context."}]},E=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:P},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),o=String.raw,x={id:"2.2.2",module:6,title:"Descriptive Statistics",subtitle:"Centre, spread and shape: mean, median, standard deviation and more",minutes:18,objectives:["Calculate and interpret the mean, median and mode, and choose between mean and median when data are skewed or contain outliers.","Calculate the range, variance and standard deviation by hand, using N for a population and n − 1 for a sample, and explain why n − 1 is used.","Find quartiles with the Minitab method, the interquartile range, the five-number summary and the 1.5 × IQR outlier fences.","Interpret skewness and kurtosis values as reported by Minitab and Excel.","Use the coefficient of variation to compare relative variation, and combine independent sources of variation through variances.","Read a Minitab Graphical Summary and explain each number in plain words."],overview:o`Descriptive statistics turn a column of numbers into a short, honest description of a process: **where it is centred**, **how much it varies** and **what shape its variation takes**. They are the backbone of the Measure-phase baseline and the first thing any reviewer will ask for.

This lesson builds each measure by hand on a small dataset — six incident-ticket resolution times — so that you know exactly what Minitab is doing when it prints “Mean 37.0, StDev 6.0, Q1 32.5”. You will then see how a single extreme ticket changes some measures dramatically and leaves others almost untouched, how to spot an outlier formally, how to read skewness and kurtosis, and how to compare variation between processes measured on very different scales. Every calculation includes the keystrokes for the exam’s virtual calculator.`,sections:[{id:"location",heading:"Measures of location: mean, median and mode",body:o`**Mean** — the arithmetic average and the balance point of the data:

$$\bar{x}=\frac{\sum x_i}{n}\qquad \mu=\frac{\sum x_i}{N}$$

The calculation is identical for a sample and a population; only the symbol changes. The mean uses every value, which makes it efficient for symmetric data and **sensitive to extreme values**.

**Median** — the middle value of the ordered data, the 50th percentile. With an odd $n$ it is the single middle value; with an even $n$ it is the average of the two middle values. It is the physical centre of the data and is **resistant to outliers** — the book’s example is average salary, where a handful of billionaires drag the mean far above what a typical worker earns.

**Mode** — the most frequent value. A dataset may have one mode, several (bimodal or multimodal), or none when every value is unique. The mode is the only measure of location that works for nominal data (“the most common incident category is Billing”). Two clear peaks in a histogram often signal two mixed populations — two shifts or two resolver groups behaving differently.

Our running dataset is six ticket resolution times, in minutes: **34, 40, 28, 46, 37, 37**.

- Mean = 222 ÷ 6 = **37 min**.
- Ordered: 28, 34, 37, 37, 40, 46. Median = (37 + 37) ÷ 2 = **37 min**.
- Mode = **37 min** (appears twice).

Mean = median = mode is the signature of a symmetric distribution. Minitab can also report a **trimmed mean** (the mean after removing the smallest and largest 5% of values), a compromise that is less affected by extreme scores.`},{id:"spread",heading:"Measures of spread: range, variance and standard deviation",body:o`**[[range|Range]]** = maximum − minimum. For the tickets, 46 − 28 = **18 min**. It is quick and fully efficient for tiny samples (it is the basis of the R chart for subgroups of 2–10), but it uses only two values and is destroyed by a single outlier.

**[[variance|Variance]]** — the average squared deviation from the mean:

$$s^2=\frac{\sum (x_i-\bar{x})^2}{n-1}\qquad \sigma^2=\frac{\sum (x_i-\mu)^2}{N}$$

Its units are **squared** (minutes²), which makes it awkward to interpret but essential in calculations: **variances of independent sources add; standard deviations do not.**

**[[standard-deviation|Standard deviation]]** — the square root of the variance, back in the original units:

$$s=\sqrt{\frac{\sum (x_i-\bar{x})^2}{n-1}}\qquad \sigma=\sqrt{\frac{\sum (x_i-\mu)^2}{N}}$$

It is roughly the typical distance of a value from the mean and is the unit of measure for “how many sigmas” throughout Six Sigma.

For the six tickets the deviations from 37 are −3, 3, −9, 9, 0, 0. Their squares sum to **180**. As a **sample**: $s^2 = 180 \div 5 = 36$ and $s = 6$ min. If the six tickets were the entire population: $\sigma^2 = 180 \div 6 = 30$ and $\sigma \approx 5.48$ min.

The book adds a practical link: a sample collected over a short period tends to be free of the drifts and shifts that accumulate over time, so its standard deviation behaves like **short-term** variation, while data spanning a long period reflect **long-term** variation — the idea behind Cp/Cpk versus Pp/Ppk in 2.4.`},{id:"why-n-1",heading:"Why divide by n − 1? Degrees of freedom",advanced:!0,body:o`If we knew $\mu$, we would measure every deviation from it and divide by $n$. We do not, so we measure deviations from $\bar{x}$ — and $\bar{x}$ is, by construction, the point that makes the sum of squared deviations **as small as possible** for that sample. Deviations from $\bar{x}$ are therefore systematically a little smaller than deviations from the true $\mu$, and dividing by $n$ would **underestimate** the population variance.

Another way to see it: the deviations from $\bar{x}$ always sum to zero (−3 + 3 − 9 + 9 + 0 + 0 = 0). Once five of the six are known, the sixth is fixed. Only $n - 1$ deviations are free to vary — the sample has $n - 1$ **[[degrees-of-freedom|degrees of freedom]]** for estimating spread. Dividing by $n-1$ makes $s^2$ an unbiased estimator of $\sigma^2$.

How much does it matter? The sample-to-population ratio $s/\sigma$ equals $\sqrt{n/(n-1)}$. For $n = 6$ it is about 1.095 (6.00 vs 5.48 min — a 9.5% difference). For $n = 30$ the gap is under 2%, and for large samples it is negligible. Small samples are exactly where the exam tests it.

**Which button?** Minitab “StDev”, Excel **STDEV.S**, and calculators’ $s_x$ or $\sigma_{n-1}$ key use $n-1$. Excel **STDEV.P** and the $\sigma_x$ or $\sigma_n$ key use $N$. In Six Sigma work, assume sample data unless the stem clearly says you measured the entire population.`},{id:"quartiles",heading:"Quartiles, IQR, the five-number summary and outliers",body:o`**[[quartile|Quartiles]]** split ordered data into four equal parts: Q1 (25th percentile), Q2 (the median) and Q3 (75th percentile). Software uses slightly different rules for positions between data points. **Minitab** (and Excel’s QUARTILE.EXC) locates

$$\text{position of } Q_1 = 0.25\,(n+1)\qquad \text{position of } Q_3 = 0.75\,(n+1)$$

and interpolates linearly between neighbours. For the six tickets (ordered 28, 34, 37, 37, 40, 46):

- Q1 position = 0.25 × 7 = 1.75 → 28 + 0.75 × (34 − 28) = **32.5**.
- Q3 position = 0.75 × 7 = 5.25 → 40 + 0.25 × (46 − 40) = **41.5**.
- **[[iqr|IQR]]** = Q3 − Q1 = **9 min** — the spread of the middle 50% of the data.

Excel’s older QUARTILE / QUARTILE.INC uses a different position rule and can return slightly different values, so match the method to the software named in the question.

The **five-number summary** — minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum — is exactly what a [[box-plot|box plot]] draws: 28, 32.5, 37, 41.5, 46.

**Outliers by the 1.5 × IQR rule.** The book’s box-plot anatomy flags a value as an [[outlier|outlier]] when it lies more than 1.5 × IQR beyond the box:

$$\text{lower fence}=Q_1-1.5\,\text{IQR}\qquad \text{upper fence}=Q_3+1.5\,\text{IQR}$$

For the tickets: 32.5 − 13.5 = **19** and 41.5 + 13.5 = **55**. Every value lies inside.

Now a seventh ticket arrives — one that waited **96 min** for a field engineer. Recalculate with $n = 7$: Q1 = 34, Q3 = 46, IQR = 12, upper fence = 46 + 18 = **64**. The value 96 is beyond it: an outlier. Meanwhile the mean jumps from 37 to **45.4**, $s$ from 6 to **23.0**, but the median stays at **37** and the IQR moves only from 9 to 12. The animation above shows this step by step.

An outlier is a **signal to investigate, not a licence to delete**. It may be a data-entry error, a special cause (a vendor outage), or the most important ticket in the dataset. Record it, find its cause, and only exclude it from a baseline when there is a documented reason.`},{id:"shape",heading:"Shape: skewness, kurtosis and mean versus median",body:o`**[[skewness|Skewness]]** measures asymmetry. Minitab and Excel (SKEW) report the adjusted Fisher–Pearson coefficient:

- **≈ 0** — symmetric; mean ≈ median.
- **Positive** — right (positive) skew: a long tail of high values; **mean > median**. Typical of times, costs and waiting — anything bounded at zero with no upper limit.
- **Negative** — left skew: a long tail of low values; mean < median.

The skew is named after the direction of the **tail**, not the peak. A common rule of thumb treats |skewness| above about 1 as strongly skewed — but in small samples the coefficient is unstable, so always look at the graph.

**[[kurtosis|Kurtosis]]** describes the tails and peak relative to a normal distribution. Minitab and Excel (KURT) report **excess kurtosis**, for which a normal distribution scores **0**:

- **Positive** (leptokurtic) — peaked with heavy, long tails; extreme values more likely than a normal curve predicts.
- **Negative** (platykurtic) — flatter with short tails.

Do not confuse this with the “kurtosis = 3 for a normal” convention used in some textbooks; Minitab’s output has already subtracted the 3.

**Mean vs median under skew.** Mean time to restore (MTTR) for 11 incidents, in hours: 1.5, 1.8, 2.0, 2.2, 2.4, 2.5, 2.8, 3.1, 3.6, 4.5 and 12.6. The **mean is 3.55 h** but the **median is 2.5 h**; skewness is **+2.89** and kurtosis **+8.88** — strongly right-skewed and heavy-tailed, driven by one 12.6-hour outage. Report “MTTR 3.55 h” and most customers will think service is worse than they typically experience; report only the median and you hide the outage that hurt most. Report **both**, with the outlier explained. The same logic applies to salaries: seven monthly salaries of 850, 900, 950, 1,000, 1,100, 1,200 and 6,500 OMR have a mean of 1,785.71 OMR but a median of 1,000 OMR.

| Shape | Skewness | Mean vs median | Better centre |
|---|---|---|---|
| Symmetric | ≈ 0 | Equal | Mean (more efficient) |
| Right-skewed (times, costs) | > 0 | Mean > median | Median (plus mean for totals) |
| Left-skewed | < 0 | Mean < median | Median |

The book lists skewness, kurtosis, multiple modes and granularity as the four classic signatures of non-normal data; it develops them as clues to causes in Analyze (3.1).`},{id:"cv-variances",heading:"Coefficient of variation, and why variances add",advanced:!0,body:o`**Coefficient of variation (CV)** expresses spread relative to the mean:

$$CV=\frac{s}{\bar{x}}\times 100\%$$

It lets you compare variation between processes on different scales. Ticket resolution times (x̄ = 37 min, s = 6 min) have **CV = 16.2%**. Six fibre installation times — 170, 195, 160, 205, 182 and 168 min — have x̄ = 180 min and s = 17.3 min, so **CV = 9.6%**. The installations have the larger *absolute* spread but the smaller *relative* spread. CV is meaningful only for **ratio** data with a positive mean — never for temperatures in °C or for data that can be negative.

**Adding variation.** When independent sources of variation combine — a diagnosis step with σ = 3 min followed by a fix step with σ = 4 min — the variances add:

$$\sigma_{total}=\sqrt{\sigma_1^2+\sigma_2^2}=\sqrt{9+16}=5\ \text{min}$$

not 3 + 4 = 7. The book states it plainly: standard deviations cannot be added; variances can. This is the arithmetic behind Gage R&R (2.3), where measurement variance and part variance are added to give total variance.`},{id:"graphical-summary",heading:"Reading a Minitab Graphical Summary in words",body:o`Minitab’s **Graphical Summary** (Stat › Basic Statistics › Graphical Summary) puts almost everything in this lesson on one page. The training book shows one for 500 observations. Read it from top to bottom like this:

1. **Anderson-Darling normality test** — A-squared 0.18, P-value 0.921. A p-value above 0.05 means there is no evidence against normality; the data are “normal enough” for tools that assume it ([[anderson-darling|Anderson-Darling]] is taught in 2.2.3). The book warns that with very large samples the test becomes very sensitive.
2. **Location and spread** — Mean 50.031, StDev 4.951, Variance 24.511 (which is 4.951²). StDev uses $n-1$.
3. **Shape** — Skewness −0.06 and Kurtosis −0.18: both close to 0, so the data are nearly symmetric with normal-looking tails.
4. **N** — 500 observations.
5. **Five-number summary** — Minimum 35.727, 1st Quartile 46.800, Median 50.006, 3rd Quartile 53.218, Maximum 62.823. Mean and median almost coincide — consistent with the low skewness.
6. **95% confidence intervals** for the mean (49.596 to 50.466), the median (49.663 to 50.500) and the standard deviation (4.662 to 5.278). These are *inferential*: the plausible range for the population values (3.2).
7. **Graphs** — a histogram with a fitted normal curve, a horizontal box plot of the same data, and interval plots of the mean and median confidence intervals.

In words: *“500 readings centred at about 50 with a standard deviation of about 5; symmetric, normal-looking tails; no evidence of non-normality; the population mean is very likely between 49.6 and 50.5.”* Learning to say that sentence from the output is exactly the skill exam questions test.

Separately, Minitab’s **Display Descriptive Statistics** table prints **SE Mean** next to StDev — do not confuse them: StDev describes individual values, SE Mean describes the precision of the average.`}],whyItMatters:o`Every baseline in a project charter, every capability study and every control-chart limit begins with a mean and a standard deviation. Choose the wrong formula (n instead of n − 1), the wrong centre (mean on badly skewed data) or ignore an outlier, and the baseline is wrong before the project starts — which makes the eventual improvement either invisible or exaggerated.

In service operations the stakes are concrete. Resolution and restoration times are almost always right-skewed. A vendor report quoting only the mean MTTR, or only the median, tells half the story; asking for the median, the mean, the IQR and the treatment of outliers is a fair, evidence-based request. On the exam, expect hand calculations of s with small datasets, quartile and fence questions, and “which measure is MOST appropriate” wording.`,businessExample:{title:"Describing incident restoration times before an improvement project",body:o`A DSO team receives eleven P1 restoration times for a quarter (hours): 1.5, 1.8, 2.0, 2.2, 2.4, 2.5, 2.8, 3.1, 3.6, 4.5, 12.6.

- **Centre:** mean 3.55 h; median 2.5 h. The gap points to right skew.
- **Spread:** s = 3.12 h; IQR = 3.6 − 2.0 = 1.6 h; CV = 88%.
- **Shape:** skewness +2.89, kurtosis +8.88 — a long right tail.
- **Outliers:** fences at −0.4 h and 6.0 h; the 12.6 h restoration lies beyond the upper fence.
- **Without the outlier** (for understanding only): mean 2.64 h, median 2.45 h, s 0.90 h — one event almost quadrupled the standard deviation.

The baseline is reported with all eleven values, both centres and the outlier annotated. In the vendor-facing summary the finding is framed as a request: *based on the quarterly data, one restoration of approximately 12.6 hours lies well outside the typical range; the team is asked to clarify the cause and provide the supporting incident timeline, and to confirm whether the reported MTTR includes this event.*`},visual:"outlierDotPlot",visualCaption:"Animated dot plot of six ticket resolution times (34, 40, 28, 46, 37, 37 min): mean = median = 37, ±1 s band with s = 6, Minitab quartiles 32.5 and 41.5 with fences at 19 and 55. A 96-minute ticket then arrives: the mean jumps to 45.4 and s to 23.0, the median stays at 37, and the new upper fence of 64 flags 96 as an outlier.",definitions:[{term:"Mean (x̄, μ)",definition:"Sum of the values divided by their number; the balance point of the data. Sensitive to extreme values."},{term:"Median",definition:"Middle value of the ordered data (50th percentile); average of the two middle values when n is even. Resistant to outliers."},{term:"Mode",definition:"Most frequently occurring value; data may have none, one or several modes."},{term:"Trimmed mean",definition:"Mean after removing a set percentage (Minitab: 5%) of the smallest and largest values; a compromise between mean and median."},{term:"Range",definition:"Maximum minus minimum; uses only two values."},{term:"Variance (s², σ²)",definition:"Average squared deviation from the mean (divide by n − 1 for a sample, N for a population); units are squared."},{term:"Standard deviation (s, σ)",definition:"Square root of the variance; typical distance of values from the mean, in the original units."},{term:"Degrees of freedom",definition:"Number of values free to vary when estimating a statistic; n − 1 for a sample standard deviation."},{term:"Quartiles (Q1, Q3)",definition:"25th and 75th percentiles; Minitab positions them at 0.25(n + 1) and 0.75(n + 1) with interpolation."},{term:"Interquartile range (IQR)",definition:"Q3 − Q1; the spread of the middle 50% of the data. Resistant to outliers."},{term:"Outlier (1.5 × IQR rule)",definition:"A value below Q1 − 1.5 IQR or above Q3 + 1.5 IQR; a signal to investigate."},{term:"Skewness",definition:"Measure of asymmetry: ≈ 0 symmetric, positive = long right tail, negative = long left tail."},{term:"Kurtosis (excess)",definition:"Tail weight and peakedness relative to a normal curve, which scores 0 in Minitab and Excel; positive = heavy tails, negative = light tails."},{term:"Coefficient of variation (CV)",definition:"s ÷ x̄ × 100%; relative spread for comparing processes on different scales (ratio data only)."}],comparisons:[{title:"Mean vs median vs mode",headers:["","Mean","Median","Mode"],rows:[["Definition","Sum ÷ count","Middle ordered value","Most frequent value"],["Uses every value?","Yes","Only their order","Only frequencies"],["Effect of one outlier","Large (37 → 45.4)","Little or none (37 → 37)","None"],["Best for","Symmetric continuous data","Skewed data: times, costs, salaries","Nominal data, finding peaks"],["Data scale needed","Interval / ratio","Ordinal and above","Any, including nominal"]]},{title:"Population vs sample formulas",headers:["","Population","Sample"],rows:[["Mean","μ = Σx ÷ N","x̄ = Σx ÷ n"],["Variance","σ² = Σ(x − μ)² ÷ N","s² = Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ (n − 1)"],["Standard deviation","σ = √σ²","s = √s²"],["Excel","STDEV.P / VAR.P","STDEV.S / VAR.S"],["Six tickets (SS = 180)","σ = √30 ≈ 5.48","s = √36 = 6.00"]]},{title:"Measures of spread compared",headers:["","Range","IQR","Standard deviation","CV"],rows:[["Formula","Max − min","Q3 − Q1","√[Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ (n − 1)]","s ÷ x̄ × 100%"],["Values used","Two","Middle 50%","All","All"],["Resistant to outliers?","No","Yes","No","No"],["Six tickets","18","9","6.0","16.2%"],["With 96-min ticket","68","12","23.0","50.5%"],["Best use","Tiny subgroups (R chart)","Skewed data, box plots","Symmetric data, capability","Comparing different scales"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"The mean is always the best summary of “typical” performance.",reality:"For skewed data such as MTTR or salaries the median describes the typical case better; report both, and explain the gap."},{myth:"Sample and population standard deviations are the same thing.",reality:"They divide by different amounts (n − 1 vs N). For the six tickets: s = 6.00, σ = 5.48 min."},{myth:"Standard deviations of independent steps can be added to get the total.",reality:"Variances add: √(3² + 4²) = 5, not 3 + 4 = 7."},{myth:"An outlier should be deleted so the statistics look normal.",reality:"An outlier is a signal to investigate. Delete only with a documented cause such as a proven data-entry error."},{myth:"A kurtosis value of 3 in Minitab means the data are normal.",reality:"Minitab and Excel report excess kurtosis, so a normal distribution scores 0; +3 indicates heavy tails."},{myth:"Right skew means most values are on the right.",reality:"Skew is named after the long tail. In right-skewed data most values are low and a few large values stretch the tail to the right."}],examTraps:[{trap:"Six sample values and options for both √(SS ÷ n) and √(SS ÷ (n − 1)).",explanation:"Sample data use n − 1 (s = 6.00 for the tickets); the N version (5.48) is the population formula — a deliberate distractor.",tag:"sample-vs-population"},{trap:"Options include the variance where the standard deviation is asked (36 vs 6).",explanation:"Variance is in squared units; take the square root for the standard deviation."},{trap:"“Which measure of central tendency is LEAST affected by an extreme value?”",explanation:"The median (and the mode). The mean moves from 37 to 45.4 when a 96-minute ticket is added; the median stays at 37.",tag:"least"},{trap:"Total variation of two independent steps with σ = 3 and σ = 4.",explanation:"Add variances, not standard deviations: √(9 + 16) = 5. The distractor 7 adds standard deviations."},{trap:"Minitab output shows “StDev 0.0101” and “SE Mean 0.000712”; the question asks for the spread of individual parts.",explanation:"StDev describes individuals; SE Mean = StDev ÷ √n describes the precision of the mean.",tag:"standard-error"},{trap:"Kurtosis reported as −0.18; an option says “far from normal because a normal distribution has kurtosis 3”.",explanation:"Minitab and Excel report excess kurtosis; a normal distribution scores 0, so −0.18 is close to normal.",tag:"normality-assumption"},{trap:"Quartile questions “by the Minitab method” with options from other position rules.",explanation:"Use positions 0.25(n + 1) and 0.75(n + 1) with interpolation. Other rules (Excel QUARTILE.INC) give different answers."},{trap:"“The data are positively skewed; which is TRUE?” with “mean < median” as a distractor.",explanation:"A long right tail pulls the mean above the median (MTTR: 3.55 h vs 2.5 h).",tag:"best"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Standard deviation by hand: six ticket resolution times",problem:o`Six sampled incident tickets took **34, 40, 28, 46, 37 and 37** minutes to resolve. Calculate the mean, median, mode, range, sample variance and sample standard deviation. Then give the population standard deviation for comparison.

| $x_i$ | $x_i-\bar{x}$ | $(x_i-\bar{x})^2$ |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 34 | −3 | 9 |
| 40 | 3 | 9 |
| 28 | −9 | 81 |
| 46 | 9 | 81 |
| 37 | 0 | 0 |
| 37 | 0 | 0 |
| **Σ = 222** | **0** | **180** |`,steps:["Mean: x̄ = 222 ÷ 6 = **37 min**. Ordered data 28, 34, 37, 37, 40, 46 → median = (37 + 37) ÷ 2 = **37**; mode = **37**; range = 46 − 28 = **18 min**.","Deviations from 37: −3, 3, −9, 9, 0, 0 (check: they sum to 0).","Squares: 9, 9, 81, 81, 0, 0 → sum of squares SS = **180**.","Sample variance s² = 180 ÷ (6 − 1) = **36 min²**; sample SD s = √36 = **6 min**.","Population version (only if these six were the whole population): σ² = 180 ÷ 6 = 30; σ = √30 ≈ **5.48 min**.","**Exam-tools tip:** on the virtual calculator press **MC**, then for each value key (value − 37) **x²** **M+** (e.g. 34 − 37 = −3, x² = 9, M+). After the sixth, **MR** = 180. Key 180 ÷ 5 = 36, then **√** = 6. Write SS = 180 on the notepad first so the N version (÷ 6) takes one extra keystroke rather than a full recalculation."],answer:"Mean 37, median 37, mode 37, range 18 min; s² = 36 min², s = 6 min (σ ≈ 5.48 min if a population).",wrongApproach:{calculation:"s = √(180 ÷ 6) = 5.48 min",whyWrong:"Divides by n instead of n − 1. Because the tickets are a sample, the deviations were measured from x̄, not μ, and n − 1 is needed to avoid underestimating the spread."}},{title:"Quartiles, five-number summary and outliers for MTTR",problem:o`Eleven P1 restoration times (hours), already ordered: **1.5, 1.8, 2.0, 2.2, 2.4, 2.5, 2.8, 3.1, 3.6, 4.5, 12.6**. Using the Minitab method, find Q1, the median, Q3, the IQR, the five-number summary and any outliers by the 1.5 × IQR rule.`,steps:["n = 11, so n + 1 = 12.","Q1 position = 0.25 × 12 = 3 → the 3rd value, **Q1 = 2.0 h**.","Median position = 0.5 × 12 = 6 → **median = 2.5 h**.","Q3 position = 0.75 × 12 = 9 → the 9th value, **Q3 = 3.6 h**. IQR = 3.6 − 2.0 = **1.6 h**.","Fences: lower = 2.0 − 1.5 × 1.6 = 2.0 − 2.4 = **−0.4 h**; upper = 3.6 + 2.4 = **6.0 h**.","12.6 h > 6.0 h → **outlier**. Nothing lies below −0.4 h (times cannot be negative).","Five-number summary: **1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.6, 12.6**.","**Exam-tools tip:** write the positions on the notepad first (3, 6, 9), then count along the ordered list — no calculator needed when positions are whole numbers. When a position is fractional, e.g. 1.75, key lower + 0.75 × (upper − lower) using the calculator’s brackets, or compute the gap first and store it with **M+**."],answer:"Q1 = 2.0, median = 2.5, Q3 = 3.6, IQR = 1.6 h; fences −0.4 and 6.0 h; 12.6 h is an outlier. Five-number summary 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.6, 12.6.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Outlier rule applied as mean ± 1.5 × IQR: 3.55 + 2.4 = 5.95, then “Q3 = 4.5” taken as the 10th value",whyWrong:"The fences are built from the quartiles, not the mean, and the Minitab Q3 position is 0.75 × (n + 1) = 9, i.e. 3.6 — not the 10th value."}},{title:"Mean vs median under skew",problem:o`For the same eleven restoration times, compute the mean and compare it with the median. Repeat without the 12.6 h value. Then compare the mean and median of seven monthly salaries: 850, 900, 950, 1,000, 1,100, 1,200 and 6,500 OMR. Which centre should each report lead with?`,steps:["MTTR: Σ = 39.0 h; mean = 39.0 ÷ 11 = **3.55 h**; median = **2.5 h**. Mean > median → right skew (skewness +2.89).","Without 12.6 h: mean = 26.4 ÷ 10 = **2.64 h**; median = (2.4 + 2.5) ÷ 2 = **2.45 h**. One value moved the mean by 0.91 h but the median by only 0.05 h.","Salaries: Σ = 12,500; mean = 12,500 ÷ 7 = **1,785.71 OMR**; median (4th value) = **1,000 OMR**. Six of seven people earn less than the mean.","Lead with the **median** for “typical” experience; keep the **mean** where totals matter (total downtime, payroll cost), and name the outlier.","**Exam-tools tip:** sum with the **M+** key as you read the list (fewer keying slips than one long chain), then **MR ÷ n**. For the median, write the ordered list on the notepad and circle the middle position (n + 1) ÷ 2."],answer:"MTTR mean 3.55 h vs median 2.5 h (2.64 vs 2.45 h without the outlier); salary mean 1,785.71 vs median 1,000 OMR. Lead with the median for skewed data.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“Typical restoration takes 3.55 h.”",whyWrong:"Only 3 of 11 incidents took longer than 3.55 h. The mean is pulled up by a single long outage; the median (2.5 h) describes the typical incident."}},{title:"Coefficient of variation: which process is relatively more variable?",problem:o`Ticket resolution times: x̄ = 37 min, s = 6 min. Fibre installation times (170, 195, 160, 205, 182, 168 min): calculate x̄ and s, then compare the two processes using CV.`,steps:["Fibre mean: Σ = 1,080; x̄ = 1,080 ÷ 6 = **180 min**.","Deviations: −10, 15, −20, 25, 2, −12; squares 100, 225, 400, 625, 4, 144; SS = **1,498**.","s² = 1,498 ÷ 5 = 299.6; s = √299.6 ≈ **17.31 min**.","CV(fibre) = 17.31 ÷ 180 × 100 = **9.6%**; CV(tickets) = 6 ÷ 37 × 100 = **16.2%**.","Fibre installations vary more in minutes, but tickets vary more **relative to their mean**.","**Exam-tools tip:** keep s in memory after the √ step (**M+** after clearing with **MC**), then key **MR ÷ 180 × 100 =** — re-typing a rounded 17.3 gives 9.61%, close enough, but carrying the full value avoids rounding drift in multi-step questions."],answer:"Fibre: x̄ = 180, s ≈ 17.31 min, CV ≈ 9.6%; tickets CV ≈ 16.2% — tickets have the higher relative variation.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“Fibre has s = 17.3 vs 6, so it is the more variable process.”",whyWrong:"Absolute standard deviations cannot be compared fairly across processes with very different means. CV puts both on a relative scale."}},{title:"Combining independent sources of variation",problem:o`An incident passes through diagnosis (σ = 3 min) and then fix (σ = 4 min). The two steps vary independently. What is the standard deviation of the total handling time?`,steps:["Convert to variances: 3² = 9 and 4² = 16.","Add variances: 9 + 16 = **25 min²**.","Take the root: σ_total = √25 = **5 min**.","**Exam-tools tip:** key 3 **x²** **M+**, 4 **x²** **M+**, **MR** **√** = 5. The same keystrokes combine repeatability and reproducibility in Gage R&R."],answer:"σ_total = 5 min.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"σ_total = 3 + 4 = 7 min",whyWrong:"Standard deviations do not add; variances of independent sources do. Adding standard deviations overstates the total variation."}},{title:"Effect of one outlier on every statistic",problem:o`Add a seventh ticket of **96 min** to the six tickets (34, 40, 28, 46, 37, 37). Recalculate the mean, median, s, Minitab quartiles, IQR and fences, and decide whether 96 is an outlier.`,steps:["Σ = 222 + 96 = 318; mean = 318 ÷ 7 = **45.43 min** (was 37).","Ordered: 28, 34, 37, 37, 40, 46, 96 → median (4th) = **37 min** (unchanged).","SS = 3,163.71; s² = 3,163.71 ÷ 6 = 527.29; s = **22.96 min** (was 6).","Positions 0.25 × 8 = 2 and 0.75 × 8 = 6 → **Q1 = 34**, **Q3 = 46**, IQR = **12**.","Upper fence = 46 + 1.5 × 12 = **64** → 96 is an **outlier**; lower fence 34 − 18 = 16.","**Exam-tools tip:** with a non-integer mean (45.43), avoid squaring rounded deviations by hand — use Σx² instead: SS = Σx² − (Σx)² ÷ n. Key each value **x² M+** to get Σx² = 17,610, then 17,610 − 318² ÷ 7 = 3,163.71, ÷ 6, √."],answer:"Mean 45.43, median 37, s 22.96, Q1 34, Q3 46, IQR 12, upper fence 64 → 96 is an outlier.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“96 is more than 2 s from the mean (45.43 + 2 × 22.96 = 91.35), so it is an outlier — and the mean of 45.4 is the best summary.”",whyWrong:"The outlier inflates s itself, making mean ± k·s rules unreliable; the IQR fences are resistant. And with an outlier present, the median (37) is the better summary of a typical ticket."}}],lab:"statsExplorer",check:[{id:"chk-2.2.2-1",topic:"2.2.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A sample of five daily backlog counts is **12, 15, 9, 14, 10**. What is the sample standard deviation?",options:["2.28","2.55","6.50","1.14"],answer:1,explanation:"x̄ = 60 ÷ 5 = 12. Squared deviations 0, 9, 9, 4, 4 sum to 26. s² = 26 ÷ 4 = 6.5; s = √6.5 ≈ 2.55.",whyWrong:["2.28 = √(26 ÷ 5) uses the population divisor N, not n − 1.","","6.50 is the variance s², not the standard deviation.","1.14 is the standard error s ÷ √n = 2.55 ÷ √5."],traps:["sample-vs-population"]},{id:"chk-2.2.2-2",topic:"2.2.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!0,stem:"Nine ordered call-transfer times (seconds) are **3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 12, 15, 30**. Using Minitab quartiles and the 1.5 × IQR rule, which statement is correct?",options:["IQR = 8 and 30 is an outlier (upper fence 25.5)","IQR = 27 and there are no outliers","IQR = 8 and there are no outliers (upper fence 37.5)","IQR = 9 and 30 is an outlier (upper fence 22.5)"],answer:0,explanation:"Positions 0.25 × 10 = 2.5 and 0.75 × 10 = 7.5 give Q1 = 5 + 0.5 × 1 = 5.5 and Q3 = 12 + 0.5 × 3 = 13.5. IQR = 8; upper fence = 13.5 + 12 = 25.5, so 30 is an outlier.",whyWrong:["","27 is the range (30 − 3), not the IQR.","37.5 adds 3 × IQR to Q3 — the multiplier is 1.5.","Q3 − Q1 is 8, not 9; the fence is 25.5."]},{id:"chk-2.2.2-3",topic:"2.2.2",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Monthly MTTR data are strongly right-skewed because of a few long outages. Which measure BEST describes the typical restoration time?",options:["Mean","Median","Range","Standard deviation"],answer:1,explanation:"The median is resistant to the long right tail; the mean is pulled upward by the few long outages.",whyWrong:["The mean is inflated by extreme values (e.g. 3.55 h vs a median of 2.5 h).","","Range measures spread, not centre.","Standard deviation measures spread, not centre."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-2.2.2-4",topic:"2.2.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Ticket resolution: x̄ = 37 min, s = 6 min. Fibre installation: x̄ = 180 min, s = 17.31 min. Which process has the higher **relative** variation?",options:["Fibre installation, CV ≈ 9.6%","Ticket resolution, CV ≈ 16.2%","Fibre installation, because 17.31 > 6","Neither — CV cannot compare different processes"],answer:1,explanation:"CV = s ÷ x̄ × 100: tickets 6 ÷ 37 = 16.2%; fibre 17.31 ÷ 180 = 9.6%. Tickets vary more relative to their mean.",whyWrong:["9.6% is the lower of the two CVs.","","Absolute standard deviations are not comparable across very different means.","Comparing processes on different scales is exactly what CV is for."]},{id:"chk-2.2.2-5",topic:"2.2.2",category:"chart",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"A Minitab Graphical Summary of restoration times shows **Skewness 2.89** and **Kurtosis 8.88**. What is the best interpretation?",options:["Symmetric data with light tails","Left-skewed data with a flat peak","Right-skewed data with heavy tails — extreme high values are more common than a normal curve predicts","Normal data, because a normal distribution has kurtosis near 9"],answer:2,explanation:"Positive skewness means a long right tail; Minitab reports excess kurtosis (normal = 0), so +8.88 indicates heavy tails.",whyWrong:["Both values are far from 0, so the data are neither symmetric nor light-tailed.","Negative skewness would indicate a left tail, and negative kurtosis a flat shape.","","Minitab’s excess kurtosis is 0 for a normal distribution."],traps:["normality-assumption"]},{id:"chk-2.2.2-6",topic:"2.2.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Why is the sample standard deviation calculated with **n − 1** rather than n?",options:["To make the answer larger so that specifications are easier to meet","Because deviations are measured from x̄, which minimises them, so dividing by n would underestimate σ","Because one data point is always an outlier and is removed","It is only a Minitab convention with no statistical reason"],answer:1,explanation:"Deviations from the sample mean are systematically smaller than from μ, and only n − 1 of them are free to vary (degrees of freedom). Dividing by n − 1 corrects the bias.",whyWrong:["A larger s makes capability look worse, not better, and that is not the reason.","","No data point is removed; all n values are used in the sum of squares.","It is a statistical correction for bias, used by all software and textbooks for sample data."],traps:["sample-vs-population"]}],takeaways:["Describe every dataset by centre, spread and shape — never centre alone.","Mean uses every value but is pulled by outliers; the median is resistant; the mode works even for categories.","Sample SD: s = √[Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ (n − 1)]; population σ divides by N. Six tickets: s = 6.00, σ = 5.48.","n − 1 corrects the bias from measuring deviations around x̄ — only n − 1 deviations are free to vary.","Minitab quartiles sit at 0.25(n + 1) and 0.75(n + 1); outliers lie beyond Q1 − 1.5 IQR or Q3 + 1.5 IQR.","Right skew (positive) → mean > median; Minitab kurtosis is excess kurtosis, so normal = 0.","CV = s ÷ x̄ compares relative variation across scales; variances add, standard deviations do not.","Read a Minitab Graphical Summary top-down: normality test, mean/StDev/variance, skewness/kurtosis, five-number summary, confidence intervals."],flashcards:[{front:"Sample standard deviation formula",back:"s = √[Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ (n − 1)]"},{front:"Population standard deviation formula",back:"σ = √[Σ(x − μ)² ÷ N]"},{front:"Why n − 1?",back:"Deviations from x̄ are smaller than from μ; n − 1 degrees of freedom corrects the underestimate."},{front:"Median with even n",back:"Average of the two middle ordered values."},{front:"Minitab quartile positions",back:"Q1 at 0.25(n + 1), Q3 at 0.75(n + 1), interpolating between values."},{front:"Outlier fences",back:"Q1 − 1.5 × IQR and Q3 + 1.5 × IQR."},{front:"Five-number summary",back:"Minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum — what a box plot draws."},{front:"Right (positive) skew",back:"Long tail to the right; mean > median; typical of times and costs."},{front:"Minitab kurtosis of a normal distribution",back:"0 (excess kurtosis). Positive = heavy tails; negative = light tails."},{front:"Coefficient of variation",back:"CV = s ÷ x̄ × 100%; for ratio data with a positive mean."},{front:"Combining independent variation",back:"Add variances: σ_total = √(σ₁² + σ₂²); √(3² + 4²) = 5."},{front:"StDev vs SE Mean in Minitab",back:"StDev = spread of individuals; SE Mean = StDev ÷ √n = precision of the average."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The Minitab quartile position rule 0.25(n + 1) / 0.75(n + 1), the explanation of n − 1 via bias and degrees of freedom, and the coefficient of variation are standard statistical practice; they are not spelled out in the supplied chapter, which shows Minitab output and names the measures (pp. 147–151).","Skewness and kurtosis conventions (adjusted Fisher–Pearson skewness; excess kurtosis with normal = 0) follow Minitab and Excel (SKEW, KURT). The book’s sign interpretation is on pp. 259–260 (Analyze context).","The |skewness| > 1 “strongly skewed” threshold is a rule of thumb, not a formal test.","Exam-tools tips refer to the PeopleCert exam’s virtual calculator and notepad (syllabus p. 6); exact key labels vary between calculators.","All ticket, MTTR, fibre and salary figures are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Measure topic 2.2.2 “Descriptive Statistics” within 2.2 Six Sigma Statistics."},{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 6",pdfPage:6,note:"Exam format: closed book with Reference Document, notepad and calculator."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 147",pdfPage:50,note:"Descriptive statistics: measures of location (mean, median, mode) and variation (range, IQR, SD, variance)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 148",pdfPage:51,note:"Mean as balance point; sample vs population mean; median resistant to large values (salary example); Minitab output with SE Mean."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 149",pdfPage:52,note:"Trimmed mean; mode and bimodal distributions."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 150",pdfPage:53,note:"Range, IQR (use with skewed data), variance; standard deviations cannot be added, variances can."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 151",pdfPage:54,note:"Standard deviation for sample vs population; link to short- and long-term variation."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 157",pdfPage:60,note:"Minitab Graphical Summary for 500 observations: A-squared, P-value, mean, StDev, variance, skewness, kurtosis, quartiles, 95% CIs."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 163",pdfPage:66,note:"Box plot anatomy: quartiles, whiskers, outliers beyond 1.5 × (Q3 − Q1)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 256–257",pdfPage:59,note:"Non-normal classes: skewness, kurtosis, multiple modes, granularity; skew named by the tail."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 259–260",pdfPage:62,note:"Skewness sign (positive = right) and kurtosis (leptokurtic vs platykurtic; negative = platykurtic)."}]},N=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:x},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),a=String.raw,A={id:"2.2.3",module:6,title:"Normal Distributions & Normality",subtitle:"The bell curve, Z-scores, the empirical rule and testing for normality",minutes:18,objectives:["Describe the properties of the normal distribution and explain how its two parameters, μ and σ, fix its location and spread.","Apply the empirical rule (68–95–99.7) and convert any value to a Z-score with Z = (x − μ) / σ.","Calculate left-tail, right-tail, between-limits and outside-limits probabilities, and convert a proportion defective to PPM, using only a calculator and a standard normal table.","Solve inverse problems: find the value that covers a given percentage, or the σ needed to meet a target.","Explain why normality matters for capability, t-tests, ANOVA and control limits — and when it does not.","Test normality with the Anderson-Darling test and a normal probability plot, state the correct conclusion, and choose a sensible response to non-normal data."],overview:a`The [[normal-distribution|normal distribution]] is the quiet engine behind most of the Measure and Analyze toolkit. Capability indices, PPM predictions, t-tests, ANOVA and the ±3σ logic of control charts all lean on it. When the data really are close to normal, two numbers — the mean and the standard deviation — let you predict how often a call will breach its handle-time target, or how many invoices will fall outside a tolerance, without counting a single extra case.

That power comes with a duty: you must check the assumption before you use it. This lesson builds both halves. First the mechanics — the bell curve, the empirical rule, [[z-score|Z-scores]] and the four kinds of area question — worked with the exam's tools: a basic calculator and the standard normal table in the Reference Document. Then the judgement — how to test normality with the [[anderson-darling|Anderson-Darling]] test and a [[probability-plot|normal probability plot]], why service and transactional data are so often non-normal, and what a Green Belt does about it. Our running example is contact-centre **call handle time**, with mean **μ = 6.2 min** and standard deviation **σ = 1.1 min**.`,sections:[{id:"bell-curve",heading:"The bell curve and its two parameters",body:a`A normal distribution is a **continuous** probability model drawn as a smooth, symmetrical, bell-shaped curve. Its key properties are worth memorising as a set:

- **Symmetric about the mean** — exactly 50% of the area lies on each side, so the [[mean]], [[median]] and [[mode]] coincide.
- **Total area = 1 (100%).** The area under the curve between two values is the proportion of the population between them. A single exact value has zero area — probabilities are always about *intervals*.
- **Unbounded tails** — the curve approaches the axis in both directions but never touches it. In theory any value is possible; in practice values beyond ±3σ are rare.
- **Fully described by two parameters** — the mean $\mu$ sets the **location** (where the peak sits) and the [[standard-deviation|standard deviation]] $\sigma$ sets the **spread** (how wide the bell is). Every combination of μ and σ gives a different curve, but they all share the same shape.

Why does nature (and some processes) produce this shape? When an outcome is the sum of many small, independent, random influences — none dominant — the result tends to be normal. The training book describes a normal process as one where only random error is present, free of assignable causes, drifts and shifts. In Six Sigma language: [[common-cause|common-cause]] variation alone. A strong [[special-cause|special cause]], a mixture of two populations, or a hard boundary usually breaks the bell.

Notation matters on the exam. Greek letters (μ, σ) are population [[parameter|parameters]]; Latin letters (x̄, s) are sample [[statistic|statistics]] that estimate them. In practice you plug x̄ and s into the formulas below and treat them as estimates of μ and σ — which is why sample size and representativeness still matter.`},{id:"empirical-rule",heading:"The empirical rule: 68 – 95 – 99.7",body:a`For **any** normal distribution, the share of values within a whole number of standard deviations of the mean is fixed. This is the [[empirical-rule|empirical rule]]:

| Interval | Area inside | Area in each tail | Handle-time range (μ = 6.2, σ = 1.1) |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| μ ± 1σ | 68.27% (≈ 68%) | 15.87% | 5.1 to 7.3 min |
| μ ± 2σ | 95.45% (≈ 95%) | 2.28% | 4.0 to 8.4 min |
| μ ± 3σ | 99.73% (≈ 99.7%) | 0.135% | 2.9 to 9.5 min |

So roughly two calls in three finish between 5.1 and 7.3 minutes, about 1 in 20 falls outside 4.0–8.4 minutes, and only about 3 in 1,000 fall outside 2.9–9.5 minutes. A call of 10 minutes would be extremely unusual if the process were stable and normal — exactly the reasoning behind ±3σ [[control-limits|control limits]].

Three cautions. First, the rule describes **normal** data; for a skewed distribution the percentages can be badly wrong. Second, the tails split the leftover area **equally** only because the curve is symmetric: 100% − 95.45% = 4.55%, so 2.28% per tail. Third, the rule only handles whole multiples of σ. For anything in between — "what fraction of calls exceed 8.0 minutes?" — you need Z-scores and the table.`},{id:"z-scores",heading:"Z-scores and the standard normal table",body:a`Because every normal curve has the same shape, we can rescale any of them to one reference curve — the **standard normal**, with mean 0 and standard deviation 1. The rescaling is the Z-score:

$$Z=\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}$$

Z tells you **how many standard deviations** a value lies from the mean, and in which direction. A call of 8.0 minutes has $Z=(8.0-6.2)/1.1=1.64$; a call of 4.5 minutes has $Z=(4.5-6.2)/1.1=-1.55$. Positive Z is above the mean, negative below. Z has no units — minutes cancel — which is what lets one table serve every process.

The table gives $\Phi(z)$, the area to the **left** of z (the cumulative probability). Every area question reduces to one of four patterns:

| You want | Use |
|---|---|
| P(X < x) — left tail | $\Phi(z)$ |
| P(X > x) — right tail | $1-\Phi(z)$ |
| P(a < X < b) — between | $\Phi(z_b)-\Phi(z_a)$ |
| P(X < a or X > b) — outside | $\Phi(z_a)+[1-\Phi(z_b)]$ |

> **Exam-tools tip.** Compute Z on the virtual calculator in one line — $(8.0-6.2)\div1.1=1.6364$ — and round to **two decimals**: 1.64. In the Reference Document's standard normal table, go down the **rows** to the first decimal (1.6) and across the **columns** to the second decimal (0.04); the cell is $\Phi(1.64)=0.9495$. Then apply the pattern above. Before you start, glance at the table's heading picture: if your table shows the area *to the right* (or between 0 and z) instead of the cumulative left area, adjust the last step accordingly. Write Z and $\Phi$ on the notepad so you do not lose a sign.

Rounding Z to two decimals introduces a small error — here 0.0505 from the table against 0.0509 exactly. Exam options are spaced far wider than that, so the table answer is always good enough.`},{id:"probabilities",heading:"From probability to percentage defective and PPM",body:a`Suppose the contact centre's [[specification-limits|upper specification limit]] is **8.0 minutes** — calls longer than that breach the service target.

**Right tail.** $Z=(8.0-6.2)/1.1=1.64$; $\Phi(1.64)=0.9495$; so $P(X>8.0)=1-0.9495=0.0505$, about **5.1%** of calls. Multiply a proportion by 1,000,000 to get **[[ppm|parts per million]]**: about **50,500 PPM** from the table (50,882 with unrounded Z).

**Between two values.** What share of calls take between 5.0 and 8.0 minutes? $Z_{5.0}=(5.0-6.2)/1.1=-1.09$, $\Phi(-1.09)=0.1379$. Area = $0.9495-0.1379=0.8116$, about **81.2%**.

**Outside two limits.** Now add a lower limit of **4.0 minutes** (very short calls are often unresolved and come back as repeat contacts) and widen the upper limit to **8.5 minutes**.

- Lower tail: $Z=(4.0-6.2)/1.1=-2.00$, $\Phi(-2.00)=0.0228$.
- Upper tail: $Z=(8.5-6.2)/1.1=2.09$, $\Phi(2.09)=0.9817$, so the tail is 0.0183.
- Total out of spec = 0.0228 + 0.0183 = 0.0411, i.e. **4.1% defective ≈ 41,000 PPM** (41,018 exactly).

Always compute the two tails **separately** and add them — the process is not centred between 4.0 and 8.5, so the tails are not equal.

**Why the mean matters as much as the spread.** If coaching moved the mean to 5.8 minutes with the same σ, $Z_{8.0}=(8.0-5.8)/1.1=2.00$ and the breach rate falls from about 5.1% to **2.28%** (22,750 PPM) — less than half — without any change in variation. This is the intuition behind [[cpk|Cpk]], which you will meet in 2.4.1: capability depends on how many standard deviations fit between the mean and the **nearest** limit.`},{id:"inverse",heading:"Inverse problems: from a percentage back to a value",body:a`Sometimes the question runs backwards: you know the area and need the value. Rearranging the Z formula gives

$$x=\mu+Z\,\sigma$$

**"What handle time covers 95% of calls?"** You want the value with 95% of the area to its **left**. Search the *body* of the table for 0.9500: it sits between $\Phi(1.64)=0.9495$ and $\Phi(1.65)=0.9505$, so $Z\approx1.645$. Then $x=6.2+1.645\times1.1=8.01$ minutes. A target of "95% of calls within 8 minutes" is therefore almost exactly what the current process delivers.

**"What range contains the middle 95%?"** Now 2.5% must sit in **each** tail, so look up 0.9750: $Z=1.96$. The range is $6.2\pm1.96\times1.1$, i.e. **4.04 to 8.36 minutes**. Mixing up 1.645 (one-sided) and 1.96 (two-sided) is one of the most common errors in this topic.

**"What σ would we need so only 1% of calls exceed 8.0 minutes?"** The 99th percentile is $Z=2.33$ (table value closest to 0.9900). Solve $8.0=6.2+2.33\,\sigma$: $\sigma=1.8/2.33\approx$ **0.77 minutes** — a 30% reduction in spread, which becomes a measurable project goal.

> **Exam-tools tip.** For inverse questions you read the table *inside-out*: find the area in the body, then read the row (first decimal) and column (second decimal) to get Z. Memorise the four you will use most: 1.28 (90%), 1.645 (95% one-sided), 1.96 (95% two-sided), 2.33 (99% one-sided).`},{id:"why-normality",heading:"Why normality matters — and where it does not",body:a`Every calculation above silently assumed the data are normal. If they are not, the arithmetic still produces a number — just the wrong one. The training book is blunt: many service and transactional processes are **not** normal, and several key tools assume they are. Where the assumption bites:

- **[[process-capability|Capability]] and PPM predictions (2.4).** Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk and their predicted defect rates are tail areas of a normal curve. On right-skewed data the long upper tail is underestimated, so predicted breaches look better than reality. The book's advice for capability is to test normality first; with non-normal data, report **observed** performance until the special causes are understood.
- **t-tests and ANOVA (3.4).** They assume roughly normal populations (or residuals), although they are fairly robust for moderate samples because they compare **means** — and by the [[central-limit-theorem|central limit theorem]] averages of samples tend toward normality.
- **Control limits (5.2).** An individuals (I-MR) chart on skewed data raises false alarms on the long side; X̄ charts are more forgiving because they plot subgroup averages.

And where it does **not** matter: descriptive graphs, Pareto analysis, attribute data, and [[non-parametric]] tests. Non-normal data is not "bad" data — the book even calls it an opportunity, because the shape is usually a clue about the process.`},{id:"testing",heading:"Testing normality: Anderson-Darling and the probability plot",body:a`A [[normality-test|normality test]] is a [[hypothesis-test|hypothesis test]] like any other:

- $H_0$: the data come from a normal distribution.
- $H_1$: the data do not come from a normal distribution.

Minitab's default is the **Anderson-Darling (AD)** test, a goodness-of-fit test that measures how far the data's cumulative distribution departs from the normal curve with the same mean and standard deviation, giving extra weight to the tails. Read the [[p-value|p-value]]:

- **p > 0.05** — **fail to reject** $H_0$: there is no significant evidence against normality, so normal-based tools are reasonable. Never write "the data are proven normal".
- **p ≤ 0.05** — reject $H_0$: the data are significantly non-normal; look at the plot to see *how*.

The **normal probability plot** is the picture behind the test. Minitab plots each data value on the x-axis against its expected cumulative percentage on a stretched y-axis, so that perfectly normal data fall on a **straight line**. Read it like this:

| Pattern | Likely meaning |
|---|---|
| Points hug the line (inside a "fat pencil") | Consistent with normal |
| One consistent curve — the upper end bends away to the right | Right-skewed (long upper tail), common for times and costs |
| S-shape — both ends peel away horizontally | Heavier tails than normal (or a mixture with different spreads) |
| Both ends curl back toward the vertical | Shorter tails than normal (flat or truncated data) |
| A step or two separate straight segments | Two populations mixed together |
| One or two points far off an otherwise straight line | Outliers — investigate them |
| Vertical columns of points ("stairs") | Coarse measurement resolution |

Two caveats from the book. With very large samples (hundreds of points), AD becomes so sensitive that trivial departures give small p-values — look at the plot and judge practical importance. With very small samples, the test has little power, so a high p-value is weak reassurance. A histogram alone is a poor normality check when n is small.`},{id:"non-normal",heading:"Common causes of non-normality and what to do",advanced:!0,body:a`*Deeper material — the Analyze phase (3.1.2) returns to distribution classes in more detail.*

**Why data are non-normal.**

1. **Natural bounds → skew.** Times, costs and counts cannot go below zero; when the mean sits near that boundary the distribution is squeezed on one side and stretched on the other. Handle times, MTTR and fibre-installation times are typically **right-skewed**.
2. **Mixtures.** Two shifts, two vendors, two call types or two network regions with different means combine into a bimodal or flattened shape. Each subgroup may be perfectly normal.
3. **Outliers and special causes** — an outage day, a data-entry slip, a one-off escalation.
4. **Truncation or sorting** — data that were screened (e.g. only tickets that met SLA were kept) or a process that caps values creates a cliff at one end.
5. **Too-coarse resolution** — handle time recorded in whole minutes gives a handful of repeated values; the probability plot shows stairs and AD rejects even though the process may be normal. This is a measurement-system issue ([[resolution]], 2.3).

**What to do, in order.**

1. **Ask whether the data *should* be normal.** If a time metric is skewed, that is expected; if an outcome that should be symmetric is not, hunt for special causes.
2. **Plot over time and stratify.** Split by shift, vendor, region or call type. If each group is normal, analyse them separately — the "non-normality" was information.
3. **Investigate outliers** — correct genuine errors, but never delete a point just to pass a test.
4. **Transform** — a mathematical function such as a log or square root can make skewed data normal for capability or testing. The [[box-cox|Box-Cox]] transformation finds the best power (λ) and is covered in 4.2.5.
5. **Use methods that do not need normality** — [[non-parametric]] tests such as [[mann-whitney|Mann-Whitney]] compare medians (3.5), and capability can be estimated from observed counts or a better-fitting distribution.`}],whyItMatters:a`Measure is the heaviest phase on the exam (32%), and this topic feeds most of it. Expect direct calculation questions — a Z-score, a tail area, a PPM figure — and interpretation questions on Anderson-Darling output and probability plots, where the trap is almost always the wording ("accept", "prove") or the direction of the conclusion.

At work, the normal model turns a baseline into a prediction. If handle time is normal with μ = 6.2 and σ = 1.1, you can tell a sponsor that about 5% of calls breach an 8-minute target and that cutting σ to 0.77 would bring that to 1%. If the data are skewed, the same calculation quietly understates breaches — and an SLA penalty forecast built on it will be wrong. Checking normality first is cheap insurance.`,businessExample:{title:"Is the vendor's 8-minute handle-time forecast credible?",body:a`A DSO engineer reviews a managed-services report that forecasts **"under 2% of priority calls above 8 minutes next quarter"**, calculated from a normal model with the quarter's mean and standard deviation.

1. **Check the assumption.** On the 1,200 call records the engineer received, the probability plot bends away to the right at the upper end and the Anderson-Darling p-value is below 0.005 — the data are right-skewed, as handle times usually are.
2. **Compare model and observation.** Counting directly, about 6% of the received calls exceed 8 minutes — roughly three times the forecast.
3. **Stratify.** Split by call type, most of the long tail comes from SIM-swap calls that require a back-office check. Each call type on its own is much closer to normal.

The finding is written as a request, not a verdict: *based on the workbook analysis of the records received, approximately 6% of priority calls exceeded 8 minutes, which could not be reconciled with the forecast of under 2%. The team is asked to clarify the dataset and the distribution assumption used, and to provide supporting evidence; the data available suggest a right-skewed distribution, so observed rates or a stratified view by call type may give a more reliable forecast.* (Figures are illustrative teaching data.)`},visual:"normalCurveZ",visualCaption:"Two animations. The bell curve for call handle time (μ = 6.2 min, σ = 1.1 min) shades the 68.27%, 95.45% and 99.73% bands, then converts the 8.0-minute limit to Z = 1.64 and shades the ≈ 5.1% right tail. The probability-plot panel shows how normal, right-skewed, heavy-tailed and mixed data look against the straight reference line.",definitions:[{term:"Normal distribution",definition:"A continuous, symmetric, bell-shaped probability distribution fully described by its mean μ and standard deviation σ; also called the Gaussian distribution."},{term:"Mean (μ)",definition:"The location parameter: the centre of the bell, where mean, median and mode coincide for normal data."},{term:"Standard deviation (σ)",definition:"The spread parameter: the typical distance of values from the mean; one σ is the distance from the centre to the curve's inflection point."},{term:"Empirical rule",definition:"For normal data about 68.27%, 95.45% and 99.73% of values lie within ±1σ, ±2σ and ±3σ of the mean."},{term:"Standard normal distribution",definition:"The normal distribution with mean 0 and standard deviation 1; the reference curve in Z tables."},{term:"Z-score",definition:"Z = (x − μ) / σ: the number of standard deviations a value lies above (+) or below (−) the mean."},{term:"Φ(z) — cumulative probability",definition:"The area under the standard normal curve to the left of z; the proportion of values below x."},{term:"Tail area",definition:"The area beyond a value — the proportion of output expected above an upper limit or below a lower limit."},{term:"PPM (parts per million)",definition:"A proportion multiplied by 1,000,000; 0.0505 out of specification = 50,500 PPM."},{term:"Percentile",definition:"The value below which a stated percentage of the distribution falls; for normal data x = μ + Zσ."},{term:"Normality test",definition:"A hypothesis test with H₀: the data follow a normal distribution; a small p-value is evidence of non-normality."},{term:"Anderson-Darling test",definition:"Minitab's default goodness-of-fit test for normality; compares the data's cumulative distribution with the fitted normal, weighting the tails."},{term:"Normal probability plot",definition:"A plot of data values against expected normal percentages on a stretched scale; normal data fall on a straight line."},{term:"Box-Cox transformation",definition:"A family of power transformations (with λ = 0 meaning the natural log) used to make skewed data approximately normal; taught in 4.2.5."}],comparisons:[{title:"Empirical rule vs exact Z-table areas",headers:["","±1σ","±2σ","±3σ","Value 8.0 (Z = 1.64)"],rows:[["Area inside","68.27%","95.45%","99.73%","94.95% below"],["Area in one tail","15.87%","2.28%","0.135%","5.05% above"],["Handle-time range","5.1–7.3 min","4.0–8.4 min","2.9–9.5 min","8.0 min"],["Tool needed","Empirical rule","Empirical rule","Empirical rule","Z-score + table"]]},{title:"Anderson-Darling result: what you may and may not say",headers:["","p > 0.05","p ≤ 0.05"],rows:[["Decision on H₀ (data are normal)","Fail to reject","Reject"],["Correct wording","No significant evidence of non-normality; normal-based tools are reasonable","The data are significantly non-normal"],["Wrong wording",'"The data are proven normal" / "accept H₀"','"The process is out of control"'],["Next step","Still look at the probability plot","Read the plot to see why: skew, mixture, outliers, resolution"]]},{title:"One-sided vs two-sided Z values",headers:["Coverage","One-sided (all area in one tail)","Two-sided (area split between tails)"],rows:[["90%","Z = 1.28","Z = ±1.645"],["95%","Z = 1.645","Z = ±1.96"],["99%","Z = 2.33","Z = ±2.576"],["Handle-time use",'"95% of calls finish within 8.01 min"','"The middle 95% lies between 4.04 and 8.36 min"']]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"A high Anderson-Darling p-value proves the data are normal.",reality:'It only means there is no significant evidence against normality. You "fail to reject" H₀; you never prove it, especially with small samples.'},{myth:"The empirical rule works for any distribution.",reality:"The 68–95–99.7 percentages are properties of the normal curve. For skewed data, the share beyond 2σ on the long side can be much larger than 2.28%."},{myth:"A Z-score is a probability.",reality:"Z is a distance in standard deviations. You still need the table (Φ) to turn Z = 1.64 into an area of 0.9495."},{myth:'Non-normal data are bad data and must be "fixed" before anything else.',reality:"Skew is natural for times and costs. Non-normality is often information — a mixture to stratify or a special cause to find — and many tools do not need normality."},{myth:"If a histogram looks bell-shaped, the data are normal.",reality:"With small samples a histogram is unreliable and depends on the bin width. Use a probability plot and an Anderson-Darling test."},{myth:"Specification limits are where the normal curve ends.",reality:"Spec limits come from the customer (VOC); the curve comes from the process (VOP). The tail areas beyond the spec limits are the predicted defect rate."}],examTraps:[{trap:'"The Anderson-Darling p-value is 0.31. Therefore the data are …" — with an option saying "proven normal" or "accept H₀".',explanation:'p > 0.05 → fail to reject H₀; there is no significant evidence of non-normality. "Proven" and "accept" are always wrong.',tag:"fail-to-reject"},{trap:'An AD p-value of 0.002 is read as "the data are normal because p is small".',explanation:'In a normality test H₀ is "normal". A small p-value rejects normality — the reverse of what many learners expect.',tag:"p-value"},{trap:'A right-tail question ("what proportion EXCEED 8.0 minutes?") with Φ(1.64) = 0.9495 among the options.',explanation:"The table gives the area to the left. Exceeding needs 1 − 0.9495 = 0.0505.",tag:"one-vs-two-tailed"},{trap:'"What range contains the middle 95%?" with options built from Z = 1.645.',explanation:"Middle 95% leaves 2.5% in each tail → Z = 1.96. 1.645 is for one-sided 95%.",tag:"one-vs-two-tailed"},{trap:"Two-sided out-of-spec questions where the process is off-centre.",explanation:"Compute each tail with its own Z and add them; do not double one tail."},{trap:"A capability or PPM prediction is requested for clearly skewed data, with the normal-model answer as the tempting option.",explanation:"Capability predictions assume normality. Check normality first; for skewed data use observed performance, a transformation or a fitted non-normal distribution.",tag:"normality-assumption"},{trap:'The stem gives sample statistics (x̄ = 6.2, s = 1.1) and asks for "the population mean".',explanation:"x̄ and s are estimates of μ and σ. Use them in the Z formula, but recognise the result is an estimate, not a known population value.",tag:"sample-vs-population"},{trap:'"Which is NOT a property of the normal distribution?"',explanation:'Look for "bounded at zero", "mean > median", or "defined by its range". The normal is symmetric, unbounded, and defined by μ and σ.',tag:"not"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Right tail: share of calls above an 8.0-minute limit",problem:a`Call handle time is normal with **μ = 6.2 min** and **σ = 1.1 min**. What proportion of calls exceed the upper limit of **8.0 min**, and what is that in PPM?`,steps:["Z = (8.0 − 6.2) ÷ 1.1 = 1.8 ÷ 1.1 = 1.636 → round to **1.64**.","Table: row 1.6, column 0.04 → Φ(1.64) = **0.9495** (area below 8.0).","Right tail = 1 − 0.9495 = **0.0505** → about **5.1%** of calls.","PPM = 0.0505 × 1,000,000 ≈ **50,500 PPM** (50,882 with unrounded Z).",'**Exam-tools tip:** type 1.8 ÷ 1.1 on the calculator, note 1.64 on the notepad, read Φ in the table, then do 1 − Φ for "exceed".'],answer:"≈ 5.1% of calls (≈ 50,500 PPM)",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Answer = Φ(1.64) = 94.95%",whyWrong:'94.95% is the proportion **below** 8.0 minutes. "Exceed" asks for the right tail, 1 − Φ(z).'}},{title:"Between two values",problem:a`Same process. What proportion of calls take **between 5.0 and 8.0 minutes**?`,steps:["Z for 5.0 = (5.0 − 6.2) ÷ 1.1 = −1.09; Z for 8.0 = 1.64.","Table: Φ(−1.09) = **0.1379**; Φ(1.64) = **0.9495**.","Between = 0.9495 − 0.1379 = **0.8116** ≈ **81.2%**.","**Exam-tools tip:** for a negative Z use the negative half of the table, or symmetry: Φ(−1.09) = 1 − Φ(1.09) = 1 − 0.8621 = 0.1379."],answer:"≈ 81.2% of calls",wrongApproach:{calculation:"0.9495 + 0.1379 = 1.0874",whyWrong:"The area between two values is a **difference** of cumulative areas. A probability above 1 is a red flag that you added instead of subtracting."}},{title:"Percentage defective with two limits → PPM",problem:a`The centre treats calls shorter than **4.0 min** (likely unresolved) and longer than **8.5 min** as defects. With μ = 6.2 and σ = 1.1, estimate the percentage defective and PPM.`,steps:["Lower: Z = (4.0 − 6.2) ÷ 1.1 = **−2.00** → Φ(−2.00) = **0.0228**.","Upper: Z = (8.5 − 6.2) ÷ 1.1 = **2.09** → Φ(2.09) = 0.9817 → tail = **0.0183**.","Total defective = 0.0228 + 0.0183 = **0.0411** ≈ **4.1%**.","PPM ≈ 0.0411 × 10⁶ ≈ **41,100** from the table (41,018 exactly).","**Exam-tools tip:** compute and write each tail separately on the notepad, then add — the process is not centred, so the tails differ."],answer:"≈ 4.1% defective (≈ 41,000 PPM)",wrongApproach:{calculation:"2 × 0.0228 = 0.0456 (doubling the lower tail)",whyWrong:"Doubling assumes the mean is centred between the limits. Here the upper limit is 2.09σ away and the lower limit 2.00σ away, so each tail needs its own Z."}},{title:"Inverse: the time that covers 95% of calls",problem:a`What handle time is exceeded by only 5% of calls? And what range holds the middle 95%?`,steps:["95% to the left → find 0.9500 in the table body: between 1.64 (0.9495) and 1.65 (0.9505) → **Z ≈ 1.645**.","x = μ + Zσ = 6.2 + 1.645 × 1.1 = **8.01 min**.","Middle 95% → 2.5% per tail → find 0.9750 → **Z = 1.96**.","Range = 6.2 ± 1.96 × 1.1 = 6.2 ± 2.16 → **4.04 to 8.36 min**.","**Exam-tools tip:** in inverse problems read the table inside-out — area in the body, Z from the row and column headings."],answer:"95th percentile ≈ 8.01 min; middle 95% ≈ 4.04 to 8.36 min",wrongApproach:{calculation:'x = 6.2 + 1.96 × 1.1 = 8.36 min for "95% of calls"',whyWrong:'1.96 leaves 2.5% above, so 8.36 min covers 97.5% of calls. "95% within" is one-sided → Z = 1.645.'}},{title:"Inverse: the σ needed to hit a 1% target",problem:a`Management wants no more than **1%** of calls above 8.0 min, keeping the mean at 6.2 min. What standard deviation is required?`,steps:["1% above → 99% below → table body 0.9900 → **Z ≈ 2.33**.","Rearrange x = μ + Zσ: σ = (x − μ) ÷ Z = (8.0 − 6.2) ÷ 2.33.","σ = 1.8 ÷ 2.33 ≈ **0.77 min** (down from 1.1 min, about a 30% reduction)."],answer:"σ ≈ 0.77 minutes",wrongApproach:{calculation:'σ = 1.8 ÷ 3 = 0.60 min ("1% means 3σ")',whyWrong:"3σ leaves only 0.135% in one tail. 1% in one tail corresponds to Z = 2.33."}},{title:"Reading an Anderson-Darling result",problem:a`Minitab reports for 70 fibre-installation times: **AD = 0.41, p-value = 0.33**; the probability plot points stay close to the line. For 400 MTTR values: **AD = 9.8, p < 0.005**; the upper points bend away to the right. Interpret both.`,steps:["Installation times: p = 0.33 > 0.05 → **fail to reject** H₀. No significant evidence against normality; normal-based tools are reasonable.","MTTR: p < 0.005 ≤ 0.05 → **reject** H₀. The data are significantly non-normal.","The plot bending right at the top shows a long upper tail → **right skew**, typical for repair times bounded at zero.","Next steps for MTTR: stratify (priority, region, vendor), check outliers, then consider a transformation or non-parametric methods."],answer:"Installation times: consistent with normal. MTTR: significantly non-normal, right-skewed.",wrongApproach:{calculation:'"p = 0.33 is small, so the installation data are non-normal"',whyWrong:"Compare p with α = 0.05. 0.33 is far above 0.05, so H₀ (normal) is not rejected."}}],lab:"normality",check:[{id:"chk-2.2.3-1",topic:"2.2.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Call handle time is normally distributed with **μ = 6.2 min** and **σ = 1.1 min**. Approximately what percentage of calls exceed **8.0 minutes**?",options:["About 94.9%","About 5.1%","About 1.6%","About 2.5%"],answer:1,explanation:"Z = (8.0 − 6.2) ÷ 1.1 = 1.64. Φ(1.64) = 0.9495, so the right tail is 1 − 0.9495 = 0.0505 ≈ 5.1%.",whyWrong:["94.9% is Φ(1.64), the share **below** 8.0 minutes.","","1.6% treats the Z-score (1.64) as if it were a percentage.","2.5% is a guess from the two-sided 95% rule; 8.0 is only 1.64σ above the mean, not 1.96σ."],traps:["one-vs-two-tailed"]},{id:"chk-2.2.3-2",topic:"2.2.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!0,stem:"With μ = 6.2 min and σ = 1.1 min, what is the Z-score of a **9.5-minute** call?",options:["3.3","0.33","3.0","8.6"],answer:2,explanation:"Z = (9.5 − 6.2) ÷ 1.1 = 3.3 ÷ 1.1 = 3.0. The call is three standard deviations above the mean.",whyWrong:["3.3 is the distance in minutes (x − μ), not divided by σ.","0.33 divides the wrong way round (σ ÷ (x − μ) = 1.1 ÷ 3.3).","","8.6 = 9.5 − 0.9; it has no meaning in the Z formula."]},{id:"chk-2.2.3-3",topic:"2.2.3",category:"concept",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"An Anderson-Darling normality test on 60 ticket-resolution times gives **p = 0.23**. Which conclusion is correct at α = 0.05?",options:["Reject H₀: the data are not normal.","Accept H₀: the data are proven to be normal.","Fail to reject H₀: there is no significant evidence that the data are non-normal.","The test is inconclusive because p is below 0.5."],answer:2,explanation:'H₀ is "the data are normal". p = 0.23 > 0.05, so we fail to reject H₀ — the data are consistent with normality, which is not the same as proven normal.',whyWrong:["We reject only when p ≤ 0.05.",'"Accept" and "prove" overstate what a hypothesis test can show.',"","The comparison is with α = 0.05, not 0.5."],traps:["fail-to-reject","p-value"]},{id:"chk-2.2.3-4",topic:"2.2.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!0,stem:"Handle time is normal with μ = 6.2 min and σ = 1.1 min. Using the empirical rule, about what percentage of calls take between **4.0 and 8.4 minutes**?",options:["68%","95%","99.7%","50%"],answer:1,explanation:"4.0 = 6.2 − 2 × 1.1 and 8.4 = 6.2 + 2 × 1.1, so the interval is μ ± 2σ, which contains about 95% (95.45%).",whyWrong:["68% is μ ± 1σ = 5.1 to 7.3 minutes.","","99.7% is μ ± 3σ = 2.9 to 9.5 minutes.","50% would be one side of the mean, not a symmetric ±2σ band."]},{id:"chk-2.2.3-5",topic:"2.2.3",category:"chart",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"On a normal probability plot of MTTR data, the points follow the line in the middle but the highest values bend sharply away to the right, and the Anderson-Darling p-value is below 0.005. What is the BEST interpretation?",options:["The data are normal; the bend is caused by the plotting scale.","The data are right-skewed with a long upper tail, as is common for repair times.","The data have shorter tails than normal.","The process is out of statistical control."],answer:1,explanation:"A small p-value rejects normality, and upper points peeling off to the right show a long upper tail — right skew, typical of times that are bounded at zero.",whyWrong:["The stretched scale is what makes normal data straight; a consistent bend means non-normal.","","Short tails make the ends curl back toward the vertical, not out to the right.","Normality and statistical control are different questions; a control chart over time is needed for stability."],traps:["normality-assumption","best"]},{id:"chk-2.2.3-6",topic:"2.2.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!0,stem:"With μ = 6.2 min and σ = 1.1 min, which handle time is exceeded by only **10%** of calls? (Φ(1.28) ≈ 0.90)",options:["7.61 min","4.79 min","8.36 min","7.30 min"],answer:0,explanation:"90% below → Z = 1.28. x = μ + Zσ = 6.2 + 1.28 × 1.1 = 7.61 minutes.",whyWrong:["","4.79 = 6.2 − 1.28 × 1.1 is the 10th percentile (10% below, not above).","8.36 uses Z = 1.96, the two-sided 95% value.","7.30 = μ + 1σ, which is exceeded by about 15.9% of calls."],traps:["one-vs-two-tailed"]}],takeaways:["The normal curve is symmetric, unbounded and fully defined by μ (location) and σ (spread); mean = median = mode.","Empirical rule: 68.27% / 95.45% / 99.73% within ±1σ / ±2σ / ±3σ — for normal data only.","Z = (x − μ) / σ counts standard deviations; the table turns Z into Φ(z), the area to the left.","Right tail = 1 − Φ(z); between = Φ(z_b) − Φ(z_a); outside = sum of the two tails, each with its own Z. × 10⁶ for PPM.","Inverse problems: x = μ + Zσ. One-sided 95% → 1.645; two-sided 95% → 1.96; 99% one-sided → 2.33.","Capability, PPM predictions, t-tests, ANOVA and I-chart limits rely on normality; Pareto, graphs and non-parametric tests do not.",'Anderson-Darling H₀ = normal: p > 0.05 → fail to reject (never "proven"); p ≤ 0.05 → significantly non-normal. Always read the probability plot too.',"Non-normal? Ask if it should be normal, stratify, investigate outliers, then transform (Box-Cox, 4.2.5) or use non-parametric tools."],flashcards:[{front:"Two parameters of a normal distribution",back:"Mean μ (location) and standard deviation σ (spread)."},{front:"Empirical rule",back:"±1σ ≈ 68.27%, ±2σ ≈ 95.45%, ±3σ ≈ 99.73% (normal data only)."},{front:"Z-score formula",back:"Z = (x − μ) / σ — standard deviations from the mean."},{front:"Right-tail probability",back:"1 − Φ(z). Example: Z = 1.64 → 1 − 0.9495 = 0.0505."},{front:"Probability between a and b",back:"Φ(z_b) − Φ(z_a)."},{front:"Proportion → PPM",back:"Multiply by 1,000,000 (0.0411 → 41,100 PPM)."},{front:"Value for a given percentile",back:"x = μ + Zσ (e.g. 95th percentile: 6.2 + 1.645 × 1.1 = 8.01)."},{front:"1.645 vs 1.96",back:"1.645 = one-sided 95%; 1.96 = two-sided 95% (2.5% per tail)."},{front:"Anderson-Darling H₀",back:"The data come from a normal distribution."},{front:"AD p = 0.41",back:"p > 0.05 → fail to reject H₀; no significant evidence of non-normality."},{front:"Probability plot bending right at the top",back:"Long upper tail → right skew (common for times and costs)."},{front:"Five common causes of non-normality",back:"Natural bounds (skew), mixtures, outliers/special causes, truncation, coarse resolution."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["Every probability, Z-value, percentile and PPM figure was computed with the app's verified statistics engine (normCdf, normInv, ppmOutOfSpec); table-based answers use Z rounded to two decimals, as an exam candidate would.","The exact empirical-rule percentages (68.27 / 95.45 / 99.73) and the one- and two-sided Z constants are standard statistical values; the book presents the empirical rule graphically.","The pattern guide for reading probability plots (S-curves, curling tails, steps, stairs) is general Lean Six Sigma practice written for this app; the book states that normal data lie on a straight line and shows which values deviate.","The exam environment's table layout is described as commonly printed (rows = z to one decimal, columns = second decimal, cumulative left area); learners are told to check the table heading in case a right-tail table is supplied.","Call-handle-time, MTTR and installation figures, and the vendor forecast scenario, are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:'Measure topic 2.2.3 "Normal Distributions & Normality" (2.2 Six Sigma Statistics).'},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 151",pdfPage:54,note:"Characteristics of a normal distribution: only random error present, free of assignable causes, drifts and shifts."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 152",pdfPage:55,note:"Normal curve: smooth, symmetric, bell-shaped; defined by mean and standard deviation; standard normal (μ = 0, σ = 1) and Z-scores."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 153",pdfPage:56,note:"Area under the curve between two points = proportion; convert to Z and use standard normal tables; the empirical rule."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 154",pdfPage:57,note:"Why assess normality: many service and transactional processes are not normal; many tools assume normality; time metrics cannot be negative."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 155",pdfPage:58,note:"Tools for assessing normality: Anderson-Darling goodness-of-fit test and the normal probability plot."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 156",pdfPage:59,note:"Probability plot: normal data lie on a straight line; AD p-value > .05 → normal enough for most purposes."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 157",pdfPage:60,note:"AD caveat: very large samples make the test highly sensitive; non-normal data are not a reason to panic."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 158",pdfPage:61,note:"Special vs common cause; data that should be normal but are not suggest special causes."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 221",pdfPage:24,note:"Capability indices assume normal data; perform a normality test before assessing capability; use observed performance for non-normal data in Measure."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 255–263",pdfPage:58,note:"Normal (Z) distribution properties; four classes of non-normality (skewness, kurtosis, multiple modes, granularity) and their causes (Analyze context)."},{doc:"book-301-400",pages:"p. 370",pdfPage:73,note:"Why normality matters for inference; primary causes of non-normal data; caution against transforming data without understanding the cause."},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 460",pdfPage:63,note:"Transforming skewed data; Box-Cox transformation and lambda (Improve context)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 587",pdfPage:90,note:"Glossary: normal distribution — smooth, bell-shaped curve, synonymous with Gaussian."}]},F=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:A},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),s=String.raw,S={id:"2.2.4",module:6,title:"Graphical Analysis",subtitle:"Histograms, box plots, dot plots, run charts and scatter plots",minutes:15,objectives:["Explain why a Green Belt graphs data before calculating statistics, and what each graph can and cannot reveal.","Build and read a histogram, recognising normal, skewed, bimodal, plateau and truncated shapes and the effect of bin width.","Construct a box plot with Minitab quartiles, the 1.5 × IQR rule, whiskers and outlier asterisks, and use box plots to compare groups.","Use dot plots and individual value plots to see individual points, granularity and hidden bimodality.","Read run charts and time series plots for trends, shifts and cycles, and distinguish a run chart from a control chart.","Interpret scatter plots (direction, strength, form, outliers) without confusing correlation with causation, and choose the right graph for a question and data type."],overview:s`Numbers summarise; graphs reveal. A mean of 6.74 minutes and a standard deviation of 1.20 tell you nothing about *how* those numbers arose — whether the data are one population or two, whether something changed half-way through the month, or whether one extreme call is inflating the spread. The training book puts it simply: the purpose of graphing is to identify potential relationships, spot risks to customer requirements, and gain insight into which X's may drive the Y.

This lesson uses **one dataset throughout**: the handle times of 30 consecutive contact-centre calls. Drawn four ways — [[histogram]], [[box-plot|box plot]], [[dot-plot|dot plot]] and [[run-chart|run chart]] — the same 30 numbers tell four different stories, and only one of the graphs exposes the most important one. You will also meet the [[scatter-plot|scatter plot]] for relationships between two variables, revisit the [[pareto-chart|Pareto chart]] for categories, and finish with a decision table for choosing the right graph for the question and the data type.`,sections:[{id:"graph-first",heading:"Graph first, calculate second",body:s`Every statistic hides something. The mean hides shape; the standard deviation hides *why* the spread exists; neither says anything about time order. Graphs come first in Measure for three reasons:

1. **To check the data before trusting it** — impossible values, typing errors, coarse resolution and gaps show up instantly on a plot.
2. **To check assumptions** — whether data look normal (2.2.3), whether the process is stable over time (2.4.2), whether groups have similar spread.
3. **To generate hypotheses about X's** — a second peak, a shift on a certain date or a difference between vendors points straight at a candidate cause for Analyze.

The book links graphing to the earlier Measure tools: the process map, X-Y matrix, FMEA and fishbone suggest which X's to collect alongside Y, and data should be tagged so they can be **stratified** — by time (shift, day of week, month), location (site, region, office), and people (team, training, experience). A graph of all the data pooled together can look perfectly ordinary while two very different sub-processes hide inside it.

Our running dataset is the handle time, in minutes, of 30 consecutive calls on one queue: **mean 6.74, median 6.65, s = 1.20, minimum 5.2, maximum 11.8**. Keep those summary numbers in mind as each graph adds something they could not.`},{id:"histogram",heading:"Histograms: shape, centre and spread",body:s`A histogram divides the measurement scale into **equal-width intervals** (bins or cells) and draws a bar whose height is the number of values in each bin. It is the quickest way to see **shape, centre and spread** of continuous data.

With 1-minute bins our 30 calls give counts of **7** (5–6 min), **13** (6–7), **9** (7–8), nothing from 8 to 11, and **1** call in 11–12. The bulk looks roughly mound-shaped, but one isolated bar sits far out on the right.

Learn to name the common shapes and what they usually mean:

| Shape | What you see | Typical cause in service operations |
|---|---|---|
| Bell / normal | Single symmetric peak | Many small random causes (common cause) |
| Right-skewed | Long tail to the right | Times and costs bounded at zero |
| Left-skewed | Long tail to the left | Scores near a ceiling (e.g. 100% SLA) |
| Bimodal | Two peaks | Two populations mixed: shifts, vendors, call types |
| Plateau / flat | Broad flat top | Several groups with different means, or a drifting mean |
| Truncated | Cut off sharply at one side | Data screened or capped (e.g. only tickets within SLA kept) |
| Isolated bar | A small group far from the rest | Outlier or special cause — investigate |

**Bin width matters.** Too few bins hide structure; too many turn the picture into noise. A common starting point is about √n bins (≈ 5–6 for 30 values), then adjust. And the book's caution is important: with small samples (it shows 20-point examples) a histogram can look non-normal when the process is normal, or vice versa — **never judge normality from a histogram alone**; use a probability plot (2.2.3).`},{id:"box-plot",heading:"Box plots: five numbers, outliers and group comparisons",body:s`A box plot condenses data into a few robust statistics, which makes it ideal for **comparing groups side by side**. Its anatomy, as drawn by Minitab:

- **Box** from [[quartile|Q1]] (25th percentile) to Q3 (75th percentile); its length is the [[iqr|interquartile range]], IQR = Q3 − Q1, and it holds the middle 50% of the data.
- **Line inside the box** at the [[median]].
- **Whiskers** extending to the most extreme data values that are still inside the limits Q1 − 1.5 × IQR and Q3 + 1.5 × IQR.
- **Asterisks (\*)** for any value beyond those limits — potential [[outlier|outliers]].

Minitab places quartiles at position $p(n+1)$ in the sorted data, interpolating when that position is not a whole number. For our 30 calls: Q1 sits at position 7.75 → **5.975**, Q3 at position 23.25 → **7.225**, IQR = **1.25**. The outlier limits are $5.975-1.875=4.1$ and $7.225+1.875=9.1$. Every call except one lies inside them, so the whiskers run from **5.2** to **7.8**, and the **11.8**-minute call is plotted as an asterisk.

**Reading symmetry.** A median near the middle of the box with whiskers of similar length suggests symmetric data. Here the median (6.65) sits slightly right of centre and the asterisk marks a single extreme value; the box itself is fairly balanced.

**Comparing groups** is where box plots shine: four vendors' MTTR, or handle time by shift, drawn on one axis, show differences in median, spread and outliers at a glance. The book's set-up-time example compares three technicians and adds a caution — the person with the most outliers may simply be the expert sent the hardest jobs. A box plot shows *what* differs, not *why*.

**Limitations.** A box plot hides the number of points, gaps and multiple peaks — a bimodal dataset can produce a perfectly symmetric box. Pair it with individual points when n is small.`},{id:"dot-ivp",heading:"Dot plots and individual value plots",body:s`A **dot plot** places one dot per observation along a number line, stacking dots that fall at (almost) the same value. It keeps every individual point visible, which makes it ideal for small and moderate samples. In our data the dot plot shows a tight cloud from 5.2 to 7.8 and one dot alone at 11.8, plus a few repeated values (6.6, 6.8 and 6.9 each occur twice).

The book highlights two things a dot plot reveals that a histogram can hide:

- **Granularity** — data recorded to a coarse resolution pile into a few columns. That points to the measurement system ([[resolution]], 2.3), and it also explains why a normality test may fail.
- **Points worth investigating** — isolated dots, or unusually tall stacks, may have special causes that should be checked in the data-collection logbook.

An **individual value plot** is the multi-group version: one column of dots per group (vendor, shift, technician), often drawn alongside box plots. It exposes what the box plot hides — the book's example shows a bimodal group whose box looks perfectly symmetric until the individual points reveal two separate clusters. When many points coincide, **jitter** (a small random sideways offset) separates them so none are hidden behind others.`},{id:"run-chart",heading:"Run charts and time series plots: what happened when",body:s`Histograms, box plots and dot plots all throw away one crucial piece of information: **time order**. A [[time-series-plot|time series plot]] or run chart plots each value in the order it occurred, joined by a line; a run chart usually adds the **median** as a centre line.

Plot our 30 calls in sequence and a new story appears. Calls 1–15 average **6.00 minutes** (median 6.0); calls 16–30 average **7.49** (median 7.2; 7.18 without the extreme call). Calls 5–15 all sit **below** the overall median of 6.65, and calls 19–30 all sit **above** it. Something changed at about call 16 — perhaps a new verification step or a CRM screen change. The 11.8-minute call (call 23) is a single spike within the new level. *None* of the earlier graphs showed this shift; the histogram even looked like one reasonable mound.

Patterns to look for:

| Pattern | What it looks like | Possible X |
|---|---|---|
| **Shift** | A sustained jump to a new level | Procedure, system, staffing or vendor change |
| **Trend** | Steady drift up or down | Wear, growing backlog, learning, seasonality |
| **Cycle** | Regular waves | Time of day, day of week, shift rotation |
| **Spike** | One point far from its neighbours | Special cause on that day or call |
| **Mixture/oscillation** | Points alternate high–low | Two sources feeding one series |

The book shows how a time series exposes a wave pattern and how a smoothing line (Lowess) helps the eye follow the underlying movement.

**Run chart vs control chart.** A run chart is the raw time plot with a median line; patterns are judged by eye or with simple run rules (supplementary: for example, a long run of consecutive points on one side of the median signals a likely shift). A [[control-chart|control chart]] is a run chart **plus a mean centre line and statistically calculated control limits** (normally ±3σ from the process's own data), with formal rules for [[special-cause|special causes]] — covered in 5.2. Note that control limits come from the process (voice of the process), never from the specification.`},{id:"scatter",heading:"Scatter plots: relationships between two variables",body:s`A scatter plot puts one continuous variable on each axis — the suspected input X horizontally and the output Y vertically — with one point per paired observation. Read it in four steps:

1. **Direction** — positive (Y rises with X), negative (Y falls), or none.
2. **Strength** — how tightly the points cluster around a line or curve.
3. **Form** — straight or curved; a strong curve can have a weak linear correlation.
4. **Unusual points** — outliers and separate clusters, which can create or hide an apparent relationship.

Example: over ten weeks, the open-ticket **backlog** at the start of each week (40 to 140 tickets) is plotted against that week's **MTTR** (3.1 to 5.8 hours). The points rise steadily from lower-left to upper-right; the [[correlation-coefficient|Pearson correlation]] is **r = 0.97** — a strong positive linear relationship.

**Correlation is not causation.** Higher backlog *might* lengthen MTTR (engineers juggle more tickets), but both could be driven by a third variable — an outage period, a vacancy in the resolver team, or a new product launch. A scatter plot of historical data shows association; proving cause needs process knowledge, stratification or a controlled change. The book makes the same point when it introduces correlation and regression in Improve. The formal tools — [[correlation]], r, [[r-squared|R²]] and regression lines — are 4.1.1–4.1.2; in Measure the scatter plot is a screening picture.`},{id:"pareto-choose",heading:"Pareto charts and choosing the right graph",body:s`For **categorical (attribute) data** — reasons, types, locations — the main graph is the [[pareto-chart|Pareto chart]] from 1.2.4: bars in descending order of frequency with a cumulative-percentage line. Of 460 repeat contacts, password resets (186, 40.4%) and billing queries (142, 30.9%) together make up **71.3%** — the vital few to stratify first. Histograms and box plots need a numeric measurement; Pareto charts need categories and counts.

Choose the graph from the **question** and the **data type**:

| Question | Data | Best first graph |
|---|---|---|
| What is the shape, centre and spread? | One continuous Y, n ≳ 30 | Histogram (+ probability plot for normality) |
| Same, with few points | One continuous Y, small n | Dot plot |
| Do groups differ in centre or spread? | Continuous Y, categorical X | Box plots side by side (+ individual value plot) |
| Has anything changed over time? | Continuous Y in time order | Run chart / time series plot |
| Is the process stable, by statistical rules? | Time-ordered Y | Control chart (5.2) |
| Are two measurements related? | Continuous X and Y, paired | Scatter plot |
| Which categories matter most? | Counts by category | Pareto chart |

Two habits make graphical analysis trustworthy: put related graphs on the **same scale** when comparing, and always ask what the graph cannot show — a box plot cannot show time order, a histogram cannot show individual points, and no graph proves cause.`}],whyItMatters:s`Graph-reading questions are a staple of the Measure section: "Which graph would BEST show …?", "What does this box plot indicate?", "What is the next step after this histogram?" The traps are predictable — choosing a histogram when the question is about change over time, reading correlation as cause, or drawing conclusions about normality from a small histogram.

At work, graphs are often the deliverable. A run chart that shows handle time stepping up after a system release is more persuasive to a vendor or a sponsor than any p-value, and side-by-side box plots of MTTR by resolver group focus a conversation on spread and outliers instead of averages. Graphing before calculating also protects you from reporting a clean-looking mean that hides two different processes.`,businessExample:{title:"The handle-time increase only the run chart could see",body:s`A DSO engineer receives a monthly KPI pack in which average handle time on the broadband support queue has risen from about 6.0 to 6.7 minutes. The vendor's commentary attributes the rise to "normal monthly variation".

1. **Histogram** of the 30 sampled calls: one broad mound plus a single long call at 11.8 minutes. Nothing obviously wrong.
2. **Box plot**: median 6.65 minutes, IQR 1.25 minutes, whiskers 5.2–7.8 minutes, one asterisk at 11.8.
3. **Run chart** in call order: calls 1–15 centre on 6.0 minutes; from call 16 onwards they centre on about 7.2–7.5 minutes, with 12 consecutive calls above the overall median. The change coincides with the date a new identity-verification step was added to the script.

The finding is written as a request for clarification: *based on the workbook analysis of the sampled calls, handle time appears to have shifted from about 6.0 to about 7.5 minutes from mid-month, coinciding with the new verification step; this could not be reconciled with "normal monthly variation" from the data available. The team is asked to confirm whether the script change applied to this queue and to provide the full call-level data for the period.* The 11.8-minute call is flagged separately for investigation rather than deleted. (Illustrative teaching data.)`},visual:"graphMorph",visualCaption:"One dataset, four graphs: the same 30 call-handle times rearrange themselves from a histogram into a box plot (Q1 5.975, median 6.65, Q3 7.225, outlier 11.8), a dot plot and finally a run chart — the only view that reveals the shift from about 6.0 to about 7.5 minutes after call 15.",definitions:[{term:"Graphical analysis",definition:"Using plots of data to see shape, centre, spread, relationships, time patterns and unusual points before (and alongside) calculating statistics."},{term:"Histogram",definition:"A bar chart of frequencies over equal-width intervals (bins) of a continuous variable; shows shape, centre and spread."},{term:"Bin (cell)",definition:"One interval of a histogram; the bin width controls how much detail the histogram shows."},{term:"Box plot",definition:"A plot of the quartiles (box from Q1 to Q3, line at the median), whiskers to the furthest points within 1.5 × IQR of the box, and asterisks for outliers."},{term:"Interquartile range (IQR)",definition:"Q3 − Q1; the range of the middle 50% of the data and the length of the box."},{term:"Outlier (box-plot rule)",definition:"A value below Q1 − 1.5 × IQR or above Q3 + 1.5 × IQR; plotted individually (Minitab uses an asterisk)."},{term:"Dot plot",definition:"One dot per observation along a number line, stacked when values coincide; keeps individual values and granularity visible."},{term:"Individual value plot",definition:"A dot plot for several groups side by side; reveals point counts, clusters and bimodality that box plots hide."},{term:"Run chart / time series plot",definition:"Data plotted in time order, joined by a line (a run chart usually adds a median line); shows shifts, trends, cycles and spikes."},{term:"Control chart",definition:"A run chart with a centre line and statistically calculated control limits, plus rules for detecting special causes (5.2)."},{term:"Scatter plot",definition:"A plot of paired observations of two continuous variables, X horizontally and Y vertically, to show direction, strength and form of a relationship."},{term:"Correlation vs causation",definition:"Correlation measures how two variables move together; causation means changing X changes Y. Correlation alone never proves causation."},{term:"Pareto chart",definition:"Bars for categories in descending order of frequency with a cumulative-percentage line; separates the vital few from the trivial many."},{term:"Stratification",definition:"Splitting data by a factor such as time, location, team or vendor so that differences between subgroups become visible."}],comparisons:[{title:"What each graph shows and hides",headers:["Graph","Best for","Shows","Hides"],rows:[["Histogram","Shape of one continuous Y (n ≳ 30)","Shape, centre, spread, multiple peaks","Individual values, time order; depends on bin width"],["Box plot","Comparing groups","Median, quartiles, spread, outliers","Number of points, gaps, bimodality, time order"],["Dot plot / individual value plot","Small samples, groups with few points","Every value, granularity, clusters","Time order; cluttered for large n"],["Run chart / time series","Change over time","Shifts, trends, cycles, spikes","Overall distribution shape"],["Scatter plot","Relationship between two continuous variables","Direction, strength, form, outliers","Cause, lurking variables"],["Pareto chart","Counts by category","Vital few categories, cumulative %","Variation within categories, time order"]]},{title:"Run chart vs control chart",headers:["","Run chart","Control chart"],rows:[["Centre line","Usually the median","Usually the mean (or average of subgroup statistics)"],["Limits","None","Upper and lower control limits, normally ±3σ, calculated from the process data"],["How signals are judged","By eye or simple run rules","Formal special-cause rules (points beyond limits, runs, trends)"],["Phase in the syllabus","Measure 2.2.4 (graphical analysis)","Control 5.2 (SPC)"],["Common error","Over-reacting to a single high point","Using specification limits as control limits"]]},{title:"Same data, different stories (30 calls)",headers:["Graph","What it told us"],rows:[["Histogram (1-min bins)","7 / 13 / 9 calls in 5–6, 6–7, 7–8 min; one isolated call at 11–12 min"],["Box plot","Q1 5.975, median 6.65, Q3 7.225, IQR 1.25; whiskers 5.2–7.8; asterisk at 11.8"],["Dot plot","Tight cloud, one isolated dot, a few repeated values"],["Run chart","Shift at call 16: median 6.0 → 7.2 min; 12 consecutive calls above the overall median"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"A box plot shows whether the data are normal.",reality:"It shows quartiles and outliers. A symmetric box can come from bimodal or flat data. Use a probability plot or normality test for normality."},{myth:"Outliers should be removed so the graph looks clean.",reality:"An outlier is a question, not an error. Investigate it; remove it only if it is a proven data error, and report what you did."},{myth:"If the histogram looks fine, the process is stable.",reality:"A histogram has no time order. Our 30 calls form one reasonable mound yet contain a clear shift that only the run chart shows."},{myth:"A run chart and a control chart are the same thing.",reality:"A control chart adds statistically calculated control limits and formal rules for special causes; a run chart has neither."},{myth:"A strong correlation on a scatter plot proves that X causes Y.",reality:"It shows association. A lurking variable may drive both, or the direction may be reversed. Cause needs process knowledge and confirmation."},{myth:"The whiskers always reach the minimum and maximum.",reality:"In Minitab the whiskers stop at the furthest values within 1.5 × IQR of the box; anything further is drawn as an asterisk."}],examTraps:[{trap:'"Which graph would BEST show whether handle time changed after the system upgrade?" with histogram and box plot among the options.',explanation:"Change over time needs time order: a run chart / time series plot. Histograms and box plots discard the sequence.",tag:"best"},{trap:"A box plot question asks for the upper whisker end and offers the maximum value.",explanation:"The whisker ends at the largest value within Q3 + 1.5 × IQR. A maximum beyond that limit is an asterisk, not the whisker.",tag:"not"},{trap:'A scatter plot shows r = 0.97 between backlog and MTTR; one option says "backlog causes long MTTR".',explanation:"Correlation shows association only. The correct option speaks of a strong positive relationship worth investigating.",tag:"correlation-causation"},{trap:'A histogram of 20 values looks lopsided, and an option concludes "the data are not normal".',explanation:"Small-sample histograms are unreliable for judging normality. Use a normality test and probability plot.",tag:"normality-assumption"},{trap:"A run-chart question treats specification limits as if they were control limits.",explanation:"Control limits come from process data (voice of the process); specification limits come from the customer. Run charts have neither statistical limit.",tag:"spec-vs-control-limits"},{trap:'"What is the FIRST thing to do with a new dataset?" with "run a hypothesis test" as an option.',explanation:"Graph the data first — check for errors, shape, outliers and time patterns before any formal test.",tag:"first"},{trap:`Quartiles computed by the "median of each half" method when the answer key uses Minitab's p(n+1) positions.`,explanation:"For 8 values 9, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 17, 20 Minitab gives Q1 = 11.25 and Q3 = 16.5, not 11.5 and 16. Follow the stated convention."},{trap:'"All of the following are uses of a box plot EXCEPT …"',explanation:"Box plots compare centre and spread across groups and flag outliers. They do not show time order or prove normality — those are the likely answers.",tag:"except"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Box plot from 11 acknowledgement times",problem:s`Minutes to acknowledge 11 priority-2 incidents: **42, 47, 51, 38, 55, 49, 61, 44, 52, 95, 46**. Find the median, Q1, Q3, IQR, the outlier limits, the whisker ends and any outliers (Minitab method).`,steps:["Sort: 38, 42, 44, 46, 47, 49, 51, 52, 55, 61, 95 (n = 11).","Median position (n + 1) ÷ 2 = 6 → **49**.","Q1 position 0.25 × 12 = 3 → **44**; Q3 position 0.75 × 12 = 9 → **55**; IQR = 55 − 44 = **11**.","Limits: 44 − 1.5 × 11 = **27.5** and 55 + 1.5 × 11 = **71.5**.","95 > 71.5 → **outlier** (asterisk). Upper whisker = largest value ≤ 71.5 = **61**; lower whisker = smallest value ≥ 27.5 = **38**.","Note the mean is 52.7 — pulled up by 95 — while the median (49) is not. That is why box plots use the median."],answer:"Median 49 · Q1 44 · Q3 55 · IQR 11 · limits 27.5 / 71.5 · whiskers 38 to 61 · outlier 95",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Upper whisker = maximum = 95",whyWrong:"The whisker stops at the furthest point inside Q3 + 1.5 × IQR (71.5). 95 is beyond it, so it is plotted separately as an outlier."}},{title:"Minitab quartiles with an even number of values",problem:s`Daily repeat-contact counts for 8 days: **12, 15, 9, 20, 14, 11, 17, 13**. Find Q1, the median and Q3 using Minitab's method.`,steps:["Sort: 9, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 17, 20 (n = 8).","Median position 4.5 → (13 + 14) ÷ 2 = **13.5**.","Q1 position 0.25 × 9 = 2.25 → 11 + 0.25 × (12 − 11) = **11.25**.","Q3 position 0.75 × 9 = 6.75 → 15 + 0.75 × (17 − 15) = **16.5**.","IQR = 16.5 − 11.25 = **5.25**."],answer:"Q1 = 11.25 · median = 13.5 · Q3 = 16.5 · IQR = 5.25",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Q1 = median of 9, 11, 12, 13 = 11.5; Q3 = median of 14, 15, 17, 20 = 16",whyWrong:'That is the "hinges" method used by some textbooks. Minitab interpolates at position p(n + 1), giving 11.25 and 16.5. Small samples make the difference visible, so follow the convention the question uses.'}},{title:"The histogram that missed the shift",problem:s`The 30 call handle times (in call order) give a histogram with 7, 13 and 9 calls in the 5–6, 6–7 and 7–8 minute bins and one call at 11.8 minutes. The overall median is 6.65 minutes. What does a run chart add?`,steps:["Plot the values in call order and draw the median line at 6.65.","Calls 1–15: mean **6.00**, median 6.0. Calls 16–30: mean **7.49**, median 7.2 (7.18 mean without the 11.8 call).","Calls 5–15 are all below the median (11 in a row); calls 19–30 are all above (12 in a row) — far too long to be chance alternation.","Conclusion: a **shift** of roughly 1.2–1.5 minutes around call 16, plus one spike (call 23). Look for what changed then."],answer:"A sustained upward shift after call 15 that no distribution graph can show",wrongApproach:{calculation:'"The histogram is one mound, so the process is stable."',whyWrong:"A histogram pools all values regardless of when they occurred. Stability is a time question; it needs a run chart or control chart."}},{title:"Scatter plot: strong association, unproven cause",problem:s`Ten weeks of data: start-of-week backlog (40–140 tickets) vs weekly MTTR (3.1–5.8 h). The points rise from lower-left to upper-right and Minitab reports **r = 0.97**. What can the team conclude?`,steps:["Direction: positive — higher backlog weeks have higher MTTR.","Strength: r = 0.97 (r² ≈ 0.94) — a very strong linear association.","Form: roughly straight; no obvious outliers or clusters.","Conclusion: backlog is a strong **candidate X** worth investigating — but a third factor (an outage period, staff absence) could raise both. Confirm before acting."],answer:"Strong positive linear relationship; not proof that backlog causes long MTTR",wrongApproach:{calculation:'"r = 0.97, so reducing backlog will cut MTTR by 97%."',whyWrong:"r measures the strength of association, not the size of an effect or a cause. Effect size comes from regression (4.1.2), and cause needs confirmation."}},{title:"Choosing the right graph",problem:s`For each question, pick the best first graph: (a) Do four resolver groups differ in MTTR? (b) Has first-time-fix % drifted over 12 months? (c) Which complaint categories make up most of the volume? (d) Is fibre installation time related to cable distance?`,steps:["(a) Continuous Y by category → **box plots side by side** (add an individual value plot if groups are small).","(b) Y in time order → **run chart / time series plot**.","(c) Counts by category → **Pareto chart**.","(d) Two paired continuous variables → **scatter plot**."],answer:"(a) box plots · (b) run chart · (c) Pareto chart · (d) scatter plot",wrongApproach:{calculation:"(b) histogram of the 12 monthly values",whyWrong:'A histogram loses the order of the months, which is exactly what "drifted" asks about.'}}],lab:"graphs",check:[{id:"chk-2.2.4-1",topic:"2.2.4",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"A team wants to see whether ticket resolution time changed after a new routing rule went live mid-month. Which graph is BEST?",options:["Histogram","Box plot of all tickets","Run chart (time series plot)","Pareto chart"],answer:2,explanation:"The question is about change over time, so the data must be plotted in time order — a run chart / time series plot.",whyWrong:["A histogram pools all tickets and loses time order.","One box plot of all tickets also loses time order (box plots before vs after could help, but the run chart shows when it changed).","","A Pareto chart ranks categories; resolution time is continuous."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-2.2.4-2",topic:"2.2.4",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A box plot of incident acknowledgement times has **Q1 = 44** and **Q3 = 55** minutes. Above what value would a point be plotted as an outlier (asterisk)?",options:["60.5","71.5","82.0","66.0"],answer:1,explanation:"IQR = 55 − 44 = 11. Upper limit = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 55 + 16.5 = 71.5.",whyWrong:["60.5 adds only 0.5 × IQR.","","82.0 adds 1.5 × IQR to Q3 twice over (55 + 27).","66.0 adds 1 × IQR instead of 1.5 × IQR."]},{id:"chk-2.2.4-3",topic:"2.2.4",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Two shifts' handle times are pooled. The box plot looks symmetric, but the analyst suspects two clusters. Which graph would BEST reveal them?",options:["A box plot with a narrower box","An individual value plot (or dot plot) of the points","A Pareto chart of handle times","A control chart with specification limits"],answer:1,explanation:"Box plots summarise quartiles and can look symmetric for bimodal data. Plotting the individual values reveals the two clusters.",whyWrong:["Changing box width does not change what a box plot summarises.","","Pareto charts are for categorical counts.","Control charts use control limits, not specification limits, and answer a time-stability question."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-2.2.4-4",topic:"2.2.4",category:"scenario",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"A scatter plot of weekly backlog vs MTTR shows a tight upward pattern with r = 0.97. Which statement is MOST appropriate?",options:["Backlog causes MTTR to rise; reducing backlog will fix MTTR.","There is a strong positive association; backlog is a candidate X to investigate further.","There is no relationship because r is not exactly 1.","MTTR causes the backlog to increase."],answer:1,explanation:"A high r shows strong linear association, not cause. It makes backlog a strong candidate X to confirm in Analyze.",whyWrong:["Correlation does not prove causation; a lurking variable may drive both.","","r = 0.97 is a very strong relationship; perfection is not required.","The direction of cause cannot be read from a scatter plot either way."],traps:["correlation-causation","most"]},{id:"chk-2.2.4-5",topic:"2.2.4",category:"chart",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A histogram of fibre installation times shows **two distinct peaks**. What is the BEST next step?",options:["Calculate Cpk using the overall mean and standard deviation.","Stratify the data (e.g. by crew, region or installation type) to find the two populations.","Remove the smaller peak as outliers.","Increase the number of bins until one peak remains."],answer:1,explanation:"A bimodal shape usually means two populations mixed together. Stratifying by a likely X separates them and points to a cause.",whyWrong:["Capability on mixed, non-normal data is misleading.","","A whole cluster is not a set of outliers; deleting it hides the real issue.","Changing bins to hide a pattern is manipulating the picture, not analysing it."],traps:["best","normality-assumption"]},{id:"chk-2.2.4-6",topic:"2.2.4",category:"compare",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"What does a control chart have that a run chart does NOT?",options:["Data plotted in time order","A centre line","Statistically calculated control limits","A line joining the points"],answer:2,explanation:"Both plot data in time order with a centre line; a control chart adds upper and lower control limits calculated from the process data, with rules for special causes.",whyWrong:["Both charts plot data in time order.","Run charts usually have a median centre line.","","Both usually join the points."],traps:["not","spec-vs-control-limits"]}],takeaways:["Graph before you calculate: check data quality, assumptions and patterns, and stratify by time, location and people.","Histograms show shape, centre and spread; name the shape (normal, skewed, bimodal, plateau, truncated) and remember bin width and small n can mislead.","Box plot: box Q1–Q3 (Minitab positions p(n + 1)), median line, whiskers to the furthest points within 1.5 × IQR, asterisks beyond — ideal for comparing groups.","Dot plots and individual value plots keep every point, revealing granularity and bimodality that box plots hide.","Only time-ordered plots show shifts, trends and cycles; our 30 calls shifted from about 6.0 to 7.5 minutes after call 15.","A control chart = run chart + calculated control limits and special-cause rules; never substitute specification limits.","Scatter plots show direction, strength, form and outliers — correlation is association, not causation.","Pick the graph from the question and data type: distribution → histogram/dot plot; groups → box plot; time → run chart; relationship → scatter; categories → Pareto."],flashcards:[{front:"Why graph before calculating?",back:"To check data quality, assumptions (shape, stability) and spot patterns or candidate X's that summary statistics hide."},{front:"Histogram",back:"Frequencies over equal-width bins of a continuous variable; shows shape, centre, spread."},{front:"Bimodal histogram usually means…",back:"Two populations mixed together — stratify (shift, vendor, region, type)."},{front:"Box plot outlier limits",back:"Q1 − 1.5 × IQR and Q3 + 1.5 × IQR; points beyond are asterisks."},{front:"Where do box-plot whiskers end?",back:"At the furthest data values still inside the 1.5 × IQR limits — not necessarily at the min and max."},{front:"Minitab quartile position",back:"p(n + 1) in sorted data, interpolating: Q1 at 0.25(n + 1), Q3 at 0.75(n + 1)."},{front:"What a box plot hides",back:"Number of points, gaps, bimodality and time order."},{front:"Dot plot strength",back:"Shows every value — reveals granularity (coarse resolution) and isolated points."},{front:"Run chart patterns",back:"Shifts, trends, cycles and spikes over time."},{front:"Run chart vs control chart",back:"Control chart adds a mean centre line, calculated control limits (≈ ±3σ) and special-cause rules."},{front:"Reading a scatter plot",back:"Direction, strength, form, unusual points — and correlation ≠ causation."},{front:"Best graph for counts by category",back:"Pareto chart (descending bars + cumulative % line)."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["All statistics for the running dataset, the box-plot examples, the scatter-plot correlation and the Pareto percentages were computed with the app's verified statistics engine (Minitab quartile method).","The √n starting point for histogram bins and the simple run rules for run charts (long runs on one side of the median) are general practice added for this app; the book discusses histograms, time series plots and Lowess smoothing but does not give numeric run rules.","Scatter plots are listed in the book's graphing overview (p. 159) but explained in the Improve chapter on correlation; this lesson treats them as a Measure screening tool and defers r, R² and regression to 4.1.","The handle-time, acknowledgement, backlog/MTTR and repeat-contact datasets, and the vendor-report scenario, are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:'Measure topic 2.2.4 "Graphical Analysis" (2.2 Six Sigma Statistics).'},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 159",pdfPage:62,note:"Purpose of graphing; graphs covered (box, scatter, dot, time series, histogram); data sources and stratification by time, location and operator."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 160",pdfPage:63,note:"Characteristics of a good graph; the histogram as equal intervals with bar heights = frequencies; shape, centring and spread."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 161",pdfPage:64,note:"Histogram caveat: small samples (20 points) can look non-normal; do not judge normality from a histogram alone."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 162",pdfPage:65,note:"Dot plot reveals granularity and individual points to investigate; box plots summarise shape, dispersion and centre and spot outliers."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 163",pdfPage:66,note:"Box plot anatomy: quartiles, median, whiskers, outlier limits Q1 − 1.5(Q3 − Q1) and Q3 + 1.5(Q3 − Q1), asterisks; reading symmetry."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 164",pdfPage:67,note:"Comparing three people's set-up times with box plots, and a caution against conclusions from box plots alone."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 165–167",pdfPage:68,note:"Individual value plots; bimodal data can give a symmetric box plot; jitter to separate identical points."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 167–169",pdfPage:70,note:"Time series plots reveal patterns over time (dynamic response, wave pattern) and Lowess smoothing."},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 431",pdfPage:34,note:"Scatter plot with Lowess smoother used to explore correlation (Improve context)."},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 446",pdfPage:49,note:"Correlation explores association; correlation and regression do not imply a causal relationship."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 584",pdfPage:87,note:"Glossary: a control chart consists of a run chart with statistically determined control limits and a centre line."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 586",pdfPage:89,note:"Glossary: histogram — bar chart of frequencies of numerical categories."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 587",pdfPage:90,note:"Glossary: Pareto chart — categories in descending order of frequency."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 588",pdfPage:91,note:"Glossary: run chart (performance over time) and scatter plot (one variable against another)."}]},X=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:S},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),h=String.raw,T={id:"2.3.1",module:7,title:"Precision & Accuracy",subtitle:"Is the gauge right on average — and does it repeat itself?",minutes:13,objectives:["Explain why a measurement system analysis must come before any baseline, capability study or hypothesis test.","Use the variance relationship σ²observed = σ²process + σ²measurement to separate true process variation from measurement error.","Distinguish accuracy (location: bias, linearity, stability) from precision (spread: repeatability, reproducibility) using the target analogy.","Define repeatability and reproducibility and recognise each in a service or manufacturing scenario.","Apply the 10% (ten-bucket) rule to judge whether a gauge’s resolution is adequate.","Identify typical sources of measurement error in service data: timestamps, ticket categorisation and survey scales."],overview:h`Every Six Sigma decision rests on data, and every data point was produced by a **measurement system** — a gauge, a database query, a stopwatch, a person reading a screen. If that system is noisy or skewed, the baseline you put in the charter is wrong, the capability you report is wrong, and the hypothesis test you run in Analyze may "prove" a cause that is really just measurement noise. That is why the training book calls [[msa|Measurement System Analysis]] a non-negotiable step in the Measure phase.

This lesson builds the two ideas the rest of the module depends on. **Accuracy** asks whether the measurements are centred on the truth. **Precision** asks whether repeated measurements agree with each other. We will separate the variation you *observe* into the part that really belongs to the process and the part the measurement system adds, look at resolution, and translate it all into the service data a telecom operations team uses every day.`,sections:[{id:"why-msa-first",heading:"Why MSA comes before the baseline",body:h`When you plot a month of fault-restoration times you see variation. Some of it is real: different faults, different engineers, different sites. Some of it is not: an engineer who closes the ticket an hour after service came back, a monitoring tool whose clock drifts, two analysts who code the same outage differently. The book defines **measurement error** as the combined effect of every source of measurement variability that makes an observed value differ from the true value.

Crucially, the *measurement system* is more than the instrument. It is the whole process that produces a number: the **item** being measured (a part, a document, a customer call), the **operator** (a person, or one of several instruments), the **equipment**, the **procedure**, the **environment** and the **reference** standard used for calibration. Each is a possible source of error, and MSA evaluates all of them together — not just whether the gauge was calibrated.

The practical rule is simple: **validate the measurement system before you trust the data.** Run MSA on the primary and secondary metrics, on key X's, before hypothesis testing or modelling, and before and after process changes. If you skip it, a Green Belt can spend weeks analysing a pattern that lives entirely inside the measuring process.`},{id:"variance-decomposition",heading:"Observed variation = process variation + measurement variation",body:h`The book stresses that the components of variation are **statistically additive** — but it is the *variances* that add, not the standard deviations:

$$\sigma^2_{\text{observed}}=\sigma^2_{\text{process}}+\sigma^2_{\text{measurement}}$$

and measurement variance itself splits into precision components:

$$\sigma^2_{\text{measurement}}=\sigma^2_{\text{repeatability}}+\sigma^2_{\text{reproducibility}}$$

**Worked illustration.** Restoration times show an observed [[standard-deviation|standard deviation]] of **5 min**. A study finds repeatability σ = 2.4 min and reproducibility σ = 1.8 min. Measurement variance = 2.4² + 1.8² = 5.76 + 3.24 = **9**, so σ_measurement = **3 min**. Process variance = 25 − 9 = **16**, so the true process σ is **4 min**. Measurement noise accounts for 9 ÷ 25 = **36%** of the observed variance (σ ratio 3 ÷ 5 = 60%) — numbers you will meet again as % contribution and % study variation in [Gage R&R (2.3.3)](#/lessons/2.3.3).

Two consequences follow. First, a noisy gauge makes the process look **worse** than it is, so capability is understated. Second, a noisy gauge **hides improvement**: if the team halves the true σ from 4 to 2 min, the observed σ only falls from 5 to √(4 + 9) ≈ 3.61 min — a 28% change that may not even show up as significant.

> **Exam-tools tip.** On the virtual calculator, square first, add or subtract the *variances*, then take the square root last. Write each intermediate value on the notepad — the most common slip is subtracting standard deviations (5 − 3 = 2).`},{id:"accuracy-vs-precision",heading:"Accuracy vs precision: the target analogy",body:h`Picture the measurement system as a shooter and the true value as the bull's-eye. Measure the same item many times and look at the pattern.

- **[[accuracy|Accuracy]]** is about **location**: how close the *average* of repeated measurements is to the reference value. A system that consistently over- or under-states the value is inaccurate. The book groups three location problems under accuracy: **[[bias|bias]]** (a fixed offset from the true value), **[[linearity|linearity]]** (bias that changes across the operating range) and **[[stability|stability]]** (bias that changes over time).
- **[[precision|Precision]]** is about **spread**: how closely repeated measurements agree with one another, quantified like any variation by the standard deviation. Precision splits into **repeatability** and **reproducibility**.

The four combinations: *accurate and precise* (tight group on the bull's-eye) — the goal; *accurate but not precise* (centred on average, widely scattered); *precise but not accurate* (tight group, but offset — classic bias); *neither*. A tight group is not proof of correctness, and an average on target is not proof that individual readings can be trusted. You need both, and they are fixed in different ways: bias is usually addressed by calibration, poor precision by better equipment, procedures or training.

| Question | Accuracy (location) | Precision (spread) |
|---|---|---|
| What it compares | Average of readings vs reference value | Readings vs each other |
| Statistic | Mean − reference | Standard deviation |
| Components | Bias, linearity, stability | Repeatability, reproducibility |
| Typical fix | Calibrate, maintain, correct the offset | Better gauge, clearer procedure, training |`},{id:"repeatability-reproducibility",heading:"The two faces of precision: repeatability and reproducibility",body:h`**[[repeatability|Repeatability]]** is the variation when **one** appraiser uses **one** instrument to measure the **same** characteristic on the **same** item several times. It is the inherent noise of the equipment and method — sometimes called equipment variation. Transactional example: one auditor re-checks the same contract on different days and records a different number of errors.

**[[reproducibility|Reproducibility]]** is the variation in the **averages** obtained by **different** appraisers (or different instruments, sites or shifts) measuring the same characteristic on the same items — sometimes called appraiser variation. Transactional example: three service-desk analysts read the same incident and assign different categories.

A memory hook: *repeat yourself* (same person, again) versus *reproduce someone else's result* (different person, same item). Together they form the measurement-system variation that a [[gage-rr|Gage R&R]] study estimates — the full calculation is the subject of [lesson 2.3.3](#/lessons/2.3.3). If repeatability dominates, look at the gauge or the method; if reproducibility dominates, look at training and operational definitions.`},{id:"resolution",heading:"Resolution and the 10% (ten-bucket) rule",body:h`**[[resolution|Resolution]]** (also called discrimination) is the smallest change a measurement system can detect and display. The book lists it among the sources of measurement error — "how detailed is the information" — and warns that readings which are *always identical* may be a red flag: the scale may not go fine enough to show any variation. You would not measure a spark-plug gap with a football field.

A widely used guideline is the **10% rule**, also called the **ten-bucket rule**: the resolution should be no coarser than one-tenth of the tolerance (USL − LSL) **or** of the process spread (6σ), whichever the decision depends on. Put differently, the gauge should be able to split the range of interest into at least ten "buckets".

**Example.** A P2 incident must receive a response within 30 minutes. If engineers type response times rounded to the nearest 15 minutes, the gauge has only 30 ÷ 15 = **2 buckets** across the target — useless for spotting improvement. If the true spread of response times is σ = 2 min, 6σ = 12 min and the ten-bucket rule asks for a resolution of 12 ÷ 10 = **1.2 min** or finer; system-generated timestamps to the minute (or second) pass.

Poor resolution also distorts statistics: data that pile up on a few values look "chunky", often fail a [[normality-test|normality test]] and understate the true variation. The book notes exactly this pattern when it discusses data that look normal but are not continuous.`},{id:"service-data",heading:"Measurement error in service and telecom data",body:h`Transactional processes rarely use calipers, but they are full of measurement systems — and the book notes that transactional projects typically rely on *attribute* measurement. Common traps in a Digital Service Operations setting:

- **Timestamps.** Manual "service restored" entries, clocks that are not synchronised between monitoring and ticketing tools, time-zone conversions, and "stop-the-clock" rules applied inconsistently. These create bias (all times late) and poor precision (random lags).
- **Ticket categorisation.** Category, priority and root-cause codes are human judgements. Two analysts may classify the same incident differently (reproducibility); one analyst may classify it differently next week (repeatability). These are assessed with attribute agreement analysis ([lesson 2.3.4](#/lessons/2.3.4)).
- **Survey scales.** A 1–5 satisfaction score is [[ordinal|ordinal]] data; wording, scale direction or a change of survey vendor can shift answers without any change in service.
- **Database queries.** The book's advice for transactional data is to reconcile the query against a known reference point — for example, totals that must agree with a finance report — before investing time in analysis.

The question to ask of every metric is the same: *if the process stood still, would this number stand still?*`}],whyItMatters:h`Capability (Cp, Cpk), control limits, hypothesis tests and regression all inherit whatever error sits in the data. Measurement noise inflates the apparent spread, so capability is understated and genuine improvements can disappear into the noise; bias moves the apparent mean, so a process can look compliant with an [[sla|SLA]] when it is not, or vice versa. Getting accuracy and precision right first is the cheapest insurance in the whole DMAIC cycle — and on the exam, accuracy-versus-precision and repeatability-versus-reproducibility are among the most frequently confused pairs.`,businessExample:{title:"Validating the MTTR baseline before an SLA review",body:h`A DSO team is preparing a baseline for mean time to restore (MTTR) on fibre faults, reported monthly by a managed-services vendor. Before using the figure, the team re-derives restoration time for 40 closed tickets from network-monitoring alarm-clear events and compares it with the restoration time recorded in the ticketing tool.

- On average the ticket times are **later** than the alarm-clear times — a possible **bias**, suggesting that "restored" may be logged when the ticket is updated rather than when service returns.
- The gap varies widely from engineer to engineer — a possible **reproducibility** issue linked to differing local practice.
- Some ticket times are rounded to the quarter-hour — a **resolution** concern.

In the vendor-facing report this is framed as a request rather than a verdict: *based on the comparison with alarm data, the recorded restoration times could not be fully reconciled with the monitoring timestamps; the team is asked to clarify the timestamp rules applied and provide supporting evidence for the sample tickets*. The baseline is set only after the timestamp definition is agreed and a re-check confirms the measurement error is small relative to the process variation.`},visual:"accuracyPrecisionTarget",visualCaption:"Four targets fill one at a time: accurate and precise, accurate only, precise only (the offset is bias), and neither. Then the observed variance of fault-restoration time (25) splits into process variance (16) and measurement variance (9): variances add, standard deviations do not.",definitions:[{term:"Measurement System Analysis (MSA)",definition:"A statistical evaluation of how much variation the act of measuring adds to the data, and where it comes from."},{term:"Measurement system",definition:"The complete process that produces a measurement: item, operator, equipment, procedure, environment and reference standard."},{term:"Measurement error",definition:"The combined effect of all sources of measurement variability that make an observed value differ from the true value."},{term:"Accuracy",definition:"Closeness of the average of repeated measurements to a reference (true) value — a location property. Includes bias, linearity and stability."},{term:"Precision",definition:"Closeness of repeated measurements to each other — a spread property, measured by the standard deviation. Includes repeatability and reproducibility."},{term:"Bias",definition:"Observed average minus reference value; a systematic offset."},{term:"Repeatability",definition:"Variation when one appraiser measures the same item with the same instrument several times (equipment variation)."},{term:"Reproducibility",definition:"Variation between the averages of different appraisers (or instruments) measuring the same items (appraiser variation)."},{term:"Resolution (discrimination)",definition:"The smallest change the measurement system can detect and display."},{term:"10% / ten-bucket rule",definition:"Guideline that resolution should be no coarser than one-tenth of the tolerance or process spread, giving at least ten divisions."},{term:"Reference value",definition:"An accepted true value for a standard, used to judge accuracy."}],comparisons:[{title:"Accuracy vs precision",headers:["","Accuracy","Precision"],rows:[["Property","Location (where the average sits)","Spread (how much readings scatter)"],["Target picture","Group centred on the bull’s-eye","Shots tightly grouped, wherever they land"],["Components","Bias, linearity, stability","Repeatability, reproducibility"],["Statistic","Average − reference value","Standard deviation of repeated readings"],["Needs a reference standard?","Yes","No — only repeated measurements"],["Typical fix","Calibration, maintenance, offset correction","Better gauge, clearer procedure, training"]]},{title:"Repeatability vs reproducibility",headers:["","Repeatability","Reproducibility"],rows:[["Who measures","One appraiser, again and again","Several appraisers (or instruments)"],["Same item & instrument?","Yes","Same item, same type of instrument"],["Also called","Equipment variation (EV)","Appraiser variation (AV)"],["Service example","One analyst re-codes the same ticket differently next week","Three analysts code the same ticket three different ways"],["Usual remedy","Repair/replace gauge, tighten method","Training, operational definitions, standard procedure"]]},{title:"Process variation vs measurement variation",headers:["","Process (part-to-part) variation","Measurement variation"],rows:[["Source","Real differences between units","The act of measuring"],["Restoration-time example","σ = 4 min (variance 16)","σ = 3 min (variance 9)"],["What we want","To understand and reduce it","To make it small relative to the process"],["How they combine","Variances add: 16 + 9 = 25","Standard deviations do not: 4 + 3 ≠ 5"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"If the gauge has been calibrated, the measurement system is fine.",reality:"Calibration addresses bias at a point in time. MSA evaluates the whole system — operators, procedure, environment, precision and stability as well."},{myth:"Accuracy and precision mean the same thing.",reality:"Accuracy is about the average versus the truth (location); precision is about agreement between repeated readings (spread). A system can have one without the other."},{myth:"Measurement error only affects manufacturing gauges.",reality:"Timestamps, ticket categories, survey scores and database queries are all measurement systems, often with large errors."},{myth:"Standard deviations of process and measurement add up to the observed standard deviation.",reality:"Variances add. With σ_process = 4 and σ_measurement = 3, the observed σ is √25 = 5, not 7."},{myth:"Identical repeated readings prove a perfect gauge.",reality:"They may reveal inadequate resolution: the scale is too coarse to show the real variation."}],examTraps:[{trap:"A stem describes readings that are tightly clustered but consistently 2 units above the reference, and asks what the gauge lacks.",explanation:"It is precise but not accurate — it has a bias problem (location), not a precision problem.",tag:"accuracy-vs-precision"},{trap:"“Different inspectors measuring the same parts get different averages.” Options include repeatability.",explanation:"Different appraisers → reproducibility. Repeatability is one appraiser repeating the measurement.",tag:"repeatability-vs-reproducibility"},{trap:"“Which is a component of precision?” with linearity among the options.",explanation:"Linearity, bias and stability are accuracy (location) components. Precision = repeatability + reproducibility.",tag:"accuracy-vs-precision"},{trap:"Observed σ and measurement σ are given; options include their simple difference.",explanation:"Subtract variances, then take the square root: √(13² − 5²) = 12, not 13 − 5 = 8."},{trap:"“What should be done FIRST before establishing the baseline capability?”",explanation:"Validate the measurement system (MSA). Capability, hypothesis tests and control charts come after.",tag:"first"},{trap:"A stem asks for the resolution needed with a tolerance of 20 minutes.",explanation:"Ten-bucket rule: 20 ÷ 10 = 2 minutes or finer. Do not divide by 6 or by 100."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Separating process and measurement variation",problem:h`Fault-restoration times show an observed standard deviation of **5 min**. An MSA estimates repeatability σ = **2.4 min** and reproducibility σ = **1.8 min**. What is the measurement σ, the true process σ, and the share of observed variance due to measurement?`,steps:["Square the precision components: 2.4² = 5.76 and 1.8² = 3.24.","Measurement variance = 5.76 + 3.24 = 9 → σ_measurement = √9 = **3 min**.","Observed variance = 5² = 25. Process variance = 25 − 9 = 16 → σ_process = **4 min**.","Share of observed variance from measurement = 9 ÷ 25 = **36%** (σ ratio 3 ÷ 5 = 60%).","Exam-tools tip: keep variances on the notepad and take square roots only at the end."],answer:"σ_measurement = 3 min · σ_process = 4 min · 36% of the observed variance is measurement error",wrongApproach:{calculation:"σ_process = 5 − (2.4 + 1.8) = 0.8 min",whyWrong:"Standard deviations do not add or subtract. Only variances are additive: 25 − 9 = 16, so σ_process = 4."}},{title:"How a noisy gauge hides a real improvement",problem:h`In the previous example the team redesigns the dispatch process and halves the true process σ from **4 min** to **2 min**. The measurement system is unchanged (σ = 3 min). What observed σ will the dashboard show?`,steps:["New process variance = 2² = 4; measurement variance still 9.","Observed variance = 4 + 9 = 13 → observed σ = √13 ≈ **3.61 min**.","Observed change = (5 − 3.61) ÷ 5 ≈ **28%**, although the true improvement is **50%**.","Conclusion: fix the measurement system first, or the improvement may look insignificant."],answer:"Observed σ ≈ 3.61 min — a 28% drop on the dashboard for a 50% real improvement",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Observed σ = 2 + 3 = 5 min, so no change is visible at all",whyWrong:"Adding standard deviations again. Variances add: 4 + 9 = 13, giving 3.61 min."}},{title:"Is the timestamp resolution good enough?",problem:h`P2 incidents must receive a response within **30 min**. Current response times have σ ≈ **2 min**. Response times are typed in manually and rounded to the nearest **15 min**. Apply the ten-bucket rule.`,steps:["Against the target window: 30 ÷ 15 = **2 buckets** — far fewer than 10.","Against the process spread: 6σ = 6 × 2 = 12 min; required resolution = 12 ÷ 10 = **1.2 min** or finer.","15-minute rounding fails both tests; system-generated timestamps to the minute (or second) pass.","Action: use the tool’s automatic timestamps for the baseline and remove manual rounding."],answer:"Inadequate — resolution must be about 1 minute or finer",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“15 minutes is smaller than the 30-minute target, so it is fine.”",whyWrong:"Resolution must divide the range of interest into at least ten parts. Two buckets cannot show variation or improvement."}},{title:"Classifying gauge behaviour",problem:h`A latency probe is tested against a network emulator that adds exactly 50.0 ms. Probe A reads 50.1, 49.9, 50.0, 50.1, 49.9. Probe B reads 52.0, 52.1, 51.9, 52.0, 52.0. Probe C reads 47.5, 52.8, 49.0, 51.6, 49.1. Classify each.`,steps:["Probe A: mean 50.0 (on target), tiny spread → **accurate and precise**.","Probe B: mean 52.0 (+2.0 ms offset), tiny spread → **precise but not accurate** (bias).","Probe C: mean 50.0 (on target), wide spread → **accurate but not precise**.","Fix B with calibration/offset correction; fix C with a better instrument or method."],answer:"A: accurate & precise · B: precise, biased · C: accurate, imprecise",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“Probe B is the best because its readings agree most closely.”",whyWrong:"Close agreement is precision only. Every B reading is about 2 ms wrong, so it is not accurate."}}],lab:"accuracyPrecision",check:[{id:"chk-2.3.1-1",topic:"2.3.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"A call-duration logger is checked against a reference recording of exactly 300 s. Ten readings are all between 311.8 s and 312.2 s. The logger is best described as:",options:["Accurate and precise","Precise but not accurate","Accurate but not precise","Neither accurate nor precise"],answer:1,explanation:"The readings agree closely (precise) but their average is about 12 s above the reference (biased, so not accurate).",whyWrong:["The average is 12 s off the reference, so it is not accurate.","","The spread is tiny, so it is precise; the problem is location.","The tight spread shows good precision."],traps:["accuracy-vs-precision"]},{id:"chk-2.3.1-2",topic:"2.3.1",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"The observed standard deviation of ticket handling time is **13 min**. The measurement system’s standard deviation is **5 min**. What is the standard deviation of the true process?",options:["8 min","12 min","13.9 min","2.6 min"],answer:1,explanation:"Variances add: σ²process = 13² − 5² = 169 − 25 = 144, so σ_process = 12 min.",whyWrong:["13 − 5 subtracts standard deviations, which are not additive.","","√(13² + 5²) adds the measurement variance instead of removing it.","13 ÷ 5 has no meaning here."]},{id:"chk-2.3.1-3",topic:"2.3.1",category:"definition",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"One quality analyst re-scores the same recorded call on three different days and gives three different scores. Which component of measurement error does this show?",options:["Reproducibility","Bias","Repeatability","Linearity"],answer:2,explanation:"Same appraiser, same item, repeated measurement → repeatability.",whyWrong:["Reproducibility needs different appraisers.","Bias needs a reference value; this is scatter, not offset.","","Linearity is a change in bias across the measurement range."],traps:["repeatability-vs-reproducibility"]},{id:"chk-2.3.1-4",topic:"2.3.1",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A provisioning time specification is 40 to 60 minutes. Using the 10% (ten-bucket) rule, what is the coarsest acceptable resolution of the timing system?",options:["0.2 min","10 min","3.3 min","2 min"],answer:3,explanation:"Tolerance = 60 − 40 = 20 min; one-tenth of 20 = 2 min.",whyWrong:["0.2 min is one-hundredth of the tolerance — finer than required.","10 min gives only two buckets.","3.3 min divides the tolerance by 6, confusing it with the 6σ spread.",""]},{id:"chk-2.3.1-5",topic:"2.3.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Which of the following is a component of **precision**?",options:["Linearity","Stability","Bias","Reproducibility"],answer:3,explanation:"Precision (spread) = repeatability + reproducibility. Bias, linearity and stability are accuracy (location) components.",whyWrong:["Linearity is an accuracy component.","Stability is an accuracy component (bias over time).","Bias is the core accuracy component.",""],traps:["accuracy-vs-precision"]},{id:"chk-2.3.1-6",topic:"2.3.1",category:"dmaic-boundary",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A Green Belt wants to calculate baseline Cpk for incident response time. What should be done FIRST?",options:["Run a hypothesis test on response time by shift","Validate the measurement system for response time","Set control limits on an I-MR chart","Brainstorm root causes on a fishbone"],answer:1,explanation:"Capability, control charts and tests all inherit measurement error, so the measurement system is validated first.",whyWrong:["Hypothesis tests come later and also depend on trustworthy data.","","Control limits also rely on valid measurement.","Root-cause brainstorming does not make the data trustworthy."],traps:["first"]}],takeaways:["MSA comes before the baseline: every later statistic inherits measurement error.","The measurement system is the whole process — item, operator, equipment, procedure, environment and reference — not just the gauge.","σ²observed = σ²process + σ²measurement: variances add, standard deviations do not.","Accuracy = location (bias, linearity, stability); precision = spread (repeatability, reproducibility).","Repeatability: same appraiser repeating. Reproducibility: different appraisers on the same items.","A noisy gauge makes the process look worse and hides real improvement.","Resolution should be at most one-tenth of the tolerance or process spread (ten-bucket rule).","Service data — timestamps, ticket categories, survey scores, queries — are measurement systems too."],flashcards:[{front:"Accuracy",back:"How close the average of repeated measurements is to the reference value (location)."},{front:"Precision",back:"How close repeated measurements are to each other (spread), measured by the standard deviation."},{front:"Components of accuracy",back:"Bias, linearity, stability."},{front:"Components of precision",back:"Repeatability and reproducibility (together = Gage R&R)."},{front:"Repeatability",back:"One appraiser, one instrument, same item, measured repeatedly."},{front:"Reproducibility",back:"Different appraisers (or instruments) measuring the same items — variation in their averages."},{front:"Variance relationship",back:"σ²observed = σ²process + σ²measurement."},{front:"Observed σ 5, measurement σ 3",back:"Process σ = √(25 − 9) = 4."},{front:"Precise but not accurate",back:"Tight cluster offset from the bull’s-eye: a bias problem."},{front:"Ten-bucket (10%) rule",back:"Resolution ≤ one-tenth of the tolerance or process spread."},{front:"Elements of a measurement system",back:"Item, operator, equipment, procedure, environment, reference standard."},{front:"Why MSA before baseline?",back:"So the baseline, capability and tests reflect the process, not measurement error."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The 10% / ten-bucket resolution rule is a widely used industry guideline (AIAG MSA practice) and is not stated in the supplied book pages; the book lists resolution as a source of measurement error and warns about scales that do not go fine enough.","The labels “equipment variation” and “appraiser variation” are common MSA terminology added for clarity; the book defines repeatability and reproducibility without those labels.","Telecom examples (restoration times, response timestamps, latency probes, call loggers) and all figures are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data.","The variance decomposition figures were computed with the app’s verified grrMetrics function."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Measure topic 2.3.1 “Precision & Accuracy”."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 172",pdfPage:75,note:"MSA as a non-negotiable step; definition of measurement error."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 173",pdfPage:76,note:"Measurement system = procedures, gages and personnel; accuracy, linearity and stability affect location; precision is variability; repeatability and reproducibility defined; MSA is the entire system, not just calibration."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 174",pdfPage:77,note:"Purpose of MSA: precision (repeatability, reproducibility) vs accuracy (stability, linearity, resolution, bias)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 175",pdfPage:78,note:"Accuracy and precision explained with the target analogy."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 176",pdfPage:79,note:"Measurement variation is additive to true variation; components of variation are statistically additive."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 178",pdfPage:81,note:"What and when to measure; components of variation tree (unit-to-unit vs measurement error; precision vs accuracy)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 179–180",pdfPage:82,note:"Precision, repeatability and reproducibility with manufacturing and transactional examples; resolution warning."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 181",pdfPage:84,note:"Accuracy vs a known standard; transactional queries reconciled to reference points such as financial reports."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 182",pdfPage:85,note:"Accuracy vs precision summary (accurate, precise, both, neither)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 184",pdfPage:87,note:"Transactional projects typically use attribute-based measurement systems."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 264",pdfPage:67,note:"Insufficient measurement resolution makes data look non-normal / not continuous."}]},j=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:T},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),p=String.raw,C={id:"2.3.2",module:7,title:"Bias, Linearity & Stability",subtitle:"Three ways a measurement system drifts from the truth",minutes:13,objectives:["Define bias as observed average minus reference value and calculate it from repeated readings of a standard.","Use a one-sample t-test idea to decide whether an observed bias is statistically different from zero.","Explain linearity as a change in bias across the operating range and interpret the slope of bias versus reference value, including % linearity.","Explain stability as a change in bias (or spread) over time and track it with a control chart of a reference standard.","Distinguish calibration from measurement system analysis.","Recognise bias, linearity and stability problems in telecom and service-operations measurement systems."],overview:p`In [lesson 2.3.1](#/lessons/2.3.1) we split measurement error into **precision** (spread) and **accuracy** (location). This lesson opens the accuracy box. The training book places three location problems inside it: **bias** — the measurement is off by an offset; **linearity** — the offset is different at different sizes; and **stability** — the offset changes over time.

All three need the same ingredient: a **reference value** you can trust — a master part, a calibrated test signal, a synthetic transaction of known duration, or a reconciled financial total. We will calculate bias and test it, fit a line to see whether bias grows across the range, chart a standard over time to catch drift, and finish by separating calibration (a maintenance activity) from MSA (a study of the whole measurement system).`,sections:[{id:"accuracy-family",heading:"The accuracy family at a glance",body:p`Think of a single question asked three ways: *Is the average reading equal to the true value?*

- **[[bias|Bias]]** asks it **once**, at one reference value, now.
- **[[linearity|Linearity]]** asks it **across the range** — at small, medium and large reference values.
- **[[stability|Stability]]** asks it **over time** — today, next week, next month.

The book also distinguishes **constant bias** (the same offset everywhere — a bathroom scale that reads 3 lb with nothing on it) from **variable bias**, typical of electronic equipment, where the offset changes with the setting level. Variable bias across the range is exactly what a linearity study detects.

| Question | Bias | Linearity | Stability |
|---|---|---|---|
| Dimension | One point, now | Across the operating range | Over time |
| Data needed | Repeated readings of one standard | Repeated readings of several standards spanning the range | Periodic readings of one standard |
| Analysis | Mean − reference; 1-sample t | Regression of bias on reference value | Control chart of the standard |
| Typical cause | Mis-set zero, wrong offset | Wear, non-linear sensor, rule that breaks for large values | Wear, environment, clock drift, software updates |`},{id:"bias",heading:"Bias: observed average minus reference value",body:p`**Definition.** Bias is the difference between the **average** of repeated measurements of a standard and its **reference value**:

$$\text{Bias}=\bar{x}_{\text{observed}}-\text{reference value}$$

A positive bias means the system reads high; a negative bias means it reads low.

**How to run a bias study.** Choose a standard whose true value is known (a reference measured by a better method). Have the usual operator measure it repeatedly under normal conditions — at least 10 readings is a common practice. Compute the mean, the bias and the standard deviation of the readings.

**Is the bias real?** Random scatter alone will almost never give an average exactly on the reference, so test it. The logic is the [[one-sample-t|one-sample t-test]] you will study in Analyze (3.4.1):

$$t=\frac{\bar{x}-\text{reference}}{s/\sqrt{n}}\qquad df=n-1$$

$H_0$: bias = 0. If the [[p-value|p-value]] is below α (usually 0.05), reject $H_0$ — the bias is statistically significant. Otherwise you *fail to reject*: there is no evidence of bias, which is not the same as proving it is exactly zero. Finally ask whether the bias matters *practically*, for example relative to the tolerance or process variation.

**Example.** A network emulator injects exactly **50.0 ms** of delay. A latency probe reads it ten times: 51.2, 50.8, 51.5, 50.9, 51.1, 51.4, 50.7, 51.3, 51.0, 51.1 ms. The mean is **51.1 ms**, so bias = **+1.1 ms**. With s = 0.258 ms, the standard error is 0.258 ÷ √10 = 0.0816 and t = 1.1 ÷ 0.0816 ≈ **13.5** (df = 9, p < 0.001). The probe reads significantly high; the 95% confidence interval for the bias is roughly 0.92 to 1.28 ms.

> **Exam-tools tip.** The Reference Document gives the t-table. Compute √n first, then s ÷ √n, then divide the bias by it; compare |t| with the critical value (2.262 for df = 9, two-sided, α = 0.05).`},{id:"linearity",heading:"Linearity: does the bias change across the range?",body:p`**Definition.** The book defines linearity as the difference in bias values throughout the range in which the gauge is intended to be used. It answers: *does my gauge have the same accuracy for small, medium and large items?*

**How to study it.** Select several standards (typically about five) spanning the operating range. Measure each one repeatedly, compute the **average bias** at each reference value, and plot bias (y) against reference value (x). Fit a straight line:

$$\text{bias}=a+b\times\text{reference}$$

- A **flat** line at zero → no bias anywhere.
- A **flat** line above or below zero → constant bias (a pure offset).
- A **sloped** line → a linearity problem: the bias depends on the size being measured.

The book summarises the result with two quantities based on the [[slope|slope]] *b*:

$$\text{Linearity}=|b|\times\text{process variation}\qquad\%\text{Linearity}=|b|\times100$$

**Example.** The same probe is checked at five emulator settings: average biases of 0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 1.4 and 1.9 ms at 10, 30, 50, 70 and 90 ms. [[regression|Regression]] gives slope b = **0.0215** and intercept −0.075 (R² = 0.994). Predicted bias rises from about 0.14 ms at 10 ms to 1.86 ms at 90 ms — the probe overstates long delays more than short ones. With a process variation (6σ) of 20 ms, linearity = 0.0215 × 20 = **0.43 ms** and %Linearity = **2.15%**.

Software such as Minitab also tests whether the slope differs significantly from zero. The book notes that a linearity problem often signals the need to maintain or replace the equipment.`},{id:"stability",heading:"Stability: bias over time",body:p`**Definition.** Stability (or drift) is the change in the measurement system's accuracy over time. The book's phrase is memorable: *stability is bias characterised as a function of time*. It also notes that stability concerns the error over time more generally — a gauge can become noisier as well as drifting.

Causes include wear, temperature, vibration, dirt, lighting, software or firmware updates and, in IT systems, clock drift between servers. Unlike reproducibility, which training can largely remove, some instability is always present; the aim is to keep it small and detect it early.

**How to study it.** Measure the **same reference standard** at regular intervals (daily, per shift) and plot the readings on a [[control-chart|control chart]] — an [[i-mr-chart|I-MR chart]] for single readings or an Xbar-R chart for small subgroups. A stable measurement system shows only common-cause variation around a constant centre line; trends, shifts or points beyond the limits are signals of drift. The book recommends a stability study as the first exercise after calibration.

**Example.** The probe measures the 50.0 ms reference once a day. Limits calculated from days 1–15 are CL = 50.02, UCL = 50.57 and LCL = 49.47 ms. From day 14 the readings rise six days in a row and days 18, 19 and 20 (50.6, 50.8, 50.9 ms) fall above the UCL: the bias is drifting upward. The response is to investigate (firmware change? clock synchronisation?) and recalibrate — and to question any latency data collected since the drift began.`},{id:"calibration-vs-msa",heading:"Calibration is not MSA",body:p`**Calibration** compares an instrument with a traceable reference and adjusts it (or records a correction) so that its bias is within limits. The book notes that calibration procedures can minimise and control bias, though wear means bias can never be eliminated entirely. Calibration is a *maintenance activity*, done on a schedule, usually by a metrology function.

**MSA** is a *statistical study* of the whole measurement system — instrument, people, method, environment and reference — and of all its error components: bias, linearity, stability, repeatability, reproducibility and resolution. A freshly calibrated gauge can still fail MSA because operators read it differently, the procedure is ambiguous, or its resolution is too coarse.

| | Calibration | Measurement System Analysis |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Align the instrument to a reference | Quantify how much error the whole system adds |
| Scope | The instrument | Instrument, operators, method, environment, reference |
| Error types | Mainly bias (at calibration points) | Bias, linearity, stability, repeatability, reproducibility, resolution |
| Output | Adjustment or correction, certificate | Statistics and a verdict: is the data fit for decisions? |
| When | Scheduled intervals | Before baseline, before key analyses, after changes |

The two work together: calibrate first, then run MSA to confirm the system as used is adequate.`},{id:"service-examples",heading:"Bias, linearity and stability in service operations",body:p`- **Bias — ticket timestamps.** If "service restored" is logged when the engineer updates the ticket rather than when the alarm clears, every restoration time is too long by roughly the update delay. Reference: the monitoring system's alarm-clear time.
- **Linearity — SLA clocks.** A business-hours SLA clock that mishandles weekends or public holidays may be accurate for short tickets but increasingly wrong for long ones: the error grows with duration.
- **Stability — clock drift and tool changes.** Servers that slowly drift out of time synchronisation, or a ticketing-tool upgrade that changes how pauses are counted, shift the bias from one month to the next. A synthetic test transaction of known duration, run daily and charted, will reveal it.
- **Bias in transactional queries.** The book's advice is to reconcile query results with a known reference — for example, totals that must match a finance report.

In each case the remedy follows the diagnosis: correct the offset (bias), fix or limit the range of use (linearity), or monitor and recalibrate (stability).`}],whyItMatters:p`Bias moves the mean you report. In a service contract that can make a compliant process look non-compliant, or the reverse, and it shifts every capability index that uses the mean (Cpk, Ppk). Linearity means the error is worst exactly where outliers and SLA breaches live — at the long end of the range. Instability quietly invalidates trend comparisons: a "deterioration" between quarters may be the gauge, not the process. Checking the accuracy family protects baselines, before-and-after comparisons and penalty calculations alike.`,businessExample:{title:"A synthetic-transaction check on vendor latency reporting",body:p`A DSO team receives monthly latency figures for a core service from a managed-services vendor. To check the measurement system, the team schedules a synthetic test that injects a known 50 ms delay through a network emulator every day and records what the vendor's probe reports.

- **Bias:** over ten readings the probe averages 51.1 ms — a statistically significant +1.1 ms.
- **Linearity:** across 10–90 ms the bias grows with delay (slope 0.0215 per ms).
- **Stability:** a daily I-MR chart of the 50 ms reference shows an upward drift from day 14, with three points above the upper limit.

The findings go to the vendor as questions rather than conclusions: *based on the synthetic-transaction analysis, the reported latency could not be fully reconciled with the reference delay, and the difference appears to increase at higher latencies and over recent weeks; the team is asked to clarify the probe configuration and calibration history and to provide supporting evidence*. Until the explanation is agreed, latency trends from the drift period are marked as provisional in the SLA pack.`},visual:"biasLinearityStability",visualCaption:"Three panels built in sequence. Bias: ten readings of a 50.0 ms reference average 51.1 ms (bias +1.1 ms). Linearity: average bias at five reference values rises along a line with slope 0.0215. Stability: an I-chart of the reference measured daily — limits from days 1–15, then drift above the UCL on days 18–20.",definitions:[{term:"Reference value",definition:"The accepted true value of a standard, established by a more accurate method."},{term:"Bias",definition:"Observed average of repeated measurements minus the reference value."},{term:"Constant bias",definition:"An offset that is the same at every measured value."},{term:"Variable bias",definition:"An offset that changes with the setting or size being measured — detected by a linearity study."},{term:"Linearity",definition:"The change in bias across the operating range of the measurement system."},{term:"Slope (of bias vs reference)",definition:"How much the bias changes per unit of reference value; zero slope means no linearity problem."},{term:"% Linearity",definition:"|slope| × 100; equivalently linearity ÷ process variation × 100."},{term:"Stability (drift)",definition:"The change in bias (or measurement error) over time."},{term:"Stability study",definition:"Periodic measurement of one reference standard plotted on a control chart to detect drift."},{term:"Calibration",definition:"Comparing an instrument with a traceable reference and adjusting or correcting it to reduce bias."}],comparisons:[{title:"Bias vs linearity vs stability",headers:["","Bias","Linearity","Stability"],rows:[["Question","Is the average right at this value?","Is the bias the same across the range?","Is the bias the same over time?"],["Data","Repeated readings of one standard","Repeated readings of ~5 standards across the range","Periodic readings of one standard"],["Tool","Mean − reference; 1-sample t","Regression of bias on reference","Control chart (I-MR or Xbar-R)"],["Latency probe example","+1.1 ms at 50 ms","Slope 0.0215; 0.14 → 1.86 ms","Days 18–20 above UCL 50.57 ms"],["Typical fix","Offset correction, calibration","Maintain/replace, limit the range of use","Find the cause, recalibrate, monitor"]]},{title:"Calibration vs MSA",headers:["","Calibration","MSA"],rows:[["Nature","Maintenance activity","Statistical study"],["Scope","The instrument","The whole measurement system"],["Errors addressed","Mainly bias","Bias, linearity, stability, repeatability, reproducibility, resolution"],["Sequence","Done first","Done after calibration, before using the data"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Bias is the spread of the readings.",reality:"Bias is a location error: observed average minus reference. Spread is precision."},{myth:"Linearity means the gauge readings form a straight line.",reality:"In MSA, linearity is about the bias changing across the range. The ideal is a flat line of bias at zero."},{myth:"A gauge that passed calibration last month is stable.",reality:"Stability can only be shown by tracking a standard over time; drift can start the day after calibration."},{myth:"A non-significant bias test proves the bias is zero.",reality:"You fail to reject H₀: there is no evidence of bias with this sample, which is not proof of zero bias."},{myth:"Bias, linearity and stability need no reference standard.",reality:"All three compare readings with a known reference value; without one you can study precision but not accuracy."}],examTraps:[{trap:"Reference 120 s, observed mean 118.5 s: options include +1.5 s.",explanation:"Bias = observed − reference = 118.5 − 120 = −1.5 s. The sign shows the gauge reads low."},{trap:"“Bias is constant at the low end but grows at the high end.” Which property is this?",explanation:"Linearity — a change in bias across the range — not stability (which is over time).",tag:"accuracy-vs-precision"},{trap:"“Which tool is commonly used to assess the stability of a measurement system?”",explanation:"A control chart of a reference standard measured over time — not a Gage R&R study.",tag:"best"},{trap:"A bias t-test gives p = 0.40 and an option says “the gauge is proven to have zero bias”.",explanation:"p > α means fail to reject H₀ — no evidence of bias. It never “proves” zero.",tag:"fail-to-reject"},{trap:"Slope of bias vs reference = −0.03; options for % linearity include −3%.",explanation:"% Linearity uses the absolute slope: |−0.03| × 100 = 3%."},{trap:"“The gauge was calibrated, so no MSA is required.”",explanation:"Calibration controls bias in the instrument; MSA evaluates the whole system, including operators, method and precision.",tag:"not"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Bias of a latency probe, with a significance check",problem:p`A network emulator injects exactly **50.0 ms**. A latency probe reports: 51.2, 50.8, 51.5, 50.9, 51.1, 51.4, 50.7, 51.3, 51.0, 51.1 ms. Find the bias and decide whether it is statistically significant at α = 0.05.`,steps:["Mean = 511.0 ÷ 10 = **51.1 ms**. Bias = 51.1 − 50.0 = **+1.1 ms**.","Sample standard deviation s = **0.258 ms** (calculator: n − 1 in the denominator).","Standard error = 0.258 ÷ √10 = 0.258 ÷ 3.162 = **0.0816 ms**.","t = 1.1 ÷ 0.0816 ≈ **13.5**, df = 9. Critical t (two-sided, 0.05) = 2.262.","|t| = 13.5 > 2.262 (p < 0.001) → reject H₀: the probe has a real positive bias. 95% CI for bias ≈ 0.92 to 1.28 ms.","Exam-tools tip: write n, x̄, s and √n on the notepad before dividing; the t-table is in the Reference Document."],answer:"Bias = +1.1 ms; t ≈ 13.5, p < 0.001 — statistically significant",wrongApproach:{calculation:"t = 1.1 ÷ 0.258 = 4.26",whyWrong:"This divides by the standard deviation of single readings instead of the standard error s/√n. The test is about the mean of ten readings."}},{title:"A probe with no detectable bias",problem:p`A second probe reads the 50.0 ms reference ten times: 50.3, 49.6, 50.1, 49.8, 50.4, 49.7, 50.2, 49.9, 50.0, 50.2. Is there evidence of bias?`,steps:["Mean = **50.02 ms**; bias = +0.02 ms; s = 0.266 ms.","SE = 0.266 ÷ √10 = 0.084; t = 0.02 ÷ 0.084 ≈ **0.24**, df = 9, p ≈ 0.82.","p > 0.05 → fail to reject H₀: no evidence of bias with this sample."],answer:"No significant bias (t ≈ 0.24, p ≈ 0.82)",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“p = 0.82, so the probe is proven to be perfectly accurate.”",whyWrong:"Failing to reject is not proof. The study simply found no evidence of bias; precision and stability still need checking."}},{title:"Linearity across the operating range",problem:p`The first probe is tested at five emulator settings. Average bias: **0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 1.4, 1.9 ms** at reference values **10, 30, 50, 70, 90 ms**. Process variation (6σ) is **20 ms**. Interpret the linearity.`,steps:["Fit bias = a + b × reference by least squares: b = **0.0215**, a = −0.075, R² = 0.994.","Predicted bias: 0.14 ms at 10 ms rising to 1.86 ms at 90 ms — bias grows with the delay being measured.","Linearity = |b| × process variation = 0.0215 × 20 = **0.43 ms**.","%Linearity = |b| × 100 = **2.15%**.","Interpretation: a single offset correction will not fix this probe; correct as a function of the reading, or maintain/replace it."],answer:"Slope 0.0215 · linearity 0.43 ms · %Linearity 2.15% — a real linearity problem",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“The average bias is (0.2 + 0.5 + 1.0 + 1.4 + 1.9) ÷ 5 = 1.0 ms, so subtract 1 ms from every reading.”",whyWrong:"Averaging hides the pattern. A constant correction over-corrects small delays and under-corrects large ones because the bias depends on the reference value."}},{title:"Detecting drift with a control chart of a standard",problem:p`The 50.0 ms reference is measured once a day. Limits from days 1–15: CL = 50.02, UCL = 50.57, LCL = 49.47 ms. Days 14–20 read 49.8, 50.1, 50.3, 50.5, 50.6, 50.8, 50.9 ms. Is the measurement system stable?`,steps:["Compare each new point with the limits: 50.6, 50.8 and 50.9 (days 18–20) exceed the UCL of 50.57.","Look for patterns: days 14–19 rise six times in a row — a trend signal even before the limit is crossed.","Conclusion: the system is **not stable**; the bias is drifting upward.","Action: investigate the cause (configuration change, clock synchronisation, hardware), recalibrate, and flag data gathered during the drift."],answer:"Not stable — upward drift from day 14, points beyond the UCL on days 18–20",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“All readings are within ±1 ms of 50, so the probe is stable.”",whyWrong:"Stability is judged against the system’s own control limits and patterns, not an arbitrary band. The chart shows a special-cause signal."}}],lab:"accuracyPrecision",check:[{id:"chk-2.3.2-1",topic:"2.3.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!0,stem:"A test call of exactly **120 s** is timed 10 times by a call-recording platform. The average reading is **118.5 s**. What is the bias?",options:["+1.5 s","−1.5 s","1.25%","118.5 s"],answer:1,explanation:"Bias = observed average − reference = 118.5 − 120 = −1.5 s; the platform reads low.",whyWrong:["The sign is reversed: reference minus observed.","","1.25% is the bias as a percentage of the reference, not the bias itself.","118.5 s is the observed average."]},{id:"chk-2.3.2-2",topic:"2.3.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A study finds that a timing system is almost exact for short tickets but over-reports long tickets by increasing amounts. This is a problem of:",options:["Repeatability","Stability","Linearity","Reproducibility"],answer:2,explanation:"Bias that changes across the range of values measured is linearity.",whyWrong:["Repeatability is scatter in repeated readings of the same item.","Stability is a change in bias over time, not across the range.","","Reproducibility is variation between appraisers."],traps:["accuracy-vs-precision"]},{id:"chk-2.3.2-3",topic:"2.3.2",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which approach is BEST for assessing the stability of a measurement system?",options:["A crossed Gage R&R with three operators","A control chart of a reference standard measured at regular intervals","A regression of bias against reference value","A single comparison with a master part after calibration"],answer:1,explanation:"Stability is bias over time, so the same standard is measured periodically and charted.",whyWrong:["Gage R&R estimates repeatability and reproducibility, not drift over time.","","Regression of bias on reference assesses linearity.","One comparison gives bias at one moment, not stability."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-2.3.2-4",topic:"2.3.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A linearity study regresses bias on reference value and obtains a slope of **−0.03**. Using the book’s definition, what is the % linearity?",options:["−3%","0.03%","3%","97%"],answer:2,explanation:"% Linearity = |slope| × 100 = 0.03 × 100 = 3%.",whyWrong:["The absolute value of the slope is used.","The slope must be multiplied by 100.","","97% has no meaning here (it is 100 − 3)."]},{id:"chk-2.3.2-5",topic:"2.3.2",category:"compare",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement about calibration and MSA is correct?",options:["Calibration replaces the need for MSA","MSA evaluates the whole measurement system; calibration mainly controls instrument bias","Calibration measures repeatability and reproducibility","MSA should be done before calibration"],answer:1,explanation:"Calibration aligns the instrument to a reference (bias). MSA studies all error sources, including people, method and precision.",whyWrong:["A calibrated gauge can still fail MSA.","","Repeatability and reproducibility are assessed by Gage R&R, not calibration.","Calibration comes first; MSA confirms the system as used."],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-2.3.2-6",topic:"2.3.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"A bias study of a billing-rating engine against known reference calls gives a one-sample t-test p-value of **0.40** (α = 0.05). What is the correct conclusion?",options:["The engine is proven to have zero bias","Reject H₀: the engine is biased","Fail to reject H₀: no evidence of bias from this study","The engine is unstable"],answer:2,explanation:"p > α → fail to reject H₀. The study shows no evidence of bias; it does not prove bias is exactly zero.",whyWrong:["Statistics never proves the null hypothesis.","Rejecting needs p < α.","","A single bias study says nothing about stability over time."],traps:["fail-to-reject","p-value"]}],takeaways:["Bias, linearity and stability are all accuracy (location) problems and all need a trusted reference value.","Bias = observed average − reference value; the sign tells you whether the system reads high or low.","Test bias with the one-sample t logic: t = bias ÷ (s/√n); p < α → significant; otherwise fail to reject.","Linearity = bias changing across the range; fit bias against reference and look at the slope.","Linearity = |slope| × process variation; %Linearity = |slope| × 100.","Stability = bias over time; chart a standard on a control chart and watch for trends and points beyond limits.","Calibration controls instrument bias; MSA judges the whole system. Calibrate first, then do MSA."],flashcards:[{front:"Bias formula",back:"Observed average − reference value."},{front:"Constant vs variable bias",back:"Constant: same offset everywhere. Variable: offset changes with setting/size (a linearity issue)."},{front:"Testing bias",back:"One-sample t: t = (x̄ − reference) ÷ (s/√n), df = n − 1."},{front:"Linearity",back:"Change in bias across the operating range of the gauge."},{front:"How to study linearity",back:"Measure ~5 standards across the range repeatedly; regress average bias on reference value."},{front:"% Linearity",back:"|slope| × 100."},{front:"Linearity (book formula)",back:"|slope| × process variation."},{front:"Stability",back:"Bias as a function of time (drift)."},{front:"Tool for stability",back:"Control chart (e.g., I-MR) of a reference standard measured periodically."},{front:"Calibration vs MSA",back:"Calibration aligns an instrument to a reference; MSA quantifies error of the whole measurement system."},{front:"p = 0.40 in a bias test",back:"Fail to reject H₀ — no evidence of bias (not proof of zero bias)."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The one-sample t-test for bias, the “at least 10 readings” practice and the slope significance test follow common AIAG/Minitab MSA practice; the supplied book pages define bias, linearity and stability but do not show a numerical bias test. Hypothesis testing itself is covered in Analyze (3.3–3.4).","The linearity formulas Linearity = |slope| × process variation and %Linearity = |slope| × 100 are as given in the book (p. 183); newer AIAG editions emphasise testing the slope and intercept rather than a single %Linearity figure.","The calibration-vs-MSA comparison table is an original synthesis of the book’s statements that MSA is the entire system, not just calibration (p. 173), and that calibration minimises bias (p. 182).","All latency, call-timing and SLA examples are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 8",pdfPage:8,note:"Measure topic 2.3.2 “Bias, Linearity & Stability”."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 173",pdfPage:76,note:"Accuracy, linearity and stability affect location; linearity = change in accuracy through the range; stability = lack of consistency over time; MSA is the entire system, not just calibration."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 174",pdfPage:77,note:"Accuracy components: stability (accuracy over time), linearity (accuracy through the range), bias (offset), constant vs variable bias."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 181",pdfPage:84,note:"Accuracy as the difference between observed average and a reference value; assessed against a known standard."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 182",pdfPage:85,note:"Bias defined; calibration can minimise and control bias; constant bias example (scale)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 183",pdfPage:86,note:"Stability as bias over time, tracked with control charts, first study after calibration; linearity as difference in bias over the range; Linearity = |Slope| × Process Variation, %Linearity = |Slope| × 100."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 192",pdfPage:95,note:"Calibrate the gauge before a Gage R&R — remember linearity, stability and bias."}]},Y=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:C},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),n=String.raw,M={id:"2.3.3",module:7,title:"Gage Repeatability & Reproducibility",subtitle:"Separate measurement noise from real part-to-part variation",minutes:20,objectives:["Explain the purpose of a Gage R&R study and split measurement-system variation into repeatability (equipment) and reproducibility (appraiser, including operator × part interaction).","Design a crossed variable Gage R&R study: parts spanning the process range, 2–3 operators, 2–3 trials, randomised and blind — and know when a nested design is required.","Compute %Contribution, %Study Variation, %Tolerance and the number of distinct categories (ndc) from variance components or standard deviations, using only a calculator and notepad.","Apply AIAG acceptance guidance (<10% acceptable, 10–30% conditional, >30% unacceptable; ndc ≥ 5) and explain why %Contribution always looks smaller than %Study Variation.","Read the six-panel Minitab Gage R&R graph in words, including why an Xbar chart with most points out of control is a good sign.","Choose the right corrective action when repeatability dominates (gauge, fixture, resolution) versus when reproducibility dominates (training, operational definition, SOP)."],overview:n`Every number in a Six Sigma project passes through a measurement system before it reaches you. If that system adds a lot of its own noise, a "process problem" may be nothing but measurement error — and an "improvement" may be invisible. A **[[gage-rr|Gage R&R]]** study is the experiment that tells you how much of the variation you observe comes from the measuring process itself, and whether what is left is mostly genuine part-to-part (or call-to-call, link-to-link) variation.

The training book is blunt about it: [[msa|MSA]] applies to almost every project and "is a show stopper" (book pp. 172, 178). In this lesson you will design a study, turn its output into the four headline numbers — %Contribution, %Study Variation, %Tolerance and ndc — judge them against the AIAG guidance, read the Minitab graphs without seeing them, and decide what to fix first. The running example is telecom: three field engineers measuring the attenuation (in dB) of ten fibre links with an optical power meter.`,sections:[{id:"purpose",heading:"Why Gage R&R: observed variation = true variation + measurement variation",body:n`Whatever you measure, the spread you see is the sum of two things: the true unit-to-unit variation and the error added by the measurement system (book p. 178). Because **variances are additive** (book p. 176),

$$\sigma^2_{\text{observed}}=\sigma^2_{\text{part}}+\sigma^2_{\text{measurement}}$$

Measurement error has an *accuracy* side (location: [[bias|bias]], [[linearity|linearity]], [[stability|stability]] — lessons 2.3.1–2.3.2) and a *[[precision|precision]]* side (spread). Gage R&R studies the precision side, which itself has two parts (book pp. 173, 179):

$$\sigma^2_{\text{GRR}}=\sigma^2_{\text{repeatability}}+\sigma^2_{\text{reproducibility}}$$

**[[repeatability|Repeatability]]** — the variation when **one** appraiser measures the **same** characteristic on the **same** part **several times** with the **same** instrument. It is the instrument's own "wobble", so AIAG calls it **equipment variation (EV)**. Book examples: one person testing the same vial repeatedly and getting different purity values; one person re-reviewing the same contract and finding different errors (p. 179).

**[[reproducibility|Reproducibility]]** — the variation in the **averages** obtained by **different** appraisers measuring the same parts with the same instrument, called **appraiser variation (AV)** (p. 180). In an ANOVA study it has two pieces:

- **Operator** — one appraiser reads systematically higher or lower than the others;
- **Operator × part interaction** — the offset between appraisers *changes from part to part* (engineer B reads high only on long links, say).

The book confirms that Minitab adds repeatability, operator and operator × part to obtain the total measurement-system variation (p. 184).

> **Memory hook:** *Repeat* = **same** person repeating; *Reproduce* = **another** person reproducing.

What a Gage R&R does **not** tell you: whether the gauge is centred on the truth. A perfectly repeatable, perfectly reproducible meter that reads 0.3 dB high has a bias problem — that needs a reference standard (lesson 2.3.2).`},{id:"study-design",heading:"Designing the study: parts, operators, trials, randomisation, blinding",body:n`The book's standard layout is a **10 × 3 × 2 crossed design**: 10 parts, 3 operators, 2 trials each = **60 measurements**; at least two trials per part per operator are required and "three is better" (p. 193). Its paper-cutting exercise uses 12 parts × 3 inspectors × 5 trials (p. 198). The step-by-step method (p. 192):

1. Brief the team — explain that the **measurement system**, not the people, is being evaluated.
2. **Select parts across the range of interest** — not a random sample. If the gauge must work across the whole process, pick parts spanning the whole process; if you only care about a narrow improvement range, pick parts across that range. Do not include grossly defective parts — they inflate part-to-part variation and make a poor gauge look good.
3. Identify the operators (or instruments) that normally do the measuring.
4. Calibrate the gauge first (bias, linearity, stability).
5. Operator 1 measures every part once **in random order**; then operator 2; then operator 3 — that completes trial 1.
6. Repeat for each further trial, with a delay between trials.
7. Analyse (Minitab: *Stat › Quality Tools › Gage Study › Gage R&R Study (Crossed)*, p. 195) and draw conclusions.

**Blind** is "extremely desirable": ideally the operator does not know the reading is part of a test; at minimum they must not know which part they are measuring (p. 192). Otherwise they remember the last value and repeatability looks better than it is.

**Why the parts must span the range.** Part-to-part variation appears in the denominator of %Study Var and in ndc. Pick ten nearly identical fibre links and even an excellent meter will look poor; pick links from 6.4 dB to 7.1 dB — the range actually met in service — and the study judges the meter against the job it really does.

### Crossed vs nested

| | Crossed | Nested |
|---|---|---|
| Who measures what | Every operator measures every part, several times | Each part is measured by **one** operator only |
| When | Non-destructive measurement — the part survives | **Destructive** testing (crash test, burst test) or parts cannot travel between sites |
| Key assumption | — | Parts within one batch are identical enough to be treated as the same part |
| Can it estimate operator × part interaction? | **Yes** | **No** |

Crossed designs are the workhorse of MSA (book p. 191). Service example of nested: each regional team audits *its own* region's tickets — no two teams ever score the same ticket.`},{id:"methods",heading:"ANOVA method vs Xbar-R method, and variance components",advanced:!0,body:n`There are two classical ways to turn the 60 readings into numbers.

**Xbar-R (average and range) method.** Repeatability is estimated from the average within-cell range, $\hat\sigma_{\text{repeat}}=\bar R/d_2$ (with 2 trials $d_2=1.128$; with 3 trials $d_2=1.693$). Reproducibility comes from the range of the operator averages, corrected for the repeatability it contains. It is quick by hand but it **cannot separate the operator × part interaction**, which stays hidden.

**ANOVA method** (Minitab's default; book pp. 184, 196). A two-way ANOVA with interaction (Part, Operator, Part × Operator, Repeatability) is run as a **random-effects** model — the parts and operators are treated as samples from larger populations. Mean squares are converted into **variance components** (VarComp). For $p$ parts, $o$ operators and $r$ trials:

$$\sigma^2_{\text{repeat}}=MS_E\qquad \sigma^2_{O\times P}=\frac{MS_{OP}-MS_E}{r}$$

$$\sigma^2_{\text{operator}}=\frac{MS_O-MS_{OP}}{p\,r}\qquad \sigma^2_{\text{part}}=\frac{MS_P-MS_{OP}}{o\,r}$$

Check with the book's caliper study (p. 196; 10 parts, 3 operators, 2 trials): $(0.001087-0.000760)/2=0.000164$ for operator × part, $(0.003532-0.001087)/20=0.000122$ for operator and $(0.210651-0.001087)/6=0.034927$ for part — exactly the VarComp column in the output. Two practical notes: a negative estimate is set to **zero**; and if the interaction term is not significant, Minitab can pool it into repeatability (an "alpha to remove interaction" option). The ANOVA method is preferred because it uses all the data and isolates the interaction.

*You will not run an ANOVA by hand in the exam. You must know which method separates the interaction (ANOVA) and that VarComps are **variances**.*`},{id:"metrics",heading:"The four headline numbers: %Contribution, %Study Var, %Tolerance, ndc",body:n`Once you have the components, everything is ratios.

**1 · %Contribution — a ratio of variances.**

$$\%\text{Contribution}=\frac{\sigma^2_{\text{GRR}}}{\sigma^2_{\text{total}}}\times100,\qquad \sigma^2_{\text{total}}=\sigma^2_{\text{GRR}}+\sigma^2_{\text{part}}$$

Computed for every source; the column always **sums to 100%** (book p. 185).

**2 · [[percent-study-variation|%Study Variation]] — a ratio of standard deviations.**

$$\%\text{Study Var}=\frac{6\,\sigma_{\text{GRR}}}{6\,\sigma_{\text{total}}}\times100=\frac{\sigma_{\text{GRR}}}{\sigma_{\text{total}}}\times100$$

"Study Var" is a multiple of each SD — Minitab now uses 6 (≈ 99.73% of a normal distribution, p. 196); older material, including the book's cheat sheet, uses 5.15 (99%, p. 185). The multiplier **cancels** in %Study Var, so it does not matter there. This column does **not** sum to 100%.

**3 · [[percent-tolerance|%Tolerance]] — measurement spread vs the customer's window.**

$$\%\text{Tolerance}=\frac{6\,\sigma_{\text{GRR}}}{\text{USL}-\text{LSL}}\times100$$

It answers "how much of the [[specification-limits|specification]] width is eaten by measurement error?" (p. 185). Use it when the gauge's job is pass/fail inspection against the spec; use %Study Var when the job is process improvement (seeing variation). Here the multiplier does **not** cancel: 5.15 vs 6 changes the answer. (Minitab's optional %Process uses a historical process SD instead of the study SD.)

**4 · [[ndc|Number of distinct categories (ndc)]].**

$$\text{ndc}=\left\lfloor 1.41\times\frac{\sigma_{\text{part}}}{\sigma_{\text{GRR}}}\right\rfloor$$

1.41 ≈ √2. ndc is how many non-overlapping groups of parts the system can reliably tell apart (p. 186). It is always **truncated** — "round down to the nearest whole number" (p. 185): 5.64 → **5**, 4.98 → **4**.

### Worked through on the fibre study

Variance components: $\sigma^2_{\text{repeat}}=0.0016$, $\sigma^2_{\text{reprod}}=0.0009$, $\sigma^2_{\text{part}}=0.0400$ dB²; acceptance window 6.0–7.5 dB (tolerance 1.5 dB).

| Step | Calculation | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Gauge variance | 0.0016 + 0.0009 | 0.0025 → σ_GRR = 0.050 dB |
| Total variance | 0.0025 + 0.0400 | 0.0425 → σ_total = 0.2062 dB |
| %Contribution | 0.0025 ÷ 0.0425 × 100 | **5.88%** |
| %Study Var | 0.050 ÷ 0.2062 × 100 | **24.25%** |
| %Tolerance | 6 × 0.050 ÷ 1.5 × 100 | **20.0%** |
| ndc | 1.41 × 0.200 ÷ 0.050 = 5.64 | **5** |

### Exam-tools tip (calculator + notepad)

The exam gives you a Reference Document, a notepad and a basic calculator. Write the components as a column on the notepad and work in this fixed order:

1. If you are given **SDs**, square them first. **Add variances, never SDs.**
2. $\sigma^2_{\text{GRR}}$ = repeat + reprod (+ operator × part if listed separately); $\sigma^2_{\text{total}}$ = GRR + part.
3. %Contribution = variance ÷ variance × 100.
4. Press **√** on the GRR and total variances, then %Study Var = SD ÷ SD × 100. Shortcut: %Study Var = √(%Contribution ÷ 100) × 100.
5. %Tolerance = 6 × σ_GRR ÷ (USL − LSL) × 100.
6. ndc = 1.41 × σ_part ÷ σ_GRR, then **drop the decimals**.`},{id:"acceptance",heading:"Acceptance criteria — and why %Contribution looks smaller",body:n`The AIAG guidance used on the Green Belt exam applies to **%Study Var** (or %Tolerance):

| %Study Var or %Tolerance | Equivalent %Contribution | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| < 10% | < 1% | **Acceptable** |
| 10% – 30% | 1% – 9% | **Marginal / conditionally acceptable** — may be accepted depending on the importance of the measurement, the cost of the gauge and the cost of repair |
| > 30% | > 9% | **Unacceptable** — improve the measurement system before using its data |

Plus: **ndc ≥ 5** is recommended; 2–4 categories give only coarse estimates; 1 category can only sort conforming from non-conforming (book p. 186).

The book's own table splits the middle band more finely — ≤10% ideal, 10–20% acceptable, 20–30% marginal, ≥30% poor, with %Contribution bands ≤1%, 1–4%, 5–9%, ≥10% (pp. 186, 190) — which is why it calls its 17.05% caliper "acceptable" (p. 196). On the exam, use the three-band rule above: 17.05% is in the 10–30% band, i.e. conditionally acceptable.

### The classic trap: variances vs standard deviations

Because %Contribution is a ratio of **variances** and %Study Var a ratio of **standard deviations**,

$$\%\text{Contribution}=\left(\frac{\%\text{Study Var}}{100}\right)^2\times100$$

So 10% Study Var = **1%** Contribution and 30% Study Var = **9%** Contribution. A gauge with 9% Contribution sounds harmless, but it is a borderline 30% Study Var system. Our fibre gauge shows 5.88% Contribution yet 24.25% Study Var. Always check **which column** a number came from before comparing it with 10% and 30%.

Two more consequences: the %Study Var column does not add up (24.25% + 97.01% ≠ 100%), and ndc and %Study Var are linked — ndc ≥ 5 needs %Study Var of roughly 27% or less; at exactly 30%, ndc = ⌊4.48⌋ = 4.`},{id:"graphs",heading:"Reading the six-panel Minitab Gage R&R graph in words",body:n`Minitab's "Gage R&R (ANOVA)" report has six panels (book pp. 187–189, 195). You will be asked what a pattern means, so learn each one as a sentence.

**1 · Components of variation (bar chart).** Groups of bars for *Gage R&R, Repeat, Reprod, Part-to-Part*, each showing %Contribution and %Study Var (plus %Tolerance if a tolerance was entered). **Good:** the Part-to-Part bars tower over the Gage R&R bars. **Bad:** Gage R&R bars as tall as or taller than Part-to-Part — most of what you see is the measuring process (p. 195).

**2 · R chart by operator.** Each point is the range of one operator's repeat readings on one part, so it shows **repeatability**. **Good:** all points inside the limits — every operator measures consistently. **Bad:** points above the UCL — that operator struggles to measure some parts consistently (p. 187). If one operator's ranges are consistently larger, that is a technique problem. *(Supplementary: ranges that are almost all zero, or show very few distinct values, suggest inadequate [[resolution|resolution]].)*

**3 · Xbar chart by operator.** Each point is one operator's average on one part. Its control limits are built from the **repeatability** (the average range), so the band shows how much an average would move from measurement noise alone. **Good: MOST points OUT of control.** Parts were chosen to span the process range, so their averages should sit far outside a band of pure gauge noise; it means the system can distinguish parts. **Bad:** most points inside the limits — parts cannot be told apart from noise (p. 188). Also compare the three operator panels: the patterns should look alike.

**4 · By part.** All readings grouped by part, with the part averages joined. **Good:** tight clusters for each part and averages that vary clearly between parts (p. 189).

**5 · By operator.** All readings grouped by operator. **Good:** the line joining operator averages is **flat** (parallel to the x-axis) and the spreads are similar. A sloping line means operators measure differently — **reproducibility** (p. 189).

**6 · Operator × part interaction.** One line per operator, plotting their average for each part. **Good:** the lines lie on top of one another and follow the same pattern. **Lines that cross or diverge** mean the operator effect depends on the part — an interaction. Common causes: misreading or mis-recording particular values, or a poor fixture for certain parts (p. 190).

> These limits are **control limits computed from the study data**, not specification limits — a classic distractor.`},{id:"actions",heading:"What to fix: repeatability-dominated vs reproducibility-dominated systems",body:n`Look at the two components separately (in %Study Var or variance terms) before acting. The book's guidance (p. 190), expanded:

**Repeatability dominates (equipment variation)**

- Calibrate, repair or replace the gauge; check wear, contamination and warm-up.
- Improve the **fixture** or set-up so the part is presented the same way every time (for fibre: clean and inspect connectors, use reference cords, fix the launch condition).
- Check **resolution** — the gauge should resolve about one-tenth of the tolerance or process spread (supplementary rule of thumb).
- If only one operator shows poor repeatability, it is their technique — retrain that person.
- Short-term only: **signal averaging** — use the average of several repeat readings (the central limit theorem shrinks the SD by √n); the book shows six repeats pulling a 35% gauge to its 15% target and stresses this is temporary (pp. 197–198).

**Reproducibility dominates (appraiser variation)**

- **Train** to a single method and observe whether the SOP is followed.
- Tighten the **[[operational-definition|operational definition]]**: when to read, where on the part, what counts as a defect, with boundary samples and anchored examples.
- Standardise the SOP, set-up and environment; for several similar machines, check that each is calibrated and uses the standard method (p. 190).
- For an operator × part interaction, find out why some parts give that operator trouble — re-measure those parts and look for fixture, linearity or gauge-design causes.

**Service and telecom applications.** Gage R&R is not only for calipers:

- **Fibre attenuation (dB):** engineers with power meters — repeatability is driven by connector cleanliness and meter resolution; reproducibility by reference-setting technique.
- **QA analysts scoring calls** on a 0–100 quality form: repeatability = the same analyst rescoring the same recorded call weeks later; reproducibility = different analysts scoring the same call. Reproducibility usually dominates, and calibration sessions plus an anchored scoring guide are the fix.
- **Timestamps and system measures:** an SLA clock that starts at different events in different tools is a reproducibility problem of the measurement *process*, even with no human appraiser.

For pass/fail judgements (call compliant/non-compliant) the variable study becomes an **[[attribute-agreement|attribute agreement analysis]]** with [[kappa|kappa]] — lesson 2.3.4.`}],whyItMatters:n`Measure carries the largest share of the Green Belt exam (32%, syllabus p. 9), and Gage R&R is one of its most heavily examined topics because it mixes vocabulary (repeatability vs reproducibility), calculation (%Study Var, %Tolerance, ndc) and interpretation (graphs and verdicts). On the job it is the cheapest insurance in the project: before you baseline an SLA, run a hypothesis test or claim a saving, you prove the numbers are signal and not noise.

For a service-operations engineer this is also a governance tool. When a vendor's quality score, a field team's test readings or a dashboard metric decides payments or penalties, a Gage R&R (or attribute agreement study) shows whether two honest people would arrive at the same number — and gives a factual basis for asking for clarification when they would not.`,businessExample:{title:"Can we trust the call-quality score that drives a service credit?",body:n`A Digital Service Operations team receives a monthly **call-quality score** (0–100) from a managed contact-centre vendor; the SLA links a service credit to the average score. Before using the score in a DMAIC baseline, the team runs a crossed study: **10** recorded calls chosen to span poor to excellent, **3** vendor QA analysts, **2** blind trials each, in random order, three weeks apart.

Variance components from the ANOVA: repeatability 4, operator 20, operator × call 5, call-to-call 64 (score points²).

- σ²_GRR = 4 + 20 + 5 = 29 → σ_GRR = 5.39; σ²_total = 93 → σ_total = 9.64.
- %Contribution = 29 ÷ 93 = **31.2%**; %Study Var = 5.39 ÷ 9.64 = **55.8%** → unacceptable.
- ndc = ⌊1.41 × 8 ÷ 5.39⌋ = ⌊2.09⌋ = **2**.
- Reproducibility is the dominant source (51.8% Study Var vs 20.7% for repeatability).

In the vendor-facing report the finding is phrased as a request, not an accusation: *based on the joint scoring study, approximately half of the observed score variation appears to come from differences between analysts; the team is asked to clarify the scoring guideline and calibration approach used and to provide supporting evidence, so that both parties can agree a common operational definition before the score is used for the credit calculation.* The agreed action is a calibration session with anchored example calls, followed by a repeat study.`},visual:"gageRRStudy",visualCaption:"A crossed study on a fibre-attenuation meter: 10 links × 3 engineers × 2 blind trials. The 60 readings are split into part-to-part, repeatability and reproducibility variance; %Contribution (5.9%) and %Study Var (24.3%) are compared side by side; and the verdict dial shows a marginal gauge with %Tolerance 20% and ndc 5. Dots are illustrative readings simulated from the stated variance components.",definitions:[{term:"Measurement system analysis (MSA)",definition:"A structured evaluation of the whole measurement process — gauge, people, method, environment — to quantify how much variation it adds to the data."},{term:"Gage R&R",definition:"A designed study in which several appraisers measure the same parts several times, to estimate repeatability and reproducibility and compare them with part-to-part variation and the tolerance."},{term:"Repeatability (equipment variation, EV)",definition:"Variation in repeated measurements of the same characteristic on the same part by the same appraiser with the same instrument."},{term:"Reproducibility (appraiser variation, AV)",definition:"Variation in the averages of measurements made by different appraisers on the same parts with the same instrument; in ANOVA it includes operator and operator × part interaction."},{term:"Operator × part interaction",definition:"The difference between appraisers is not constant but depends on which part is measured; shown by crossing lines on the interaction plot."},{term:"Part-to-part variation",definition:"The genuine variation between the units measured; in a good study it is by far the largest component."},{term:"Variance component (VarComp)",definition:"The estimated variance attributable to one source (part, operator, operator × part, repeatability), derived from ANOVA mean squares; components add to the total variance."},{term:"%Contribution",definition:"A source’s variance component ÷ total variance × 100; the column sums to 100%."},{term:"%Study Variation",definition:"A source’s standard deviation ÷ total standard deviation × 100 (the 6σ or 5.15σ multiplier cancels); the column does not sum to 100%."},{term:"%Tolerance",definition:"6 × σ_GRR ÷ (USL − LSL) × 100; the share of the specification width consumed by measurement variation."},{term:"Number of distinct categories (ndc)",definition:"1.41 × σ_part ÷ σ_GRR, truncated to a whole number; how many groups of parts the system can reliably distinguish. Five or more is recommended."},{term:"Crossed design",definition:"Every operator measures every part several times; used for non-destructive measurement and able to estimate operator × part interaction."},{term:"Nested design",definition:"Each part is measured by only one operator; used for destructive testing or when parts cannot be shared, assuming parts within a batch are alike."},{term:"Blind study",definition:"Appraisers do not know which part they are measuring (ideally not even that it is a test), so memory cannot flatter repeatability."}],comparisons:[{title:"Repeatability vs reproducibility",headers:["","Repeatability","Reproducibility"],rows:[["Also called","Equipment variation (EV), within-appraiser","Appraiser variation (AV), between-appraiser"],["Held constant","Appraiser, part, gauge, characteristic","Part, gauge, characteristic"],["What changes","Only the trial (repeat readings)","The appraiser (and, for the interaction, the part)"],["Seen on","R chart by operator; spread within each part","By-operator plot; interaction plot"],["Typical causes","Gauge wear, poor fixture, low resolution, environment","Training, unclear operational definition, different methods"],["Typical fix","Repair/replace gauge, fixture, resolution; signal averaging (temporary)","Training, SOP, operational definition, boundary samples"],["Telecom example","One engineer reads the same link 6.81 then 6.88 dB","Engineer C reads every link ~0.05 dB higher than A"]]},{title:"%Contribution vs %Study Variation vs %Tolerance",headers:["","%Contribution","%Study Var","%Tolerance"],rows:[["Ratio of","Variances","Standard deviations","6σ_GRR to spec width"],["Formula","σ²_GRR ÷ σ²_total","σ_GRR ÷ σ_total","6σ_GRR ÷ (USL − LSL)"],["Sums to 100%?","Yes","No","No"],["Acceptable / marginal / unacceptable","< 1% / 1–9% / > 9%","< 10% / 10–30% / > 30%","< 10% / 10–30% / > 30%"],["Best for","Ranking which source to attack","Improvement work: can we see process variation?","Inspection: can we sort good from bad?"],["Fibre example","5.88%","24.25%","20.0%"]]},{title:"ANOVA method vs Xbar-R method",headers:["","ANOVA method","Xbar-R (average & range) method"],rows:[["Basis","Two-way random-effects ANOVA → variance components","Average ranges and range of operator averages"],["Operator × part interaction","Estimated separately","Cannot be separated (hidden in other terms)"],["Use of data","All of it","Ranges only"],["Typical use","Minitab default, preferred","Hand calculation, older AIAG worksheets"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Repeatability is about different operators getting the same answer.",reality:"That is reproducibility. Repeatability is one operator getting the same answer again on the same part with the same gauge."},{myth:"A %Contribution of 8% is below 10%, so the gauge is acceptable.",reality:"The 10%/30% limits apply to %Study Var. 8% Contribution is about 28% Study Var — marginal."},{myth:"You can add the repeatability and reproducibility standard deviations to get σ_GRR.",reality:"Only variances add: σ_GRR = √(σ²_repeat + σ²_reprod). Adding SDs overstates measurement error (0.07 instead of 0.05 dB in the fibre study)."},{myth:"An Xbar chart by operator full of out-of-control points means the measurement system is out of control.",reality:"Its limits come from repeatability. Points outside them show that part differences are larger than gauge noise — the desired result."},{myth:"Gage R&R parts should be a random sample from today’s production.",reality:"Parts are deliberately chosen to span the process (or improvement) range. A narrow set of near-identical parts makes any gauge look poor."},{myth:"A good Gage R&R proves the gauge is accurate.",reality:"Gage R&R measures precision only. A gauge can be repeatable and reproducible yet biased; accuracy needs bias, linearity and stability studies."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“Same operator, same part, same gauge, measured several times” — is this repeatability or reproducibility?",explanation:"Repeatability (equipment variation). As soon as the stem says different operators/appraisers, it is reproducibility.",tag:"repeatability-vs-reproducibility"},{trap:"A stem gives %Contribution = 7% and asks whether the gauge passes the 10% guideline.",explanation:"7% Contribution ≈ √0.07 = 26.5% Study Var — marginal, not acceptable. Convert before comparing.",tag:"not"},{trap:"Options compute σ_GRR as σ_repeat + σ_reprod.",explanation:"Square, add, then square-root. 0.04 and 0.03 give 0.05, not 0.07."},{trap:"ndc = 1.41 × 0.20 ÷ 0.05 = 5.64 — options include 6.",explanation:"ndc is truncated, never rounded: 5. Likewise 4.98 → 4, which fails the ≥ 5 rule."},{trap:"“The Xbar chart by operator shows MOST points outside the control limits. What does this indicate?”",explanation:"The system can distinguish between parts — a good result. The distractor says “the measurement system is unstable”.",tag:"most"},{trap:"The question asks for %Tolerance and an option divides by 6σ_total instead of the spec width.",explanation:"That is %Study Var. %Tolerance = 6σ_GRR ÷ (USL − LSL) — it uses specification limits, not study variation or control limits.",tag:"spec-vs-control-limits"},{trap:"“Gauge is repeatable and reproducible, therefore accurate.”",explanation:"R&R is precision. Accuracy (bias) is a separate property — check it against a reference standard.",tag:"accuracy-vs-precision"},{trap:"“Reproducibility is the largest component. What is the BEST first action?” — options include buying a new gauge.",explanation:"Reproducibility points to people and method: train, clarify the operational definition, standardise the SOP. A new gauge targets repeatability.",tag:"best"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Fibre attenuation meter: all four metrics from variance components",problem:n`Three engineers measure 10 fibre links twice each (crossed, blind, random order). Variance components (dB²): repeatability **0.0016**, reproducibility **0.0009**, part-to-part **0.0400**. Acceptance window 6.0–7.5 dB. Find %Contribution, %Study Var, %Tolerance and ndc, and give the AIAG verdict.`,steps:["σ²_GRR = 0.0016 + 0.0009 = 0.0025 → σ_GRR = √0.0025 = **0.050 dB**.","σ²_total = 0.0025 + 0.0400 = 0.0425 → σ_total = √0.0425 = **0.2062 dB**; σ_part = √0.04 = 0.200.","%Contribution = 0.0025 ÷ 0.0425 × 100 = **5.88%** (repeatability 3.76%, reproducibility 2.12%, part 94.12%).","%Study Var = 0.050 ÷ 0.2062 × 100 = **24.25%** (repeatability 19.40%, reproducibility 14.55%).","%Tolerance = 6 × 0.050 ÷ (7.5 − 6.0) × 100 = 0.30 ÷ 1.5 × 100 = **20.0%**.","ndc = 1.41 × 0.200 ÷ 0.050 = 5.64 → **5** (truncate).","Verdict: %Study Var and %Tolerance in 10–30% → **marginal / conditionally acceptable**; ndc meets ≥ 5. Repeatability is the larger source, so start with the meter and connectors."],answer:"%Contribution 5.88% · %Study Var 24.25% · %Tolerance 20.0% · ndc 5 → marginal",wrongApproach:{calculation:"σ_GRR = 0.04 + 0.03 = 0.07 dB → %Tolerance = 6 × 0.07 ÷ 1.5 = 28%",whyWrong:"Standard deviations do not add; variances do. Adding SDs inflates σ_GRR from 0.050 to 0.070 and pushes %Tolerance from 20% to 28%."}},{title:"Rebuilding the book’s caliper output from its VarComp column",problem:n`The training book’s Minitab session window (p. 196) lists variance components: repeatability **0.0007600**, operator **0.0001222**, operator × part **0.0001636**, part-to-part **0.0349273**. Tolerance = 1.0. Verify %Contribution, %Study Var, %Tolerance and ndc.`,steps:["Reproducibility = 0.0001222 + 0.0001636 = 0.0002858; σ²_GRR = 0.0007600 + 0.0002858 = **0.0010458**.","σ²_total = 0.0010458 + 0.0349273 = 0.0359731.","%Contribution = 0.0010458 ÷ 0.0359731 × 100 = **2.91%**.","σ_GRR = √0.0010458 = 0.032339; σ_total = √0.0359731 = 0.189666; %Study Var = **17.05%**.","%Tolerance = 6 × 0.032339 ÷ 1.0 × 100 = **19.40%**.","σ_part = √0.0349273 = 0.186889; ndc = 1.41 × 0.186889 ÷ 0.032339 = 8.15 → **8**.","Three-band AIAG verdict: 17.05% → conditionally acceptable (the book’s finer table labels 10–20% “acceptable”)."],answer:"%Contribution 2.91% · %Study Var 17.05% · %Tolerance 19.40% · ndc 8",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“%Contribution is 2.91%, well below 10%, so the gauge is ideal.”",whyWrong:"The 10% line is for %Study Var. 2.91% Contribution is 17.05% Study Var — inside the 10–30% conditional band."}},{title:"Call-quality scoring: when reproducibility dominates",problem:n`Three QA analysts score 10 recorded calls twice each (0–100 form). Variance components: repeatability **4**, operator **20**, operator × call **5**, call-to-call **64**. The score has no two-sided specification. Evaluate the system and recommend an action.`,steps:["Reproducibility = operator + interaction = 20 + 5 = 25; σ²_GRR = 4 + 25 = **29** → σ_GRR = 5.385.","σ²_total = 29 + 64 = 93 → σ_total = 9.644.","%Contribution = 29 ÷ 93 = **31.2%**; %Study Var = 5.385 ÷ 9.644 = **55.8%** → unacceptable.","Split: repeatability 2 ÷ 9.644 = 20.7% Study Var; reproducibility 5 ÷ 9.644 = **51.8%** — dominant.","ndc = 1.41 × 8 ÷ 5.385 = 2.09 → **2**.","%Tolerance is not reported — without USL and LSL there is no tolerance to compare with.","Action: calibration sessions, an anchored scoring guide and a clearer operational definition for each form item; then repeat the study."],answer:"%Study Var 55.8% (unacceptable), %Contribution 31.2%, ndc 2; fix reproducibility first",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Reproducibility = operator only = 20 → σ_GRR = √24 = 4.90 → %Study Var = 52.2%",whyWrong:"In the ANOVA method reproducibility = operator + operator × part. Dropping the interaction understates measurement error."}},{title:"Transceiver receive power: when repeatability dominates (and the ndc trap)",problem:n`A handheld meter is used to verify optical receive power on transceivers (spec −14 to −2 dBm). Study SDs: repeatability **0.9**, reproducibility **0.3**, part-to-part **2.0** dB. Find %Study Var, %Tolerance and ndc. After the meter is replaced, repeatability falls to **0.3**. Recompute.`,steps:["σ²_GRR = 0.81 + 0.09 = 0.90 → σ_GRR = 0.9487; σ²_total = 0.90 + 4.00 = 4.90 → σ_total = 2.2136.","%Study Var = 0.9487 ÷ 2.2136 = **42.86%**; %Contribution = 0.90 ÷ 4.90 = 18.37%.","Repeatability alone = 0.9 ÷ 2.2136 = 40.66% Study Var vs reproducibility 13.55% → the gauge is the problem.","%Tolerance = 6 × 0.9487 ÷ 12 = **47.43%**; ndc = 1.41 × 2.0 ÷ 0.9487 = 2.97 → **2**. Unacceptable.","After replacement: σ²_GRR = 0.09 + 0.09 = 0.18 → σ_GRR = 0.4243; σ_total = √4.18 = 2.0445.","New %Study Var = **20.75%**, %Tolerance = 6 × 0.4243 ÷ 12 = **21.21%**, ndc = 1.41 × 2.0 ÷ 0.4243 = 6.65 → **6**. Marginal but much improved."],answer:"Before: 42.86% Study Var, 47.43% Tolerance, ndc 2. After: 20.75%, 21.21%, ndc 6.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"ndc = 2.97 rounded to 3",whyWrong:"ndc is always truncated (rounded down). 2.97 gives 2 distinct categories."}},{title:"Exam-speed conversions between %Study Var, %Contribution and ndc",problem:n`(a) A report shows %Contribution = 9%. What is %Study Var? (b) %Study Var = 20%: what %Contribution? (c) %Study Var = 30%: what ndc?`,steps:["(a) %Study Var = √(9 ÷ 100) × 100 = √0.09 × 100 = **30%** — the edge of unacceptable.","(b) %Contribution = (20 ÷ 100)² × 100 = **4%**.","(c) σ_GRR/σ_total = 0.30 → σ_part/σ_total = √(1 − 0.09) = 0.954; σ_part/σ_GRR = 0.954 ÷ 0.30 = 3.18; ndc = 1.41 × 3.18 = 4.48 → **4**.","So a gauge right on the 30% line also fails the ndc ≥ 5 recommendation; ndc 5 needs about ≤ 27% Study Var."],answer:"(a) 30% · (b) 4% · (c) ndc 4",wrongApproach:{calculation:"(a) “9% < 10%, so acceptable.”",whyWrong:"A %Contribution figure cannot be compared with %Study Var limits. 9% Contribution equals 30% Study Var."}}],lab:"gageRR",check:[{id:"chk-2.3.3-1",topic:"2.3.3",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"One NOC engineer measures the latency of the same test circuit five times with the same probe and gets slightly different values. Which component of measurement variation does this describe?",options:["Reproducibility","Repeatability","Bias","Part-to-part variation"],answer:1,explanation:"Same person, same item, same instrument, repeated readings → repeatability (equipment variation).",whyWrong:["Reproducibility needs different appraisers measuring the same item.","","Bias is an offset of the average from a reference value, not scatter between repeats.","Only one circuit is measured, so there is no part-to-part variation."],traps:["repeatability-vs-reproducibility"]},{id:"chk-2.3.3-2",topic:"2.3.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A Gage R&R gives σ_repeatability = 3, σ_reproducibility = 4 and σ_part = 12 (same units). What is the %Study Variation for Gage R&R?",options:["14.8%","38.5%","41.7%","58.3%"],answer:1,explanation:"σ_GRR = √(9 + 16) = 5; σ_total = √(25 + 144) = 13; %Study Var = 5 ÷ 13 × 100 = 38.5% (unacceptable).",whyWrong:["14.8% = 25 ÷ 169 is %Contribution (a variance ratio).","","41.7% = 5 ÷ 12 divides by σ_part instead of σ_total.","58.3% = (3 + 4) ÷ 12 adds SDs and divides by the part SD."]},{id:"chk-2.3.3-3",topic:"2.3.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!0,stem:"σ_part = 0.60 and σ_GRR = 0.15. What is the number of distinct categories (ndc)?",options:["4","5","5.64","6"],answer:1,explanation:"ndc = 1.41 × 0.60 ÷ 0.15 = 5.64, truncated to 5. It meets the ndc ≥ 5 recommendation.",whyWrong:["4 = 0.60 ÷ 0.15 omits the 1.41 factor.","","ndc is a whole number of categories; it is truncated.","6 rounds 5.64 up — ndc is always rounded down."]},{id:"chk-2.3.3-4",topic:"2.3.3",category:"chart",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"In a Gage R&R report, the Xbar chart by operator shows **most** part averages outside the control limits for all three operators. What does this indicate?",options:["The measurement system is unstable and must be recalibrated","The operators are not reproducible","The measurement system can distinguish between the parts — a desirable result","The parts are out of specification"],answer:2,explanation:"The limits are based on repeatability (gauge noise). Part averages far outside them show part-to-part differences are much larger than measurement noise.",whyWrong:["Stability is judged over time with a reference part, not from this chart; out-of-limit points here are expected.","Reproducibility is judged by comparing the operator panels and the by-operator plot, not by points outside the limits.","","These are control limits from the study data, not specification limits."],traps:["most","spec-vs-control-limits"]},{id:"chk-2.3.3-5",topic:"2.3.3",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A Gage R&R on ticket-priority scoring shows reproducibility contributing far more than repeatability, and the operator × part plot shows crossing lines. What is the BEST first action?",options:["Replace the scoring tool with a more precise one","Clarify the operational definition and train all analysts to one standard method","Increase the number of trials per analyst to reduce repeatability","Widen the specification limits"],answer:1,explanation:"Reproducibility (including interaction) points to differences between appraisers — method, training and definitions — so standardise how people judge.",whyWrong:["A new instrument targets repeatability, which is already the smaller component.","","More trials only average away repeatability; they do not remove between-analyst differences.","Changing specs hides the problem and does not affect %Study Var at all."],traps:["best","repeatability-vs-reproducibility"]},{id:"chk-2.3.3-6",topic:"2.3.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!0,stem:"Variance components: repeatability 0.0009, reproducibility 0.0007, part-to-part 0.0084. What is %Study Variation for Gage R&R, and the AIAG verdict?",options:["16% — marginal","40% — unacceptable","40% — marginal","4% — acceptable"],answer:1,explanation:"σ²_GRR = 0.0016, σ²_total = 0.0100. %Contribution = 16%, but %Study Var = √0.0016 ÷ √0.01 = 0.04 ÷ 0.10 = 40% → above 30%, unacceptable.",whyWrong:["16% is %Contribution; it must not be judged against the %Study Var bands.","","40% is above the 30% limit, so it is unacceptable, not marginal.","4% is σ_GRR × 100, not a ratio to total variation."]}],takeaways:["Gage R&R splits measurement precision into repeatability (same appraiser, repeat readings — equipment) and reproducibility (different appraisers — operator plus operator × part).","Variances add; standard deviations do not. Square, add, then take the square root.","Design: parts spanning the process range, 2–3 operators, 2–3 trials (10 × 3 × 2 = 60 is standard), randomised and blind; crossed for non-destructive, nested for destructive testing.","%Contribution = variance ratio (sums to 100%); %Study Var = SD ratio; %Tolerance = 6σ_GRR ÷ (USL − LSL).","AIAG: < 10% acceptable, 10–30% conditional on importance and cost, > 30% unacceptable — on %Study Var or %Tolerance, never on %Contribution.","ndc = 1.41 × σ_part ÷ σ_GRR, truncated; aim for ≥ 5.","In the graphs: Part-to-Part bars should dominate, R chart in control, Xbar chart mostly OUT of control, flat by-operator line, parallel interaction lines.","Repeatability problems → gauge, fixture, resolution; reproducibility problems → training, operational definition, SOP."],flashcards:[{front:"Repeatability",back:"Same appraiser, same part, same gauge, repeated readings — equipment variation (EV)."},{front:"Reproducibility",back:"Different appraisers, same parts, same gauge — appraiser variation (AV) = operator + operator × part."},{front:"σ_GRR",back:"√(σ²_repeatability + σ²_reproducibility) — add variances, not SDs."},{front:"%Contribution",back:"σ²_source ÷ σ²_total × 100; the column sums to 100%."},{front:"%Study Variation",back:"σ_GRR ÷ σ_total × 100 (6σ or 5.15σ multiplier cancels)."},{front:"%Tolerance",back:"6 × σ_GRR ÷ (USL − LSL) × 100."},{front:"ndc formula",back:"1.41 × σ_part ÷ σ_GRR, truncated. Recommended ≥ 5."},{front:"AIAG %Study Var bands",back:"< 10% acceptable · 10–30% conditional (importance, cost) · > 30% unacceptable."},{front:"10% and 30% Study Var as %Contribution",back:"1% and 9% (square the ratio)."},{front:"Xbar chart by operator — what is good?",back:"Most points OUT of control: parts are distinguishable from gauge noise."},{front:"Standard crossed study size",back:"10 parts × 3 operators × 2 trials = 60 readings; three trials is better."},{front:"Crossed vs nested",back:"Crossed: everyone measures every part (non-destructive). Nested: each part measured by one operator (destructive)."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The training book (pp. 186, 190) uses a four-band AIAG table (≤10% ideal, 10–20% acceptable, 20–30% marginal, ≥30% poor). This lesson teaches the widely used three-band form (<10% / 10–30% / >30%) required by the course brief and notes the difference.","The variance-component formulas from mean squares, the Xbar-R method details (d₂ constants), the resolution rule of thumb (≈ 1/10 of tolerance or process spread), the “alpha to remove interaction” option and %Process are written from general MSA practice (AIAG MSA conventions, Minitab behaviour), not from the supplied pages; the book’s p. 196 ANOVA table was used to confirm the variance-component arithmetic.","The ndc ↔ %Study Var relationship (ndc 5 needs ≈ ≤ 27% Study Var) is derived algebraically from the ndc and %Study Var definitions.","Fibre-attenuation, transceiver, call-quality and ticket examples are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data. The visual’s dots are simulated from the stated variance components.","Attribute (pass/fail) measurement systems are covered in lesson 2.3.4; bias, linearity and stability in 2.3.2."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 8",pdfPage:8,note:"Measure topic 2.3.3 “Gage Repeatability & Reproducibility”."},{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 9",pdfPage:9,note:"Exam weighting: Measure 32%."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 172–173",pdfPage:75,note:"Purpose of MSA; repeatability and reproducibility as the primary contributors to measurement error; Gage R&R study."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 176, 178",pdfPage:79,note:"Components of variation are additive; observed variation = unit-to-unit + measurement error; MSA is a “show stopper”."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 179–180",pdfPage:82,note:"Precision = repeatability + reproducibility; definitions with manufacturing and transactional examples."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 184",pdfPage:87,note:"Variable MSA: random-effects model; VarComp; repeatability, operator and operator×part summed for measurement variation."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 185",pdfPage:88,note:"Session window cheat sheet: %Contribution, %Study Var (5.15 SD), %Tolerance; distinct categories rounded down."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 186",pdfPage:89,note:"Number of distinct categories guidance (≥ 5 recommended) and AIAG gauge acceptance table."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 187–189",pdfPage:90,note:"Minitab graphical output: components of variation, R and Xbar charts by operator, by part, by operator, operator×part interaction."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 190",pdfPage:93,note:"Practical conclusions; actions for repeatability and reproducibility problems."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 191",pdfPage:94,note:"Crossed vs nested designs; destructive testing."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 192–193",pdfPage:95,note:"Study steps, parts across the range, blind study; 10 × 3 × 2 crossed design = 60 measurements; three trials better."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 195–196",pdfPage:98,note:"Minitab Gage R&R (Crossed) menu path, graphical output and session window (ANOVA table, VarComp, %Study Var 17.05, %Tolerance 19.40, ndc 8)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 197",pdfPage:100,note:"Signal averaging to reduce repeatability error temporarily."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 198",pdfPage:1,note:"Signal averaging example concluded (average of 6 repeats); paper-cutting Gage R&R exercise (12 × 3 × 5)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 205",pdfPage:8,note:"MSA module objectives: repeatability & reproducibility, components of variation, variable and attribute MSA."}]},B=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:M},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),m=String.raw,R={id:"2.3.4",module:7,title:"Variable & Attribute MSA",subtitle:"Gage R&R for numbers, agreement analysis for judgements",minutes:15,objectives:["Choose between a variable MSA (Gage R&R) and an attribute MSA based on the type of data the measurement system produces.","Distinguish crossed from nested Gage R&R designs and know when each is required.","Describe the four views of attribute agreement: within appraiser, each appraiser vs standard, between appraisers and all appraisers vs standard.","Calculate percent agreement and Cohen’s kappa from an agreement table and interpret kappa against common guidelines.","Design a sound attribute agreement study: known standards, a mix of good, bad and borderline items, blind and randomised trials.","Apply attribute MSA to telecom classifications such as incident priority (P1–P4) and call-quality pass/fail."],overview:m`Measurement systems produce two kinds of data. Some return a **number on a scale** — a latency in milliseconds, a handling time in seconds, a diameter in millimetres. Others return a **category** — pass or fail, P1 to P4, "billing", "network" or "device". The first family is assessed with a **variable MSA**, usually a [[gage-rr|Gage R&R]] study; the second with an **attribute MSA**, usually an [[attribute-agreement|attribute agreement analysis]].

The book observes that transactional projects typically rely on attribute measurement systems, which makes this lesson especially relevant to service operations: every ticket category, priority and quality score is a human judgement — a measurement made by a person. We will place variable MSA in context briefly (the full mathematics is in [lesson 2.3.3](#/lessons/2.3.3)), then concentrate on how to design, calculate and interpret an attribute agreement study, including [[kappa|kappa]].`,sections:[{id:"two-families",heading:"Two families of MSA",body:m`| | Variable MSA | Attribute MSA |
|---|---|---|
| Data produced | [[continuous-data|Continuous]] measurements (or fine-grained counts) | Categories: pass/fail, go/no-go, [[nominal|nominal]] or [[ordinal|ordinal]] classes |
| Typical study | Gage R&R (crossed or nested) | Attribute agreement analysis |
| Key outputs | % contribution, % study variation, % tolerance, ndc | % agreement (four views), kappa |
| Information per reading | High | Low — so more items are needed |
| Service examples | Response time, latency, call duration | Incident priority, root-cause code, call-QA pass/fail |

The book is clear that **variable data are preferred** where possible because each reading carries more information. If you can measure "minutes late" rather than "late / on time", do. When only a judgement is possible, use an attribute study — and expect to need a larger sample: the book suggests that an attribute study needs considerably more samples than a continuous one.`},{id:"variable-msa",heading:"Variable MSA in brief: crossed and nested Gage R&R",body:m`A **Gage R&R** study has several appraisers measure several items several times, then partitions the variation into [[repeatability|repeatability]], [[reproducibility|reproducibility]] and part-to-part variation. The workhorse is the **crossed** design, for example **10 parts × 3 operators × 2 trials = 60 readings**, where every operator measures every part. It separates measurement variation from part-to-part variation and also reveals operator × part interaction.

A **nested** design is used when every operator *cannot* measure every part — most importantly in **destructive testing** (once tested, the item is changed) or when operators work at different sites with different items. Each item is measured by only one operator, and the design assumes items within a batch are effectively identical.

Practical rules from the book: select items that span the **range of interest** (not a random sample, and not grossly defective items that inflate part-to-part variation); calibrate first; measure in **random order**; leave a delay between trials; ideally run the study **blind**. Results are judged against AIAG guidance — % study variation or % tolerance under 10% good, 10–30% marginal, over 30% unacceptable, and at least 5 [[ndc|distinct categories]]. All of these calculations are worked through in [lesson 2.3.3](#/lessons/2.3.3).`},{id:"attribute-agreement",heading:"Attribute agreement analysis: four views of agreement",body:m`In an attribute study, several appraisers classify the same set of items, usually two or three times each, and their answers are compared with each other and with a **known standard** (the agreed correct classification). The book frames the purposes as: do all inspectors use the same criteria; does the inspection standard match the customer's requirement; how consistent is each inspector with himself or herself; and how often do inspectors pass bad items or reject good ones?

These questions map onto four agreement measures, each expressed as a percentage of items:

1. **Within appraiser (repeatability)** — items on which an appraiser gave the *same* answer on every trial.
2. **Each appraiser vs standard (accuracy of one appraiser)** — items on which all of that appraiser's trials match the standard.
3. **Between appraisers (reproducibility)** — items on which *all* appraisers gave the same answer on every trial.
4. **All appraisers vs standard (overall system accuracy)** — items on which every trial of every appraiser matches the standard.

$$\%\text{ agreement}=\frac{\text{items matched}}{\text{items inspected}}\times100$$

Each view can only be equal to or stricter than the one before it, so the "all appraisers vs standard" figure is usually the lowest. The book suggests it should exceed **75%** for the assessment to be considered acceptable, and it reports a confidence interval for each percentage — with only 15–30 items those intervals are wide, which is a good reason to inspect more items.

Reading the pattern matters more than any single number. Appraisers who agree with themselves but not with each other or the standard point to **training on the standard**. If one appraiser disagrees and others agree, coach that person. And, as the book warns, if the "standard" itself is wrong, everyone else will look wrong.`},{id:"kappa",heading:"Kappa: agreement beyond chance",body:m`Percent agreement has a weakness: two people can agree often **by chance**, especially when one category dominates. If 90% of calls pass, two evaluators who both say "pass" almost every time will agree most of the time without exercising any judgement. **Kappa** corrects for this:

$$\kappa=\frac{p_o-p_e}{1-p_e}$$

where $p_o$ is the observed proportion of agreement (the diagonal of the agreement table divided by the total) and $p_e$ is the agreement expected by chance, computed from the row and column totals:

$$p_e=\sum_i\frac{\text{row}_i}{n}\times\frac{\text{column}_i}{n}$$

Kappa runs from −1 to +1. **κ = 1** is perfect agreement, **κ = 0** is no better than chance, and **κ < 0** is worse than chance (rare). A commonly used guideline — consistent with the book, which asks for at least 0.70 and prefers values near 0.90 — is:

| Kappa | Interpretation |
|---|---|
| above 0.9 | Excellent agreement |
| 0.7 – 0.9 | Acceptable |
| below 0.7 | Needs improvement |

The calculation above is **Cohen's kappa**, for two raters or one rater versus the standard. With several appraisers, Minitab reports **Fleiss' kappa**, which extends the same idea; it can also report kappa for each category, showing exactly which classification an appraiser struggles with.

> **Exam-tools tip.** Build the table on the notepad: add the diagonal for $p_o$; multiply matching row and column totals, add them and divide by $n^2$ for $p_e$; then one subtraction and one division on the calculator.`},{id:"study-design",heading:"Designing an attribute agreement study",body:m`A badly designed study gives a falsely reassuring answer. Good practice:

- **Enough items.** A common practical minimum is **20–30** items; the book describes a typical study of at least two people judging 20 items twice each, and elsewhere suggests attribute studies need several times as many samples as a continuous study (for example 3 operators × 50 items).
- **Known standards.** Each item's correct classification is agreed in advance by experts or by the customer — and recorded where appraisers cannot see it.
- **A realistic mix.** Include clearly good, clearly bad **and borderline** items, roughly balanced between categories. Only obvious items make agreement look excellent; a sample of almost all "pass" items makes kappa unstable.
- **Blind and randomised.** Ideally appraisers do not know they are being tested; at minimum they must not know which item they are judging. Shuffle the order and hide identifiers between trials.
- **2–3 appraisers × 2–3 trials**, using the people who normally make the judgement, with time between trials so memory does not inflate repeatability.

After the study, use the disagreements constructively: clarify [[operational-definition|operational definitions]], create boundary samples and visual aids, standardise lighting or screen layouts, and retrain where the pattern points to it.`},{id:"telecom-cases",heading:"Telecom cases: incident priority and call-quality scoring",body:m`**Incident priority (P1–P4).** Priority drives response and resolution targets, escalation and SLA penalties, so mis-prioritisation is a measurement error with a financial consequence. A study of 30 closed incidents with priorities agreed by the problem manager (6 P1, 8 P2, 9 P3, 7 P4) is classified blind by a service-desk analyst. The diagonal shows 24 matches: **80% agreement**. Chance agreement is $p_e$ = (6×6 + 8×8 + 9×9 + 7×7) ÷ 900 = 230 ÷ 900 = 0.256, so **κ = (0.80 − 0.256) ÷ (1 − 0.256) ≈ 0.73** — acceptable but not excellent. Every disagreement is one level apart, suggesting the boundaries between neighbouring priorities need sharper definitions (for example, an explicit customer-count threshold separating P2 from P3).

**Call-quality pass/fail.** Two evaluators score 20 recorded calls. In a balanced sample they agree on 17 calls (85%) and κ = 0.70. In a sample where B passes 17 of 20 calls, they again agree on 17 (85%) — but κ falls to **0.32**, because most of that agreement would have happened by chance. Same headline, very different measurement system.`}],whyItMatters:m`In service operations, most KPIs are built on categorical judgements: how many P1s, what share of tickets were "network", how many calls passed QA. If those judgements are not repeatable and reproducible, Pareto charts, defect rates and SLA penalties are built on sand, and an improvement project may chase a category that exists only because analysts code it differently. An attribute MSA is usually quick — a morning's work with 30 tickets — and it frequently improves the process on its own by forcing clear operational definitions. On the exam, expect questions on choosing the right type of MSA, interpreting kappa and recognising good study design.`,businessExample:{title:"Checking incident-priority consistency in a managed-services contract",body:m`A DSO team notices that the share of P2 incidents reported by a managed-services vendor varies from month to month more than the underlying alarm volumes would suggest. Before analysing the trend, the team runs an attribute agreement study.

- **Items:** 30 closed incidents across all priorities, including deliberately borderline cases, with the correct priority agreed in advance by the service owner and problem manager.
- **Appraisers:** three vendor service-desk analysts, two trials each, one week apart; identifiers removed and order randomised.
- **Results (illustrative):** within-appraiser agreement of 87–93%, between-appraiser agreement of 63%, all-appraisers-vs-standard 57%, and overall kappa of about 0.65.

The findings are shared as a joint improvement item rather than a criticism: *based on the agreement study, the priority classification could not be reproduced consistently across analysts, particularly at the P2/P3 boundary; the team requests clarification of the priority matrix in use and supporting guidance for borderline cases*. A revised priority matrix with worked examples is agreed, and a repeat study is scheduled before priority-based SLA figures are used as a project baseline.`},visual:"attributeAgreementMatrix",visualCaption:"An agreement matrix for 30 incidents (rows = standard priority, columns = appraiser) fills cell by cell. The diagonal gives 24/30 = 80% agreement; chance agreement from the totals is 0.256; kappa = 0.73. The last step shows two call-QA studies with the same 85% agreement but kappa 0.70 versus 0.32.",definitions:[{term:"Variable MSA",definition:"An MSA for continuous (or fine-grained) measurements, usually a Gage R&R study."},{term:"Attribute MSA",definition:"An MSA for categorical judgements (pass/fail, classes), usually an attribute agreement analysis."},{term:"Crossed Gage R&R",definition:"Every appraiser measures every item several times; used for non-destructive measurement."},{term:"Nested Gage R&R",definition:"Each item is measured by only one appraiser; used for destructive testing or when items cannot be shared."},{term:"Known standard",definition:"The agreed correct classification of each item, set in advance by experts or the customer."},{term:"Within-appraiser agreement",definition:"% of items on which an appraiser gives the same answer on every trial (repeatability)."},{term:"Between-appraiser agreement",definition:"% of items on which all appraisers give the same answer on every trial (reproducibility)."},{term:"Each appraiser vs standard",definition:"% of items on which all of one appraiser’s trials match the standard."},{term:"All appraisers vs standard",definition:"% of items on which every trial of every appraiser matches the standard — overall system accuracy."},{term:"Kappa (κ)",definition:"(pₒ − pₑ) ÷ (1 − pₑ): agreement beyond chance, from −1 to +1."},{term:"Chance agreement (pₑ)",definition:"Agreement expected by chance: sum of (row total × column total) ÷ n²."},{term:"Blind study",definition:"Appraisers do not know they are being tested, or at least do not know which item they are judging."}],comparisons:[{title:"Variable MSA vs attribute MSA",headers:["","Variable MSA (Gage R&R)","Attribute MSA (agreement analysis)"],rows:[["Data type","Continuous measurements","Categories (binary, nominal, ordinal)"],["Main statistics","% study variation, % tolerance, % contribution, ndc","% agreement (four views), kappa"],["Typical acceptance","< 10% good, 10–30% marginal, > 30% unacceptable; ndc ≥ 5","κ ≥ 0.7 acceptable, > 0.9 excellent; all-vs-standard > 75% (book)"],["Sample size","e.g. 10 parts × 3 operators × 2 trials","More items: 20–30 minimum, often 50"],["Reference needed?","Not for R&R itself (needed for bias)","Yes — a known standard for accuracy views"],["Telecom example","Latency probe, call-duration timer","Incident priority, call-QA pass/fail"]]},{title:"Crossed vs nested Gage R&R",headers:["","Crossed","Nested"],rows:[["Who measures which items","Every appraiser measures every item","Each item measured by one appraiser only"],["When used","Non-destructive measurement","Destructive testing; appraisers at different sites"],["Key assumption","Items unchanged by measuring","Items within a batch are effectively identical"],["Operator × part interaction","Can be estimated","Cannot be estimated"]]},{title:"Percent agreement vs kappa",headers:["","% agreement","Kappa"],rows:[["Formula","Matches ÷ items × 100","(pₒ − pₑ) ÷ (1 − pₑ)"],["Corrects for chance?","No","Yes"],["Range","0–100%","−1 to +1"],["Balanced QA sample","85%","0.70"],["Skewed QA sample","85%","0.32"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"High percent agreement means the classification system is reliable.",reality:"Agreement can be high by chance when one category dominates; kappa corrects for this (85% agreement gave κ = 0.32 in a skewed sample)."},{myth:"Attribute MSA needs no reference — just check that appraisers agree with each other.",reality:"Appraisers can agree with each other and all be wrong. A known standard is needed to assess accuracy."},{myth:"Use only clear-cut good and bad items so the test is fair.",reality:"Borderline items are where measurement systems fail; leaving them out makes agreement look better than it is."},{myth:"A nested design is just a smaller crossed design.",reality:"Nested designs are required when items cannot be measured by every appraiser, such as destructive tests; they use a different model and cannot estimate operator × part interaction."},{myth:"Kappa of 0 means the appraisers never agreed.",reality:"κ = 0 means agreement is exactly what chance alone would produce — they may still agree on many items."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“Analysts classify incidents as P1–P4. Which MSA should be used?” Options include a crossed Gage R&R.",explanation:"Categorical (ordinal) judgements need an attribute agreement analysis, not a variable Gage R&R.",tag:"best"},{trap:"“The test destroys the sample.” Options include a crossed design.",explanation:"Destructive testing requires a nested design: each item can be measured only once, by one appraiser."},{trap:"Two evaluators agree on 85% of calls; an option claims the measurement system is therefore excellent.",explanation:"Percent agreement ignores chance. Compute kappa — it may be far lower when one category dominates."},{trap:"“Agreement between appraisers” versus “appraiser agreement with the standard”.",explanation:"Between appraisers = reproducibility (consistency across people). Versus standard = accuracy. Do not swap them.",tag:"repeatability-vs-reproducibility"},{trap:"“Within-appraiser agreement measures…” with reproducibility as an option.",explanation:"Within appraiser = the same person across trials = repeatability.",tag:"repeatability-vs-reproducibility"},{trap:"“Which is NOT good practice in an attribute study?” with “tell appraisers which items are part of the test”.",explanation:"The study should be blind and randomised; knowing the test items changes behaviour.",tag:"not"},{trap:"A kappa of 0.65 is described as “acceptable because it is above 0.5”.",explanation:"Under the commonly used guideline, below 0.7 needs improvement."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Kappa for incident-priority classification",problem:m`30 incidents with agreed priorities are classified blind by an analyst. Rows = standard, columns = analyst:

| | P1 | P2 | P3 | P4 | total |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **P1** | 5 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 6 |
| **P2** | 1 | 6 | 1 | 0 | 8 |
| **P3** | 0 | 1 | 7 | 1 | 9 |
| **P4** | 0 | 0 | 1 | 6 | 7 |
| **total** | 6 | 8 | 9 | 7 | 30 |

Find % agreement and kappa.`,steps:["Diagonal = 5 + 6 + 7 + 6 = 24 → pₒ = 24 ÷ 30 = **0.80** (80% agreement).","Chance agreement: pₑ = (6×6 + 8×8 + 9×9 + 7×7) ÷ 30² = 230 ÷ 900 = **0.256**.","κ = (0.80 − 0.256) ÷ (1 − 0.256) = 0.544 ÷ 0.744 ≈ **0.73**.","Interpretation: acceptable (0.7–0.9) but not excellent; all six errors are one level apart, so sharpen the boundary definitions.","Exam-tools tip: write row and column totals beside the table before multiplying."],answer:"80% agreement · κ ≈ 0.73 (acceptable)",wrongApproach:{calculation:"κ = 0.80 because 24 of 30 match",whyWrong:"That is percent agreement (pₒ). Kappa removes the 0.256 that chance alone would produce."}},{title:"Reading the four agreement views",problem:m`Three appraisers judge 20 call recordings twice each against a known standard. Results: appraiser A agrees with herself on 18 items and with the standard (both trials) on 16; all three appraisers agree with each other on 13 items; all trials of all appraisers match the standard on 12. Convert to percentages and interpret.`,steps:["Within appraiser (A): 18 ÷ 20 = **90%** — A is consistent with herself (repeatability).","A vs standard: 16 ÷ 20 = **80%** — A is usually right.","Between appraisers: 13 ÷ 20 = **65%** — the three disagree on 7 items (reproducibility problem).","All appraisers vs standard: 12 ÷ 20 = **60%** — below the book’s 75% guideline: the system is not acceptable.","Diagnosis: individuals are consistent but do not share the same criteria → train on the standard and clarify definitions."],answer:"90% · 80% · 65% · 60% — reproducibility is the weak point",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Average the views: (90 + 80 + 65 + 60) ÷ 4 = 73.75% “overall agreement”",whyWrong:"The four views answer different questions and are not averaged. Overall accuracy is the all-appraisers-vs-standard figure, 60%."}},{title:"Same agreement, different kappa",problem:m`Evaluators A (rows) and B (columns) score 20 calls pass/fail. Sample 1: [[9, 2], [1, 8]]. Sample 2: [[16, 2], [1, 1]]. Both show 17 agreements. Compare kappa.`,steps:["Both: pₒ = 17 ÷ 20 = 0.85.","Sample 1 totals: rows 11, 9; columns 10, 10 → pₑ = (11×10 + 9×10) ÷ 400 = 0.50 → κ = 0.35 ÷ 0.50 = **0.70**.","Sample 2 totals: rows 18, 2; columns 17, 3 → pₑ = (18×17 + 2×3) ÷ 400 = 0.78 → κ = 0.07 ÷ 0.22 ≈ **0.32**.","Lesson: when almost everything passes, high agreement is expected by chance. Use a balanced sample with borderline items."],answer:"κ = 0.70 vs κ ≈ 0.32 despite identical 85% agreement",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“Both samples show 85% agreement, so the evaluators are equally reliable.”",whyWrong:"Percent agreement ignores chance; in the skewed sample most of the agreement would occur even if the evaluators guessed “pass”."}},{title:"Choosing the right MSA",problem:m`Choose the MSA for: (a) a probe measuring round-trip latency in ms; (b) analysts classifying complaints into five reason codes; (c) a cable pull-strength test that breaks the sample, with each batch tested by a single technician.`,steps:["(a) Continuous, non-destructive, repeatable → **crossed variable Gage R&R** (plus bias/linearity checks).","(b) Nominal categories by human judgement → **attribute agreement analysis** with kappa.","(c) Continuous but destructive, each batch measured by one technician → **nested Gage R&R**."],answer:"(a) crossed Gage R&R · (b) attribute agreement · (c) nested Gage R&R",wrongApproach:{calculation:"(c) crossed Gage R&R with each technician re-testing the same cable",whyWrong:"A destroyed sample cannot be measured again, so every operator cannot measure every part; the design must be nested."}}],lab:"attributeMsa",check:[{id:"chk-2.3.4-1",topic:"2.3.4",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Two QA evaluators score 100 calls. Both pass 40, both fail 50, and they disagree on 10 (5 each way), giving the table [[40, 5], [5, 50]]. What is Cohen’s kappa (to 2 dp)?",options:["0.90","0.80","0.51","0.40"],answer:1,explanation:"pₒ = 90/100 = 0.90. Row and column totals are 45 and 55, so pₑ = (45×45 + 55×55) ÷ 10,000 = 0.505. κ = (0.90 − 0.505) ÷ 0.495 ≈ 0.80.",whyWrong:["0.90 is the percent agreement, not kappa.","","0.51 is the chance agreement pₑ.","0.40 is pₒ − pₑ without dividing by 1 − pₑ."]},{id:"chk-2.3.4-2",topic:"2.3.4",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Service-desk analysts assign each incident a priority from P1 to P4. Which study BEST evaluates this measurement system?",options:["Crossed variable Gage R&R","Attribute agreement analysis","Linearity study","Process capability analysis"],answer:1,explanation:"Priorities are categorical judgements, so an attribute agreement analysis (with kappa) is used.",whyWrong:["Variable Gage R&R needs continuous measurements.","","Linearity studies bias across a continuous range.","Capability analysis assumes the measurement system is already validated."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-2.3.4-3",topic:"2.3.4",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A fibre-splice tensile test destroys the splice, and each batch is tested by only one technician. Which Gage R&R design is required?",options:["Crossed","Nested","Attribute agreement","Linearity"],answer:1,explanation:"Destructive testing, with each item measured by only one appraiser, requires a nested design.",whyWrong:["Crossed designs need every operator to measure every part repeatedly.","","The data are continuous, so attribute agreement is not appropriate.","Linearity is a bias study, not an R&R design."]},{id:"chk-2.3.4-4",topic:"2.3.4",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"In an attribute study of 30 tickets, every trial of every appraiser matched the standard on **21** tickets. Using the book’s guideline, what is the result?",options:["70% — acceptable","70% — below the 75% guideline","30% — not acceptable","21% — not acceptable"],answer:1,explanation:"All appraisers vs standard = 21 ÷ 30 = 70%, which is below the book’s 75% guideline.",whyWrong:["70% is below the 75% guideline, so it is not acceptable.","","30% is the disagreement rate.","21 is the count, not the percentage."]},{id:"chk-2.3.4-5",topic:"2.3.4",category:"definition",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A kappa value of **0** means:",options:["The appraisers never agreed","Agreement is no better than chance","Perfect agreement","Agreement is worse than chance"],answer:1,explanation:"κ = 0 means observed agreement equals the agreement expected by chance.",whyWrong:["They may agree on many items — just no more than chance predicts.","","Perfect agreement is κ = 1.","Worse than chance is κ < 0."]},{id:"chk-2.3.4-6",topic:"2.3.4",category:"incorrect-statement",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Which is NOT good practice when designing an attribute agreement study?",options:["Including borderline items as well as clearly good and bad ones","Randomising the order of items between trials","Telling appraisers which items are part of the test so they take extra care","Agreeing the correct classification of each item before the study"],answer:2,explanation:"The study should be blind; appraisers who know they are being tested behave differently from normal work.",whyWrong:["Borderline items are essential — that is where disagreement shows.","Randomisation prevents memory from inflating repeatability.","","A known standard is needed to assess accuracy."],traps:["not"]}],takeaways:["Continuous data → variable MSA (Gage R&R); categorical judgements → attribute MSA (agreement analysis).","Crossed Gage R&R: every appraiser measures every item; nested: each item measured by one appraiser (destructive tests).","Four agreement views: within appraiser, each vs standard, between appraisers, all vs standard (book: > 75%).","Kappa = (pₒ − pₑ) ÷ (1 − pₑ) removes chance agreement; 1 perfect, 0 chance, < 0 worse than chance.","Common guideline: κ > 0.9 excellent, 0.7–0.9 acceptable, < 0.7 needs improvement.","High % agreement with a dominant category can hide a weak system — check kappa.","Design: 20–30+ items with known standards, good/bad/borderline mix, blind, randomised, 2–3 appraisers × 2–3 trials.","Service classifications (priority, root cause, QA pass/fail) are measurement systems and deserve MSA."],flashcards:[{front:"Variable vs attribute MSA",back:"Variable: continuous data, Gage R&R. Attribute: categories, agreement analysis with kappa."},{front:"Crossed design",back:"Every appraiser measures every part multiple times; non-destructive."},{front:"Nested design",back:"Each part measured by one appraiser only; destructive testing."},{front:"Within-appraiser agreement",back:"Same answer on every trial by one appraiser — repeatability."},{front:"Between-appraiser agreement",back:"All appraisers give the same answer — reproducibility."},{front:"All appraisers vs standard",back:"Every trial of every appraiser matches the standard — overall accuracy; book guideline > 75%."},{front:"Kappa formula",back:"(pₒ − pₑ) ÷ (1 − pₑ)."},{front:"pₑ (chance agreement)",back:"Σ (row total × column total) ÷ n²."},{front:"Kappa guideline",back:"> 0.9 excellent · 0.7–0.9 acceptable · < 0.7 needs improvement."},{front:"Kappa = 0",back:"Agreement equal to chance."},{front:"Attribute study design",back:"20–30+ items, known standard, good/bad/borderline mix, blind, random order, 2–3 appraisers × 2–3 trials."},{front:"Cohen vs Fleiss kappa",back:"Cohen: two raters (or rater vs standard). Fleiss: extends to several appraisers (Minitab)."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The kappa interpretation bands (> 0.9 excellent, 0.7–0.9 acceptable, < 0.7 needs improvement) are a commonly used guideline consistent with the book’s statement that at least 0.70 is typically required and values near 0.90 are preferred (p. 203); other published scales exist.","Cohen’s kappa formula and the hand calculation of chance agreement are standard statistics added for exam preparation; the book shows Minitab’s Fleiss’ kappa output without the formula.","The 20–30 item practical minimum and the 2–3 appraisers × 2–3 trials guidance reflect common practice; the book describes at least two people judging 20 items twice (p. 192) and suggests attribute studies need several times more samples than continuous ones (p. 199).","AIAG Gage R&R thresholds are summarised here only for context; the full treatment is in lesson 2.3.3.","Incident-priority, call-QA and vendor examples are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 8",pdfPage:8,note:"Measure topic 2.3.4 “Variable & Attribute MSA”."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 184",pdfPage:87,note:"Types of MSA: attribute vs variable; variable preferred; transactional projects typically attribute-based; variance components (ANOVA method)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 186",pdfPage:89,note:"Number of distinct categories and AIAG gage acceptance table."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 191",pdfPage:94,note:"Crossed vs nested designs; nested required for destructive testing."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 192–193",pdfPage:95,note:"Gage R&R steps, blind study, part selection across the range, randomisation and delay between trials; 10 × 3 × 2 crossed design."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 199",pdfPage:2,note:"Attribute MSA: multiple operators categorise samples repeatedly, blind and randomised; more samples than a continuous study; purposes of attribute MSA."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 200",pdfPage:3,note:"Visual inspection limitations and ways to improve (training, boundary samples, standards)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 201–202",pdfPage:4,note:"Attribute agreement analysis: each appraiser vs standard, between appraisers, all appraisers vs standard with confidence intervals; all-vs-standard above 75%; wrong standard warning."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 203",pdfPage:6,note:"Kappa statistics: range −1 to +1; 1 perfect, 0 chance, < 0 worse than chance; at least 0.70 typically required, near 0.90 preferred."}]},V=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:R},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),e=String.raw,g=b=>e`**Practise it:** open the [Capability Analyzer](#/labs/capability) to move the mean and shrink the spread while Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk and PPM update live, then drill generated problems in the [Formula Master](#/formulas/${b}).`,$={id:"2.4.1",module:8,title:"Capability Analysis",subtitle:"Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk — how well the process fits the specification",minutes:20,objectives:["Contrast the voice of the customer (specification limits) with the voice of the process (natural tolerance, ±3σ) and state the prerequisites for a valid capability study: stability, normality (or a transformation) and an acceptable measurement system.","Distinguish within (short-term) from overall (long-term) standard deviation, estimate within σ from R̄/d₂, and explain why the long-term spread is always the larger.","Calculate and interpret Cp, Cpk (via CPU and CPL), Pp and Ppk, including one-sided specifications where only CPU or CPL exists.","Diagnose a process from the pattern of indices: Cp versus Cpk (centring) and Cpk versus Ppk (stability, shift and drift between subgroups).","Convert capability to expected PPM out of specification with Z-scores, report Z.bench and sigma level, and apply the conventional 1.5σ shift correctly.","Read a Minitab capability report in words and choose the right improvement lever: centre the mean or reduce variation."],overview:e`Every process speaks in two voices. The customer tells you what is acceptable — a fibre connection installed within 48 hours, a call handled in four to eight minutes, network latency below 20 ms. The process tells you what it actually does — its average and its natural spread. **[[process-capability|Capability analysis]]** puts the two voices on the same scale and asks a precise statistical question: *what is the probability that this process produces output outside the customer's limits?* The training book defines capability as the inherent ability of a process to meet customer expectations without any additional effort — no extra time, money, manpower, technology or manipulation — and stresses that it reveals whether the problem is centring, variation, both, or unrealistic specifications.

In the Measure phase capability gives you the **[[baseline|baseline]]** that the whole project will be judged against. This lesson builds the four indices — **[[cp|Cp]]**, **[[cpk|Cpk]]**, **[[pp|Pp]]** and **[[ppk|Ppk]]** — from first principles, then turns them into the language executives understand: parts per million out of specification, **[[z-bench|Z.bench]]** and sigma level. Running examples come from a telecom operator's service desk: average handle time (AHT), fibre installation time and core-network latency.`,sections:[{id:"voc-vop",heading:"Voice of the customer vs voice of the process",body:e`**The voice of the customer ([[voc|VOC]])** is written as **[[specification-limits|specification limits]]**: a lower specification limit (LSL), an upper specification limit (USL), or both. The book's glossary defines them simply as the lowest and highest acceptable values of a characteristic. The distance between them, $USL-LSL$, is the **specification width** or tolerance. Specs come from customers, from the business (targets and benchmarks), from regulation, or from design (book p. 212) — and they are *chosen*, not calculated.

**The voice of the process ([[vop|VOP]])** is the distribution the process actually produces. For a normal process, almost all output (99.73%) falls within $\mu\pm3\sigma$. That $6\sigma$ band is the **natural tolerance** of the process — the width the process "needs" — and it is calculated from data, not chosen by anyone.

Capability is a ratio of these two widths. In the book's words (p. 208), capability analysis "compares the VOP to the VOC":

$$\text{Capability}\;\approx\;\frac{\text{width the customer allows}}{\text{width the process needs}}=\frac{USL-LSL}{6\sigma}$$

A ratio above 1 means the process fits inside the window with room to spare; below 1 means the natural spread spills over the limits and defects are guaranteed even if the process is perfectly centred.

> **Spec limits are not control limits.** Specification limits are the customer's voice and can be placed anywhere. [[control-limits|Control limits]] are the process's voice (±3σ around the centre line of a control chart) and tell you about stability. A process can be in statistical control and still produce defects (stable but incapable), or be capable today yet out of control. Never compare a single data point with a control limit to judge whether it is a defect.

The book's figure on p. 209 sorts outputs into three pictures: **incapable** (spread wider than the specification), **off target** (narrow enough but in the wrong place) and **capable and on target**. Every capability index you will meet is a numerical way of saying which picture you are looking at.`},{id:"prerequisites",heading:"Before you calculate: stability, normality and a valid measurement system",body:e`A capability index is a **prediction**: it uses the mean and standard deviation to forecast how much output will fall outside the specs in the future. That prediction is only as good as three assumptions.

1. **The process is stable.** Only [[common-cause|common-cause]] variation is present, so the mean and σ you measure today describe tomorrow too. Check this with a time-series plot or a [[control-chart|control chart]] (lesson 2.4.2). If [[special-cause|special causes]] are present, the "capability" describes a moving target. The book is pragmatic here (pp. 215–217): in Measure you often baseline a process that is *not* yet stable — that is why it is a project — but you must say so, treat the number as a snapshot that "draws a line in the sand", and avoid using it to predict.
2. **The data are normal (or transformed).** Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk and the PPM estimates all assume a [[normal-distribution|normal distribution]]. Run a [[normality-test|normality test]] first — in Minitab the [[anderson-darling|Anderson-Darling]] test, where p > 0.05 means you have no evidence against normality. Time-based data such as installation or handle times are often right-skewed; options are a [[box-cox|Box-Cox]] [[transformation|transformation]] (transform the specs too), a non-normal capability analysis, or reporting observed performance only. The book warns (p. 221) that applying normal capability to non-normal data is "one way to lie with statistics", and that bimodal data are a clue to go and look at the process, not a calculation problem.
3. **The measurement system is acceptable.** If the [[gage-rr|gage R&R]] consumes much of the tolerance, measurement error inflates the observed σ and every index looks worse than the process really is. Validate the [[msa|MSA]] (lesson 2.3) before you trust capability.

The book's seven **steps to capability** (p. 211) give the order of work: select the output → verify customer requirements → validate the specification limits → collect sample data → determine whether the data are long- or short-term → check normality → calculate Z-score, PPM, yield, Cp, Cpk, Pp and Ppk. Step 2 matters more than it looks: the book notes that some internal specifications turn out to be much tighter than the customer actually needs (p. 212), and an unrealistic spec produces a misleading "incapable" verdict.`},{id:"within-overall",heading:"Within (short-term) vs overall (long-term) standard deviation",body:e`Capability uses two different estimates of σ, and the four indices split cleanly between them.

**Within (short-term) σ** — written $\sigma_{within}$ or $\sigma_{ST}$ — measures the variation *inside* rational subgroups: consecutive calls handled by one agent in one hour, five orders keyed in one batch. It captures only the noise the process makes when nothing changes. It is the process's **potential** or **entitlement**. Common estimates:

$$\hat\sigma_{within}=\frac{\bar R}{d_2}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\hat\sigma_{within}=s_{pooled}=\sqrt{\frac{\sum (n_i-1)s_i^2}{\sum (n_i-1)}}$$

where $\bar R$ is the average subgroup range and $d_2$ is a constant that depends on subgroup size ($d_2=1.128$ for $n=2$, $2.326$ for $n=5$). For individual data Minitab uses the average moving range, $\overline{MR}/1.128$. Minitab's default for subgrouped data is the pooled standard deviation.

**Overall (long-term) σ** — $\sigma_{overall}$ or $\sigma_{LT}$ — is the ordinary sample standard deviation of *all* the data, ignoring subgroups:

$$s=\sqrt{\frac{\sum (x_i-\bar x)^2}{n-1}}$$

It includes everything: within-subgroup noise **plus** the shifts and drifts **between** subgroups — different shifts, agents, vendors, software releases, traffic peaks. The book (p. 214) shows the decomposition: overall variation = between-group variation + within-group variation (formally $SS_{total}=SS_{between}+SS_{within}$, p. 215). Hence the rule:

$$\sigma_{overall}\ \ge\ \sigma_{within}\quad\text{(in practice)}$$

The book's bottle-filling illustration (p. 212) makes this concrete: each lot looks tight, but the lot averages wander, so what the customer experiences across all lots is wider. Short-term data are a **snapshot** (one shift, one batch, one agent, one supplier — typically 20–50 points; the book gives both 30–50 on p. 212 and 20–30 on p. 213); long-term data are a **video** (many shifts, many agents, many suppliers — typically 100–200 points over weeks or months). Volume is not the test: 100,000 transactions from one day are still short-term (pp. 213–214). And whenever someone says **baseline**, they mean long-term performance (p. 213).

> **Telecom translation.** One week of AHT from a single team is short-term. Three months across all teams, day and night shifts, two vendors and a CRM upgrade is long-term — and its σ will be larger.`},{id:"cp-cpk",heading:"Cp and Cpk — potential and actual short-term capability",body:e`### Cp — process potential

$$C_p=\frac{USL-LSL}{6\,\sigma_{within}}$$

Cp compares the specification width with the natural tolerance. It **ignores where the mean is** — it answers "*could* this process fit if it were perfectly centred?" The book calls Cp and Pp the "hope" measures: what is possible, the best the process can be (p. 216). The glossary defines Cp as the ratio of specification width to short-term performance width (p. 585).

### Cpk — actual short-term capability

Cpk measures the distance from the mean to the **nearest** specification limit, in units of $3\sigma$:

$$C_{PU}=\frac{USL-\bar x}{3\,\sigma_{within}}\qquad C_{PL}=\frac{\bar x-LSL}{3\,\sigma_{within}}\qquad C_{pk}=\min(C_{PU},\,C_{PL})$$

Cpk is the "reality" measure: how the process is actually running relative to the limits (p. 216). The glossary describes it as an adjusted short-term index that reduces the score in proportion to the offset of the process centre from target (p. 585).

**Reading Cp against Cpk.**

- $C_{pk}=C_p$ → the process is perfectly centred.
- $C_{pk}<C_p$ → the mean is off-centre; the gap is the capability you would gain simply by centring.
- $C_{pk}\le C_p$ always. $C_{pk}$ can be zero (mean exactly on a limit) or negative (mean outside the specs).

**Worked illustration — AHT.** The service desk's AHT spec is 240–480 s (shorter calls signal unresolved contacts; longer calls breach the SLA). Mean 330 s, $\sigma_{within}=30$ s.

$$C_p=\frac{480-240}{6\times30}=\frac{240}{180}=1.33\qquad C_{PU}=\frac{150}{90}=1.67\qquad C_{PL}=\frac{90}{90}=1.00\qquad C_{pk}=1.00$$

The process *could* be a respectable 1.33, but because the mean sits 30 s below the mid-point (360 s), it performs at 1.00 against the lower limit.

### One-sided specifications

Many service metrics have only one limit: installation time (USL only), first-contact resolution (LSL only), latency (USL only). Then:

- **Cp and Pp do not exist** — there is no specification width. Minitab shows them as "*".
- Cpk is simply the one index that exists: $C_{pk}=C_{PU}$ for an upper-only spec, $C_{pk}=C_{PL}$ for a lower-only spec. The same applies to Ppk.

Inventing a missing limit (for example LSL = 0 for a time metric) to force a Cp is a common error — it produces a meaningless, usually flattering, number.

${g("cpk")}`},{id:"pp-ppk",heading:"Pp and Ppk — long-term performance, and what the Cpk–Ppk gap tells you",body:e`Pp and Ppk use **exactly the same formulas** with the overall (long-term) σ:

$$P_p=\frac{USL-LSL}{6\,\sigma_{overall}}\qquad P_{pk}=\min\!\left(\frac{USL-\bar x}{3\,\sigma_{overall}},\ \frac{\bar x-LSL}{3\,\sigma_{overall}}\right)$$

The book's formula page (p. 216) labels $s$ as the long-term standard deviation for these performance indices. Because $\sigma_{overall}\ge\sigma_{within}$, you will normally see $P_p\le C_p$ and $P_{pk}\le C_{pk}$.

Continuing the AHT case with $\sigma_{overall}=36$ s: $P_p=240/216=1.11$ and $P_{pk}=90/108=0.83$.

**The diagnostic pattern.** Compare the four indices in pairs:

| Comparison | What a gap means | AHT example |
|---|---|---|
| **Cp vs Cpk** | Centring problem — the mean is not in the middle | 1.33 vs 1.00 → mean too low |
| **Cpk vs Ppk** | Stability problem — the process shifts or drifts between subgroups | 1.00 vs 0.83 → some drift across shifts/teams |
| **Cp vs Pp** | Same story as Cpk vs Ppk, ignoring centring | 1.33 vs 1.11 |

A large Cpk–Ppk gap says the process is capable *in the moment* but loses that capability over time — special causes, shift-to-shift differences, vendor changes. The book's Minitab example uses exactly this reasoning: comparing Cpk with Ppk for two suppliers, it concludes Supplier 2 is "more prone to shifting over time" (p. 218). The fix for that gap is **control** (find and remove the between-subgroup causes), not a new process design.

When the indices are almost equal (Cpk ≈ Ppk), the process is behaving consistently; any remaining shortfall is either centring or inherent variation.

${g("ppk")}`},{id:"targets-shift",heading:"Benchmarks, Six Sigma and the 1.5σ shift",body:e`What counts as "capable"? These are widely used conventions (organisations and customers set their own):

| Index (centred, normal) | Expected PPM out of spec | Typical verdict |
|---|---:|---|
| 1.00 | ≈ 2,700 | Barely capable — the ±3σ tails touch both limits |
| 1.33 | ≈ 63 | The usual minimum for an existing process |
| 1.67 | ≈ 0.57 | Common target for new or critical processes |
| 2.00 | ≈ 0.002 | Six Sigma design capability |

The book's sidebar (p. 225) states that a Cpk of at least 1.33 is desired. One-sided, a Cpk of 1.33 corresponds to a Z of 4 and about 32 PPM in the single tail.

**Six Sigma in index terms.** A six-sigma process has specs at ±6σ from target, so $C_p=12\sigma/6\sigma=2.0$. Six Sigma practice then assumes that, over the long term, the mean can drift by up to **1.5σ** — the book states that even stable processes "will drift and shift over time by as much as 1.5 standard deviations" (p. 214). With the mean 1.5σ off-centre, the nearest limit is 4.5σ away:

$$C_{pk}=\frac{4.5\sigma}{3\sigma}=1.5\qquad\Rightarrow\qquad \text{PPM}\approx 3.4$$

That is where the famous **3.4 defects per million** comes from: it is the long-term defect rate of a process whose short-term capability is 6σ, *after* allowing the 1.5σ [[sigma-shift|shift]]. A perfectly centred Cp = 2.0 process would produce about 0.002 PPM.

**The convention in one line:** $Z_{ST}=Z_{LT}+1.5$, or equivalently $Z_{LT}=Z_{ST}-1.5$. The book's table (p. 223) pairs each short-term sigma level with its long-term DPMO: 3σ ↔ 66,807; 4σ ↔ 6,210; 5σ ↔ 233; 6σ ↔ 3.4. The book also says not to worry about the theory behind 1.5 (p. 223) — treat it as an industry convention, not a law of nature. When your data genuinely contain both within and overall σ, prefer the real Cpk–Ppk comparison to the assumed 1.5.`},{id:"ppm-z",heading:"From capability to PPM, Z.bench and sigma level",body:e`Indices are abstract; managers want to know *how many* will fail. With a normal model you convert each distance-to-limit into a [[z-score|Z-score]] and read the tail area:

$$Z_{USL}=\frac{USL-\bar x}{\sigma}\qquad Z_{LSL}=\frac{\bar x-LSL}{\sigma}\qquad \text{PPM}=\big[P(Z>Z_{USL})+P(Z>Z_{LSL})\big]\times10^6$$

Notice $Z_{USL}=3C_{PU}$ and $Z_{LSL}=3C_{PL}$, so **$Z_{min}=3C_{pk}$** (or $3P_{pk}$ with overall σ). The book uses this relationship on p. 224: divide a Z by 3 to get the index, multiply an index by 3 to get Z.

**The book's own Z example (pp. 222–223).** Mean 50, σ = 3, USL 54. $Z=(54-50)/3=1.33$; the table area beyond 1.33 is 0.09176, so about 9.2% or **91,760 PPM** fall above the limit. (Using the unrounded Z = 1.3333 gives 91,211 PPM — the difference is table rounding.)

**[[z-bench|Z.bench]]** combines both tails into one number: add the two tail probabilities, then find the single Z that leaves that total in one tail:

$$Z_{bench}=\Phi^{-1}\!\left(1-\frac{\text{PPM}_{total}}{10^6}\right)$$

For a one-sided spec, Z.bench is just that side's Z. For a two-sided spec it is slightly *smaller* than $Z_{min}$, because the far tail adds a few defects. Minitab reports Z.bench twice: **potential (within)** from $\sigma_{within}$ and **overall** from $\sigma_{overall}$. The overall Z.bench is long-term; adding 1.5 converts it to the short-term **sigma level** that is usually quoted.

**AHT, overall σ = 36 s.** $Z_{LSL}=90/36=2.50$ → tail 0.00621; $Z_{USL}=150/36=4.17$ → tail 0.0000155. Total ≈ **6,225 PPM**, $Z_{bench}\approx2.50$ (long term), sigma level ≈ **4.0** with the shift. Roughly 6 of every 1,000 calls end too quickly.

**Observed vs expected PPM.** Minitab also prints *observed* PPM — the actual count outside spec in your sample, scaled to a million. With 100 points, one bad value is already 10,000 PPM, so observed PPM is coarse; expected PPM comes from the fitted normal curve and is only trustworthy when the data are normal.

${g("sigmaLevel")}`},{id:"minitab",heading:"Reading a Minitab capability report in words",body:e`Minitab's *Stat > Quality Tools > Capability Analysis > Normal* report (book pp. 217–220) has four zones. Here is the book's **Supplier 1** camshaft study (LSL 598, USL 602, n = 100, subgroups of 5) read aloud — rebuilt in words, not copied.

**1 · Process data (left panel).** LSL 598, USL 602, no target, sample mean 599.115, StDev(Within) 0.559, StDev(Overall) 0.603. *Read it as:* the mean sits 0.885 below the mid-point of 600, and the overall spread is only a little larger than the within spread — so drift is modest.

**2 · The histogram with two curves.** The solid curve is drawn with the **within** σ (the short-term, potential picture); the dashed curve uses the **overall** σ (the long-term, actual picture). When the dashed curve is visibly wider, between-subgroup variation is significant. The vertical lines mark LSL and USL. Here the left tail crosses LSL.

**3 · Potential (within) capability.** Cp 1.19, CPL 0.66, CPU 1.72, Cpk 0.66. *Read it as:* the spread would fit (Cp > 1), but the process is too close to the lower limit — CPL is the binding side. (The book's commentary on p. 218 calls the .66 a "Cp index"; the report itself shows .66 is the **Cpk** and Cp is 1.19.)

**4 · Overall capability.** Pp 1.11, PPL 0.62, PPU 1.60, Ppk 0.62. *Read it as:* long-term performance is a little worse than short-term, as expected.

**5 · Performance table.** Observed PPM < LSL = 30,000 (3 of 100 parts); expected within ≈ 23,088 PPM; expected overall ≈ **32,131 PPM**. With Benchmark Z's switched on (p. 219), Z.bench within ≈ 1.99 and overall ≈ 1.85.

**The decision.** Supplier 2 (mean 600.06, so well centred, but σ within 1.01 and σ overall 1.15) has Cp 0.66, Cpk 0.64, Pp 0.58, Ppk 0.56 and about 81,400 expected PPM spread over *both* tails. Looking only at means, Supplier 2 seems better. The book chooses Supplier 1 (p. 218): its high Cp shows the spread already fits, and moving a mean is usually far easier than cutting variation.

> **Minitab default.** The book notes (p. 220) that Minitab's default assumes long-term data. For a purely short-term study the book suggests either setting the subgroup size equal to the total number of points or turning off the within-subgroup analysis — otherwise the "within" figures are not what you think.`},{id:"improve",heading:"Improving capability: centre the mean or reduce variation",body:e`The indices point straight at the lever to pull. The book (pp. 209–210) lists four options:

1. **Shift the mean** — find the X's that move the average to target. Usually the easiest option; it raises Cpk towards Cp but can never lift Cpk *above* Cp.
2. **Reduce variation** — find and control the inputs that create spread. Harder, but it raises Cp *and* Cpk, and is the only route when Cp itself is below target.
3. **Shift and reduce** — the book calls this the primary objective of Six Sigma projects.
4. **Widen the specifications** — only with genuine customer agreement, and only after the specs have been validated. Customers rarely accept it.

When a process is both off-centre and too variable, the book advises getting variation under control first (high variation makes it hard to tell whether a mean shift worked) — unless centring is a simple adjustment, in which case do that first (p. 209).

**Decision rule from the indices:**

| Pattern | Diagnosis | First lever |
|---|---|---|
| Cp ≥ target, Cpk < target | Off-centre only | Centre the mean |
| Cp < target | Spread too wide | Reduce variation |
| Cpk ≫ Ppk | Drift between subgroups | Remove special causes, control the inputs |
| All low, data non-normal | Model does not fit | Investigate the process; transform or use observed PPM |

**AHT revisited.** Centring the mean at 360 s lifts Cpk from 1.00 to **1.33** and drops expected within PPM from about 1,350 to about **63**. Alternatively, leaving the mean at 330 s but cutting $\sigma_{within}$ from 30 to 22.5 s (a 25% reduction) also gives Cpk **1.33** (Cp 1.78) and about **32 PPM** — slightly fewer defects because the far tail is now negligible, but a much harder change to deliver. For a call centre, centring might mean coaching agents not to close calls prematurely; reducing variation might mean knowledge-base and routing redesign.`}],whyItMatters:e`Capability is the number that turns "the vendor's installation times feel slow" into "expected 8,200 installations per million breach the 48-hour SLA, a long-term Z of 2.4". It is the Measure-phase **baseline** in the project charter, the yardstick for the Improve-phase pilot, and the proof at Control that the gain has held. Because it separates centring, short-term variation and long-term drift, it also tells you *which kind* of project you are running — an adjustment, a variation-reduction effort or a control problem.

On the exam, capability is one of the densest sources of numerical and trap questions: which σ goes with which index, Cp versus Cpk, one-sided specs, spec limits confused with control limits, and the 1.5σ shift in both directions. Learn the four formulas, the Z-to-PPM step with the standard normal table, and the diagnostic patterns, and these questions become quick marks.`,businessExample:{title:"Fibre installation SLA: a one-sided capability baseline",body:e`A fixed-broadband team commits to installing new fibre connections within **48 hours** of order acceptance — an upper specification only. The Measure-phase data set covers three months, all regions and both installation vendors. Within-subgroup σ (daily subgroups) is **5 h**, overall σ is **7.5 h**, and the mean is **30 h**. The Anderson-Darling test on the installation times shows no evidence against normality and the gage study on timestamps is acceptable.

- $C_{pk}=C_{PU}=(48-30)/(3\times5)=$ **1.20** — short-term, the process would breach the SLA for about 159 orders per million (Z = 3.6).
- $P_{pk}=(48-30)/(3\times7.5)=$ **0.80** — long-term, $Z=2.4$ and expected **≈ 8,200 PPM** (about 0.8% of installations) exceed 48 hours; sigma level ≈ **3.9**.
- Cp and Pp do not exist (no LSL).

The large Cpk–Ppk gap is the headline: day to day the process is close to capable, but something between days, regions or vendors pushes the long-term spread up by 50%. In the vendor-facing report this is framed as a request: *based on the workbook analysis, approximately 0.8% of installations are expected to exceed 48 hours, with variation between days larger than within days; the team is asked to clarify the scheduling and dispatch practices by region and provide supporting data so that the sources of between-day variation can be confirmed.* The project's first lever is control (stratify by region and vendor), not a redesign.`},visual:"capabilityWindow",visualCaption:"The specification window (LSL 20, USL 80) stays fixed while the process curve changes: centred but too wide (Cp = Cpk = 0.83) → variation reduced (1.67) → mean shifted towards the USL (Cp 1.67, Cpk 1.00) → long-term drift widens the overall curve (Pp 1.25, Ppk 0.75). Readouts and expected PPM are computed live from the app’s verified capability functions.",definitions:[{term:"Process capability",definition:"The inherent ability of a process to meet customer requirements without extra effort; a comparison of the specification width with the process’s actual performance width."},{term:"Specification limits (LSL, USL)",definition:"The lowest and highest acceptable values of a characteristic — the voice of the customer, chosen not calculated."},{term:"Natural tolerance",definition:"The width the process itself needs, conventionally μ ± 3σ (6σ), containing about 99.73% of normal output."},{term:"Within (short-term) σ",definition:"Variation inside rational subgroups, estimated by R̄/d₂, pooled s or M̄R/1.128; used for Cp and Cpk."},{term:"Overall (long-term) σ",definition:"The ordinary standard deviation of all data, including between-subgroup shifts and drifts; used for Pp and Ppk."},{term:"Cp",definition:"(USL − LSL) ÷ 6σ_within; potential short-term capability if the process were perfectly centred."},{term:"CPU / CPL",definition:"(USL − x̄) ÷ 3σ_within and (x̄ − LSL) ÷ 3σ_within; capability against each limit separately."},{term:"Cpk",definition:"min(CPU, CPL); actual short-term capability, accounting for centring. Cpk ≤ Cp."},{term:"Pp",definition:"(USL − LSL) ÷ 6σ_overall; long-term potential performance."},{term:"Ppk",definition:"min of the two one-sided indices using σ_overall; actual long-term performance."},{term:"Z.bench",definition:"The single Z-value whose one-sided tail equals the total out-of-spec proportion (both tails combined)."},{term:"PPM (expected)",definition:"Parts per million out of specification predicted from the fitted normal distribution; observed PPM is the actual sample count scaled to a million."},{term:"Sigma level",definition:"Short-term Z, conventionally long-term Z.bench + 1.5; 6σ ↔ 3.4 long-term DPMO."},{term:"1.5σ shift",definition:"The Six Sigma convention that a stable process’s mean can drift by up to 1.5σ over the long term, so Z_ST = Z_LT + 1.5."}],comparisons:[{title:"Cp vs Cpk vs Pp vs Ppk",headers:["","Cp","Cpk","Pp","Ppk"],rows:[["σ used","Within (short-term)","Within (short-term)","Overall (long-term)","Overall (long-term)"],["Accounts for centring?","No","Yes — nearest limit","No","Yes — nearest limit"],["Formula","(USL − LSL) ÷ 6σw","min(USL − x̄, x̄ − LSL) ÷ 3σw","(USL − LSL) ÷ 6σo","min(USL − x̄, x̄ − LSL) ÷ 3σo"],["Book label (p. 216)","Hope / potential","Reality","Hope / potential","Reality"],["One-sided spec?","Does not exist","CPU or CPL","Does not exist","PPU or PPL"],["AHT example","1.33","1.00","1.11","0.83"]]},{title:"Short-term vs long-term data",headers:["","Short-term (within)","Long-term (overall)"],rows:[["Analogy (book p. 213)","A snapshot","A video"],["Conditions","One shift, batch, agent, supplier, service type","Many shifts, agents, suppliers, releases"],["Typical size","20–50 points over days","100–200 points over weeks or months"],["Variation included","Common cause within subgroups only","Within + between subgroups (shifts, drift)"],["Indices","Cp, Cpk","Pp, Ppk"],["Use","Entitlement — what the process can do","Baseline — what the customer experiences"]]},{title:"Specification limits vs control limits",headers:["","Specification limits","Control limits"],rows:[["Voice of","The customer (VOC)","The process (VOP)"],["Source","Customer, business, regulation, design","Calculated from process data (±3σ of the plotted statistic)"],["Answers","Is this output acceptable?","Is the process stable — only common cause present?"],["Applies to","Individual units","Plotted statistics (individuals, subgroup means, ranges…)"],["Used in","Capability analysis","Control charts / stability"]]},{title:"Which lever? Centre the mean vs reduce variation",headers:["","Centre the mean","Reduce variation"],rows:[["Signal","Cpk well below Cp","Cp itself below target"],["Effect on Cp","None","Increases"],["Effect on Cpk","Rises towards Cp (never above)","Increases"],["Difficulty (book p. 209–210)","Usually easiest","Usually harder"],["AHT result","Cpk 1.00 → 1.33, ≈ 63 PPM","σ 30 → 22.5 s: Cpk 1.33, ≈ 32 PPM"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"A process inside its control limits is capable.",reality:"Control limits describe stability, not customer requirements. A stable process can still spill far outside the specification limits."},{myth:"A high Cp means few defects.",reality:"Cp ignores the mean. A Cp of 2.0 process with its mean sitting on the USL has Cpk 0 and about 50% defects."},{myth:"Cpk and Ppk are interchangeable.",reality:"Cpk uses within (short-term) σ; Ppk uses overall (long-term) σ. A large gap between them is itself a finding — the process drifts between subgroups."},{myth:"You can calculate Cp for a one-sided specification by assuming the missing limit is zero.",reality:"With one limit there is no specification width, so Cp and Pp do not exist; report CPU or CPL (Cpk) and PPU or PPL (Ppk)."},{myth:"Capability indices work on any data.",reality:"They assume a stable process and normal data measured with an adequate gage. Skewed time data need a transformation or a non-normal method; otherwise PPM predictions are wrong."},{myth:"“Six Sigma” means Cpk = 2.0 and 3.4 defects per million in the short term.",reality:"Six Sigma is Cp = 2.0; with the conventional 1.5σ long-term shift the nearest limit is 4.5σ away, giving Cpk ≈ 1.5 and 3.4 PPM long-term."}],examTraps:[{trap:"An option offers the overall standard deviation for calculating Cp or Cpk.",explanation:"Cp and Cpk use within (short-term) σ; Pp and Ppk use overall (long-term) σ.",tag:"short-vs-long-term"},{trap:"The stem says Cp = 1.8 and asks whether the process is capable.",explanation:"Cp alone ignores centring. You need Cpk (or the mean) before judging — the process may be off-centre and producing defects.",tag:"cp-vs-cpk"},{trap:"Data points fall outside the control limits, and an option calls them “defects”.",explanation:"Control limits are not specification limits. Out-of-control signals special causes; out-of-spec means defects. They are different questions.",tag:"spec-vs-control-limits"},{trap:"“Which index can NEVER exceed Cp?”",explanation:"Cpk — it equals Cp only when the process is perfectly centred. (Pp is usually lower than Cp too, but because of σ, not by definition.)",tag:"not"},{trap:"A one-sided spec (USL only) and options include a Cp value.",explanation:"Cp needs both limits. Calculate CPU = (USL − x̄)/3σ, which is the Cpk.",tag:"cp-vs-cpk"},{trap:"Given Z_LT = 3.0, “what is the sigma level?” with 3.0 among the options.",explanation:"By convention sigma level is short-term: Z_ST = Z_LT + 1.5 = 4.5. Watch which direction the stem converts.",tag:"short-vs-long-term"},{trap:"Cpk = 1.4 and Ppk = 0.9: “the BEST explanation is…”",explanation:"The process is capable short-term but shifts or drifts between subgroups (instability). It is not mainly a centring problem — both indices already account for the mean.",tag:"best"},{trap:"Capability is quoted from a heavily skewed or bimodal data set without comment.",explanation:"Normal-based indices and PPM are invalid on non-normal data. Transform, use a non-normal analysis, or report observed performance.",tag:"normality-assumption"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Average handle time: Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk and PPM",problem:e`AHT specification: LSL = 240 s, USL = 480 s. Data from 25 subgroups: mean = 330 s, $\sigma_{within}=30$ s, $\sigma_{overall}=36$ s. Normality and MSA are acceptable. Calculate Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk and the expected long-term PPM.`,steps:[e`Cp = (480 − 240) ÷ (6 × 30) = 240 ÷ 180 = **1.33**.`,e`CPU = (480 − 330) ÷ (3 × 30) = 150 ÷ 90 = 1.67; CPL = (330 − 240) ÷ 90 = 1.00; Cpk = min = **1.00** (lower limit binds).`,e`Pp = 240 ÷ (6 × 36) = 240 ÷ 216 = **1.11**; Ppk = min(150, 90) ÷ (3 × 36) = 90 ÷ 108 = **0.83**.`,e`Long-term Z: $Z_{LSL}=90/36=2.50$ → tail 0.00621; $Z_{USL}=150/36=4.17$ → tail ≈ 0.0000155. Total ≈ **6,225 PPM**; Z.bench ≈ 2.50 → sigma level ≈ 4.0.`,e`Diagnosis: Cp > Cpk → centring problem (mean 30 s low); Cpk > Ppk → some drift between subgroups.`,e`**Exam-tools tip:** on the virtual calculator key (480−240)÷(6×30) and 90÷(3×30) — never round σ before dividing. For PPM, look up Z = 2.50 in the **standard normal table** of the Reference Document (right-tail 0.00621), multiply by 1,000,000; add the far tail only if it is visible to four decimals.`],answer:"Cp 1.33 · Cpk 1.00 · Pp 1.11 · Ppk 0.83 · ≈ 6,225 PPM long-term (≈ 4.0σ)",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Cpk = (480 − 330) ÷ (3 × 30) = 1.67",whyWrong:"That is only CPU. Cpk is the minimum of CPU and CPL; the mean is closer to the LSL, so CPL = 1.00 governs."}},{title:"One-sided spec: fibre installation within 48 hours",problem:e`Fibre installation time has USL = 48 h and no lower limit. Mean = 30 h, $\sigma_{within}=5$ h, $\sigma_{overall}=7.5$ h. Find Cpk, Ppk, expected long-term PPM and the sigma level.`,steps:[e`Only the upper side exists: Cpk = CPU = (48 − 30) ÷ (3 × 5) = 18 ÷ 15 = **1.20**. Cp does not exist.`,e`Ppk = PPU = 18 ÷ (3 × 7.5) = 18 ÷ 22.5 = **0.80**.`,e`Long-term Z = 18 ÷ 7.5 = 2.40 (= 3 × Ppk) → right-tail 0.0082 → **≈ 8,200 PPM**.`,e`Z.bench (LT) = 2.40 → sigma level ≈ 2.40 + 1.5 = **3.9**.`,e`Short-term for contrast: Z = 18 ÷ 5 = 3.6 → ≈ 159 PPM. The 1.20 vs 0.80 gap says the process drifts between days.`,e`**Exam-tools tip:** with one limit, Z = 3 × index, so you can go straight from Ppk 0.80 to Z 2.40 and the **standard normal table**; there is no second tail to add.`],answer:"Cpk 1.20 · Ppk 0.80 · ≈ 8,200 PPM above 48 h · ≈ 3.9σ",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Assume LSL = 0: Cp = (48 − 0) ÷ (6 × 5) = 1.6",whyWrong:"There is no lower specification; inventing one creates a fictitious specification width and an inflated, meaningless Cp."}},{title:"Estimating within σ from R̄/d₂",problem:e`Order-entry time for broadband orders: LSL = 10 min, USL = 30 min. Twenty subgroups of **n = 5** consecutive orders give $\bar R=4.652$ min and $\bar{\bar x}=21$ min. The overall standard deviation of all 100 orders is 2.5 min. Find $\sigma_{within}$, Cp, Cpk, Pp and Ppk.`,steps:[e`$d_2$ for n = 5 is 2.326, so $\sigma_{within}=4.652/2.326=$ **2.0 min**.`,e`Cp = (30 − 10) ÷ (6 × 2.0) = 20 ÷ 12 = **1.67**.`,e`CPU = (30 − 21) ÷ 6 = 1.50; CPL = (21 − 10) ÷ 6 = 1.83; Cpk = **1.50**.`,e`Pp = 20 ÷ (6 × 2.5) = **1.33**; Ppk = min(9, 11) ÷ 7.5 = **1.20**.`,e`Verdict: comfortably above 1.33 short-term; long-term Ppk 1.20 (≈ 165 PPM) shows some between-subgroup drift.`,e`**Exam-tools tip:** take $d_2$ from the **control-chart constants table** in the Reference Document for the subgroup size given (not the number of subgroups).`],answer:"σw = 2.0 · Cp 1.67 · Cpk 1.50 · Pp 1.33 · Ppk 1.20",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Cp = 20 ÷ (6 × 4.652) = 0.72",whyWrong:"R̄ is an average range, not a standard deviation. Divide it by d₂ first."}},{title:"Reproducing the book’s Minitab report (Supplier 1)",problem:e`From the book (pp. 218–219): LSL 598, USL 602, mean 599.115, StDev(Within) 0.559239, StDev(Overall) 0.602583. Recalculate the indices and expected PPM and interpret.`,steps:[e`Cp = 4 ÷ (6 × 0.559239) = **1.19**. CPL = 1.115 ÷ 1.6777 = **0.66**; CPU = 2.885 ÷ 1.6777 = 1.72; Cpk = **0.66**.`,e`Pp = 4 ÷ (6 × 0.602583) = **1.11**; PPL = 1.115 ÷ 1.8077 = **0.62**; Ppk = **0.62**.`,e`Overall $Z_{LSL}=1.115/0.602583=1.85$ → ≈ 32,130 PPM below LSL; above USL ≈ 1 PPM; total ≈ **32,131 PPM**; Z.bench overall ≈ **1.85**. Within: ≈ 23,088 PPM, Z.bench ≈ 1.99.`,e`These match the Minitab output printed in the book. Diagnosis: spread fits (Cp 1.19) but the mean is too low — centre it first.`,e`**Exam-tools tip:** compute $3\sigma$ once (1.6777) and reuse it for CPL and CPU; read Z = 1.85 in the **standard normal table** (tail 0.0322).`],answer:"Cp 1.19 · Cpk 0.66 · Pp 1.11 · Ppk 0.62 · ≈ 32,131 PPM · Z.bench 1.85 (overall)",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“Supplier 1 has Cp 0.66, so its spread does not fit.”",whyWrong:"0.66 is the Cpk (centring-adjusted). Cp is 1.19 — the spread does fit; the problem is location."}},{title:"Six Sigma in index terms: where 3.4 PPM comes from",problem:e`A process has specs at ±6σ from target. Over the long term the mean drifts 1.5σ towards the USL. Find Cp, Cpk and the expected PPM.`,steps:[e`Cp = 12σ ÷ 6σ = **2.0** (centring does not matter).`,e`Nearest limit is 6 − 1.5 = 4.5σ away: Cpk = 4.5 ÷ 3 = **1.5**.`,e`Tail beyond Z = 4.5 ≈ 0.0000034 → **≈ 3.4 PPM** (the far tail at Z = 7.5 is negligible).`,e`Without the shift, a centred Cp 2.0 process gives ≈ 0.002 PPM.`,e`**Exam-tools tip:** you will not find Z = 4.5 in most Z tables — use the **sigma-level/DPMO table** (6σ short-term ↔ 3.4 long-term DPMO).`],answer:"Cp 2.0 · Cpk 1.5 · ≈ 3.4 PPM",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Cpk = 6 ÷ 3 = 2.0, so 0.002 PPM",whyWrong:"This ignores the conventional 1.5σ long-term shift that defines the Six Sigma 3.4 DPMO figure."}},{title:"Latency: from PPM to Z.bench to sigma level",problem:e`Core-network latency must stay below USL = 20 ms. Long-term mean = 14 ms, $\sigma_{overall}=2.0$ ms; $\sigma_{within}=1.5$ ms. Find Ppk, expected long-term PPM, Z.bench and sigma level, and Cpk.`,steps:[e`Ppk = (20 − 14) ÷ (3 × 2.0) = **1.00**.`,e`Z = 6 ÷ 2 = 3.00 → tail 0.00135 → **≈ 1,350 PPM** above 20 ms.`,e`One-sided, so Z.bench (LT) = **3.00**; sigma level = 3.00 + 1.5 = **4.5σ**.`,e`Cpk = 6 ÷ (3 × 1.5) = **1.33** (short-term ≈ 32 PPM).`,e`**Exam-tools tip:** Z = 3.00 is a table value you should recognise (0.00135). Check yourself against the sigma table: 4.5σ lies between 4σ (6,210 DPMO) and 5σ (233 DPMO).`],answer:"Ppk 1.00 · ≈ 1,350 PPM · Z.bench 3.0 → 4.5σ · Cpk 1.33",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Sigma level = Z.bench = 3.0σ",whyWrong:"Overall Z.bench is long-term. The conventional sigma level is short-term: add 1.5."}},{title:"Choosing the lever: centre or reduce?",problem:e`For the AHT process (LSL 240, USL 480, mean 330, $\sigma_{within}=30$), compare (a) centring the mean at 360 s with (b) keeping the mean and reducing $\sigma_{within}$ to 22.5 s.`,steps:[e`(a) Centred: Cpk = 120 ÷ 90 = **1.33** = Cp; expected within PPM ≈ 2 × 31.7 ≈ **63**.`,e`(b) Reduced σ: Cp = 240 ÷ 135 = 1.78; CPL = 90 ÷ 67.5 = **1.33**; PPM ≈ **32** (only the near tail matters).`,e`Both reach Cpk 1.33. (a) is usually a coaching or routing adjustment; (b) needs a 25% cut in inherent variation — harder, but it also raises Cp.`,e`**Exam-tools tip:** centring never increases Cp — if an option claims it does, eliminate it.`],answer:"Both give Cpk 1.33; centring is the easier first move, reducing σ raises Cp as well",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“Centring raises Cp from 1.33 to 1.78.”",whyWrong:"Cp does not depend on the mean. Only reducing σ changes Cp."}}],lab:"capability",check:[{id:"chk-2.4.1-1",topic:"2.4.1",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A ticket-response process has LSL = 90 min and USL = 110 min. The mean is 104 min and $\\sigma_{within}$ = 2.5 min. What are Cp and Cpk?",options:["Cp 1.33, Cpk 0.80","Cp 0.80, Cpk 1.33","Cp 1.33, Cpk 1.87","Cp 2.67, Cpk 0.80"],answer:0,explanation:"Cp = 20 ÷ (6 × 2.5) = 1.33. CPU = 6 ÷ 7.5 = 0.80; CPL = 14 ÷ 7.5 = 1.87; Cpk = min = 0.80.",whyWrong:["","The two indices are swapped; Cpk can never exceed Cp.","1.87 is CPL — Cpk takes the minimum, not the maximum.","2.67 divides by 3σ instead of 6σ for Cp."],traps:["cp-vs-cpk"]},{id:"chk-2.4.1-2",topic:"2.4.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Which standard deviation should be used to calculate **Ppk**?",options:["Within-subgroup σ estimated from R̄/d₂","Pooled within-subgroup σ","Overall (long-term) sample standard deviation of all the data","The standard deviation of the subgroup means"],answer:2,explanation:"Pp and Ppk are performance indices and use the overall (long-term) σ, which includes between-subgroup variation. Cp and Cpk use within σ.",whyWrong:["R̄/d₂ estimates within σ — used for Cp and Cpk.","Pooled σ is also a within estimate.","","The σ of subgroup means describes only between-subgroup variation and is smaller than the spread of individual values."],traps:["short-vs-long-term"]},{id:"chk-2.4.1-3",topic:"2.4.1",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Priority-2 incidents have a mean resolution time of 100 min, σ = 5 min (normal, stable), and an upper specification of 110 min. About how many PPM exceed the USL?",options:["1,350","22,750","45,500","158,655"],answer:1,explanation:"Z = (110 − 100) ÷ 5 = 2.0; the standard normal tail beyond 2.0 is 0.02275 → 22,750 PPM.",whyWrong:["1,350 PPM is the tail beyond Z = 3.","","45,500 doubles the tail as if there were two limits.","158,655 is the tail beyond Z = 1."]},{id:"chk-2.4.1-4",topic:"2.4.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"A capability study reports **Cp = 1.6, Cpk = 1.5, Pp = 1.0, Ppk = 0.9**. What is the BEST interpretation?",options:["The process is badly off-centre; adjust the mean","The process is capable in the short term but shifts or drifts between subgroups over time","The specification limits are too tight and should be widened","The measurement system is perfect because Cp is high"],answer:1,explanation:"Cp ≈ Cpk shows good centring; the big drop from Cpk to Ppk shows overall variation is much larger than within variation — instability between subgroups.",whyWrong:["Cp and Cpk are close (1.6 vs 1.5), so centring is not the main issue.","","Nothing here shows the specs are invalid; the process loses capability over time.","Capability indices say nothing about the gage; MSA is a separate study."],traps:["short-vs-long-term","best"]},{id:"chk-2.4.1-5",topic:"2.4.1",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Long-term data give an overall **Z.bench of 3.0**. Using the conventional 1.5σ shift, what sigma level is reported?",options:["1.5σ","3.0σ","4.5σ","6.0σ"],answer:2,explanation:"Sigma level is quoted short-term: Z_ST = Z_LT + 1.5 = 3.0 + 1.5 = 4.5σ.",whyWrong:["This subtracts the shift instead of adding it.","This reports the long-term Z without conversion.","","This doubles the Z-value; there is no such rule."],traps:["short-vs-long-term"]},{id:"chk-2.4.1-6",topic:"2.4.1",category:"incorrect-statement",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement about capability analysis is **NOT** correct?",options:["Cpk can never be greater than Cp","For a specification with only a USL, Cp cannot be calculated","A process whose points all fall inside the control limits is capable of meeting the specification","Capability indices assume the data are approximately normal"],answer:2,explanation:"Control limits describe stability (voice of the process); specification limits describe customer requirements. A stable process can still be incapable.",whyWrong:["True: Cpk = Cp only when centred, otherwise lower.","True: without both limits there is no specification width.","","True: normal-based indices and PPM require approximate normality."],traps:["spec-vs-control-limits","not"]}],takeaways:["Capability compares the voice of the customer (USL − LSL) with the voice of the process (natural tolerance 6σ).","Validate stability, normality and the measurement system — and the specs themselves — before trusting any index.","Cp and Cpk use within (short-term) σ; Pp and Ppk use overall (long-term) σ, which is always the larger.","Cpk = min(CPU, CPL) ≤ Cp; the Cp–Cpk gap is a centring problem, the Cpk–Ppk gap a stability problem.","With one-sided specs, Cp and Pp do not exist — report CPU or CPL.","1.33 is the usual minimum; Six Sigma = Cp 2.0, Cpk 1.5 after the 1.5σ shift = 3.4 PPM.","Z = 3 × index; PPM comes from the standard normal tail; sigma level = Z.bench(LT) + 1.5.","Centring raises Cpk towards Cp; only reducing variation raises Cp itself."],flashcards:[{front:"Cp formula",back:"(USL − LSL) ÷ (6 × σ_within)."},{front:"Cpk formula",back:"min[(USL − x̄) ÷ 3σ_within, (x̄ − LSL) ÷ 3σ_within]."},{front:"Pp and Ppk differ from Cp and Cpk how?",back:"Same formulas, but they use overall (long-term) σ."},{front:"Cp ≫ Cpk means…",back:"The process is off-centre — centre the mean."},{front:"Cpk ≫ Ppk means…",back:"The process shifts or drifts between subgroups — a stability problem."},{front:"Within σ from ranges",back:"σ = R̄ ÷ d₂ (d₂ = 2.326 for n = 5)."},{front:"One-sided spec (USL only)",back:"Cp and Pp do not exist; Cpk = CPU, Ppk = PPU."},{front:"Centred Cp = 1.33 ≈ ? PPM",back:"≈ 63 PPM (Z = 4 on each side)."},{front:"Six Sigma in indices",back:"Cp 2.0; with 1.5σ shift Cpk 1.5 → ≈ 3.4 PPM."},{front:"Z from Cpk",back:"Z_min = 3 × Cpk (and Cpk = Z ÷ 3, book p. 224)."},{front:"Sigma level from long-term Z.bench",back:"Z_ST = Z_LT + 1.5."},{front:"Spec limits vs control limits",back:"Spec = customer’s voice (acceptability); control = process’s voice (stability)."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The pooled-σ formula, Minitab’s use of pooled σ as the default within estimate and M̄R/1.128 for individuals are standard Minitab/IASSC practice and are not printed on the supplied book pages.","The benchmark table (1.00 / 1.33 / 1.67 / 2.00 with PPM) and Z.bench’s definition are general Lean Six Sigma knowledge; PPM values were computed with the app’s verified normal functions. The book itself only states that a Cpk of at least 1.33 is desired (p. 225).","Book inconsistencies noted honestly: p. 218’s commentary calls Supplier 1’s .66 a “Cp index”, but the printed report shows Cp 1.19 and Cpk 0.66; short-term sample size is given as 30–50 on p. 212 and 20–30 on p. 213.","Telecom data (AHT, fibre installation, latency, order entry) are illustrative teaching figures, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 8",pdfPage:8,note:"Measure topic 2.4.1 “Capability Analysis” (2.4 Process Capability)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 207",pdfPage:10,note:"Definition of process capability; centring vs variation issues; baseline metric."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 208",pdfPage:11,note:"Capability compares VOP with VOC; ratio of variation width to specification width."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 209–210",pdfPage:12,note:"Incapable / off-target / capable pictures; shift the mean, reduce variation, both, or move specs."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 211–212",pdfPage:14,note:"Seven steps to capability; verifying specifications; short-term vs long-term data collection."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 213–215",pdfPage:16,note:"Baseline = long-term; snapshot vs video; 1.5σ drift; components of variation; stability and entitlement."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 216",pdfPage:19,note:"Cp/Pp as “hope”, Cpk/Ppk as “reality”; capability formulas."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 217–220",pdfPage:20,note:"Minitab camshaft example: normality check, Supplier 1 and 2 reports, Benchmark Z, short-term options."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 221",pdfPage:24,note:"Capability indices assume normal data; bimodal data warning."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 222–224",pdfPage:25,note:"Z-score example (mean 50, σ 3, USL 54 → 91,760 PPM); sigma/DPMO table with 1.5σ shift; Cpk = Z_ST/3, Ppk = Z_LT/3."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 225",pdfPage:28,note:"A Cpk of at least 1.33 is desired; Cpk short-term vs Ppk long-term."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 584",pdfPage:87,note:"Glossary: capability expressed as yield, defect rate, Cp/Cpk/Pp/Ppk or sigma score."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 585",pdfPage:88,note:"Glossary: Cp (spec width ÷ short-term performance width) and Cpk (offset-adjusted)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 586",pdfPage:89,note:"Glossary: long-term variation; lower specification limit."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 588",pdfPage:91,note:"Glossary: short-term variation; sigma score (Z)."}]},Z=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:$},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),u=String.raw,L={id:"2.4.2",module:8,title:"Concept of Stability",subtitle:"Common cause vs special cause — capability only means something when stable",minutes:13,objectives:["Distinguish common-cause from special-cause variation and explain why each needs a different response.","Define a stable (in-control) process as one whose behaviour is predictable, and explain why stability is not the same as capability.","Assess stability with a run chart and an I-MR control chart, applying the standard pattern tests (points beyond limits, shifts, trends, oscillation).","Calculate Individuals and Moving Range control limits by hand with X̄ ± 2.66·MR̄ and 3.267·MR̄.","Explain why control limits (voice of the process) must never be confused with specification limits (voice of the customer).","Recognise tampering — reacting to common-cause noise — and describe what it does to variation."],overview:u`Every process varies. Mean time to restore (MTTR) is never exactly the same two days running, a contact-centre agent never handles two calls in identical times, and weekly SLA compliance wobbles even when nothing has changed. The question that **stability** answers is not *"does it vary?"* but *"does it vary in a predictable way?"*

A process is **stable** — also called *in statistical control* — when only [[common-cause|common-cause variation]] is present: the routine, built-in noise of the system. When something unusual intrudes — a vendor outage, a new release, an untrained shift — it leaves a [[special-cause|special-cause]] signal. In Measure you plot the baseline over time, look for those signals and acknowledge them **before** you trust any capability number. This lesson shows you how, using a 30-day MTTR baseline in which a single vendor outage hides inside an otherwise well-behaved process.`,sections:[{id:"two-kinds",heading:"Two kinds of variation: common cause and special cause",body:u`Walter [[shewhart|Shewhart]] (Bell Laboratories, control charts from 1924) split variation into two kinds; W. Edwards [[deming|Deming]] popularised today's names.

**[[common-cause|Common-cause variation]]** is the natural, random variation produced by the process as designed: many small, unknown factors (ticket complexity, engineer experience, traffic levels, travel time to site) that are always present. It produces a steady, random scatter around the average — in the book's words, the variation *left over after special causes have been removed*.

**[[special-cause|Special-cause variation]]** (*assignable-cause*) comes from a specific, identifiable source that is **not** part of the everyday process: a fibre cut, a vendor outage, a mis-configured release. The book names two types:

- **Assignable** — a point that is too far from the centre to be chance;
- **Pattern change** — a trend, shift or unusual pattern even though every point is within the limits.

| | Common cause | Special cause |
|---|---|---|
| Source | The system itself — many small, ever-present factors | A specific event or change outside the normal system |
| Looks like | Random scatter inside a predictable band | Point beyond a limit, shift, trend, cycle, oscillation |
| Predictable? | Yes — within limits | No |
| Who usually fixes it | Management / a project that redesigns the process | The people closest to the process, once found |
| Right response | Leave it alone day to day; improve the system with DMAIC | Investigate now, find the cause, remove it (or keep it if good) |

Deming estimated that around 94% of problems belong to the system, not to individuals — so blaming a team for a common-cause bad day is unfair and ineffective.`},{id:"stable-predictable",heading:"Stable means predictable — not good",body:u`The book's glossary defines a stable process as one that *shows no recognisable pattern of change and no special causes of variation*. A stable process is **consistent over time**, so you can predict the range of tomorrow's result. That is all stability promises.

Stability says nothing about whether the customer is happy. **Capability** compares the process with the requirement (the [[specification-limits|specification limits]] or SLA). The two questions are independent, which gives four possibilities:

| | **Capable** (spread fits inside the spec) | **Not capable** (spread spills outside the spec) |
|---|---|---|
| **Stable** | Predictable *and* meets the requirement. Monitor it; improve cost or speed if worthwhile. | Predictably bad. Defects come from the common-cause system — a DMAIC project must change the process. |
| **Unstable** | Meets the requirement *today*, but cannot be trusted tomorrow. Find and remove special causes first. | Worst case. Remove special causes first, then shift the mean and/or reduce variation. |

**Why capability requires stability.** Cp, Cpk, Pp and Ppk (lesson [2.4.1](#/lessons/2.4.1)) use one mean and one standard deviation to predict future performance. If the process is unstable, there is no single distribution to describe — the mean or spread is moving — so a capability index is only a snapshot of the past, not a prediction. The book's Measure roadmap therefore puts **Assess Stability (Statistical Control)** immediately *before* **Assess Capability**, and notes that capability studies are *most meaningful when conducted on stable, predictable processes*.

One nuance: the book also warns *do not try to make your process stable before working on it*. In Measure you still record a baseline capability — it "draws a line in the sand" — while flagging any instability and the special causes found. Removing them is often the project's fastest early win.`},{id:"assess",heading:"How to assess stability: run charts and control charts",body:u`Stability is judged on **time-ordered** data — never on a histogram, which throws the order away. The book names the [[time-series-plot|time series plot]] (run chart) and the [[control-chart|control chart]].

**1 · Run chart.** Plot the data in time order with a **median** line and look for four non-random patterns (Minitab's Run Chart gives each an approximate p-value; below 0.05 suggests the pattern is present):

| Pattern | What you see | What it suggests |
|---|---|---|
| **Clustering** | Groups of points close together in one area (too few runs about the median) | Measurement problems, batch or shift effects |
| **Mixtures** | Points alternate between two levels, few near the median (too many runs) | Two processes or sources mixed in one data set |
| **Trends** | A sustained drift up or down | Wear, growing backlog, learning, fatigue |
| **Oscillation** | Rapid saw-tooth up and down | Over-adjustment (tampering) or an unstable control loop |

**2 · Control chart.** A time series plot with a centre line and **control limits calculated from the data themselves**, normally ±3σ of the short-term (within) variation. For a Normal process 99.73% of points fall inside ±3σ, so common cause alone puts a point outside only about 0.27% of the time (≈ 0.135% each side) — something special has probably happened.

For one value per period (daily MTTR, weekly SLA %) use the [[i-mr-chart|Individuals & Moving Range (I-MR) chart]]. Quick view (full method in [5.2.2 I-MR Chart](#/lessons/5.2.2)):

$$MR_i=|x_i-x_{i-1}|,\qquad \overline{MR}=\frac{\sum MR_i}{k-1}$$

$$UCL_X=\bar{X}+2.66\,\overline{MR},\quad CL_X=\bar{X},\quad LCL_X=\bar{X}-2.66\,\overline{MR}$$

$$UCL_{MR}=3.267\,\overline{MR},\qquad LCL_{MR}=0,\qquad \hat\sigma_{within}=\overline{MR}/1.128$$

The constants come from the control-chart table for a moving range of two points: $d_2 = 1.128$, so $3/d_2 = 2.66$ (the book's $E_2$), and $D_4 = 3.267$.

**Signals that the process is not stable** (the book lists eight tests; these four apply to every chart, including MR charts):

1. One point more than 3σ from the centre line — the most common test.
2. Nine points in a row on the same side of the centre line — a **shift** in the mean.
3. Six points in a row all increasing or all decreasing — a **trend** (the book notes you need seven plotted points to see six consecutive rises).
4. Fourteen points in a row alternating up and down — **oscillation**, a non-random pattern.

The other four tests (2 of 3 beyond 2σ, 4 of 5 beyond 1σ, 15 hugging the centre, 8 avoiding it) are covered in [5.2.10 Control Methods](#/lessons/5.2.10). Choose a rule set and use it consistently.

> **Exam-tools tip.** You have the formula Reference Document, a notepad and an on-screen calculator. List the moving ranges on the notepad, add them, divide by *k − 1* (not *k*), then multiply MR̄ by **2.66** (I-chart half-width) and **3.267** (MR-chart UCL). Check the constants in the reference table for *n* = 2 rather than trusting memory under pressure.`},{id:"limits-vs-specs",heading:"Control limits are not specification limits",body:u`This is the single most tested trap in the topic.

| | **Control limits** | **Specification limits** |
|---|---|---|
| Voice of… | The process ([[vop|VOP]]) | The customer or business ([[voc|VOC]]) |
| Where they come from | **Calculated** from process data (X̄ ± 3σ within) | **Set** by customers, contracts (SLA), regulation or design |
| Answer the question | Is the process stable / predictable? | Is the output acceptable? |
| Plotted on a control chart? | Yes | **No** — the book advises never to put specs on a control chart |

Consequences to reason through:

- **Inside the spec, outside the control limits** — the customer is fine today, but something unusual happened: investigate.
- **Inside the control limits, outside the spec** — normal behaviour that is not good enough: a capability problem, not a special cause.
- Control limits **wider** than the spec limits — stable, but defects are routine (Cp < 1).
- *In control* never means *meeting requirements*; the book says it means stable, consistent and predictable — nothing more.`},{id:"tampering",heading:"Responding wrongly: tampering and Deming’s funnel",body:u`Both **wrong** responses are expensive:

- **Treating common cause as special** (*tampering* or over-adjustment): the team investigates or "fixes" every bad day. Effort is wasted, people are blamed for noise, and the adjustments themselves add variation.
- **Treating special cause as common**: a real signal (the vendor outage) is averaged into the baseline and nobody asks what happened, so it happens again.

Deming illustrated tampering with his **funnel experiment**: marbles are dropped through a funnel onto a target.

| Funnel rule | What the operator does | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 — Leave it alone | Funnel stays aimed at the target | Minimum variation — the common-cause spread |
| 2 — Compensate for the last error | Move the funnel from its *last position* by the opposite of the last miss | Spread grows to about √2 ≈ 1.41× (variance doubles) |
| 3 — Compensate relative to the target | Set the funnel at the target minus the last miss | Swings grow larger and larger — unstable |
| 4 — Aim where the last one landed | Move the funnel over the last drop | A random walk that drifts away from the target |

The book makes the same point: variability **increases** when operators adjust on every point away from the centre line, so *with no out-of-control signal, make no adjustment*. In service operations tampering looks like re-prioritising the queue after every slow day or changing dispatch rules weekly — oscillation on a run chart is often its fingerprint.`},{id:"telecom-mttr",heading:"Telecom walk-through: 30 days of MTTR with one vendor outage",body:u`A Digital Service Operations team baselines **daily mean time to restore** for priority-2 incidents over 30 days; the SLA requires MTTR ≤ **6.0 h**. (The full arithmetic is in the worked examples below.)

1. **Run chart.** Values move between 3.7 and 4.8 h around a 4.2 h median with no trend or cycle — except **day 18 at 7.9 h**.
2. **I-MR limits from all 30 days:** MR̄ = 14.4 ÷ 29 = 0.4966 h, so the I chart runs **2.99 – 5.63 h**. Day 18 is beyond the UCL (Test 1), and its moving ranges also break the MR-chart UCL of 1.62 h. Nothing else fires.
3. **Investigate.** The incident log shows a transmission-vendor platform outage that held every P2 ticket in a queue — a genuine **assignable cause**. It is acknowledged in the Measure report and passed to vendor management as a request to clarify the outage timeline and provide evidence of the preventive actions taken.
4. **Recompute without the explained point:** MR̄ falls to 0.2643 h and the limits tighten to **3.49 – 4.89 h**; no tests fire. One special cause had nearly doubled the width of the "normal" band and could have hidden smaller signals.
5. **Stable and capable?** The common-cause band tops out at 4.89 h, well under the 6.0 h SLA. The routine process is stable and capable; the only breach came from a special cause, so the project should target vendor-outage prevention — not a redesign of restoration.

Only remove a point from the limit calculation once its cause is **found and documented**; unexplained outliers stay in and the process is reported as unstable.`}],whyItMatters:u`Stability is the gatekeeper for everything else in Measure. A capability index, a sigma level or a baseline in the project charter is only trustworthy as a *prediction* if the process is stable; otherwise it is a description of the past. The book lists **stability graphs** among the Measure deliverables you present to your Champion, and the Measure checklist asks: *Is the voice of the process stable? If not, have the special causes been acknowledged? Can the good signals be incorporated and the bad ones removed?*

For a service-operations engineer, stability thinking separates "a bad day with a specific cause — let us find it" from "how the system performs — blaming people will not change it". It stops tampering with noise, and stops special causes being averaged quietly into monthly SLA figures.`,businessExample:{title:"Monthly SLA review: was February really worse?",body:u`A managed-services vendor's weekly P1 response-time compliance has hovered around 95–97% for six months. February comes in at 94.6% and the monthly review proposes a corrective action plan.

The OE engineer first plots the 26 weekly values on an I-MR chart. February's weeks are **inside** the control limits and no run test fires — common-cause noise, the same variation that produced 95% in October without comment. A corrective action would be tampering.

One week in January, however, showed 89% — **below the LCL** — just after an untested configuration push. That is the real special cause, and the review is redirected: the vendor is asked to clarify what happened and to provide supporting evidence of the change controls now in place. Because the stable band itself dips below the 95% target at times, the engineer also records the process as **stable but not fully capable** — a candidate for a DMAIC project on the common-cause system.`},visual:"stabilityRunChart",visualCaption:"Thirty days of P2 MTTR. The run chart appears first, then the common-cause band from I-MR limits (2.99–5.63 h). Day 18 (7.9 h, a vendor outage) is flagged as a special cause; once it is explained and removed, the limits tighten to 3.49–4.89 h. The SLA limit of 6.0 h is drawn separately to show that control limits are not specification limits, and the 2×2 places the process as stable and capable.",definitions:[{term:"Common-cause variation",definition:"Natural, random variation from many small, ever-present factors in the process as designed; predictable within limits."},{term:"Special-cause (assignable-cause) variation",definition:"Non-random variation from a specific, identifiable source outside the normal process, shown as an out-of-limit point or a non-random pattern."},{term:"Stable process (in statistical control)",definition:"A process showing no recognisable pattern of change and no special causes; its future output is predictable within limits."},{term:"Unstable process (out of control)",definition:"A process with one or more special-cause signals; its future performance cannot be predicted reliably."},{term:"Run chart",definition:"A time-ordered plot of data with a median line, used to spot clustering, mixtures, trends and oscillation."},{term:"Control chart",definition:"A time-ordered plot with a centre line and control limits (normally ±3σ within) calculated from the process data."},{term:"Control limits",definition:"The voice of the process: boundaries of common-cause variation calculated from the data, used to judge stability."},{term:"Specification limits",definition:"The voice of the customer or business: boundaries of acceptable output set externally (SLA, contract, design)."},{term:"Moving range (MR)",definition:"The absolute difference between consecutive individual values; MR̄ estimates short-term variation (σ ≈ MR̄ / 1.128)."},{term:"I-MR chart",definition:"A pair of control charts for individual values and moving ranges, used when each measurement is one data point per period."},{term:"Tampering",definition:"Adjusting a stable process in response to common-cause variation; it increases variation (Deming’s funnel rules 2–4)."},{term:"Entitlement",definition:"The best short-term performance a process has already demonstrated, visible as a tight stable stretch on a time series plot."}],comparisons:[{title:"Common cause vs special cause",headers:["","Common cause","Special cause"],rows:[["Also called","Chance, random, natural, inherent","Assignable, unnatural, exceptional"],["Source","Many small factors built into the system","A specific event or change"],["On a control chart","Random points inside the limits","Point beyond a limit, or a shift / trend / pattern"],["Telecom example","Day-to-day MTTR scatter from ticket mix and travel time","Day 18 MTTR spike from a vendor platform outage"],["Correct response","Do not react point by point; improve the system (DMAIC)","Investigate immediately; remove (or adopt, if beneficial)"],["Wrong response","Tampering — chasing noise","Ignoring it — averaging it into the baseline"]]},{title:"Control limits vs specification limits",headers:["","Control limits","Specification limits"],rows:[["Voice of","The process (VOP)","The customer / business (VOC)"],["Origin","Calculated from data: X̄ ± 3σ within","Set by SLA, contract, regulation, design"],["Question answered","Is it stable and predictable?","Is the output acceptable?"],["MTTR example","3.49 – 4.89 h (after the outage day is removed)","MTTR ≤ 6.0 h"],["Belongs on","Control chart","Capability study, histogram, SLA report"]]},{title:"Run chart vs I-MR control chart for stability",headers:["","Run chart","I-MR control chart"],rows:[["Reference line","Median","Mean (centre line) plus ±3σ control limits"],["Detects","Clustering, mixtures, trends, oscillation","Points beyond limits plus run rules (shift, trend, oscillation…)"],["Needs a σ estimate?","No","Yes — MR̄ / 1.128"],["Best for","Quick first look, small data sets, early Measure","Formal stability assessment and ongoing monitoring"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"A stable process is a good process.",reality:"Stable means predictable. A process can be stable and still miss the SLA every week — that is a capability problem."},{myth:"If every point is inside the control limits, the process is stable.",reality:"Run rules matter too: nine points on one side, a six-point trend or fourteen alternating points are special-cause signals even inside the limits."},{myth:"Control limits can be set from the SLA target or specification.",reality:"Control limits are calculated from process data (voice of the process). Specs come from the customer and never belong on a control chart."},{myth:"Any bad result deserves a root-cause investigation and a corrective action.",reality:"Reacting to common-cause variation is tampering and makes the process worse. Investigate signals; improve the system for common-cause problems."},{myth:"You can delete an out-of-control point to make the baseline look stable.",reality:"Exclude a point only after its special cause has been found and documented; otherwise it stays in and the process is reported as unstable."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“The process is in statistical control, therefore it meets customer requirements.”",explanation:"Control (stability) and capability are independent. In-control only means predictable; compare the output with the specification to judge capability.",tag:"spec-vs-control-limits"},{trap:"A point lies inside the specification limits but outside the control limits — options say “no action needed”.",explanation:"It is a special-cause signal: investigate. The spec only tells you the customer is unaffected today.",tag:"spec-vs-control-limits"},{trap:"“What should be verified FIRST, before calculating Cp and Cpk?”",explanation:"Process stability (statistical control) — after the measurement system is confirmed adequate. Capability indices assume a stable process.",tag:"first"},{trap:"All points are within the limits but nine consecutive points sit above the centre line.",explanation:"That is a shift — a special-cause pattern. “All points inside the limits” is not enough to call the process stable.",tag:"most"},{trap:"I-MR limits computed as X̄ ± 3s, where s is the overall sample standard deviation.",explanation:"Control limits use the short-term (within) estimate: X̄ ± 2.66·MR̄ (σ = MR̄/1.128). The overall s is inflated by any shifts or special causes.",tag:"short-vs-long-term"},{trap:"MR̄ calculated by dividing the sum of moving ranges by the number of data points.",explanation:"k data points give k − 1 moving ranges; divide by k − 1."},{trap:"“Which is NOT a special-cause signal?” with one option “a single point close to, but inside, the UCL”.",explanation:"A lone point inside the limits is not a signal on its own. Points beyond 3σ, 9-in-a-row, 6-point trends and 14-point oscillation are.",tag:"not"}],workedExamples:[{title:"I-MR stability check on 30 days of MTTR",problem:u`Daily P2 MTTR (hours) for 30 days gives X̄ = 4.3133 and a sum of the 29 moving ranges of 14.4. Day 18 is 7.9 h. Calculate the I-chart and MR-chart limits and decide whether the process is stable.`,steps:["MR̄ = 14.4 ÷ 29 = **0.4966 h** (k − 1 = 29 moving ranges).","Half-width = 2.66 × 0.4966 = 1.321 h.","UCL = 4.3133 + 1.321 = **5.634 h**; LCL = 4.3133 − 1.321 = **2.993 h**.","MR-chart UCL = 3.267 × 0.4966 = **1.622 h**; LCL = 0.","Day 18 (7.9 h) > 5.634 → Test 1 fires; its moving ranges (3.5 and 4.1 h) also exceed 1.622 h.","Conclusion: **not stable** — one special cause. Investigate day 18 before quoting any capability."],answer:"I chart 2.993 – 5.634 h (CL 4.313); MR UCL 1.622 h; day 18 is a special cause, so the baseline is unstable.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"UCL = 4.3133 + 3 × 0.720 (overall s) = 6.47 h → “day 18 is still a special cause, and everything else is fine”.",whyWrong:"Using the overall standard deviation inflates the limits because it includes the outage itself. Here the conclusion survives by luck, but a smaller special cause would be hidden. Use σ within = MR̄ / 1.128."}},{title:"Recompute after the special cause is explained",problem:u`Investigation confirms day 18 was a transmission-vendor outage. Without that day, the 29 values have X̄ = 4.1897 and ΣMR = 7.4 over 28 moving ranges. The SLA is MTTR ≤ 6.0 h. Is the remaining process stable and capable?`,steps:["MR̄ = 7.4 ÷ 28 = **0.2643 h**; σ within = 0.2643 ÷ 1.128 = **0.234 h**.","UCL = 4.1897 + 2.66 × 0.2643 = **4.893 h**; LCL = 4.1897 − 0.703 = **3.487 h**.","No point lies outside 3.487 – 4.893 h and no run rule fires → **stable**.","Compare with the spec: UCL 4.89 h is well below the 6.0 h SLA. One-sided within capability ≈ (6.0 − 4.19) ÷ (3 × 0.234) ≈ **2.6**.","Stable and capable: the only SLA breach came from a special cause, so the project should target vendor-outage prevention and containment."],answer:"Limits 3.487 – 4.893 h; stable; capable against the 6.0 h SLA (≈ 2.6). Focus on the special cause.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“One day breached the 6-hour SLA, so the process is incapable — redesign the whole restoration workflow.”",whyWrong:"That judges the common-cause system by a special-cause event. The routine process sits comfortably inside the SLA; redesigning it is effort spent on the wrong problem (and a form of tampering)."}},{title:"Hand calculation: eight call handle times",problem:u`Eight consecutive average handle times (minutes): 11, 12, 12, 13, 14, 13, 15, 14. Calculate the I-MR limits.`,steps:["X̄ = 104 ÷ 8 = **13.0 min**.","Moving ranges: 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1 → sum 7; MR̄ = 7 ÷ 7 = **1.0 min**.","UCL = 13 + 2.66 × 1.0 = **15.66**; LCL = 13 − 2.66 = **10.34**.","MR UCL = 3.267 × 1.0 = **3.267**; σ within = 1.0 ÷ 1.128 = 0.887 min.","All points and all moving ranges are inside their limits."],answer:"I chart 10.34 – 15.66 min (CL 13.0); MR chart 0 – 3.267 min.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"X̄ ± 3s with s = 1.309 → 9.07 – 16.93 min.",whyWrong:"The overall s (1.309) is larger than σ within (0.887) because the data drift upward over the eight calls. Overall s absorbs that drift and gives limits about 50% wider, making the chart blind to real shifts."}},{title:"Inside the limits but not stable: a trend",problem:u`Daily repeat-call counts for 12 days: 20, 22, 19, 21, 23, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25. I-MR limits are 16.99 – 26.18 (CL 21.58). Is the process stable?`,steps:["Test 1: every point lies between 16.99 and 26.18 → no point beyond the limits.","Look at the order: from day 6 (19) the values rise 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25 — six consecutive increases across seven points.","Test 3 (six points in a row all increasing) fires by day 12 at the latest; software that counts six points rather than six rises flags day 11.","Conclusion: **not stable** — a trend special cause. Ask what changed around day 6 (a release, a knowledge-base article withdrawn, a new channel)."],answer:"Unstable: a six-step upward trend from day 6 to day 12, despite all points being inside the limits.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“No point is outside 16.99 – 26.18, so the process is in control.”",whyWrong:"Points-beyond-limits is only one test. Shifts, trends and oscillation are special-cause patterns even when every point is within the limits."}}],lab:"stability",check:[{id:"chk-2.4.2-1",topic:"2.4.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A process is in statistical control. Which statement is **always** true?",options:["It meets the customer specification limits","Its future output is predictable within the control limits","Its Cpk is at least 1.33","It contains no variation"],answer:1,explanation:"In control (stable) means only common-cause variation is present, so output is predictable within the control limits. It says nothing about meeting specifications.",whyWrong:["Stability and capability are independent — a stable process can sit outside the spec.","","Cpk depends on the specification; a stable process can have any Cpk.","Every process has common-cause variation."],traps:["spec-vs-control-limits"]},{id:"chk-2.4.2-2",topic:"2.4.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Five daily values are 10, 12, 11, 13, 9. What is the upper control limit of the **moving range** chart?",options:["2.25","7.35","16.99","5.99"],answer:1,explanation:"Moving ranges: 2, 1, 2, 4 → sum 9; MR̄ = 9 ÷ 4 = 2.25. UCL(MR) = 3.267 × 2.25 = 7.35.",whyWrong:["2.25 is MR̄, the centre line of the MR chart.","","16.99 is the I-chart UCL (11 + 2.66 × 2.25).","5.99 uses 2.66 instead of 3.267 (2.66 × 2.25) — the wrong constant for the MR chart."]},{id:"chk-2.4.2-3",topic:"2.4.2",category:"scenario",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"On an I-MR chart of daily SLA compliance, one day falls **inside the specification** but **below the lower control limit**. What should the team do?",options:["Nothing — the SLA was met","Investigate for a special cause","Widen the control limits to include the point","Recalculate the specification limits"],answer:1,explanation:"A point beyond a control limit is a special-cause signal regardless of the specification. Investigate what happened that day.",whyWrong:["Meeting the spec does not mean the process behaved normally; control limits judge stability, not acceptability.","","Control limits are calculated from data, not adjusted to swallow inconvenient points.","Specifications come from the customer and are unrelated to this signal."],traps:["spec-vs-control-limits"]},{id:"chk-2.4.2-4",topic:"2.4.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which pattern on a control chart is **NOT** by itself evidence of a special cause?",options:["Nine consecutive points above the centre line","Six consecutive points steadily decreasing","A single point just inside the upper control limit","Fourteen consecutive points alternating up and down"],answer:2,explanation:"A lone point inside the limits is consistent with common-cause variation. The other three are standard run-rule signals (shift, trend, oscillation).",whyWrong:["Nine in a row on one side is the shift test.","Six in a row steadily moving is the trend test.","","Fourteen alternating points is the oscillation test."],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-2.4.2-5",topic:"2.4.2",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A stable process has control limits of 3.5 – 4.9 hours for MTTR. The SLA requires MTTR ≤ 4.0 hours. How should the process be described?",options:["Unstable and capable","Stable but not capable","Stable and capable","Unstable and not capable"],answer:1,explanation:"It is stable (in control), but its common-cause band extends well above the 4.0 h requirement, so it will routinely breach the SLA — not capable. The fix is a change to the system, not a special-cause hunt.",whyWrong:["The process is stated to be stable.","","Much of the natural band lies above 4.0 h, so it is not capable.","It is stable — no special causes."],traps:["spec-vs-control-limits"]},{id:"chk-2.4.2-6",topic:"2.4.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"A supervisor changes the dispatch rule every time the previous day’s MTTR is above average, and reverses it when it is below. The process was stable. What is the **most likely** effect?",options:["Variation decreases because problems are corrected quickly","No effect, because the adjustments cancel out","Variation increases — this is tampering","The mean shifts permanently towards the target"],answer:2,explanation:"Adjusting a stable process in response to common-cause variation is tampering (Deming’s funnel rules 2–4); it adds variation rather than removing it.",whyWrong:["The “problems” are common-cause noise; reacting to them adds variation.","The adjustments react to random noise and add their own variation; they do not cancel.","","Tampering does not fix the mean; it inflates spread and can even make the process drift."],traps:["most"]}],takeaways:["Common-cause variation is the system’s built-in noise; special-cause variation comes from a specific, identifiable event.","Stable = predictable within limits. It does not mean good — stability and capability are independent (the 2×2).","Capability indices assume stability; assess stability first, but still record a baseline snapshot in Measure.","Judge stability on time-ordered data: run chart (clustering, mixtures, trends, oscillation) and control chart (beyond 3σ, 9-in-a-row, 6-point trend, 14 alternating).","I-MR: MR̄ = ΣMR ÷ (k − 1); limits X̄ ± 2.66·MR̄; MR UCL = 3.267·MR̄; σ within = MR̄ / 1.128.","Control limits are calculated (voice of the process); spec limits are set (voice of the customer). Never mix them.","Reacting to common cause is tampering and increases variation; ignoring special causes lets them recur.","Remove a point from the limit calculation only after its special cause is found and documented."],flashcards:[{front:"Common-cause variation",back:"Natural, random variation from many small, ever-present factors; predictable within limits."},{front:"Special-cause variation",back:"Assignable, non-random variation from a specific source; shows as a point beyond limits or a pattern."},{front:"Stable process",back:"No special causes and no recognisable pattern of change — predictable (not necessarily capable)."},{front:"Why assess stability before capability?",back:"Cp/Cpk use one mean and σ to predict the future; an unstable process has no single distribution to predict."},{front:"I-chart limits",back:"X̄ ± 2.66 × MR̄ (2.66 = 3 ÷ 1.128)."},{front:"MR-chart UCL",back:"3.267 × MR̄ (D4 for n = 2); LCL = 0."},{front:"σ within from MR̄",back:"MR̄ ÷ 1.128 (d2 for n = 2)."},{front:"MR̄ denominator",back:"k − 1 (k values give k − 1 moving ranges)."},{front:"Four run-chart patterns",back:"Clustering, mixtures, trends, oscillation."},{front:"Shift / trend / oscillation tests",back:"9 in a row on one side; 6 in a row all rising or falling; 14 in a row alternating."},{front:"Control limits vs spec limits",back:"Control = calculated, voice of the process (stability). Spec = set externally, voice of the customer (capability)."},{front:"Tampering",back:"Adjusting a stable process in response to common-cause noise — it increases variation (Deming’s funnel)."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The run-chart pattern tests (clustering, mixtures, trends, oscillation) and their p-values follow Minitab’s Run Chart convention; they are not described in the supplied book pages, which use time series plots and control-chart tests instead.","Deming’s funnel experiment, the “94% belongs to the system” estimate and the attribution of the common/special-cause names to Deming are general quality-management history, not taken from the supplied pages. The book supports the underlying point that adjusting on every point increases variability (p. 544).","The stable/capable 2×2 matrix is an independently written teaching device built on the book’s statement that in-control does not mean capable (p. 518).","The constants 2.66, 3.267 and 1.128 are the standard control-chart constants for a moving range of two; the book shows the I-MR formulas symbolically with E2 and D4 (p. 538).","All MTTR, handle-time, repeat-call and SLA figures are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data. Every limit was computed with the app’s verified statistics engine."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 8",pdfPage:8,note:"Measure topic 2.4.2 “Concept of Stability”."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 209",pdfPage:12,note:"Variability is tolerable when small relative to requirements and the process shows long-term stability (predictable)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 211",pdfPage:14,note:"A stable process is consistent with time; time series plots and control charts check stability; capability studies are most meaningful on stable processes."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 212",pdfPage:15,note:"Short-term data potentially free of special causes; long-term data subject to both common and special causes."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 215",pdfPage:18,note:"Stability established with a time series plot or control chart; no reason to assume stability in Measure; entitlement region."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 217",pdfPage:20,note:"Baseline capability is a snapshot; do not try to make the process stable before working on it."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 229–231",pdfPage:32,note:"Measure deliverables include stability graphs; roadmap “Assess Stability (Statistical Control)” before “Assess Capability”; stability assessment checklist."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 158",pdfPage:61,note:"Special cause (assignable, known factors) vs common cause (unknown factors, random, left after special causes removed)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 518",pdfPage:21,note:"Specification limits are not evaluated on a control chart; in control does not mean capable; I-MR chart description."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"pp. 523–525",pdfPage:26,note:"Common vs special cause (assignable, pattern change); Shewhart control charts; never put specs on a control chart; ±3σ limits are the voice of the process."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 527",pdfPage:30,note:"99.73% inside ±3σ; about 0.15% beyond each limit, so a point outside is attributed to a special cause."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"pp. 532–536",pdfPage:35,note:"The eight special-cause tests; four apply to all charts; nine-in-a-row shift, six-point trend (seven plotted points), fourteen alternating."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 538",pdfPage:41,note:"I-MR centre line and control-limit formulas (X̄ ± E2·MR̄, D4·MR̄)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 544",pdfPage:47,note:"Adjusting on every point increases variability; act only on out-of-control signals."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 589",pdfPage:92,note:"Glossary: stability of a process; special cause variation; trend."}]},U=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:L},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),t=String.raw,I={id:"2.4.3",module:8,title:"Attribute & Discrete Capability",subtitle:"Capability for defects and defectives: DPMO, PPM and Z.bench",minutes:13,objectives:["Explain why Cp/Cpk formulas cannot be applied directly to pass/fail or count data, and follow the book’s attribute capability steps.","Distinguish defectives (binomial: proportion defective) from defects (Poisson: DPU, DPMO) and choose the right capability measure for each.","Convert a proportion defective or a DPMO into PPM and a long-term Z.bench using the standard normal table.","Convert long-term Z to short-term sigma level (Z_ST = Z_LT + 1.5) and read the sigma–DPMO table in both directions.","Express attribute capability as approximate Cpk and Ppk (Z ÷ 3) and recognise the book’s call-centre example, including its printed inconsistency.","Apply discrete capability to telecom metrics such as failed activations and billing errors per 1,000 invoices."],overview:t`Not every CTQ is a measurement. A SIM activation either succeeds or fails; an invoice either carries billing errors or it does not; a call is answered or dropped. For such outputs there is no mean and standard deviation to drop into $C_{pk}=\min(USL-\bar x,\ \bar x-LSL)/3\sigma$, and the book puts it plainly (p. 221): following the continuous capability steps with attribute data, "we hit a wall" at the normality step, so a different method is used.

That method is simple and powerful: **count the failures, turn them into a rate, and convert the rate into a Z-value** using the normal table. From there you can report [[ppm|PPM]], [[dpmo|DPMO]], [[z-bench|Z.bench]], a sigma level, or approximate Cpk/Ppk — the same language as continuous capability, so a failed-activation rate can be compared with an installation-time Cpk on one dashboard.`,sections:[{id:"attribute-steps",heading:"Why discrete capability is different",body:t`For continuous data, capability starts with a fitted normal curve. For [[attribute-data|attribute]] and [[discrete-data|discrete]] data, the out-of-spec fraction is **observed directly** — you already know how many units failed. The book's attribute capability steps (p. 222) replace the normality check:

1. Select the output → 2. verify customer requirements → 3. validate the specification (what counts as a defect) → 4. collect sample data → 5. **calculate DPU** (or the proportion defective) → 6. **find the Z-score** from the normal table → 7. **convert Z to Cp & Cpk** (or report sigma level).

Two facts drive everything that follows:

- **Attribute data are long-term.** The book (p. 223) explains that attribute data are always treated as long-term, "shifted" data, because so many samples — gathered over a long period — are needed to estimate a rate with reasonable confidence. The Z you calculate is therefore $Z_{LT}$.
- **Short-term capability is usually what gets reported,** so the 1.5σ [[sigma-shift|shift]] is *added* to estimate it: $Z_{ST}=Z_{LT}+1.5$.

Before calculating, settle the operational definitions agreed in Measure: what is a **unit**, what is a **[[defect|defect]]**, what makes a unit **[[defective|defective]]**, and — if you will report DPMO — how many **[[opportunity|opportunities]]** each unit has. Changing these definitions mid-project changes the capability more than any improvement would.`},{id:"defectives",heading:"Defectives: proportion defective, PPM and Z.bench (binomial)",body:t`When each unit is classified **good or bad** — activation succeeded or failed, call answered or abandoned, ticket within SLA or breached — the count of bad units follows the **[[binomial-distribution|binomial distribution]]** (fixed number of units, each independent, with the same chance $p$ of failing). Capability is built from the **proportion defective**:

$$\hat p=\frac{\text{defective units}}{\text{units inspected}}\qquad \text{PPM}=\hat p\times10^6\qquad \text{Yield}=1-\hat p$$

Then find the Z that leaves $\hat p$ in one tail of the standard normal curve:

$$Z_{bench,LT}=\Phi^{-1}(1-\hat p)\qquad Z_{ST}=Z_{LT}+1.5$$

**Telecom example — failed activations.** In one quarter, **20,000** SIM activations were attempted and **340** failed.

- $\hat p=340/20{,}000=0.017$ → yield 98.3% → **17,000 PPM**.
- $Z_{LT}=\Phi^{-1}(0.983)\approx$ **2.12** (table: area 0.0170 beyond Z ≈ 2.12).
- Sigma level $=2.12+1.5\approx$ **3.62σ**.
- Book-style indices (p. 224): $P_{pk}\approx Z_{LT}/3=$ **0.71**; $C_{pk}\approx Z_{ST}/3=$ **1.21**.

The Cpk here is an *estimate implied by the shift convention*, not a measured within-subgroup capability — no within σ exists for pass/fail data. Treat it as a translation into a familiar scale.

> **Stability first, here too.** A single overall proportion is only a fair capability if the rate is stable over time. Plot it on a **[[p-chart|p-chart]]** (lesson 5.2) first; if one week's migration spike produced half the failures, the "capability" mixes two different processes.`},{id:"defects",heading:"Defects: DPU, DPMO and Z (Poisson)",body:t`When a unit can carry **several** defects — wrong tariff *and* wrong VAT *and* duplicate charge on one invoice — you count defects, and the count per unit follows the **[[poisson-distribution|Poisson distribution]]** (defects occur independently and at a constant average rate within an "area of opportunity"). Capability is built from:

$$\text{DPU}=\frac{D}{U}\qquad \text{DPMO}=\frac{D}{U\times O}\times10^6\qquad Z_{LT}=\Phi^{-1}\!\left(1-\frac{\text{DPMO}}{10^6}\right)$$

**Telecom example — billing errors per 1,000 invoices.** An audit of **1,000** invoices finds **48** billing errors. Each invoice has **8** agreed opportunities (customer ID, tariff, usage rating, discounts, VAT, roaming charges, due date, payment allocation).

- DPU $=48/1{,}000=$ **0.048** errors per invoice ("48 per 1,000 invoices").
- DPMO $=48/(1{,}000\times8)\times10^6=$ **6,000**.
- $Z_{LT}=\Phi^{-1}(0.994)\approx$ **2.51** → sigma level ≈ **4.0σ** — very close to the table's 4σ row (6,210 DPMO).

**Per-unit view (supplementary).** If you instead ask "what fraction of *invoices* are error-free?", the Poisson model gives $Y\approx e^{-\text{DPU}}=e^{-0.048}\approx0.953$, so about 4.7% of invoices carry at least one error: $Z\approx1.68$, sigma ≈ 3.2. Both answers are correct — they describe different units (opportunities vs invoices). DPMO-based sigma flatters complex products because it divides by opportunities; that is why the opportunity count must be honest and fixed, and why you must state which basis you are quoting.

**The book's DPU route (p. 225).** The book looks DPU up in the Z-table directly. That is exact when each unit has one opportunity — as in its call-centre example, where each call is either dropped or not, so DPU equals the proportion defective. For multi-opportunity units, convert to DPMO (or to the proportion of defective units) first.`},{id:"sigma-table",heading:"Converting to sigma level: the table, the shift and the book’s example",body:t`The exam's Reference Document gives you tables, so learn to read them in both directions. The book's sigma table (p. 223), recomputed:

| Sigma level ($Z_{ST}$) | Short-term DPMO | Long-term DPMO ($Z_{LT}=Z_{ST}-1.5$) |
|---|---:|---:|
| 1 | 158,655 | 691,462 |
| 2 | 22,750 | 308,538 |
| 3 | 1,350 | 66,807 |
| 4 | 31.7 | 6,210 |
| 5 | 0.3 | 233 |
| 6 | 0.0 | 3.4 |

Attribute data are long-term, so **always enter the table in the long-term DPMO column** and read across to the sigma level. Between rows, interpolate roughly or use the Z-table: $Z_{LT}$ from the tail area, then add 1.5.

**The book's call-centre example (pp. 224–225).** 20,000 calls; 2,666 dropped before being answered.

1. DPU $=2{,}666/20{,}000=$ **0.1333** (one opportunity per call, so also the proportion defective).
2. Table area 0.1335 sits at Z = 1.11 → $Z_{LT}=$ **1.11**.
3. $Z_{ST}=1.11+1.5=$ **2.61**.
4. $C_{pk}\approx2.61/3=$ **0.87**; $P_{pk}\approx1.11/3=$ **0.37**.

> **A printed inconsistency.** The sidebar on the same page says "the above Cpk of .54 is about 1.5 sigma or a 50% yield". That does not match the page's own worked result (Cpk = .87 from $Z_{ST}=2.61$); a Cpk of .54 would need $Z_{ST}\approx1.62$. Trust the worked calculation — it follows the method the book itself sets out — and treat the .54 note as a leftover error. The sidebar's other point stands: a Cpk of at least 1.33 is desired.`},{id:"when-to-use",heading:"When to use discrete capability — and its limits",body:t`**Use it when** the CTQ is genuinely pass/fail or a count: activation success, dropped calls, SLA met/breached per ticket, billing errors, provisioning errors, defects per site survey. Use it also when only attribute data exist yet, as a Measure-phase baseline.

**Prefer continuous capability when you can.** If the defect is defined by a measurement — "ticket breached the 4-hour SLA" — the underlying resolution time is continuous. Converting it to pass/fail throws information away: you need far larger samples to see the same change, and you learn nothing about how close the passing units were to the limit.

**Check the model's assumptions.**

| | Defectives (binomial) | Defects (Poisson) |
|---|---|---|
| Counts | Units with ≥ 1 defect | Defects (several per unit possible) |
| Metric | p, % defective, PPM | DPU, DPMO |
| Assumes | Fixed n, independent units, constant p | Independent defects, constant rate per unit/opportunity |
| Control chart | p-chart / np-chart | u-chart / c-chart |
| Telecom example | Failed activations | Billing errors per 1,000 invoices |

**Sample size matters.** Rare events need large samples: at 1,000 PPM you expect only 1 failure per 1,000 units, so a few hundred observations cannot distinguish 3.5σ from 4.5σ. This is the very reason the book treats attribute capability as long-term.

**In Minitab (supplementary).** *Stat > Quality Tools > Capability Analysis* offers **Binomial** (for defectives) and **Poisson** (for defects) options. Instead of a histogram with Cp and Cpk, the report shows a p-chart or u-chart to check stability, a cumulative plot of the rate against sample number (has the estimate settled down?), the overall % defective or DPU with a confidence interval, and a **process Z** — the long-term Z.bench described above. Read the charts first: if the rate is unstable or the cumulative line is still drifting, the capability number is not yet trustworthy. The exam is closed book with no Minitab, so the hand method with the Z-table and the sigma table remains essential.

**Watch the basis.** Always label results — "17,000 PPM defective activations, $Z_{LT}$ 2.12, ≈ 3.6σ short-term with the 1.5σ convention" — so that nobody compares a per-opportunity sigma with a per-unit one.`}],whyItMatters:t`Most service-operations CTQs are discrete: SLA met or missed, activation success, first-contact resolution, billing accuracy. Discrete capability lets you baseline them in the same sigma language as continuous metrics, compare very different processes on one scale, and set a credible target in the charter ("from 3.6σ to 4.0σ" means cutting failed activations from about 17,000 to about 6,200 per million).

On the exam, expect questions that hand you counts and ask for DPMO, a Z-value or a sigma level, and traps about defects versus defectives, opportunities in the denominator, and which way to apply the 1.5σ shift. The book's call-centre figures (DPU .1333, Z 1.11 → 2.61) are a likely style of question.`,businessExample:{title:"Billing accuracy baseline for a vendor-run billing operation",body:t`A Digital Service Operations team receives a monthly billing-quality report from its managed-services vendor. To baseline capability, a sample of **1,000** invoices is audited against **8** agreed opportunities each; **48** errors are found on **45** invoices.

- DPU = **0.048** (48 errors per 1,000 invoices); DPMO = **6,000** → $Z_{LT}\approx2.51$ → **≈ 4.0σ** (per-opportunity basis).
- Proportion of invoices with at least one error = 45 ÷ 1,000 = **4.5%** → 45,000 PPM defective invoices → $Z_{LT}\approx1.70$ → **≈ 3.2σ** (per-invoice basis).

Both figures are reported, clearly labelled, because the customer experiences *invoices*, while the process team improves *opportunities*. The vendor-facing note is written as a request rather than a verdict: *based on the sample audit, approximately 4.5% of invoices carry at least one billing error; the team is asked to clarify the error categories used in the monthly report and provide supporting evidence so that the opportunity list and the baseline can be agreed.* The agreed 8-opportunity list is then frozen for the after-improvement comparison.`},visual:"discreteSigmaPipeline",visualCaption:"Animated pipeline from counts to sigma level. Defectives: 340 failed of 20,000 activations → p = 0.017 → 17,000 PPM → the normal tail gives Z_LT ≈ 2.12 → + 1.5 → ≈ 3.62σ. Switch to the Defects tab for 48 billing errors on 1,000 invoices × 8 opportunities → DPMO 6,000 → Z_LT ≈ 2.51 → ≈ 4.0σ. All values are computed live from the app’s verified functions.",definitions:[{term:"Attribute (discrete) capability",definition:"Capability estimated from counts of defects or defectives rather than from a mean and standard deviation."},{term:"Defective (unit)",definition:"A unit with at least one defect; counted once. Proportions of defectives follow the binomial distribution."},{term:"Defect",definition:"A single failure to meet a requirement; a unit may contain several. Defect counts follow the Poisson distribution."},{term:"Proportion defective (p)",definition:"Defective units ÷ units inspected; × 10⁶ gives PPM defective."},{term:"Yield",definition:"1 − p: the proportion of units that are good."},{term:"DPU",definition:"Total defects ÷ units; can exceed 1."},{term:"DPMO",definition:"Defects ÷ (units × opportunities per unit) × 10⁶."},{term:"Opportunity",definition:"A measurable, customer-relevant characteristic that can be judged acceptable or not — a chance for a defect."},{term:"Z_LT (long-term Z)",definition:"Φ⁻¹(1 − p) or Φ⁻¹(1 − DPMO/10⁶); attribute data give long-term Z."},{term:"Z_ST / sigma level",definition:"Short-term Z, by convention Z_LT + 1.5; the number usually quoted as “sigma level”."},{term:"Binomial distribution",definition:"Number of defectives in n independent units, each with the same probability p of being defective."},{term:"Poisson distribution",definition:"Number of independent defects occurring at a constant average rate in a unit or area of opportunity."}],comparisons:[{title:"Defectives vs defects capability",headers:["","Defectives (binomial)","Defects (Poisson)"],rows:[["Question","Is the unit good or bad?","How many things are wrong?"],["Rate","p = defectives ÷ units","DPU = defects ÷ units; DPMO per opportunity"],["Out-of-spec measure","PPM defective = p × 10⁶","DPMO"],["Z_LT from","Tail area p","Tail area DPMO ÷ 10⁶"],["Telecom example","340 failed of 20,000 activations → 3.62σ","48 errors, 1,000 invoices × 8 opp. → 4.0σ"]]},{title:"Continuous vs attribute capability",headers:["","Continuous","Attribute / discrete"],rows:[["Inputs","Mean, within σ, overall σ, LSL/USL","Counts of defects or defectives, units, opportunities"],["Normality check","Required","Not applicable — the rate is observed"],["Natural time frame","Short- and long-term both available","Long-term (book p. 223)"],["Main outputs","Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk, expected PPM","p or DPMO, Z_LT, sigma level, implied Cpk ≈ Z_ST/3"],["Data efficiency","High — small samples informative","Low — rare events need large samples"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Cp and Cpk can be calculated from pass/fail data by using the proportion as the mean.",reality:"There is no measurement scale or standard deviation. Discrete capability converts the observed failure rate into a Z-value instead."},{myth:"The Z you calculate from attribute data is short-term.",reality:"Attribute data are long-term (book p. 223). Add 1.5 to estimate the short-term sigma level."},{myth:"DPU can be looked up in the Z-table for any process.",reality:"Only when each unit has one opportunity (DPU = proportion defective), as in the book’s dropped-call example. Otherwise use DPMO or the proportion of defective units."},{myth:"A higher sigma level from DPMO proves the customer gets fewer bad invoices.",reality:"Per-opportunity and per-unit sigma levels differ. Adding opportunities raises DPMO-based sigma without changing a single invoice."},{myth:"A few hundred observations are enough to confirm a 4.5σ attribute process.",reality:"At about 1,350 PPM you expect barely one failure per 740 units; attribute capability needs large samples."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“45 errors were found on 38 invoices” and the question asks for DPMO.",explanation:"DPMO uses all defects (45) divided by units × opportunities; 38 is the count of defectives.",tag:"total-defects"},{trap:"DPMO computed without the opportunities, e.g. DPU × 10⁶.",explanation:"That is defects per million units. Divide by units × opportunities per unit.",tag:"per-opportunity"},{trap:"Attribute data give Z = 2.12; options include 0.62 (2.12 − 1.5) and 3.62.",explanation:"Attribute data are long-term; the sigma level is Z_LT + 1.5 = 3.62. Subtracting goes the wrong way.",tag:"short-vs-long-term"},{trap:"Reading the sigma table with long-term DPMO in the short-term column.",explanation:"6,210 DPMO is 4σ only in the long-term column; in the short-term column 4σ is 31.7 DPMO.",tag:"short-vs-long-term"},{trap:"“Percentage of units defective” in a stem full of defect counts.",explanation:"Now the numerator is defective units, not defects — a binomial proportion.",tag:"defective-units"},{trap:"Choosing a u-chart for the proportion of failed activations.",explanation:"Defectives (pass/fail units) → p-chart; defects per unit → u-chart.",tag:"defects-vs-defectives-chart"},{trap:"Book example: Z_LT = 1.11; “what is Cpk?” with 0.37 and 0.54 among the options.",explanation:"Cpk uses Z_ST = 2.61 → 0.87. 0.37 is Ppk (Z_LT ÷ 3); 0.54 is the book’s inconsistent sidebar figure.",tag:"cp-vs-cpk"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Failed activations: defectives to sigma level",problem:t`20,000 SIM activations; 340 failed. Find the proportion defective, PPM, $Z_{LT}$, sigma level and the implied Ppk and Cpk.`,steps:[t`p = 340 ÷ 20,000 = **0.017** (yield 98.3%); PPM = 0.017 × 10⁶ = **17,000**.`,t`$Z_{LT}$: find Z with right-tail area 0.0170 → **≈ 2.12**.`,t`Sigma level = 2.12 + 1.5 = **≈ 3.62σ**.`,t`Ppk ≈ 2.12 ÷ 3 = **0.71**; Cpk ≈ 3.62 ÷ 3 = **1.21** (book convention, p. 224).`,t`**Exam-tools tip:** in the **standard normal table** of the Reference Document find the body area 0.9830 (or tail 0.0170) — it sits between Z = 2.12 (0.9830) and 2.13; then add 1.5 on the virtual calculator. Cross-check with the **sigma table**: 17,000 DPMO lies between 3σ (66,807) and 4σ (6,210).`],answer:"17,000 PPM · Z_LT ≈ 2.12 · ≈ 3.62σ · Ppk ≈ 0.71 · Cpk ≈ 1.21",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Sigma level = 2.12 − 1.5 = 0.62σ",whyWrong:"Attribute data are already long-term; the shift is added to estimate short-term capability, not subtracted."}},{title:"Billing errors per 1,000 invoices: defects to DPMO to sigma",problem:t`1,000 invoices are audited; 48 billing errors are found; each invoice has 8 opportunities. Find DPU, DPMO, $Z_{LT}$ and sigma level.`,steps:[t`DPU = 48 ÷ 1,000 = **0.048**.`,t`DPMO = 48 ÷ (1,000 × 8) × 10⁶ = 48 ÷ 8,000 × 10⁶ = **6,000**.`,t`$Z_{LT}$ for tail 0.006 → **≈ 2.51**; sigma level ≈ 2.51 + 1.5 = **≈ 4.0σ**.`,t`Sanity check: the table's 4σ row is 6,210 long-term DPMO — 6,000 is just above 4σ.`,t`**Exam-tools tip:** for DPMO questions go straight to the **sigma–DPMO table** (long-term column); use the Z-table only if the stem asks for Z to two decimals.`],answer:"DPU 0.048 · DPMO 6,000 · Z_LT ≈ 2.51 · ≈ 4.0σ",wrongApproach:{calculation:"DPMO = 0.048 × 10⁶ = 48,000 → Z_LT 1.66 → 3.16σ",whyWrong:"This ignores the 8 opportunities per invoice — it is defects per million invoices, not per million opportunities."}},{title:"The book’s dropped-call example (pp. 224–225)",problem:t`A call centre received 20,000 calls in a month; 2,666 were dropped before being answered. Estimate $Z_{LT}$, $Z_{ST}$, Cpk and Ppk.`,steps:[t`DPU = 2,666 ÷ 20,000 = **0.1333**; each call has one opportunity, so this is also the proportion defective.`,t`Z-table: tail area 0.1335 at Z = 1.11 → $Z_{LT}$ = **1.11**.`,t`$Z_{ST}$ = 1.11 + 1.5 = **2.61**.`,t`Cpk ≈ 2.61 ÷ 3 = **0.87**; Ppk ≈ 1.11 ÷ 3 = **0.37** — far below the desired 1.33.`,t`**Exam-tools tip:** in the **standard normal table** look for the tail value nearest 0.1333 (0.1335 at Z = 1.11). Note the book sidebar’s “Cpk .54” does not match this worked answer.`],answer:"Z_LT 1.11 · Z_ST 2.61 · Cpk ≈ 0.87 · Ppk ≈ 0.37",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Cpk = 1.11 ÷ 3 = 0.37",whyWrong:"That is Ppk. Cpk is short-term, so it uses Z_ST = 2.61."}},{title:"Reverse lookup: how many failures does 4σ allow?",problem:t`The charter targets **4σ** (short-term, with the 1.5σ convention) for 20,000 activations per quarter. What is the maximum number of failed activations?`,steps:[t`4σ short-term ↔ $Z_{LT}$ = 2.5 ↔ **≈ 6,210 PPM** long-term (sigma table).`,t`Allowed failures = 6,210 ÷ 10⁶ × 20,000 = 124.2 → **at most 124**.`,t`Today’s 340 failures must fall by about 64% to reach the target.`,t`**Exam-tools tip:** read the **sigma table** right-to-left: sigma level → long-term DPMO → multiply by units ÷ 10⁶.`],answer:"At most 124 failed activations per 20,000 (≈ 6,210 PPM)",wrongApproach:{calculation:"4σ → 31.7 PPM → about 0.6 failures allowed",whyWrong:"31.7 is the short-term DPMO column. Attribute counts are long-term, so use 6,210."}}],lab:"capability",check:[{id:"chk-2.4.3-1",topic:"2.4.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Of **4,000** incident tickets, **120** breached their SLA. Using the 1.5σ convention, what is the approximate sigma level?",options:["1.88σ","3.38σ","0.38σ","4.50σ"],answer:1,explanation:"p = 120 ÷ 4,000 = 0.03 → Z_LT ≈ 1.88 (tail 0.030). Attribute data are long-term, so sigma level = 1.88 + 1.5 ≈ 3.38σ.",whyWrong:["1.88 is the long-term Z before adding the shift.","","Subtracts the 1.5σ shift instead of adding it.","4.5σ would correspond to about 1,350 PPM, not 30,000."],traps:["short-vs-long-term"]},{id:"chk-2.4.3-2",topic:"2.4.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"**1,500** provisioning orders, each with **4** opportunities, contain **90** errors. What is the DPMO?",options:["60,000","15,000","6,000","22,500"],answer:1,explanation:"DPMO = 90 ÷ (1,500 × 4) × 10⁶ = 90 ÷ 6,000 × 10⁶ = 15,000 (≈ 3.67σ).",whyWrong:["60,000 = 90 ÷ 1,500 × 10⁶ — ignores opportunities.","","6,000 slips a factor of 2.5 (divides by 15,000 opportunities).","22,500 = 90 ÷ 4,000 × 10⁶ — wrong denominator."],traps:["per-opportunity"]},{id:"chk-2.4.3-3",topic:"2.4.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!0,stem:"A process shows **6,210** long-term DPMO. Using the standard sigma table (1.5σ shift), what is its sigma level?",options:["2.5σ","3σ","4σ","5σ"],answer:2,explanation:"6,210 long-term DPMO is the 4σ row of the table (Z_LT = 2.5 + 1.5 = 4.0).",whyWrong:["2.5 is Z_LT; the sigma level adds 1.5.","3σ corresponds to 66,807 long-term DPMO.","","5σ corresponds to 233 long-term DPMO."],traps:["short-vs-long-term"]},{id:"chk-2.4.3-4",topic:"2.4.3",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A team records, for each invoice, the **number of billing errors** it contains. Which distribution and capability metric fit best?",options:["Binomial; proportion defective","Poisson; DPU and DPMO","Normal; Cp and Cpk","Binomial; DPMO without opportunities"],answer:1,explanation:"Counting defects per unit (several possible per invoice) is Poisson; capability is expressed as DPU and DPMO.",whyWrong:["Binomial fits good/bad classification of whole invoices, not error counts.","","Counts are discrete; Cp/Cpk need a continuous measurement.","DPMO requires opportunities, and error counts are not binomial."],traps:["total-defects","defective-units"]},{id:"chk-2.4.3-5",topic:"2.4.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!0,stem:"In the training book’s call-centre example, DPU = 0.1333 gives **Z_LT = 1.11**. What is the estimated **Cpk**?",options:["0.37","0.54","0.87","2.61"],answer:2,explanation:"Z_ST = 1.11 + 1.5 = 2.61; Cpk ≈ Z_ST ÷ 3 = 0.87.",whyWrong:["0.37 = 1.11 ÷ 3 is Ppk (long-term).","0.54 is the book sidebar’s figure, inconsistent with its own worked result.","","2.61 is Z_ST, not Cpk — divide by 3."],traps:["cp-vs-cpk","short-vs-long-term"]},{id:"chk-2.4.3-6",topic:"2.4.3",category:"concept",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"Why does the training book treat attribute capability data as **long-term**?",options:["Because attribute data are always normally distributed","Because many samples, collected over a long period, are needed to estimate a defect rate with reasonable confidence","Because Minitab cannot calculate short-term attribute capability","Because the 1.5σ shift only applies to continuous data"],answer:1,explanation:"Book p. 223: attribute data require so many samples that they inevitably span long-term variation, so they are treated as shifted (long-term) data.",whyWrong:["Attribute data are not normal; that is why a different method is used.","","This is not the reason given; the issue is the sample size needed.","The shift is applied to attribute data precisely to estimate short-term capability."],traps:["short-vs-long-term"]}],takeaways:["Pass/fail and count data have no σ to plug into Cp/Cpk; convert the observed rate to a Z-value instead.","Defectives (good/bad units) → binomial → p, % defective, PPM.","Defects (several per unit) → Poisson → DPU, DPMO (divide by units × opportunities).","Z_LT = Φ⁻¹(1 − p) or Φ⁻¹(1 − DPMO/10⁶), read from the standard normal table.","Attribute data are long-term: sigma level = Z_LT + 1.5; enter the sigma table in the long-term DPMO column.","Book convention: Ppk ≈ Z_LT/3, Cpk ≈ Z_ST/3 — call centre: Z 1.11 → 2.61 → Cpk 0.87.","Per-opportunity and per-unit sigma levels differ; always label the basis.","Check stability (p- or u-chart) and use large samples before trusting attribute capability."],flashcards:[{front:"Proportion defective",back:"Defective units ÷ units inspected; × 10⁶ = PPM."},{front:"DPMO",back:"Defects ÷ (units × opportunities) × 10⁶."},{front:"Distribution for defectives",back:"Binomial (fixed n, independent units, constant p)."},{front:"Distribution for defects",back:"Poisson (independent defects at a constant rate per unit)."},{front:"Z_LT from attribute data",back:"Z with right-tail area = p (or DPMO ÷ 10⁶)."},{front:"Attribute data: short- or long-term?",back:"Long-term (book p. 223) — add 1.5 for sigma level."},{front:"6,210 long-term DPMO",back:"4σ (Z_LT 2.5)."},{front:"66,807 long-term DPMO",back:"3σ (Z_LT 1.5)."},{front:"Cpk and Ppk from Z (book p. 224)",back:"Cpk ≈ Z_ST ÷ 3; Ppk ≈ Z_LT ÷ 3."},{front:"Book call centre",back:"2,666 / 20,000 = .1333 → Z_LT 1.11 → Z_ST 2.61 → Cpk .87."},{front:"340 failed of 20,000 activations",back:"17,000 PPM → Z_LT 2.12 → ≈ 3.62σ."},{front:"48 errors, 1,000 invoices, 8 opportunities",back:"DPU 0.048 · DPMO 6,000 · ≈ 4.0σ."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The binomial/Poisson framing, the per-unit Poisson view (Y ≈ e^−DPU) and the sample-size caution are standard Lean Six Sigma knowledge rather than text on the supplied capability pages; the distributions themselves are taught in Analyze (3.1.2).","Book inconsistency: p. 225’s sidebar says “the above Cpk of .54 is about 1.5 sigma or a 50% yield”, which conflicts with the page’s own worked result (Z_ST 2.61, Cpk .87). This lesson follows the worked calculation.","The description of Minitab’s Binomial and Poisson capability reports is general Minitab knowledge; the supplied book pages do not show these reports.","Implied Cpk from attribute data (Z_ST ÷ 3) is a convention from the book (p. 224), not a measured within-subgroup capability.","Telecom figures (activations, invoices, tickets, provisioning orders) are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 8",pdfPage:8,note:"Measure topic 2.4.3 “Attribute & Discrete Capability” (2.4 Process Capability)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 211",pdfPage:14,note:"Capability studies can be conducted on both discrete and continuous data."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 221–222",pdfPage:24,note:"Attribute data “hit a wall” at the normality step; attribute capability steps: DPU → Z-score → Cp & Cpk."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 222–223",pdfPage:25,note:"Z-score and Z-table example (mean 50, σ 3, USL 54 → 91,760 PPM)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 223",pdfPage:26,note:"Attribute data are long-term; add/subtract 1.5; sigma level vs short- and long-term DPMO table."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 224",pdfPage:27,note:"Cpk = Z_ST ÷ 3, Ppk = Z_LT ÷ 3; call-centre data (20,000 calls, 2,666 dropped)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 225",pdfPage:28,note:"DPU .1333 → Z_LT 1.11 → Z_ST 2.61 (Cpk .87); sidebar “Cpk .54” inconsistency; Cpk ≥ 1.33 desired."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 584",pdfPage:87,note:"Glossary: capability may be expressed as yield, defect rate (dpm, dpmo), index or sigma score."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 585",pdfPage:88,note:"Glossary: DPU — total defects ÷ units."}]},Q=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:I},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),f=String.raw,O={id:"2.4.4",module:8,title:"Monitoring Techniques",subtitle:"Keeping watch on the baseline while you improve",minutes:11,objectives:["Explain why the baseline must keep being monitored throughout a DMAIC project, not just measured once.","Choose between run charts, control charts and dashboard KPI trend views for ongoing monitoring.","Build a data collection plan for ongoing measurement, including sampling frequency and a preview of rational subgrouping.","Distinguish leading from lagging indicators, and monitoring outputs (Y) from controlling inputs (X).","Separate alerting thresholds (targets, SLA floors) from control limits, and use each correctly.","Describe how project monitoring flows into the Control-phase control plan and response plan."],overview:f`A baseline is not a photograph you take once in Measure and file away. While the project runs, the process keeps moving: demand grows, a vendor changes staff, a new release lands, and people behave differently simply because they know they are being watched. **Monitoring** keeps a continuous, time-ordered view of the primary metric and its key drivers so that you can tell, at any moment, whether a change in performance is noise, an unrelated special cause, or the effect of your improvement.

This lesson covers the toolkit (run charts, control charts, dashboard trend views), the data collection plan that feeds it, how often to sample, how to group data sensibly, which indicators warn you early, and why an alert threshold is not a control limit. It ends where monitoring becomes permanent: the Control-phase control plan. Detailed SPC charts are taught in [5.2 Statistical Process Control](#/lessons/5.2.1) — here we use them as a monitoring lens.`,sections:[{id:"why-monitor",heading:"Why keep monitoring the baseline during the project?",body:f`The book’s Measure roadmap ends with *assess stability, assess capability, estimate the process sigma level* — and every later phase depends on that baseline staying honest. Ongoing monitoring protects it in four ways:

1. **It separates your effect from everything else.** If first-contact resolution rises after your pilot, was it the pilot, a seasonal drop in complex calls, or a vendor’s new team leader? A time-ordered chart with the baseline period marked shows *when* the change started and whether it lines up with your intervention.
2. **It catches special causes early.** A vendor outage or a bad release in the middle of the project can contaminate the data you will use in Analyze.
3. **It shows whether the process was stable when you measured it.** Capability calculated on an unstable baseline is only a snapshot (lesson [2.4.2](#/lessons/2.4.2)).
4. **It builds the habit and the infrastructure Control will need.** The chart you maintain during the project often becomes the control chart in the control plan.

The SPC chapter of the book makes a useful distinction. Applying control-chart techniques to a process **output** (a Y such as SLA %, MTTR, FCR) is properly called **statistical process monitoring (SPM)**; you can only *control* an output by controlling the inputs (X’s) that drive it, which is **statistical process control (SPC)**. During Measure you mostly *monitor* the Y; as Analyze reveals the vital few X’s, you add them to the monitoring set. The book also lists determining baseline performance and comparing a process before and after a change among the main uses of control charts.`},{id:"toolkit",heading:"The monitoring toolkit: run charts, control charts and dashboards",body:f`| Tool | What it shows | Strength | Limitation | Typical use in the project |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **[[run-chart|Run chart]]** (time series plot with median) | Data in time order | Quick, no assumptions, anyone can read it | No statistical limits; patterns judged by run tests | Early Measure, small data sets, KPI packs |
| **[[control-chart|Control chart]]** (I-MR, Xbar-R, p, u…) | Data in time order with centre line and ±3σ limits from the data | Objective rules separate common from special cause | Needs enough data (≈ 20–25 points) and a correct chart choice | Formal stability check, before/after comparison, Control |
| **Dashboard / KPI trend view** | Several metrics side by side, often with targets and red/amber/green status | Visibility for managers; links Y’s and X’s | Status colours usually reflect **targets**, not stability | Weekly project reviews, operational meetings |

Good monitoring combines them: a dashboard tile that shows the latest value **and** a small control-chart trend behind it, with special-cause signals marked. What you should avoid is the classic monthly pack that compares *this month with last month* — two points can never tell you whether a difference is signal or noise.

Choosing the control chart is a Control-phase skill ([5.2.1 Data Collection for SPC](#/lessons/5.2.1)). As a preview: one value per period (weekly SLA %, daily MTTR) → [[i-mr-chart|I-MR]]; small subgroups of measurements → [[xbar-r-chart|Xbar-R]]; proportion of defective units with varying sample size → [[p-chart|p chart]]; defects per unit → [[u-chart|u chart]].`},{id:"data-plan",heading:"A data collection plan for ongoing measurement",body:f`Monitoring is only as good as the data behind it. Before you start, write a short **data collection plan** — the same questions the book’s control-chart “cookbook” asks (select the characteristic, purpose, collection points, sub-grouping, chart type, measurement method, sampling interval, sample size, how limits are calculated, forms or software, instructions, training).

| Element | Question it answers | Telecom example (FCR) |
|---|---|---|
| Metric & [[operational-definition|operational definition]] | Exactly what is counted? | Contact resolved with no repeat contact on the same issue within 7 days |
| Purpose | Why are we collecting it? | Baseline and verify the knowledge-base pilot |
| Data source & collection point | Where does it come from? | CRM case table, closed cases, all channels |
| Who / how | Who extracts it, manual or automatic? | Automated weekly query; OE analyst checks it |
| Sampling frequency & size | How often, how many? | All closed cases, summarised weekly |
| Stratification factors | What else must be recorded to slice the data later? | Channel, queue, vendor team, call reason, shift |
| Measurement system | Has the data been checked ([[msa|MSA]])? | Attribute agreement on “resolved” coding |
| Display & review | Where is it shown, who reviews it, how often? | I-MR chart on the project dashboard, weekly review |

Two cautions from the book. First, let the process **run normally** while you sample — special treatment during data collection makes the chart meaningless. Second, avoid biased sampling: do not chart only the first shift, only one vendor’s team, or only the hour before the daily call.`},{id:"frequency-subgroups",heading:"Sampling frequency and rational subgrouping (preview)",body:f`**How often?** Sampling frequency is a balance between the cost of collecting data and the cost of missing a shift. The book’s guidance:

- When in doubt, sample **more** often at first, then reduce the frequency once the process has shown it stays in control.
- A rule of thumb: sample **at least ten times more often** than out-of-control conditions occur.
- Given the choice, most practitioners prefer smaller samples taken more frequently over large samples taken rarely.
- Automated data (ticketing systems, network probes) makes frequent sampling cheap — use it.

**How to group?** A [[rational-subgroup|rational subgroup]] is a small set of items produced under very similar conditions over a short time — typically 3 to 5 consecutive units. The idea: put only **common-cause** variation *inside* each subgroup, so that differences *between* subgroups expose special causes. In a service setting, five consecutive tickets from the same queue in the same hour make a sensible subgroup; mixing the internal L1 queue and the vendor’s L2 queue in one subgroup would bury the very difference you might be looking for. The book warns that the most common Xbar-R error is getting the subgrouping wrong. The full treatment is in [5.2.1](#/lessons/5.2.1) and [5.2.3 Xbar-R Chart](#/lessons/5.2.3).`},{id:"leading-thresholds",heading:"Leading indicators, and alert thresholds vs control limits",body:f`**Leading vs lagging.** A [[lagging-indicator|lagging indicator]] tells you what already happened — monthly SLA compliance, customer complaints, churn. A [[leading-indicator|leading indicator]] moves *before* the outcome and gives you time to act — the number of open tickets older than half their SLA clock, repeat-contact rate, alarm volume, technician utilisation. In Y = f(X) terms, leading indicators are usually X’s or early-stage Y’s. A good monitoring set pairs the primary metric (lagging Y) with two or three leading indicators that the team can influence.

**Alert thresholds vs control limits.** Dashboards often turn red when a KPI crosses a target. That target is a **specification-type** limit — the voice of the customer or the business — and it answers *“are we acceptable?”*. Control limits are the voice of the process and answer *“has something changed?”*. You need both, and you must not confuse them:

| | Alert threshold / target | Control limits |
|---|---|---|
| Source | SLA, contract, management decision | Calculated from process data (±3σ within) |
| Question | Is performance acceptable? | Is the process stable — has it changed? |
| A breach means | Customer or business impact; escalate | Special cause; investigate what changed |
| Can be inside the natural band? | Yes — then it will trigger on pure noise | No — by definition it bounds the noise |
| Shown on a control chart? | No; display it on the dashboard instead | Yes |

If an alert threshold sits **inside** the control limits, it will fire on ordinary common-cause variation and invite tampering. If it sits far **outside**, real special causes will happen long before anyone is alerted. Showing both — status colour for the target, special-cause markers for the control chart — is the professional answer.`},{id:"into-control",heading:"From monitoring to Control: the control plan",body:f`Everything you monitor during the project is a rehearsal for the [[control-plan|control plan]]. The book lists five elements: a **documentation plan**, a **monitoring plan**, a **response plan**, a **training plan** and **aligning systems and structures**, with process owners accountable for sustaining the new level of performance.

- The **monitoring plan** states what is charted, where the chart is posted, how often points are plotted, and who reviews it — exactly your data collection plan, now owned by the process owner instead of the project team.
- The **[[response-plan|response plan]]** (out-of-control action plan, OCAP) states who does what when a chart signals: an out-of-control point, non-random behaviour within the limits, or a known failure condition. The book stresses that the power of SPC lies in reacting to signals, not in drawing limits — and in *not* reacting to anything else.
- **Recalculating limits.** After a verified improvement (confirmed with a hypothesis test from Analyze), the centre line and limits are recalculated for the new process; afterwards the book suggests reviewing limits on a set cycle such as every 3, 6 or 12 months.

Lesson links: [5.3.2 Elements of the Control Plan](#/lessons/5.3.2), [5.3.3 Elements of the Response Plan](#/lessons/5.3.3), [5.2.10 Control Methods](#/lessons/5.2.10).`}],whyItMatters:f`Most improvement claims that fall apart in a steering meeting fail on monitoring, not on analysis: the baseline was a single month, the “after” period coincided with a seasonal dip, or the dashboard turned green because a target was moved. Continuous, time-ordered monitoring with the baseline period marked is what lets you say, with evidence, *the process changed in week 21, it was our change, and here is how we will hold it*.

For service operations, monitoring is also where vendor governance becomes fair. Control limits separate the weeks a vendor genuinely needs to explain from ordinary noise, while SLA thresholds remain the contractual yardstick. On the exam, expect questions on what to monitor (Y vs X, leading vs lagging), how often to sample, why control limits are not targets, and how monitoring hands over to the control plan.`,businessExample:{title:"Knowledge-base pilot in a contact centre: proving the shift",body:f`A contact-centre project aims to raise **first-contact resolution (FCR)**. The team charts 20 weeks of baseline FCR on an I-MR chart: centre line **71.96%**, limits **69.09% – 74.83%**, no special causes. The contractual floor is 70%, shown separately on the dashboard as an alert threshold — and the team notes that it sits *inside* the natural band, so an occasional red week could be pure noise.

In week 21 the improved knowledge base and a call-back rule go live. Weekly FCR moves to around 73% — no single week beyond the old UCL, so a points-only view would say “nothing has happened”. By **week 29**, however, nine consecutive weeks sit above the baseline centre line: the shift test fires. Leading indicators tell the same story — repeat contacts within 7 days fall in the same weeks, and the knowledge-base article hit rate rises.

The team confirms the improvement with a hypothesis test, calculates provisional limits for the new process (centre line 73.08%), and writes the monitoring and response plan into the control plan: weekly I-MR chart owned by the contact-centre manager, a response plan for any out-of-control signal, and a review of limits after 20–25 new weeks of data.`},visual:"monitoringDashboard",visualCaption:"A monitoring dashboard for weekly first-contact resolution. Twenty baseline weeks set the frozen I-MR limits (69.09–74.83%, centre 71.96%); the 70% alert threshold is shown separately. The improvement goes live in week 21; no week crosses the old UCL, but by week 29 nine consecutive points sit above the centre line and the shift is flagged. Monitoring then hands over to the control plan with provisional new limits.",definitions:[{term:"Baseline monitoring",definition:"Continuous, time-ordered tracking of the primary metric and key drivers from Measure onwards, so changes can be attributed and special causes caught."},{term:"Statistical process monitoring (SPM)",definition:"Applying control-chart techniques to process outputs (Y’s); the book reserves SPC for controlling the inputs (X’s)."},{term:"Statistical process control (SPC)",definition:"Using control charts and their rules to detect special causes and keep a process stable, ideally applied to the critical X’s."},{term:"Data collection plan",definition:"A written plan stating what is measured, its operational definition, source, who collects it, frequency, sample size, stratification factors and how it is displayed."},{term:"Sampling frequency",definition:"How often data are collected; a balance between sampling cost and the cost of missing a shift (rule of thumb: at least 10× more often than out-of-control events)."},{term:"Rational subgroup",definition:"A small group of items (typically 3–5) produced under very similar conditions over a short time, so only common-cause variation is inside each subgroup."},{term:"Leading indicator",definition:"A measure that changes before the outcome and allows early action (e.g. tickets older than half their SLA clock)."},{term:"Lagging indicator",definition:"A measure of an outcome that has already happened (e.g. monthly SLA compliance)."},{term:"Alert threshold",definition:"A target or SLA-based trigger on a dashboard; a voice-of-the-customer limit that says whether performance is acceptable."},{term:"Control plan",definition:"The Control-phase document describing how the improved process is monitored and controlled: documentation, monitoring, response, training, systems and structures."},{term:"Response plan (OCAP)",definition:"Pre-agreed actions, owners and documentation for when monitoring shows an out-of-control signal or a known failure condition."}],comparisons:[{title:"Run chart vs control chart vs dashboard trend view",headers:["","Run chart","Control chart","Dashboard KPI view"],rows:[["Reference lines","Median","Centre line ± 3σ control limits","Targets / thresholds (RAG status)"],["Answers","Any obvious pattern over time?","Stable? Special cause? Before vs after?","Are we on target this period?"],["Data needed","A handful of points","≈ 20–25 points to set limits","Latest value plus history"],["Main risk","Subjective reading","Wrong chart type or bad subgroups","Reacting to noise; month-vs-month comparisons"]]},{title:"Alert threshold vs control limit",headers:["","Alert threshold","Control limit"],rows:[["Voice of","Customer / business","Process"],["Set by","SLA, contract, management","Calculation from data"],["FCR example","Floor of 70%","LCL 69.09%, UCL 74.83%"],["Breach means","Unacceptable performance — escalate","Something changed — investigate"]]},{title:"Leading vs lagging indicators in service operations",headers:["Outcome (lagging)","Leading indicator(s)","Why it leads"],rows:[["Monthly SLA compliance %","Open tickets older than 50% of SLA clock","Breaches are visible days before month-end"],["Ticket backlog","Daily arrivals vs closures; aged-ticket count","Backlog grows only when arrivals exceed closures"],["First-contact resolution","Knowledge-base hit rate; transfers per contact","Agents who find answers resolve first time"],["Customer complaints","Repeat contacts within 7 days","Repeat contacts precede formal complaints"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"The baseline is measured once in Measure and then fixed.",reality:"The baseline must be monitored throughout the project; otherwise seasonal effects, special causes or unrelated changes are mistaken for your improvement."},{myth:"A dashboard turning red means the process has a special cause.",reality:"Red usually means a target was missed. Whether the process changed is a control-limit question; a target inside the natural band can turn red on pure noise."},{myth:"Comparing this month with last month shows whether we improved.",reality:"Two points cannot separate signal from noise. Use a time-ordered chart with limits and run rules."},{myth:"If no point crosses the old control limit, the improvement has not worked.",reality:"Small sustained shifts are caught by run rules (e.g. nine in a row above the centre line) long before a single point crosses 3σ."},{myth:"Monitoring the output Y is the same as controlling the process.",reality:"Monitoring the Y (SPM) shows results; control comes from managing the critical X’s that drive it (SPC on inputs)."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“Set the control limits at the SLA target so the chart alarms when we breach.”",explanation:"Control limits are calculated from the data. The SLA target is an alert threshold (voice of the customer) and belongs on the dashboard, not as a control limit.",tag:"spec-vs-control-limits"},{trap:"After an improvement, nine weeks sit above the old centre line but none above the UCL — options include “no evidence of change”.",explanation:"Nine in a row on one side is a shift signal. Run rules detect small sustained shifts that a beyond-3σ test misses.",tag:"after"},{trap:"“How often should we sample?” with options built around cost alone.",explanation:"Frequency balances sampling cost against the cost of missing a shift; the book’s rule of thumb is at least 10× more often than out-of-control conditions occur — start frequent, then reduce."},{trap:"Limits recalculated every week using all data, including the post-change period, to “keep them current”.",explanation:"Freeze the baseline limits to detect the change; recalculate only after a verified, sustained change. Rolling limits absorb the shift and hide it.",tag:"before"},{trap:"“Which is the BEST leading indicator of monthly SLA breaches?” — options include last month’s SLA %.",explanation:"Last month’s SLA % is lagging. A count of tickets already past half their SLA clock moves before the breach.",tag:"best"},{trap:"A subgroup formed by taking one ticket from each of five different queues.",explanation:"That mixes sources of variation inside the subgroup. Rational subgroups are consecutive items made under the same conditions."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Freeze baseline limits, then detect the shift",problem:f`Weekly FCR baseline (weeks 1–20) gives X̄ = 71.955% and a sum of 19 moving ranges of 20.5. After the week-21 change, weeks 21–30 are 74.2, 73.2, 72.6, 72.8, 72.9, 72.8, 73.3, 72.8, 73.3, 72.9. Calculate the baseline limits and decide when a shift is detected.`,steps:["MR̄ = 20.5 ÷ 19 = **1.0789**; half-width = 2.66 × 1.0789 = 2.870.","UCL = 71.955 + 2.870 = **74.83%**; LCL = 71.955 − 2.870 = **69.09%**. Freeze these limits.","Test 1: the highest new value is 74.2% < 74.83% → no point beyond the UCL.","Test 2: every week from 21 onward is above the centre line 71.96%. The ninth consecutive point is **week 29** → shift detected.","Act: confirm with a hypothesis test, then set provisional new limits (weeks 21–30: CL 73.08%, limits 71.93–74.23%) and recalculate once 20–25 weeks are available."],answer:"Baseline limits 69.09–74.83% (CL 71.96%); shift detected at week 29 by the nine-in-a-row rule, although no point crossed the UCL.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“No week exceeded 74.83%, so the knowledge-base pilot made no difference.”",whyWrong:"Only Test 1 was applied. A sustained shift of about one σ rarely produces a point beyond 3σ; the run rules exist precisely to catch it."}},{title:"Alert threshold vs control limit on ticket backlog",problem:f`Daily open backlog for 20 days gives X̄ = 180.5 tickets and ΣMR = 191 over 19 moving ranges. Management’s capacity alert is set at 230 tickets. On day 21 the backlog is 214. What should happen?`,steps:["MR̄ = 191 ÷ 19 = **10.05**; 2.66 × 10.05 = 26.74.","UCL = 180.5 + 26.74 = **207.2**; LCL = 180.5 − 26.74 = **153.8**.","Day 21 = 214 > 207.2 → **special cause**: something has changed (an arrivals spike, a vendor team short-staffed, a new fault type).","214 < 230 → the capacity alert has **not** fired; the customer is not yet affected.","Response: investigate the cause now, while there is still headroom — this is exactly the early warning the control chart provides."],answer:"Investigate a special cause (214 > UCL 207.2) even though the 230 capacity alert has not been reached.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“Backlog is below the 230 alert, so no action is required.”",whyWrong:"The alert threshold answers “is it acceptable?”, not “has the process changed?”. Waiting for the threshold throws away the early warning."}},{title:"Choosing a sampling frequency",problem:f`Provisioning failures caused by vendor configuration errors have historically disrupted the process about twice a month — roughly once every 10 working days. The current practice is a monthly SLA report. How often should the provisioning error rate be sampled during the project?`,steps:["Out-of-control conditions occur about once per 10 working days.","Rule of thumb: sample at least 10× more often → at least once per working day.","Data are automated in the provisioning system, so sampling more often (per shift) costs almost nothing — start there, as the book advises.","Reduce the frequency later only if the process demonstrates it stays in control."],answer:"At least daily — preferably per shift at the start — not monthly.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“The monthly SLA report already shows the error rate, so one point per month is enough.”",whyWrong:"With roughly two disruptions per month, a monthly point averages them away; it could take a year to collect enough points to set limits, and shifts would be found far too late."}}],lab:"stability",check:[{id:"chk-2.4.4-1",topic:"2.4.4",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Five weekly SLA compliance values are 96.0, 95.5, 96.4, 95.8 and 96.2% (mean 95.98%). What is the upper control limit of the Individuals chart?",options:["97.78%","97.58%","97.94%","96.58%"],answer:1,explanation:"Moving ranges 0.5, 0.9, 0.6, 0.4 → MR̄ = 2.4 ÷ 4 = 0.6. UCL = 95.98 + 2.66 × 0.6 = 97.58%.",whyWrong:["97.78 uses 3 × MR̄ instead of 2.66 × MR̄.","","97.94 uses the MR-chart constant 3.267.","96.58 just adds MR̄ to the mean."]},{id:"chk-2.4.4-2",topic:"2.4.4",category:"concept",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"A team wants its FCR control chart to alarm whenever FCR falls below the contractual 70%. What is the best advice?",options:["Set the LCL at 70%","Keep calculated control limits on the chart and show the 70% floor separately as an alert threshold","Replace the control chart with a red/amber/green tile","Set both limits at ±3 percentage points around 70%"],answer:1,explanation:"Control limits are the voice of the process and must be calculated from the data; the 70% floor is a voice-of-the-customer threshold. Show both, but keep them distinct.",whyWrong:["That turns a specification into a control limit — the classic trap.","","A RAG tile shows target status only; it cannot separate common from special cause.","Control limits are not set around a target."],traps:["spec-vs-control-limits"]},{id:"chk-2.4.4-3",topic:"2.4.4",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"After an improvement, nine consecutive weekly points fall above the baseline centre line, but none exceed the baseline UCL. What is the **best** conclusion?",options:["No change — all points are within the limits","A shift has occurred; verify it and consider recalculating limits for the new process","The control limits are wrong and should be widened","The process has become incapable"],answer:1,explanation:"Nine in a row on one side of the centre line is a special-cause shift. If the direction is the intended one, confirm it (e.g. hypothesis test) and set new limits.",whyWrong:["Points-beyond-limits is only one test; run rules detect sustained shifts.","","Limits are not widened to hide signals.","Capability compares with the specification; nothing here suggests the spec is being missed."],traps:["after"]},{id:"chk-2.4.4-4",topic:"2.4.4",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which is the **best** leading indicator of monthly incident SLA breaches?",options:["Last month’s SLA compliance %","Number of open tickets already past 50% of their SLA clock","Customer satisfaction survey score","Total incidents closed last quarter"],answer:1,explanation:"Tickets well into their SLA clock are about to breach, giving time to act. The others describe outcomes that have already happened.",whyWrong:["That is a lagging measure of the same outcome.","","CSAT lags the service experience and is influenced by many factors.","A historical volume total does not warn of upcoming breaches."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-2.4.4-5",topic:"2.4.4",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Out-of-control conditions in a process occur roughly once a week. According to the common rule of thumb, how often should the process at least be sampled?",options:["Once a month","Once a week","About ten times a week (e.g. twice per working day)","Only when a customer complains"],answer:2,explanation:"Sample at least ten times more often than out-of-control conditions occur: once a week × 10 → about ten samples a week.",whyWrong:["Monthly sampling would average several special causes into one point.","Sampling at the same rate as the disturbances cannot reliably detect them.","","Complaint-driven sampling is reactive and biased."]},{id:"chk-2.4.4-6",topic:"2.4.4",category:"dmaic-boundary",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"In the Control phase, the monitoring developed during the project is formalised **primarily** in which document?",options:["The project charter","The control plan (with its monitoring and response plans)","The FMEA","The SIPOC"],answer:1,explanation:"The control plan contains the monitoring plan (what, how, how often, who) and the response plan (what to do when a signal appears).",whyWrong:["The charter is a Define document.","","FMEA feeds the response plan but is not the monitoring document itself.","SIPOC is a high-level process scoping tool."],traps:["primary"]}],takeaways:["Monitor the baseline continuously from Measure onwards — a single snapshot cannot separate your effect from noise, seasons or special causes.","Run charts give a fast first look; control charts add objective limits and rules; dashboards add visibility — combine them.","Write a data collection plan: definition, source, owner, frequency, sample size, stratification factors, MSA status, display.","Sample at least 10× more often than out-of-control events occur; start frequent, reduce later. Rational subgroups keep only common cause inside each subgroup.","Pair the lagging primary metric with leading indicators the team can influence; monitor Y’s (SPM), control X’s (SPC).","Alert thresholds (targets, SLA floors) and control limits answer different questions — show both, never merge them.","Freeze baseline limits to detect a change; recalculate only after a verified, sustained shift.","Project monitoring becomes the control plan’s monitoring and response plans, owned by the process owner."],flashcards:[{front:"Why monitor the baseline during the project?",back:"To attribute changes to the right cause, catch special causes early, confirm stability and prepare Control."},{front:"SPM vs SPC (book)",back:"SPM = control-chart techniques on outputs (Y). SPC = controlling the inputs (X) that drive the Y."},{front:"Sampling frequency rule of thumb",back:"At least 10× more often than out-of-control conditions occur; start frequent, reduce once in control."},{front:"Rational subgroup",back:"Small group (3–5) of consecutive items made under the same conditions — only common cause inside."},{front:"Leading indicator",back:"Moves before the outcome, giving time to act (e.g. tickets past 50% of SLA clock)."},{front:"Lagging indicator",back:"Records an outcome already happened (e.g. monthly SLA %)."},{front:"Alert threshold vs control limit",back:"Threshold = target (voice of customer: acceptable?). Control limit = calculated (voice of process: changed?)."},{front:"Why freeze baseline limits?",back:"Rolling limits absorb a shift and hide it; recalculate only after a verified change."},{front:"Shift test",back:"Nine points in a row on the same side of the centre line."},{front:"Five elements of a control plan (book)",back:"Documentation, monitoring, response, training, aligning systems and structures."},{front:"Response plan / OCAP",back:"Pre-agreed triggers, actions, owners and records for when a chart signals."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["The supplied book has no single chapter titled “Monitoring Techniques”; this lesson draws on the Measure stability material (pp. 211–231) and the Control-phase SPC and control-plan chapters (pp. 516–571), where monitoring, sampling frequency, rational subgroups and the monitoring/response plans are described.","Leading vs lagging indicators, the caution against month-vs-month comparisons and the alert-threshold vs control-limit table are written from general Lean Six Sigma and performance-management practice; they are consistent with, but not quoted from, the book.","The book’s “at least 20 samples” guidance appears for P charts (p. 521); the 20–25 point figure used here is a common general guideline for setting limits.","All FCR, backlog, SLA and provisioning figures are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data. Every limit and signal was computed with the app’s verified statistics engine."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 8",pdfPage:8,note:"Measure topic 2.4.4 “Monitoring Techniques”."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 211",pdfPage:14,note:"Time series plots and control charts check stability; collect data over a representative reference frame."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 230",pdfPage:33,note:"Measure roadmap: assess stability, assess capability, estimate process sigma level; Control: implement control plan."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 231",pdfPage:34,note:"Measure checklist: where are the data collection points; stability assessment questions."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 516",pdfPage:19,note:"SPC on outputs is statistical process monitoring (SPM); control charts determine baseline performance and compare before/after a change."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 517",pdfPage:20,note:"Sampling must not bias the chart; sampling frequency; rational subgroups of 3–5 units; unbiased sampling (not just one shift or vendor)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 525",pdfPage:28,note:"Control limits unrelated to customer specs (VOP vs VOC); the 13-step control chart cookbook."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 526",pdfPage:29,note:"Y = f(X): monitor the Y, control the X’s; consider SPC on the vital few X’s."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"pp. 528–529",pdfPage:31,note:"Subgroups should contain only common-cause variation; sampling frequency balances cost vs missed shifts; sample ≥ 10× more often than out-of-control conditions."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 530",pdfPage:33,note:"Control chart selection by data type and subgroup size; SPC on X’s vs SPM on CTQs."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 544",pdfPage:47,note:"The power of SPC is reacting to out-of-control signals with an OCAP; do not adjust on every point."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 553",pdfPage:56,note:"Recalculate centre line and limits after a verified shift (confirmed with hypothesis testing); periodic review every 3, 6 or 12 months."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 564",pdfPage:67,note:"Five control plan elements: documentation, monitoring, response, training, aligning systems and structures."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"pp. 568–571",pdfPage:71,note:"Monitoring plan purpose and sustainment; control charts posted and plotted in real time; visual management alerts; response plan triggers and components."}]},G=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:O},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"}));export{D as _,q as a,W as b,_ as c,E as d,N as e,F as f,X as g,j as h,Y as i,B as j,V as k,Z as l,U as m,Q as n,G as o};
